window.DORINA_DOCS=[{"slug":"readme","file":"README.md","title":"Package Guide","blurb":"Start here. What the package is, the reading order, and the boundary that governs everything.","track":"Orientation","n":1,"html":"<h1 id=\"dorina-salman-mental-health-management-authority-package\">Dorina Salman — Mental Health Management Authority Package</h1>\n<p><strong>Prepared for:</strong> Dorina Salman (Lebanon) <strong>Package purpose:</strong> A complete, ready-to-execute strategy to build Dorina's public authority in <strong>mental health management</strong> — meaning practical, non-clinical workplace wellbeing education: stress literacy, burnout prevention, boundaries, routines, emotional organization, and help-seeking. <strong>Date:</strong> July 2026</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"what-this-package-is-and-is-not\">What this package is (and is not)</h2>\n<p>This package positions Dorina as a <strong>workplace wellbeing educator and mental health literacy advocate</strong> — a bridge between administrative systems expertise and workplace wellbeing.</p>\n<p>It deliberately does <strong>not</strong> position Dorina as a therapist, psychologist, counselor, clinician, or treatment provider, because no clinical credential is on file. Every document in this package is built on that boundary. Educational content only; not therapy, diagnosis, crisis support, medical, legal, or HR/legal advice. Crisis situations are always referred to emergency services or qualified professionals.</p>\n<p>This boundary is not a limitation — it is the strategy. The world's most trusted workplace wellbeing voices are educators and advocates, and the WHO explicitly recommends worker mental health literacy, awareness-building, and stress-management skills training as core workplace actions. That is exactly the lane this package builds.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-reading-order\">Recommended reading order</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Document</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>Read when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>SENDABLE_SUMMARY_FOR_DORINA.md</code></td><td>The warm, human explanation of the strategy and why the positioning is what it is</td><td>First — 10 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>STRATEGIC_PROPOSAL_MENTAL_HEALTH_AUTHORITY.md</code></td><td>The full proposal: vision, phases, 12-month roadmap, revenue paths, success metrics</td><td>Day 1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>POSITIONING_AND_BRAND_ARCHITECTURE.md</code></td><td>Niche, title options, audience, personal story, authority ladder, boundary language</td><td>Day 1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code></td><td>The safety rails: what Dorina can and cannot say, disclaimers, crisis referral protocol</td><td>Before publishing anything</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code></td><td>Full LinkedIn profile rewrite, connection strategy, posting system, approval workflow</td><td>Week 1</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code></td><td>Month-by-month themes, weekly cadence, sample posts, Lebanon seasonal hooks, KPIs</td><td>Week 1</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code></td><td>Workshop portfolio, session designs, facilitator guide, logistics, participant journey</td><td>Before first workshop</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code></td><td>Ready-to-use exercises, worksheets, opening/closing scripts, safety disclaimers</td><td>Before first workshop</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code></td><td>Funnel, offers, landing page copy, DM scripts, launch plan, partnerships, nurture</td><td>Month 1–2</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><code>OPPORTUNITY_MAP_LEBANON.md</code></td><td>Lebanon-specific opportunities by sector, season, and partnership channel</td><td>Month 1–2</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code></td><td>The approval-gated drafting and posting workflow — nothing publishes without Dorina's sign-off</td><td>When scaling content</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code></td><td>Engineered AI prompts for every content, workshop, marketing, graphics, calendar, and automation asset</td><td>Ongoing</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><code>INSTAGRAM_EXTENSION_ENGINE.md</code></td><td>The later Instagram expansion: formats, repurposing engine, approval workflow</td><td>Month 7+</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td><code>EXTERNAL_STRATEGIC_REVIEW.md</code></td><td>External strategic review findings and improvements incorporated</td><td>Final review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"the-strategy-in-one-paragraph\">The strategy in one paragraph</h2>\n<p>Dorina rebuilds her LinkedIn presence around one clear promise — <em>practical mental health management for working professionals in Lebanon and the region</em> — using her 12+ years of administrative and executive-support experience as her unique lens: <strong>she knows how workplaces actually run</strong>, and she translates wellbeing science communicated by authorities like the WHO into systems, routines, and habits people can actually use at a desk. She earns authority through consistent educational content (3 posts/week), then converts attention into non-clinical workshops for companies and teams, supported by partnerships with HR communities and organizations aligned with Lebanon's National Mental Health Programme ecosystem. Every piece of content and every workshop carries clear educational boundaries and referral pathways.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-base\">Evidence base</h2>\n<p>All factual claims in this package trace to four authoritative sources:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>WHO — Mental health at work (fact sheet):</strong> <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li><strong>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li><strong>Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li><strong>WHO — World Mental Health Day (October 10):</strong> <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<p>Where a document needs information we do not yet have (e.g., Dorina's current role details, preferred languages for delivery, budget), it says so explicitly in a <strong>\"What we still need from Dorina\"</strong> section rather than inventing it.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-start-first-7-days\">How to start (first 7 days)</h2>\n<ol><li>Read documents 1–4 above.</li><li>Answer the consolidated questions in <code>SENDABLE_SUMMARY_FOR_DORINA.md</code> (§ \"What we still need from you\").</li><li>Approve or adjust the positioning title in <code>POSITIONING_AND_BRAND_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>.</li><li>Apply the LinkedIn profile rewrite from <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code>.</li><li>Approve the first month of the content calendar and begin the approval-gated posting workflow.</li></ol>\n<p><em>This package is educational and strategic material. It does not constitute medical, psychological, legal, or HR advice.</em></p>","toc":[{"id":"what-this-package-is-and-is-not","text":"What this package is (and is not)","lvl":2},{"id":"recommended-reading-order","text":"Recommended reading order","lvl":2},{"id":"the-strategy-in-one-paragraph","text":"The strategy in one paragraph","lvl":2},{"id":"evidence-base","text":"Evidence base","lvl":2},{"id":"how-to-start-first-7-days","text":"How to start (first 7 days)","lvl":2}],"words":772,"mins":3},{"slug":"sendable-summary-for-dorina","file":"SENDABLE_SUMMARY_FOR_DORINA.md","title":"Executive Summary for Dorina","blurb":"The warm, plain-English explanation of the strategy and the first decisions.","track":"Orientation","n":2,"html":"<h1 id=\"dear-dorina-a-summary-of-your-authority-strategy\">Dear Dorina — A Summary of Your Authority Strategy</h1>\n<p>Welcome to your strategy package. Before you open the bigger documents, this note explains — in plain language — what we built for you, why it is shaped the way it is, and what we need from you to make it fully yours.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"the-big-idea\">The big idea</h2>\n<p>You asked for a path to becoming an authority in <strong>mental health management</strong>. We built you one — and we built it on the strongest possible foundation: honesty about what you offer.</p>\n<p>Here is the truth that makes this strategy work rather than break: you are not a therapist, and you never need to pretend to be one. The most valuable, most missing voice in workplace mental health is not another clinician — it is the person who understands <strong>how work actually works</strong>. You have spent more than a decade inside demanding professional environments as an executive administrator. You know deadlines, difficult inboxes, invisible labor, pressure that never announces itself, and the systems that either protect people or quietly grind them down.</p>\n<p>That is your authority. The strategy positions you as a <strong>Workplace Wellbeing Educator and Mental Health Literacy Advocate</strong> — someone who translates guidance from bodies like the World Health Organization into practical systems, routines, boundaries, and habits that working people in Lebanon can actually use.</p>\n<p>And the need is enormous and documented. According to the WHO, an estimated 12 billion working days are lost every year to depression and anxiety, at a cost of about US$1 trillion in lost productivity, and 15% of working-age adults were estimated to live with a mental disorder in 2019. The WHO's recommended workplace responses include exactly what this plan trains you to deliver: mental health literacy and awareness to reduce stigma, and practical stress-management skills. You would be doing recognized, recommended work — not improvising.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-the-positioning-is-deliberately-non-clinical\">Why the positioning is deliberately non-clinical</h2>\n<p>Because no clinical license is part of your profile, everything in this package draws one bright line:</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>You educate. You do not diagnose, treat, counsel, or handle crises. Anyone in distress is warmly referred to qualified professionals and emergency services.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>This line is your greatest asset. It protects the people you serve, it reduces legal/reputational risk when reviewed with local counsel, and it makes you <em>more</em> bookable, not less — companies and HR teams can invite an educator without the liability questions a clinical claim would raise. Lebanon's Ministry of Public Health runs a National Mental Health Programme with workplace campaigns and anti-stigma materials; your work aligns with and points toward that national ecosystem instead of competing with it.</p>\n<p>Every document you'll read repeats this boundary. Please never let anyone — including an enthusiastic client — pull you across it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"whats-in-the-package\">What's in the package</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>A full strategic proposal</strong> — your 12-month roadmap from a quiet LinkedIn profile to a recognized regional voice, with phases, milestones, and revenue paths (paid corporate workshops, partnerships, and eventually digital products).</li><li><strong>Positioning and brand architecture</strong> — your title options, your story, your audience, and your signature framework: <strong>the DESK Method (proposed)</strong> (Detect, Establish, Support, Keep) — a proposed, brandable, non-clinical framework that turns your administrative superpower into your wellbeing teaching method.</li><li><strong>A LinkedIn authority engine</strong> — a complete profile rewrite (headline, About, experience, featured), a connection-building strategy that respects your starting point of ~4 connections, and a posting system.</li><li><strong>A 12-month content calendar</strong> — three posts a week, monthly themes, seasonal Lebanese hooks, World Mental Health Day (October 10) as your annual flagship moment, sample posts already written, and KPIs.</li><li><strong>A complete workshop system</strong> — a portfolio of four non-clinical workshops plus a lunch-and-learn format, with facilitator guides, exercises, handouts, opening and closing scripts, safety language, and follow-up sequences.</li><li><strong>Marketing funnel and campaigns</strong> — offers, landing page copy, outreach scripts, a launch plan, and partnership targets.</li><li><strong>A Lebanon opportunity map</strong> — where the demand is, by sector and season.</li><li><strong>An approval-first automation workflow</strong> — AI drafts content and graphics; <strong>nothing is ever posted until you have approved both the words and the visual</strong>. You stay in full control of your name and voice.</li><li><strong>A premium prompt library</strong> — ready-made AI prompts for every asset in this system, so future content can be generated quickly without losing your voice or your boundaries.</li><li><strong>Risk, ethics, and referral protocol</strong> — the exact disclaimer language, the prohibited claims list, and what to do if someone discloses distress in a workshop or in your DMs.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"how-your-life-changes-practically\">How your life changes, practically</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Weeks 1–2:</strong> You update your LinkedIn profile using the rewrite we prepared, and quietly begin connecting with HR professionals, office managers, and colleagues in Lebanon.</li><li><strong>Months 1–3:</strong> You post three times a week (drafts are prepared for your approval — expect ~30 minutes of review time per week). You start being seen.</li><li><strong>Months 3–6:</strong> You deliver your first free pilot workshops to build testimonials, then your first paid sessions.</li><li><strong>Months 6–12:</strong> You raise rates, build partnerships, anchor your year around World Mental Health Day in October, and open the Instagram extension when LinkedIn is running smoothly.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-still-need-from-you\">What we still need from you</h2>\n<p>The plan works today, but these answers will sharpen it. Nothing here blocks the start.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Current role:</strong> Your LinkedIn shows Executive Assistant at Sami Nahas Law Firm (Feb 2014 – present), and separately we understand you may now be an Administrator at TiKiT. Which is current? Exact titles and dates so your profile is factually clean.</li><li><strong>Third language:</strong> Your profile implies a third language beyond Arabic and English — which one, and at what level?</li><li><strong>Your \"why\":</strong> Is there a personal (comfortable-to-share) reason workplace wellbeing matters to you? Your story is the heart of your About section — we drafted a version, but your truth will beat our draft.</li><li><strong>Training appetite:</strong> Would you complete a recognized non-clinical certificate (e.g., psychological first aid or mental health awareness training offered within the WHO/national programme ecosystem)? It strengthens credibility while staying safely non-clinical.</li><li><strong>Delivery language:</strong> Workshops in Arabic, English, or bilingual? Our materials assume bilingual with Arabic-first slides for local audiences.</li><li><strong>Availability &amp; format:</strong> Hours per week you can commit; comfort with in-person vs. online delivery; access to a venue through your employer or network.</li><li><strong>Photography &amp; visuals:</strong> A professional headshot and 2–3 workplace photos; brand color preferences.</li><li><strong>Approval channel:</strong> WhatsApp, email, or a shared document for approving weekly content drafts? (See the approval workflow document.)</li><li><strong>Employer alignment:</strong> Does your current employer know and support this personal brand? Any conflict-of-interest constraints we should design around?</li><li><strong>Budget:</strong> Roughly what can you invest monthly (design tools, scheduling tool, possible certificate course)? The plan includes a zero-budget path, but knowing helps.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"one-promise-before-you-begin\">One promise before you begin</h2>\n<p>Everything in this package was built from four authoritative sources — two WHO fact sheets, the WHO World Mental Health Day campaign page, and Lebanon's Ministry of Public Health National Mental Health Programme — plus your own visible profile facts. Nothing clinical was invented, and no statistic appears anywhere in these documents that doesn't trace back to those sources. When you speak from this material, you can do so with a straight back.</p>\n<p>We're proud of this plan. More importantly, it is <em>safe to be proud of</em> — for you and for every person who will sit in your workshops.</p>\n<p>Warmly, Your strategy team</p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"sources\">Sources</h3>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon MoPH — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<p><em>Educational and strategic material only — not medical, psychological, legal, or HR advice.</em></p>","toc":[{"id":"the-big-idea","text":"The big idea","lvl":2},{"id":"why-the-positioning-is-deliberately-non-clinical","text":"Why the positioning is deliberately non-clinical","lvl":2},{"id":"whats-in-the-package","text":"What's in the package","lvl":2},{"id":"how-your-life-changes-practically","text":"How your life changes, practically","lvl":2},{"id":"what-we-still-need-from-you","text":"What we still need from you","lvl":2},{"id":"one-promise-before-you-begin","text":"One promise before you begin","lvl":2},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources","lvl":3}],"words":1294,"mins":6},{"slug":"strategic-proposal-mental-health-authority","file":"STRATEGIC_PROPOSAL_MENTAL_HEALTH_AUTHORITY.md","title":"Strategic Proposal","blurb":"Vision, evidence base, the DESK Method, three engines, 12-month roadmap, KPIs, risk register.","track":"Strategy","n":3,"html":"<h1 id=\"strategic-proposal-building-dorina-salmans-authority-in-ment\">Strategic Proposal: Building Dorina Salman's Authority in Mental Health Management</h1>\n<p><strong>Prepared for:</strong> Dorina Salman — Lebanon <strong>Scope:</strong> 12-month authority-building strategy, non-clinical positioning <strong>Status:</strong> Ready for Dorina's review and approval</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-executive-summary\">1. Executive summary</h2>\n<p>This proposal describes a 12-month, phased plan to establish Dorina Salman as a recognized voice in <strong>mental health management</strong> — defined throughout this package as <em>practical, non-clinical workplace wellbeing education</em>: stress literacy, burnout prevention, boundaries, routines, emotional organization, and help-seeking behavior.</p>\n<p>The plan converts Dorina's genuine, verifiable assets — 12+ years of executive administration experience, a business administration degree, Arabic/English bilingualism, and deep familiarity with Lebanese professional life — into a differentiated authority position that no clinical credential is required to hold, and that global health authorities explicitly endorse as necessary work.</p>\n<p><strong>The core thesis:</strong> The workplace mental health field has many clinicians and few translators. Dorina becomes the translator — the person who turns wellbeing guidance from authorities like the WHO into desk-level systems working people actually use. Authority is built through consistent LinkedIn education, converted into revenue through corporate workshops, and compounded through partnerships aligned with Lebanon's national mental health ecosystem.</p>\n<p><strong>Twelve-month outcome targets</strong> (defined fully in §8):</p>\n<ul><li>A rebuilt LinkedIn presence: from ~4 connections to 1,500+ relevant connections and 2,500+ followers.</li><li>140+ published educational posts with a consistent voice and visual identity.</li><li>8–12 workshops delivered (2–3 free pilots, then paid), with collected testimonials.</li><li>3+ active partnerships (HR communities, companies, aligned organizations).</li><li>A flagship World Mental Health Day (October 10) campaign delivered.</li><li>A repeatable, approval-gated content engine that costs Dorina ~2–3 hours per week.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-this-why-now-why-dorina\">2. Why this, why now, why Dorina</h2>\n<h3 id=\"21-the-documented-need\">2.1 The documented need</h3>\n<p>The World Health Organization's <em>Mental health at work</em> fact sheet establishes the scale of the problem this strategy addresses:</p>\n<ul><li>Almost <strong>60% of the world population is in work</strong>.</li><li><strong>15% of working-age adults</strong> were estimated to have a mental disorder in 2019.</li><li>An estimated <strong>12 billion working days are lost every year</strong> to depression and anxiety, costing <strong>US$1 trillion per year</strong> in lost productivity.</li><li>Workplace risks to mental health include excessive workloads and work pace, understaffing, long or inflexible hours, lack of control over one's work, poor physical conditions, and organizational culture problems.</li></ul>\n<p>Critically for this strategy, the WHO states that <strong>effective actions exist</strong> — to prevent risks, to protect and promote mental health at work, and to support workers — and its recommended actions include <strong>manager training, worker mental health literacy and awareness (to improve knowledge and reduce stigma), and interventions that build stress-management skills</strong>. These recommendations describe <em>educational</em> work. They are precisely the lane Dorina will occupy.</p>\n<p>The WHO's <em>Mental health: strengthening our response</em> fact sheet adds the framing that gives the brand its moral center: <strong>mental health is a basic human right</strong>, exists on a continuum, and is shaped by individual, family, community, and structural factors. It also notes that the care gap for common conditions pushes countries toward innovative, non-specialist approaches — while treatment itself must remain in qualified pathways. Dorina's positioning sits exactly on the right side of that line: she widens literacy and prevention; she never touches treatment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"22-the-lebanese-context\">2.2 The Lebanese context</h3>\n<p>Lebanon has a <strong>National Mental Health Programme (NMHP)</strong> under the Ministry of Public Health, which has run national awareness campaigns, produced anti-stigma and psychological first aid materials, and — most relevantly — ran a <strong>\"Mental Health in the Workplace\"</strong> campaign under the slogan <strong>\"Don't miss the opportunity / ما تضيعوا الفرصة\"</strong>, with partners including WHO, ABAAD, ACTED, ESA, the HR Association of Lebanon, IMC, Médecins du Monde, and SKOUN.</p>\n<p>Three strategic implications:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Legitimacy:</strong> Workplace mental health is nationally recognized as a priority in Lebanon. Dorina's work echoes an existing government campaign rather than importing a foreign idea.</li><li><strong>Partnership map:</strong> The NMHP's partner list is a ready-made map of organizations that already care about this topic (see <code>OPPORTUNITY_MAP_LEBANON.md</code>).</li><li><strong>Referral infrastructure:</strong> The NMHP's materials and pathways give Dorina credible, local places to point people who need real support — the backbone of her referral protocol.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"23-why-dorina-specifically\">2.3 Why Dorina specifically</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>Strategic use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>12+ years as an executive assistant/administrator in a demanding professional environment</td><td>Lived authority on workplace pressure, invisible labor, and the systems that create or prevent burnout — the \"insider\" credibility clinicians lack</td></tr><tr><td>B.B.A., Arab Open University – Lebanon</td><td>Business literacy: she speaks the language of managers and ROI, which is how workshops get bought</td></tr><tr><td>Administrative systems expertise</td><td>The differentiator: wellbeing delivered as <em>systems and routines</em>, not vague inspiration — productized as the DESK Method (proposed) (see §4)</td></tr><tr><td>Arabic + English</td><td>Bilingual delivery for the Lebanese market and regional (GCC/diaspora) reach</td></tr><tr><td>Marketing-company environment (TiKiT, to be confirmed)</td><td>In-house sensibility for content, campaigns, and brand-building</td></tr><tr><td>A nearly blank LinkedIn (~4 connections, no recent posts)</td><td>Counterintuitively an asset: no contradictory history; the profile can be built clean and on-message from day one</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"24-the-honest-constraint-and-why-it-is-the-strategy\">2.4 The honest constraint — and why it is the strategy</h3>\n<p>No clinical license, psychology degree, or counseling credential is on file. Therefore Dorina will <strong>never</strong> be positioned as a therapist, psychologist, counselor, clinician, psychiatrist, trauma expert, or treatment provider. All materials define \"mental health management\" as self-management, workplace habits, stress literacy, boundaries, routines, and help-seeking — plus non-clinical workshops with clear referral protocols. This is stated in every deliverable and governed in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</p>\n<p>This constraint is a positioning gift: \"the educator who knows her limits\" is a <em>more</em> trustworthy brand than an ambiguous one, and it is the only durable one.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-positioning-statement\">3. Positioning statement</h2>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Dorina Salman helps working professionals and teams in Lebanon manage stress, prevent burnout, and build mentally healthier work routines — through practical education grounded in guidance from the World Health Organization and aligned with Lebanon's National Mental Health Programme. She is an educator and advocate, not a clinician: her work is literacy, prevention, and systems, with clear referral pathways to qualified professionals.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>Primary title: <strong>Workplace Wellbeing Educator | Mental Health Literacy Advocate</strong> Full option set, audience definitions, and brand story: <code>POSITIONING_AND_BRAND_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-the-signature-framework-the-desk-method-proposed-proposed\">4. The signature framework: the DESK Method (proposed) (proposed)</h2>\n<p>Authority requires a teachable, ownable idea. We propose the <strong>DESK Method (proposed)</strong> — named for where working life actually happens, and for Dorina's administrative roots:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D — Detect.</strong> Stress literacy: learning to notice early personal and team signals of overload, using awareness-based education of the kind WHO recommends for improving knowledge and reducing stigma.</li><li><strong>E — Establish.</strong> Boundaries, routines, and workload structures: the administrative craft of protecting time, energy, and attention — mapped against WHO-identified workplace risks such as excessive workload, long/inflexible hours, and lack of control.</li><li><strong>S — Support.</strong> Help-seeking and peer support: normalizing asking for help, knowing what workplace support can and cannot do, and knowing the referral pathways to qualified professionals (aligned with Lebanon's NMHP resources).</li><li><strong>K — Keep.</strong> Sustaining the habits: review rhythms, relapse-into-overwork prevention, and leisure/physical activity commitments — echoing WHO's recommendation of interventions for stress management and leisure-based physical activity.</li></ul>\n<p>Every workshop, content pillar, and product in this package hangs off this framework. It is deliberately non-clinical: it contains no assessment, no diagnosis, no therapeutic technique — only literacy, structure, and signposting.</p>\n<p><em>(Marked \"proposed\" until Dorina approves the name; alternatives in the positioning document.)</em></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-the-three-engine-model\">5. The three-engine model</h2>\n<p><strong>Engine 1 — Visibility (LinkedIn).</strong> Three educational posts weekly under four content pillars, an authority-grade profile, and deliberate network building toward HR professionals, office managers, team leads, and business owners in Lebanon and the region. Full system: <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code> and <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Engine 2 — Revenue (Workshops).</strong> A four-workshop portfolio plus a 45-minute lunch-and-learn entry format, sold to companies and communities: literacy sessions, burnout-prevention systems, emotional organization for busy professionals, and a team-wellbeing session for managers (awareness-level, non-clinical, consistent with WHO's manager-training recommendation). Full system: <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>, <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code>, pricing in <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Engine 3 — Leverage (Partnerships &amp; seasonal campaigns).</strong> Alliances with HR communities, universities, and organizations in the NMHP orbit; an annual flagship moment on <strong>World Mental Health Day, October 10</strong>; seasonal campaigns matched to the Lebanese work calendar. Full system: <code>OPPORTUNITY_MAP_LEBANON.md</code>, <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>.</p>\n<p>All three engines are fed by an <strong>approval-gated content system</strong>: AI-assisted drafting of posts, graphics briefs, and documents using <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code>, with nothing published until Dorina approves both copy and visual (<code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code>).</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-twelve-month-phased-roadmap\">6. Twelve-month phased roadmap</h2>\n<p><em>Assumed start: September 2026 (August 2026 = preparation). Dates shift cleanly if the start moves.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"phase-0-foundation-august-2026\">Phase 0 — Foundation (August 2026)</h3>\n<ul><li>Dorina answers the \"What we still need\" questions; facts corrected (role, dates, third language).</li><li>Positioning title and DESK Method (proposed) name approved (or alternates chosen).</li><li>LinkedIn profile fully rewritten and photographed; banner and visual identity produced.</li><li>Approval workflow channel agreed; first month of content drafted and approved in advance.</li><li>Optional but recommended: enroll in a recognized non-clinical course (e.g., psychological first aid–type training within the WHO/NMHP ecosystem) to add a legitimate credential line.</li><li><strong>Exit criteria:</strong> profile live, 12 posts pre-approved, referral protocol memorized.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"phase-1-presence-septembernovember-2026\">Phase 1 — Presence (September–November 2026)</h3>\n<ul><li>Posting cadence begins: 3 posts/week. Connection building: 15–25 targeted invitations/week with personal notes.</li><li><strong>October flagship:</strong> World Mental Health Day (Oct 10) mini-campaign — a week of workplace mental health literacy content, culminating in an offer of a free lunch-and-learn to 3 companies.</li><li>Deliver 2–3 <strong>free pilot workshops</strong> (employer network, HR contacts) in exchange for written testimonials and photos.</li><li><strong>Exit criteria:</strong> 400+ connections, 3 testimonials, one workshop fully rehearsed and timed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"phase-2-proof-december-2026-february-2027\">Phase 2 — Proof (December 2026 – February 2027)</h3>\n<ul><li>December theme: end-of-year fatigue and rest — seasonal, high-resonance content.</li><li>Convert pilots into the first <strong>paid</strong> bookings (introductory corporate pricing).</li><li>January \"new work-year reset\" campaign; first partnership conversations (HR communities, coworking spaces, universities).</li><li>Publish first long-form LinkedIn article: \"What 12 years behind the executive desk taught me about burnout.\"</li><li><strong>Exit criteria:</strong> 2+ paid workshops delivered, 800+ connections, 1 partnership in writing.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"phase-3-expansion-marchmay-2027\">Phase 3 — Expansion (March–May 2027)</h3>\n<ul><li>Raise workshop rates; introduce the manager-focused session.</li><li>Ramadan-aware content and scheduling (dates to be confirmed each year): energy, routine, and workload-compassion themes designed for the fasting month, delivered with cultural fluency.</li><li>Speak at or co-host one partner event; begin collecting a simple email list from workshop attendees (with consent).</li><li><strong>Instagram extension launches</strong> once LinkedIn is stable (repurposing engine: <code>INSTAGRAM_EXTENSION_ENGINE.md</code>).</li><li><strong>Exit criteria:</strong> 1,200+ connections, 6+ total workshops delivered, IG live with 2 posts/week repurposed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"phase-4-authority-juneaugust-2027\">Phase 4 — Authority (June–August 2027)</h3>\n<ul><li>Summer series: rest, recovery, and sustainable pace (Lebanese summer/holiday season).</li><li>Package a half-day corporate program (two workshops + follow-up session) at premium pricing.</li><li>Begin planning the <strong>October 2027 World Mental Health Day</strong> campaign as an anchor event (possibly with a partner organization).</li><li>Review year-one metrics against §8; set year-two strategy (digital product, e.g., a paid \"Emotional Organization\" toolkit, only after live-delivery proof).</li><li><strong>Exit criteria:</strong> 1,500+ connections, 8–12 workshops total, 3 partnerships, year-two plan approved.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-revenue-model-year-one-conservative\">7. Revenue model (year one, conservative)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stream</th><th>Timing</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Free pilots (3)</td><td>Phase 1</td><td>Paid in testimonials, photos, referrals — the real currency of month 1–3</td></tr><tr><td>Introductory paid workshops</td><td>Phase 2</td><td>Priced accessibly for the Lebanese market; exact pricing set with Dorina once budget/market questions are answered — see <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code> for the pricing framework (entry, standard, premium tiers)</td></tr><tr><td>Standard corporate sessions</td><td>Phase 3</td><td>Rate increase justified by testimonials and demand</td></tr><tr><td>Half-day corporate program</td><td>Phase 4</td><td>Bundled premium offer</td></tr><tr><td>Year-two options</td><td>2027–28</td><td>Digital toolkit, retainer \"wellbeing communications\" support for SMEs, train-the-champion programs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Deliberately <strong>not</strong> in the model: anything billed as therapy, counseling, coaching for mental disorders, or crisis services. Also excluded in year one: paid ads (organic + partnerships first), speaking fees (visibility first).</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-success-metrics-kpis\">8. Success metrics &amp; KPIs</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>90 days</th><th>6 months</th><th>12 months</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>LinkedIn connections</td><td>400+</td><td>800+</td><td>1,500+</td></tr><tr><td>Followers</td><td>500+</td><td>1,200+</td><td>2,500+</td></tr><tr><td>Posting consistency</td><td>≥ 90% of planned posts</td><td>≥ 90%</td><td>≥ 90%</td></tr><tr><td>Avg. engagements/post (reactions+comments)</td><td>15+</td><td>40+</td><td>80+</td></tr><tr><td>Workshops delivered (cumulative)</td><td>2–3 (pilots)</td><td>5–6</td><td>8–12</td></tr><tr><td>Written testimonials</td><td>3</td><td>6</td><td>10+</td></tr><tr><td>Partnerships in writing</td><td>0–1</td><td>1–2</td><td>3+</td></tr><tr><td>Inbound inquiries/month</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>6+</td></tr><tr><td>Email list (workshop attendees, consented)</td><td>—</td><td>50+</td><td>150+</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Quality guardrail metrics (tracked alongside growth): zero boundary violations (see <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>), 100% of posts approved by Dorina before publishing, referral information present in 100% of workshops.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"9-risk-register-summary\">9. Risk register (summary)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Likelihood</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Perceived as claiming clinical expertise</td><td>Medium</td><td>Bright-line language in every asset; prohibited-claims list; standing disclaimer; title never includes clinical terms</td></tr><tr><td>Participant discloses crisis/distress</td><td>Medium</td><td>Referral protocol, facilitator scripts, NMHP/emergency signposting in every session</td></tr><tr><td>Employer conflict of interest</td><td>Unknown</td><td>Question 9 to Dorina; content avoids commenting on her employers or clients</td></tr><tr><td>Lebanese economic pressure on pricing</td><td>High</td><td>Tiered pricing, corporate (not individual) buyers, partnerships that subsidize community sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Consistency fatigue (solo creator)</td><td>High</td><td>Approval-gated batch drafting (weekly, ~30 min of Dorina's time), prompt library, pre-built calendar</td></tr><tr><td>Copycat positioning</td><td>Low–Medium</td><td>Signature framework (DESK Method (proposed)), personal story, testimonial moat</td></tr><tr><td>Stigma-driven low engagement early on</td><td>Medium</td><td>Lead with productivity/energy framing (business-safe) while carrying the literacy message; WHO framing lends authority</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Full treatment: <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"10-governance-and-ethical-commitments\">10. Governance and ethical commitments</h2>\n<ol><li>Educational content only; never therapy, diagnosis, treatment, crisis support, or medical/legal/HR advice.</li><li>Standing disclaimer on workshops and the profile; session-level safety language in every facilitation script.</li><li>Crisis situations referred immediately to emergency services and qualified professionals; NMHP materials used as the local signpost.</li><li>No invented statistics: all data cited from the four sources listed below; anything else is clearly framed as Dorina's experience or opinion.</li><li>No content is published without Dorina's explicit approval of copy and graphic.</li><li>Participant privacy: no photos, stories, or quotes used without consent.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"11-what-we-still-need-from-dorina\">11. What we still need from Dorina</h2>\n<p>Consolidated in <code>SENDABLE_SUMMARY_FOR_DORINA.md</code> §\"What we still need from you\": current role/dates confirmation, third language, personal story, certificate appetite, delivery language, availability and venue access, photos/brand colors, approval channel, employer alignment, and monthly budget. None of these block Phase 0 from beginning; all sharpen it.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"12-sources\">12. Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon MoPH — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<p><em>This proposal is educational and strategic material. It is not medical, psychological, legal, or HR advice, and it does not confer or imply any clinical qualification.</em></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"final-review-addendum-preconditions-before-public-delivery\">Final Review Addendum — Preconditions Before Public Delivery</h2>\n<p>Before Dorina publishes the profile positioning or delivers paid workshops, complete these checks:</p>\n<ul><li>Confirm exact employment history and current employer restrictions; do not publish any dates or titles that Dorina has not verified.</li><li>Complete at least one non-clinical mental-health-literacy or Psychological First Aid training and one basic facilitation practice cycle before paid delivery.</li><li>Confirm local public/professional liability insurance options and obtain local legal review for disclaimers, privacy boundaries, workshop terms, and employer/moonlighting issues.</li><li>Verify current Lebanon crisis/referral contacts and operating hours before every printed handout or live workshop. Embrace Lebanon publicly lists 1564 for its Lifeline, but all details must be re-checked close to use.</li><li>Budget honestly: content drafting may take 2–3 hours/week, but the full engine including engagement, approvals, outreach, workshop preparation, delivery, and review can require 6–10+ hours/week during launch periods.</li><li>Track safeguarding metrics alongside growth: referral signposts made, boundary escalations, stopped exercises, post-workshop debrief notes, and whether the protocol was followed.</li></ul>","toc":[{"id":"1-executive-summary","text":"1. Executive summary","lvl":2},{"id":"2-why-this-why-now-why-dorina","text":"2. Why this, why now, why Dorina","lvl":2},{"id":"21-the-documented-need","text":"2.1 The documented need","lvl":3},{"id":"22-the-lebanese-context","text":"2.2 The Lebanese context","lvl":3},{"id":"23-why-dorina-specifically","text":"2.3 Why Dorina specifically","lvl":3},{"id":"24-the-honest-constraint-and-why-it-is-the-strategy","text":"2.4 The honest constraint — and why it is the strategy","lvl":3},{"id":"3-positioning-statement","text":"3. Positioning statement","lvl":2},{"id":"4-the-signature-framework-the-desk-method-proposed-proposed","text":"4. The signature framework: the DESK Method (proposed) (proposed)","lvl":2},{"id":"5-the-three-engine-model","text":"5. The three-engine model","lvl":2},{"id":"6-twelve-month-phased-roadmap","text":"6. Twelve-month phased roadmap","lvl":2},{"id":"phase-0-foundation-august-2026","text":"Phase 0 — Foundation (August 2026)","lvl":3},{"id":"phase-1-presence-septembernovember-2026","text":"Phase 1 — Presence (September–November 2026)","lvl":3},{"id":"phase-2-proof-december-2026-february-2027","text":"Phase 2 — Proof (December 2026 – February 2027)","lvl":3},{"id":"phase-3-expansion-marchmay-2027","text":"Phase 3 — Expansion (March–May 2027)","lvl":3},{"id":"phase-4-authority-juneaugust-2027","text":"Phase 4 — Authority (June–August 2027)","lvl":3},{"id":"7-revenue-model-year-one-conservative","text":"7. Revenue model (year one, conservative)","lvl":2},{"id":"8-success-metrics-kpis","text":"8. Success metrics & KPIs","lvl":2},{"id":"9-risk-register-summary","text":"9. Risk register (summary)","lvl":2},{"id":"10-governance-and-ethical-commitments","text":"10. Governance and ethical commitments","lvl":2},{"id":"11-what-we-still-need-from-dorina","text":"11. What we still need from Dorina","lvl":2},{"id":"12-sources","text":"12. Sources","lvl":2},{"id":"final-review-addendum-preconditions-before-public-delivery","text":"Final Review Addendum — Preconditions Before Public Delivery","lvl":2}],"words":2693,"mins":12},{"slug":"positioning-and-brand-architecture","file":"POSITIONING_AND_BRAND_ARCHITECTURE.md","title":"Positioning & Brand Architecture","blurb":"Niche, title options, audience, brand story, authority ladder, boundary language.","track":"Strategy","n":4,"html":"<h1 id=\"positioning-brand-architecture-dorina-salman\">Positioning &amp; Brand Architecture — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Define exactly who Dorina is in the market, who she serves, what she promises, how she talks about herself, and where the hard boundaries sit. Every other document inherits from this one.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-the-niche\">1. The niche</h2>\n<p><strong>Category:</strong> Workplace wellbeing education &amp; mental health literacy (non-clinical). <strong>Micro-niche (the wedge):</strong> <em>Emotional organization</em> — applying the craft of administrative systems (calendars, boundaries, workload structure, communication hygiene) to how working people manage stress and energy.</p>\n<p>Why this wedge wins:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>It is unoccupied.</strong> Wellbeing voices are mostly clinicians (who can't speak \"office operations\") or motivational speakers (who have no systems). An administrator who systematizes wellbeing is a genuinely new archetype.</li><li><strong>It is credible without a clinical license.</strong> Nothing in \"how to structure your workload, protect your boundaries, and know when and where to seek help\" requires — or implies — clinical authority.</li><li><strong>It is WHO-aligned.</strong> The WHO's recommended workplace actions include worker mental health literacy and awareness (knowledge + stigma reduction) and stress-management skills interventions. The WHO also names concrete workplace risks — excessive workloads and pace, understaffing, long/inflexible hours, lack of control — that are <em>organizational and administrative</em> in nature. Dorina teaches at exactly that intersection.</li><li><strong>It is nationally resonant.</strong> Lebanon's Ministry of Public Health has run a national \"Mental Health in the Workplace\" campaign (\"Don't miss the opportunity / ما تضيعوا الفرصة\"). The topic has local legitimacy and an existing partner ecosystem.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>One-line niche statement:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Practical mental health management for working professionals — taught as systems, not slogans.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-title-options-choose-one-primary\">2. Title options (choose one primary)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Text</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A (recommended)</strong></td><td>Workplace Wellbeing Educator \\</td><td>Mental Health Literacy Advocate</td><td>Clear, safe, search-friendly, bilingual-adaptable</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Burnout-Prevention Educator \\</td><td>Workplace Wellbeing Speaker</td><td>Sharper problem focus; strong for corporate buyers</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Mental Health Literacy Advocate \\</td><td>Emotional Organization Trainer</td><td>Leads with the mission, introduces the signature concept</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Workplace Wellbeing Communicator \\</td><td>Admin-Systems × Wellbeing Bridge</td><td>Most distinctive; needs explaining — better as a tagline than a title</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Arabic rendering (recommended, for bilingual materials):</strong> مدرِّبة توعية بالصحة النفسية في بيئة العمل — <em>Workplace mental health awareness educator.</em> (To be confirmed with Dorina for dialect/register comfort.)</p>\n<p><strong>Never in any title or bio:</strong> therapist, psychologist, counselor, coach for mental disorders, clinician, psychiatrist, trauma expert, healer, or any implication of treatment. Full prohibited list: <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-audience-architecture\">3. Audience architecture</h2>\n<h3 id=\"primary-audiences-content-buyers\">Primary audiences (content + buyers)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>HR professionals &amp; people managers in Lebanon</strong> — the <em>buyers</em>. They need affordable, safe, credible wellbeing programming; a non-clinical educator is easier to book than a clinical service. WHO's manager-training recommendation gives them a mandate.</li><li><strong>Administrative professionals, executive assistants, office managers</strong> — the <em>tribe</em>. Dorina is their voice: overworked, invisible, holding companies together. High identification, high sharing, high loyalty.</li><li><strong>Young professionals &amp; team leads (25–40) in Lebanese SMEs, agencies, law firms, and startups</strong> — the <em>audience</em>. They live the WHO risk list daily (workload, hours, lack of control) and want practical tools, not lectures.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"secondary-audiences\">Secondary audiences</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Business owners of Lebanese SMEs</strong> — buy workshops when framed as productivity and retention (WHO's US$1 trillion productivity-loss figure is the door-opener).</li><li><strong>Lebanese diaspora &amp; regional (GCC) professionals</strong> — extend reach and future online-workshop demand; English content serves them.</li><li><strong>nonprofit/humanitarian organization / development-sector staff in Lebanon</strong> — high-stress sector, workshop demand, and adjacency to the NMHP partner ecosystem.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"explicitly-not-the-audience\">Explicitly <em>not</em> the audience</h3>\n<p>People seeking help for mental disorders, therapy alternatives, or crisis support. Content will occasionally be seen by them; every asset therefore carries referral signposting (see §8).</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-brand-story-the-narrative-spine\">4. Brand story (the narrative spine)</h2>\n<p><em>Drafted for Dorina's approval — her authentic version should replace or refine this. Placeholders marked.</em></p>\n<blockquote><p>For more than twelve years, I've been the person behind the desk that keeps a professional office running — the calendars, the deadlines, the difficult days that never make it into anyone's job description. I learned something in those years that no textbook taught me: <strong>most workplaces don't break people loudly. They break people quietly</strong> — one skipped lunch, one \"quick favor\" at 7 pm, one blurred boundary at a time.</p>\n<p>I also learned the opposite: the right systems protect people quietly, too. A guarded calendar. A workload that's visible instead of endless. A team that knows the early signs of burnout and isn't ashamed to name them. The World Health Organization calls this work protecting and promoting mental health at work — I call it what I've always done, finally pointed at what matters most.</p>\n<p>I'm not a therapist, and I'll never pretend to be one. When someone needs professional support, the most caring thing I can do is help them reach qualified help — and Lebanon's National Mental Health Programme means those pathways exist. What I do is everything <em>before</em> that point: the literacy, the habits, the boundaries, the systems that keep working life livable.</p>\n<p>[PLACEHOLDER — Dorina's personal moment: the experience that made this personal. To be added only with her comfort and consent.]</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Story rules:</strong> Always include the \"I'm not a therapist\" beat — it is a trust accelerator, not a weakness. Never share others' stories without consent. Never dramatize or invent hardship.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-signature-framework-the-desk-method-proposed\">5. Signature framework: the DESK Method (proposed)</h2>\n<p>The ownable intellectual property. One page Dorina can draw on a whiteboard in 60 seconds:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Name</th><th>What it teaches</th><th>WHO/NMHP grounding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>D</strong></td><td><strong>Detect</strong></td><td>Stress literacy: noticing early personal and team overload signals; naming stress without stigma</td><td>WHO: literacy/awareness improves knowledge and reduces stigma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>E</strong></td><td><strong>Establish</strong></td><td>Boundaries, routines, workload visibility, communication hygiene</td><td>WHO risk list: workload, pace, hours, control — all addressable by structure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>S</strong></td><td><strong>Support</strong></td><td>Help-seeking as a skill; what workplace support is (and is not); referral pathways</td><td>WHO: support for workers; NMHP materials as local signposts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>K</strong></td><td><strong>Keep</strong></td><td>Sustaining habits: weekly reviews, rest as a system, leisure and physical activity</td><td>WHO: stress-management and leisure/physical-activity interventions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Naming alternatives if Dorina prefers:</strong> \"The Organized Mind Method,\" \"WorkWell Systems,\" \"الترتيب النفسي (Emotional Organization) Framework.\" The structure stays; the label is hers to choose.  is aspirational branding, not a registered mark — registration can be explored later.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-the-authority-ladder\">6. The authority ladder</h2>\n<p>How a stranger becomes a client, and how Dorina's stature compounds. Each rung has an owning document:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Seen</strong> — consistent LinkedIn posts under four pillars → <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code></li><li><strong>Followed</strong> — profile converts visitors (headline, About, Featured) → <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code></li><li><strong>Trusted</strong> — signature framework + WHO/NMHP grounding + visible boundaries → this document + <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code></li><li><strong>Invited</strong> — lunch-and-learns, pilot workshops, partner events → <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>, <code>OPPORTUNITY_MAP_LEBANON.md</code></li><li><strong>Paid</strong> — corporate workshop portfolio and packages → <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code></li><li><strong>Referenced</strong> — testimonials, case summaries, World Mental Health Day campaigns, partnerships → compounding loop back to rung 1</li></ol>\n<p>Rule of the ladder: <strong>never sell above the rung the audience is on.</strong> Months 1–2 sell nothing; they teach.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-content-pillars-the-four-voices-of-the-brand\">7. Content pillars (the four voices of the brand)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Share</th><th>Voice</th><th>Example angle</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1. Stress &amp; burnout literacy</strong></td><td>30%</td><td>The educator</td><td>\"Burnout doesn't start with exhaustion. It starts with a calendar that has no white space.\" (WHO risk framing)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. Systems &amp; boundaries (DESK in practice)</strong></td><td>30%</td><td>The practitioner</td><td>\"The 15-minute Friday workload review that protects your Monday.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Story &amp; advocacy</strong></td><td>25%</td><td>The human</td><td>Dorina's admin-life lessons; stigma-reduction; mental health as a human right (WHO framing); Lebanese work-culture observations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. Workplace culture &amp; managers</strong></td><td>15%</td><td>The bridge</td><td>What managers can do; psychologically healthier team habits; the business case (12 billion lost working days / US$1 trillion, WHO)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every pillar is educational. None ever crosses into assessment, diagnosis, or treatment advice.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-boundary-architecture-inherited-by-every-asset\">8. Boundary architecture (inherited by every asset)</h2>\n<p><strong>Standing bio/disclaimer line (short):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Extended version</strong> for workshops, landing pages, and the About section, plus the crisis-response scripts, the prohibited-claims list, and the referral protocol: <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>. That document is canonical; this one only inherits it.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"9-verbal-identity\">9. Verbal identity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Tone:</strong> warm, structured, direct; \"an organized friend who has seen real offices.\" Confident about systems, humble about science: statistics are attributed (\"According to the WHO…\"), lived experience is owned (\"In twelve years behind the desk…\").</li><li><strong>Vocabulary to use:</strong> wellbeing, stress literacy, burnout prevention, boundaries, routines, workload, energy, help-seeking, psychological safety (as a workplace-culture concept), mental health literacy, emotional organization.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary to avoid:</strong> diagnose, treat, heal, therapy/therapeutic (about her own work), patients, disorders (except when citing WHO data), trauma work, \"fix your anxiety/depression,\" any promise of clinical outcomes.</li><li><strong>Bilingual principle:</strong> English for regional/professional reach; Arabic for resonance and stigma-reduction at home. High-emotion advocacy posts land hardest in Arabic; systems posts work well in English. Key posts can run bilingually (Arabic first).</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"10-visual-identity-direction-brief-for-design\">10. Visual identity direction (brief for design)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Feel:</strong> calm authority — closer to a premium consultancy than a wellness aesthetic. No lotus flowers, no head-in-hands stock photos (stigma-reinforcing), no medical imagery (boundary-blurring).</li><li><strong>Palette direction:</strong> a grounded base (deep teal or navy) + a warm accent (sand/amber) + generous white space. Final palette chosen with Dorina's preference (open question #7).</li><li><strong>Typography:</strong> clean humanist sans-serif; genuinely bilingual — Arabic type treated as a first-class citizen, not an afterthought.</li><li><strong>Recurring assets:</strong> LinkedIn banner, post/carousel templates per pillar, quote cards, workshop slide master, one-page handout template. Production briefs and prompts: <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code> §G.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"11-profile-fact-corrections-required-accuracy-debt\">11. Profile-fact corrections required (accuracy debt)</h2>\n<p>The public profile currently shows <em>Executive Assistant, Sami Nahas Law Firm, Feb 2014 – Present</em>; separately, Dorina's current role has been described as <em>Administrator at TiKiT (marketing)</em>. Before launch, Dorina must confirm actual roles and dates so the profile is factually clean — <strong>authority cannot sit on an inaccurate timeline.</strong> No end dates or titles will be invented on her behalf. Education (B.B.A., Arab Open University – Lebanon) and languages (Arabic, English, third to be confirmed) will be presented exactly as verified.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"12-what-we-still-need-from-dorina-positioning-specific\">12. What we still need from Dorina (positioning-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li>Primary title choice (A–D above) and Arabic rendering approval.</li><li>DESK Method (proposed) name approval or alternative choice.</li><li>Her personal story beat for §4 — only what she is comfortable sharing publicly.</li><li>Role/dates confirmation (§11), third language, and preferred delivery language mix.</li><li>Brand color and photography preferences.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"13-sources\">13. Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon MoPH — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<p><em>Educational and strategic material only — not medical, psychological, legal, or HR advice.</em></p>","toc":[{"id":"1-the-niche","text":"1. The niche","lvl":2},{"id":"2-title-options-choose-one-primary","text":"2. Title options (choose one primary)","lvl":2},{"id":"3-audience-architecture","text":"3. Audience architecture","lvl":2},{"id":"primary-audiences-content-buyers","text":"Primary audiences (content + buyers)","lvl":3},{"id":"secondary-audiences","text":"Secondary audiences","lvl":3},{"id":"explicitly-not-the-audience","text":"Explicitly not the audience","lvl":3},{"id":"4-brand-story-the-narrative-spine","text":"4. Brand story (the narrative spine)","lvl":2},{"id":"5-signature-framework-the-desk-method-proposed","text":"5. Signature framework: the DESK Method (proposed)","lvl":2},{"id":"6-the-authority-ladder","text":"6. The authority ladder","lvl":2},{"id":"7-content-pillars-the-four-voices-of-the-brand","text":"7. Content pillars (the four voices of the brand)","lvl":2},{"id":"8-boundary-architecture-inherited-by-every-asset","text":"8. Boundary architecture (inherited by every asset)","lvl":2},{"id":"9-verbal-identity","text":"9. Verbal identity","lvl":2},{"id":"10-visual-identity-direction-brief-for-design","text":"10. Visual identity direction (brief for design)","lvl":2},{"id":"11-profile-fact-corrections-required-accuracy-debt","text":"11. Profile-fact corrections required (accuracy debt)","lvl":2},{"id":"12-what-we-still-need-from-dorina-positioning-specific","text":"12. What we still need from Dorina (positioning-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"13-sources","text":"13. Sources","lvl":2}],"words":1850,"mins":8},{"slug":"risk-ethics-and-referral-protocol","file":"RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md","title":"Risk, Ethics & Referral Protocol","blurb":"The safety rails. Prohibited claims, disclaimers, crisis wording, privacy rules.","track":"Governance","n":5,"html":"<h1 id=\"risk-ethics-referral-protocol\">Risk, Ethics &amp; Referral Protocol</h1>\n<h3 id=\"dorina-salman-mental-health-management-authority-plan-lebano\">Dorina Salman — Mental Health Management Authority Plan (Lebanon)</h3>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Status:</strong> Binding operating document. Every piece of content, every workshop, every DM reply, and every partnership operates inside the boundaries defined here. If any other document in this package ever appears to conflict with this protocol, <strong>this protocol wins</strong>.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-scope\">1. Purpose &amp; Scope</h2>\n<h3 id=\"11-why-this-document-exists\">1.1 Why this document exists</h3>\n<p>Dorina is building authority in <strong>mental health management</strong> — meaning practical self-management, workplace habits, stress literacy, boundaries, routines, help-seeking, and non-clinical educational workshops. She is <strong>not</strong> a licensed clinician, and nothing in this strategy positions her as one.</p>\n<p>This is not a limitation to apologize for. It is the foundation of her credibility. The World Health Organization explicitly recommends <strong>worker mental health literacy and awareness training</strong> as an effective workplace intervention — delivered by educators and trained facilitators, not only clinicians. That is exactly the lane this plan occupies. Staying visibly, rigorously inside that lane is what will make employers, partners, and Lebanon's mental health ecosystem trust her.</p>\n<h3 id=\"12-what-is-in-scope-dorina-may-do\">1.2 What is in scope (Dorina may do)</h3>\n<ul><li>Deliver <strong>educational</strong> workshops and content on: stress literacy, burnout prevention, workload and boundary habits, emotional organization, routines, workplace wellbeing culture, help-seeking awareness, and stigma reduction.</li><li>Share <strong>general, source-based information</strong> from authoritative bodies (WHO, Lebanon's Ministry of Public Health National Mental Health Programme) with citation.</li><li>Facilitate structured, non-clinical group exercises (reflection, planning, habit design, communication practice).</li><li>Signpost and refer people to qualified professionals and official services.</li><li>Speak from personal and professional experience, clearly labeled as such.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"13-what-is-out-of-scope-dorina-must-never-do\">1.3 What is out of scope (Dorina must never do)</h3>\n<ul><li>Diagnose, assess, screen, or label any person's mental condition — formally or informally, even casually (\"that sounds like anxiety/ADHD/depression/trauma\").</li><li>Provide therapy, counseling, psychological treatment, or any intervention presented as treatment.</li><li>Provide crisis intervention or act as a crisis service. Her role in a crisis is <strong>recognize → stabilize the moment → refer → hand over</strong>.</li><li>Give medical advice, comment on medication (starting, stopping, dosing, side effects), or recommend for/against any treatment.</li><li>Give legal advice or formal HR advice (disciplinary matters, terminations, accommodations rulings, labor disputes).</li><li>Run one-on-one \"sessions\" that resemble therapy in structure, privacy, or depth — even if labeled \"coaching.\"</li><li>Interpret anyone's past trauma, relationships, or symptoms.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"14-who-this-protocol-binds\">1.4 Who this protocol binds</h3>\n<p>Dorina, anyone assisting her (content support, moderation, workshop assistants), and any partner co-delivering an event under her name.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-prohibited-claims\">2. Prohibited Claims</h2>\n<p>The following claims must never appear in any bio, post, caption, slide, handout, proposal, media interview, or private message — in English or Arabic, directly or by implication:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Prohibited claim type</th><th>Examples (never use)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Clinical identity</td><td>\"therapist,\" \"psychologist,\" \"counselor,\" \"clinician,\" \"psychiatrist,\" \"psychotherapist,\" \"mental health professional/practitioner/specialist/expert\"</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Treatment capability</td><td>\"I treat anxiety/depression/burnout,\" \"healing sessions,\" \"therapy for teams,\" \"recovery program\"</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Diagnostic capability</td><td>\"I can assess your mental health,\" \"burnout diagnosis,\" \"stress assessment\" (as a clinical instrument), \"screening\"</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Trauma expertise</td><td>\"trauma expert,\" \"trauma-informed therapist,\" \"trauma healing,\" \"release your trauma\"</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Outcome guarantees</td><td>\"cure burnout,\" \"eliminate anxiety,\" \"guaranteed results,\" \"transform your mental health in 30 days\"</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Crisis capability</td><td>\"I'm here for you 24/7,\" \"message me if you're in crisis,\" anything implying she is a helpline or safe crisis contact</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Medical/medication commentary</td><td>any statement about medication, supplements-as-treatment, or \"natural alternatives to medication\"</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Implied credentials</td><td>unexplained acronyms, \"certified\" without naming the exact certificate and issuer, \"years of clinical experience\"</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Statistics without source</td><td>any number about mental health not traceable to the cited WHO/MoPH sources or another named authoritative source</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Substitution claims</td><td>\"you don't need therapy, you need better habits,\" or anything positioning her work as a replacement for professional care</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The implication test:</strong> if a reasonable participant could walk away believing Dorina is clinically qualified or that her workshop treats a condition, the wording fails — even if no prohibited word was used. Rewrite it.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-titles-prohibited-and-approved\">3. Titles: Prohibited and Approved</h2>\n<h3 id=\"31-titles-dorina-cannot-use-in-any-language\">3.1 Titles Dorina cannot use (in any language)</h3>\n<ul><li>Therapist / معالجة نفسية</li><li>Psychologist / أخصائية علم نفس / اختصاصية نفسية</li><li>Counselor / مرشدة نفسية</li><li>Clinician, psychiatrist, psychotherapist / طبيبة نفسية</li><li>Mental health professional, practitioner, specialist, or expert / خبيرة صحة نفسية / أخصائية صحة نفسية</li><li>Trauma expert / خبيرة صدمات</li><li>Life coach for mental health, \"mental health coach\" (too easily read as clinical in this market)</li><li>Any title containing \"clinical,\" \"certified\" (unless a real, named, verifiable certificate exists), or \"licensed\"</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"32-approved-title-options-choose-per-context\">3.2 Approved title options (choose per context)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Workplace Wellbeing Communicator</strong> — منسّقة/مثقِّفة رفاه مهني</li><li><strong>Mental Health Literacy Advocate</strong> — داعية للتثقيف في الصحة النفسية</li><li><strong>Burnout-Prevention &amp; Emotional-Organization Educator</strong> — مثقِّفة في الوقاية من الاحتراق الوظيفي والتنظيم الانفعالي</li><li><strong>Workplace Wellbeing &amp; Administrative Systems Bridge</strong> (leveraging her BBA and administration background)</li><li><strong>Stress-Management Skills Facilitator (non-clinical)</strong> — ميسِّرة مهارات إدارة الضغوط (غير علاجية) — <em>only</em> when paired with visible referral protocols and educational framing</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> wherever a title appears in a formal context (workshop flyer, proposal, media), it is accompanied by the short disclaimer from Section 4.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-standard-educational-disclaimers-english-arabic\">4. Standard Educational Disclaimers (English &amp; Arabic)</h2>\n<p>These are the canonical texts. Do not paraphrase them in official materials; copy them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"41-full-disclaimer-for-handouts-workshop-slides-opening-slid\">4.1 Full disclaimer — for handouts, workshop slides (opening slide), proposals, landing pages</h3>\n<p><strong>English:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>This content is provided for educational and awareness purposes only. It is not therapy, counseling, medical care, diagnosis, or treatment, and it is not a substitute for professional mental health care. Dorina Salman is an educator and facilitator, not a licensed mental health clinician. If you are experiencing emotional distress, a mental health condition, or a crisis, please contact a qualified mental health professional or your local emergency services. In Lebanon, support is available through the National Mental Health Programme of the Ministry of Public Health and the national Emotional Support and Suicide Prevention Helpline.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Arabic:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>هذا المحتوى مُقدَّم لأغراض التثقيف والتوعية فقط، وهو ليس علاجًا نفسيًا أو استشارة نفسية أو رعاية طبية أو تشخيصًا، ولا يُغني بأي شكل عن الرعاية النفسية المتخصصة. دورينا سلمان مثقِّفة وميسِّرة، وليست معالجة نفسية مرخّصة. إذا كنت تمرّ بضيق نفسي أو حالة صحية نفسية أو أزمة، يُرجى التواصل مع اختصاصي صحة نفسية مؤهَّل أو مع خدمات الطوارئ المحلية. في لبنان، تتوفر خدمات الدعم عبر البرنامج الوطني للصحة النفسية التابع لوزارة الصحة العامة وعبر الخط الوطني للدعم النفسي والوقاية من الانتحار.</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"42-short-disclaimer-for-linkedininstagram-bios-and-post-foot\">4.2 Short disclaimer — for LinkedIn/Instagram bios and post footers</h3>\n<p><strong>English:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content only — not therapy, diagnosis, or medical advice. In crisis, contact emergency services or a qualified professional.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Arabic:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>محتوى تثقيفي فقط — ليس علاجًا نفسيًا ولا تشخيصًا ولا استشارة طبية. في الحالات الطارئة، تواصلوا مع خدمات الطوارئ أو مع اختصاصي مؤهَّل.</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"43-verbal-disclaimer-spoken-at-the-start-of-every-workshop\">4.3 Verbal disclaimer — spoken at the start of every workshop</h3>\n<p><strong>English (say naturally, do not read robotically):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>\"Before we start, one important thing. Today is an educational workshop about skills and habits — it is not therapy, and I am not a therapist or clinician. Nothing here replaces professional care. If anything we discuss today touches something personal or painful for you, you are free to step out at any time, no explanation needed. And at the end, I'll share where you can find qualified professional support in Lebanon.\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Arabic:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>\"قبل ما نبدأ، شي مهم بدي قوله. اليوم ورشة تثقيفية عن مهارات وعادات — هي مش جلسة علاج، وأنا مش معالجة نفسية. ما في شي بهالورشة بيغني عن الرعاية المتخصصة. إذا أي موضوع منحكيه اليوم لمس شي شخصي أو مؤلم عندكم، فيكم تطلعوا من القاعة بأي وقت، بدون أي تفسير. وبآخر الورشة رح شارككم وين فيكم تلاقوا دعم متخصص بلبنان.\"</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"44-placement-rules\">4.4 Placement rules</h3>\n<ul><li>Full disclaimer: first content slide of every deck, footer of every handout, bottom of every landing page, inside every workshop proposal.</li><li>Short disclaimer: LinkedIn \"About\" section, Instagram bio, and the footer of any post discussing stress, burnout, or emotional topics.</li><li>Verbal disclaimer: within the first 3 minutes of every session, every time, including repeat audiences.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-workshop-safety-rules\">5. Workshop Safety Rules</h2>\n<p>These rules are part of the facilitator standard, announced at the opening of each session:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Education, not therapy.</strong> The verbal disclaimer (4.3) opens every session.</li><li><strong>No forced disclosure.</strong> No exercise ever requires a participant to share personal emotional content. Every sharing prompt includes a pass option: \"share only what feels comfortable, or pass — passing is always fine.\"</li><li><strong>Work-level, not wound-level.</strong> Exercises target habits, workload, routines, and communication — never childhood, trauma, grief, relationships, or clinical symptoms. If a participant takes an exercise there, gently redirect (see Section 10).</li><li><strong>Group agreements at the start:</strong> confidentiality (\"what's shared here stays here\"), no advice-giving between participants on personal matters, respect for silence and passing, phones away or on silent.</li><li><strong>The exit is always open.</strong> Participants may leave any exercise or the room without explanation. State this explicitly.</li><li><strong>No singling out.</strong> Never ask a specific person to share how they \"really feel.\" Prompts go to the room, volunteers respond.</li><li><strong>Referral information visible.</strong> Every session ends with a \"Where to find real support in Lebanon\" slide/handout (Section 7). This is non-negotiable, even for corporate audiences.</li><li><strong>Two-adult standard for follow-up.</strong> Any post-workshop conversation that turns personal happens in a semi-public space (open room, corner of the venue), not behind closed doors, and is kept short and referral-oriented.</li><li><strong>No recordings of participant sharing.</strong> Sessions may record the facilitator segments only. Group discussion is never recorded (see Section 9).</li><li><strong>Debrief every session.</strong> After each workshop, Dorina notes (without names): did any red flags occur, was a referral given, did any exercise need to be stopped. This log improves the protocol.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-participant-red-flags\">6. Participant Red Flags</h2>\n<p>Dorina and any assistant must know these signals. A red flag does not mean panic — it means <strong>shift from facilitator mode to referral mode</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"61-immediate-action-red-flags-respond-now-session-pauses-if-\">6.1 Immediate-action red flags (respond now, session pauses if needed)</h3>\n<ul><li>Any mention of wanting to die, ending their life, self-harm, or \"not wanting to be here anymore\" — even said jokingly.</li><li>Statements suggesting intent to harm someone else.</li><li>A participant appears disoriented, dissociated (unresponsive, \"not there\"), or has a panic episode that is not settling.</li><li>Disclosure of ongoing abuse or violence (domestic, workplace, or otherwise).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Response pattern:</strong> stay calm → move the person gently out of the group's focus (offer to step aside with them and an assistant/organizer) → do not probe for details → use the crisis referral wording (Section 7) → hand over to the workplace contact person or accompany them to call the helpline → never leave a person who mentioned suicidal thoughts alone before a handover happens → document factually (Section 9.4).</p>\n<h3 id=\"62-elevated-concern-red-flags-do-not-interrupt-the-session-a\">6.2 Elevated-concern red flags (do not interrupt the session; act at the next break or end)</h3>\n<ul><li>Crying that does not settle after a normal pause, or visible distress disproportionate to the exercise.</li><li>Disclosures of a diagnosed condition with questions about treatment or medication (\"should I stop my medication?\" → this is always and only referred out).</li><li>Repeated self-critical or hopeless language (\"nothing will ever change,\" \"I'm broken\").</li><li>A participant seeking one-on-one \"sessions\" or repeated private emotional conversations with Dorina.</li><li>Signs of substance-related impairment during a session.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Response pattern:</strong> acknowledge without analyzing (\"I hear that this is heavy — thank you for trusting the room\") → redirect the exercise to the group → at the break, check in briefly and privately, offer the referral handout, and if relevant use the soft referral script (7.2).</p>\n<h3 id=\"63-what-is-not-a-red-flag\">6.3 What is <em>not</em> a red flag</h3>\n<p>Normal emotion is expected in wellbeing workshops: brief tears, sighs, frustration about workload, \"this job is burning me out.\" These get empathy and normal facilitation, not a referral escalation. Over-escalating is its own harm — it stigmatizes normal feelings.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-crisis-referral-wording\">7. Crisis Referral Wording</h2>\n<h3 id=\"71-referral-resources-lebanon\">7.1 Referral resources (Lebanon)</h3>\n<blockquote><p>⚠️ <strong>Verification note:</strong> phone numbers and service hours change. Before printing any handout or publishing any post containing these, verify each number on the official source listed. This table must be re-verified at least every 6 months and before every workshop series.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>What it is</th><th>Where to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Embrace Lifeline — 1564</strong></td><td>Lebanon's national Emotional Support and Suicide Prevention Helpline, operating in partnership with the National Mental Health Programme</td><td>embrace.org.lb and moph.gov.lb</td></tr><tr><td><strong>National Mental Health Programme (Ministry of Public Health)</strong></td><td>National authority: service directories, awareness material, psychological first aid resources</td><td>moph.gov.lb — National Mental Health Program page</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Qualified private professionals</strong></td><td>Licensed psychologists and psychiatrists</td><td>Referred via MoPH resources, reputable hospitals, or organizations active in the national workplace mental health campaign (e.g., partners listed by MoPH)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Dorina should additionally build (and keep updated) a small vetted list of 3–5 licensed professionals/clinics across regions and price ranges who have agreed to receive referrals. Until that list exists, refer to the helpline and MoPH resources only.</p>\n<h3 id=\"72-soft-referral-script-for-elevated-concern-private-convers\">7.2 Soft referral script — for elevated concern, private conversation</h3>\n<p><strong>English:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>\"Thank you for sharing that with me. I want to be honest with you: what you're describing deserves more than a workshop can offer, and it deserves better than my opinion — because I'm an educator, not a therapist. Speaking with a qualified professional could genuinely help. In Lebanon there's a free, confidential line — 1564 — and I have a handout with other options. Would it be okay if I gave it to you?\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Arabic:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>\"شكرًا لأنك شاركتني هالشي. بدي كون صريحة معك: يلي عم تحكيه يستاهل أكتر من ورشة، ويستاهل أكتر من رأيي أنا — لأني مثقِّفة، مش معالجة نفسية. الحكي مع اختصاصي مؤهَّل ممكن يساعدك فعلًا. بلبنان في خط مجاني وسرّي — ١٥٦٤ — وعندي ورقة فيها خيارات تانية. بتحب/بتحبي أعطيك ياها؟\"</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"73-crisis-script-when-someone-mentions-suicide-self-harm-or-\">7.3 Crisis script — when someone mentions suicide, self-harm, or harm to others</h3>\n<p><strong>English:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>\"I'm really glad you told me. I want you to know I take what you said seriously, and you deserve real support from people trained for exactly this. I'm not able to provide that support myself, but I will stay with you right now while we contact someone who can. Embrace Lebanon publicly lists 1564 for its Lifeline; if this is still current and available, can we call together now? If not, we will contact the verified local emergency/support service.\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Arabic:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>\"أنا فعلًا مبسوطة إنك حكيتلي. بدي تعرف/تعرفي إني آخذ كلامك على محمل الجد، وإنت تستاهل/تستاهلي دعم حقيقي من ناس متدرّبين تمامًا لهالشي. أنا ما فيني قدّم هالدعم بنفسي، بس رح ضل معك هلق لنتواصل مع حدا بيقدر. الخط الساخن هو ١٥٦٤ — مجاني وسرّي. فينا نتصل سوا؟\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Rules for crisis moments:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Never promise secrecy (\"I won't tell anyone\") — say instead: \"I'll only share what's needed to keep you safe.\"</li><li>Never probe for method, plan, or history. That is a clinician's conversation.</li><li>If the person refuses help and there is immediate danger, involve the venue/employer contact person and the currently verified local emergency service. Safety overrides confidentiality. Verify the correct emergency number before each workshop.</li><li>Follow up is a handover, not a treatment relationship: one check-in message referring back to the resources is appropriate; ongoing emotional support conversations are not.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-dm-comment-response-scripts\">8. DM &amp; Comment Response Scripts</h2>\n<p>As Dorina's audience grows, people <strong>will</strong> message her with personal struggles. Every response follows one shape: <strong>warmth → honesty about role → referral → boundary.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"81-dm-someone-shares-personal-struggles-non-crisis\">8.1 DM: someone shares personal struggles (non-crisis)</h3>\n<blockquote><p>\"Thank you for trusting me with this — it takes courage to write it. I want to be straight with you: I'm an educator, not a therapist, so it wouldn't be right for me to advise you on something this personal. What I can tell you is that talking to a qualified professional genuinely helps, and in Lebanon the Lifeline 1564 is free and confidential, and the Ministry of Public Health's National Mental Health Programme lists real services. My content will always be here for the skills side — but for what you're carrying, you deserve proper support. 🤍\"</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"82-dm-crisis-language-mentions-of-suicide-or-self-harm\">8.2 DM: crisis language (mentions of suicide or self-harm)</h3>\n<blockquote><p>\"I'm very glad you reached out, and I'm taking what you wrote seriously. Please contact the Lifeline right now at 1564 — it's free, confidential, and they are trained for exactly this. If you are in immediate danger, contact the currently verified local emergency service immediately. I'm an educator, not a crisis professional, so I can't give you the support you deserve here — but the people at 1564 can, today. Please message them now.\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><em>Then:</em> do not continue a back-and-forth emotional conversation. If they reply, restate the referral once, warmly. Never go silent without the referral; never become the support line.</p>\n<h3 id=\"83-dm-asking-for-a-private-session\">8.3 DM: asking for a private \"session\"</h3>\n<blockquote><p>\"Thank you for asking — I'm honored. To be transparent: I don't offer one-on-one sessions, because personal support is clinical work and I'm an educator, not a therapist. What I do offer is group workshops on stress skills and workplace habits — and if that's useful for you or your team, I'd love to have you. For personal support, I always recommend a licensed professional; the National Mental Health Programme (moph.gov.lb) and Lifeline 1564 are the right doors.\"</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"84-dm-asking-about-medication-or-diagnosis\">8.4 DM: asking about medication or diagnosis</h3>\n<blockquote><p>\"That's an important question — and exactly the kind that deserves a qualified answer, which I can't responsibly give. Anything about diagnosis or medication belongs with a doctor or licensed mental health professional. Please don't change anything based on posts or messages — including mine. 🤍\"</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"85-public-comment-someone-discloses-distress-under-a-post\">8.5 Public comment: someone discloses distress under a post</h3>\n<blockquote><p>\"Thank you for sharing this here — I know that's not easy. 🤍 I've sent you a private message with support options. For anyone reading who's struggling: the Lifeline 1564 (Lebanon) is free and confidential.\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><em>Then send 8.1 or 8.2 privately. Never discuss their situation in public comments.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"86-public-comment-hostile-youre-not-even-qualified\">8.6 Public comment: hostile / \"you're not even qualified\"</h3>\n<blockquote><p>\"You're right that I'm not a clinician — and I say so in every workshop and post. My work is educational: skills, habits, and awareness, based on WHO and Ministry of Public Health resources. Anything clinical, I refer to licensed professionals. Thanks for giving me the chance to make that clear.\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><em>One reply maximum. No arguments in comments. Repeat offenders are ignored, not blocked, unless abusive.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"87-response-time-boundary\">8.7 Response-time boundary</h3>\n<p>Set a visible norm (e.g., in bio or auto-reply): messages are answered within 1–2 business days. This prevents the audience from treating DMs as a live support channel. Crisis DMs are the only exception: answer with 8.2 as soon as seen, whatever the hour.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"9-privacy-rules\">9. Privacy Rules</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Participant stories are never content.</strong> No workshop moment, quote, or anecdote appears in any post — even anonymized — without written consent from the person involved. \"A participant once told me…\" posts require consent or must be fully fictionalized and labeled as illustrative.</li><li><strong>Photos and recordings:</strong> photos at workshops require announced consent and an opt-out (a no-photo zone or sticker). Group discussions are never recorded. Attendee lists are never published.</li><li><strong>DMs are confidential.</strong> Screenshots of private messages are never posted, even praised, even anonymized, without explicit written consent.</li><li><strong>Red-flag documentation is minimal and factual:</strong> date, context, what was observed (behavior, not interpretation — \"participant mentioned self-harm,\" not \"participant seemed suicidal/depressed\"), action taken, who it was handed to. No diagnosis language ever. Stored privately (password-protected, not in shared drives), retained for accountability, never shared with the employer beyond what safety required in the moment.</li><li><strong>Employer reporting:</strong> post-workshop reports to a client contain themes and attendance only — never individual names linked to what they said, asked, or disclosed. If an employer asks \"who seemed to be struggling,\" the answer is a polite refusal plus an offer to share how the company can provide support channels for everyone.</li><li><strong>Contact data</strong> (registration emails/phones) is used only for the stated purpose (logistics, follow-up materials) and never sold, shared, or moved into marketing lists without explicit opt-in.</li><li><strong>Testimonials</strong> require written consent, show only what the person approved (name/initials/role level), and never reference personal mental health details.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"10-when-to-stop-a-workshop-exercise\">10. When to Stop a Workshop Exercise</h2>\n<p>An exercise stops — smoothly, without drama — when any of the following occurs:</p>\n<ol><li>A participant shows immediate-action red flags (6.1).</li><li>Multiple participants become visibly distressed and the room's emotional temperature keeps rising rather than settling.</li><li>An exercise drifts from work-level to wound-level: participants begin disclosing trauma, grief, family crises, or clinical symptoms.</li><li>Participants begin diagnosing or \"fixing\" each other (\"you clearly have anxiety, my cousin took…\").</li><li>A conflict between participants turns personal.</li><li>Dorina herself feels out of her depth. This instinct is always honored, never overridden by schedule pressure.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>The graceful stop (script):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>\"I'm going to pause us here — thank you all for how seriously you took this. Some of what's coming up is bigger than an exercise, and it deserves more than an exercise. Let's take a five-minute breather, and when we come back we'll move to [next activity]. And a reminder that the resource sheet at the end has real support options — I'd genuinely encourage using them.\"</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Rules for the stop:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Never name the person or moment that triggered the stop.</li><li>Always pivot <em>to</em> something (a break, a lighter structured activity), never just end abruptly.</li><li>Check in privately with any visibly affected participant at the break (Section 6 response patterns).</li><li>Log it in the post-session debrief (Rule 10, Section 5) and adjust or retire the exercise if it repeatedly runs hot.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Design rule upstream of all of this:</strong> exercises should be built so this section is rarely needed — prompts about workload, schedules, habits, and communication, not feelings-excavation. Prevention beats intervention.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"11-content-review-checklist\">11. Content Review Checklist</h2>\n<p>Every post, carousel, script, slide deck, handout, and landing page passes this checklist <strong>before</strong> entering the approval queue. Nothing publishes without Dorina's explicit approval of both text and graphics — and nothing enters her queue without passing this list.</p>\n<p><strong>A. Claims &amp; framing</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] No prohibited claim (Section 2) or prohibited title (Section 3.1), in either language — checked by the <em>implication test</em>, not just word-matching.</li><li>[ ] The content is educational (skills, habits, awareness), not advisory-clinical (\"if you feel X, you should do Y about your condition\").</li><li>[ ] No promise of outcomes; language of \"can help,\" \"many people find,\" never \"will fix.\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>B. Facts &amp; sources</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] Every statistic traces to a named authoritative source (WHO, MoPH NMHP, or another verified source) and is cited or linkable.</li><li>[ ] No invented studies, quotes, or numbers. If a claim can't be sourced, it's cut.</li><li>[ ] Helpline numbers and URLs verified within the last 6 months.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>C. Safety</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] Emotionally heavy topics (burnout, hopelessness, crisis awareness) carry the short disclaimer and the Lifeline reference.</li><li>[ ] Nothing invites the audience to DM Dorina for personal support; invitations point to workshops, resources, or professionals.</li><li>[ ] Nothing could reasonably shame someone for seeking (or needing) therapy or medication.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>D. Privacy</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] No identifiable participant, client, or DM content without written consent (Section 9).</li><li>[ ] Illustrative stories are labeled or fully fictionalized.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>E. Bilingual integrity</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] Arabic and English versions carry the same meaning and the same boundaries — the Arabic is not softer on claims than the English, or vice versa.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>F. Brand &amp; tone</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] Tone is warm, credible, and modest about role — authority through usefulness and sources, not through implied credentials.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"12-employer-workplace-boundaries\">12. Employer &amp; Workplace Boundaries</h2>\n<p>Corporate workshops are the core commercial channel, and they carry their own ethical lines:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Contracted scope is educational.</strong> Every proposal and invoice describes deliverables as educational workshops/training on workplace wellbeing and stress-management skills — never \"employee mental health support,\" \"counseling,\" or \"psychological services.\"</li><li><strong>No individual reporting.</strong> Dorina never reports to management on individuals — who attended reluctantly, who cried, who asked what. See Privacy Rule 5. This is stated <em>up front</em> in the proposal so it never becomes a negotiation.</li><li><strong>Not an HR instrument.</strong> Dorina's workshops are not: a substitute for an employee assistance program, a performance-management tool, evidence-gathering before terminations, or a box-ticking response to a specific internal incident (harassment case, suicide of an employee, layoffs) — unless the engagement is redesigned with appropriate professionals involved and her role clearly limited to the educational component.</li><li><strong>No mediation or investigations.</strong> Interpersonal conflicts, grievances, and disputes are HR/legal territory. If they surface in a session, they are acknowledged and redirected to the company's own channels.</li><li><strong>The employer gets recommendations, not gossip.</strong> Post-engagement reports may include anonymized themes (\"workload and unclear priorities were recurring topics\") plus WHO-aligned organizational recommendations (manager training, workload review, flexibility) — this positions Dorina as strategic while protecting participants.</li><li><strong>Attendance should be voluntary or at minimum non-punitive.</strong> Dorina requests that employers frame attendance as offered, not enforced, and never tie attendance to evaluation.</li><li><strong>A named employer contact</strong> (HR or organizer) is agreed before every corporate session as the handover point for red-flag situations. Dorina does not carry safety follow-up alone inside someone else's organization.</li><li><strong>Conflict-of-interest hygiene:</strong> while employed at TiKiT (or any employer), Dorina's own workplace is not used as content material without explicit employer consent, colleagues are not case studies, and any workshop delivered to her own employer follows all the same rules — plus extra care that colleagues never confuse her educational role with a confidential support role.</li><li><strong>Right to decline:</strong> engagements that pressure her toward clinical territory (\"can you also do one-on-ones with the struggling employees?\") are declined or reshaped, with a referral to qualified providers. The polite line: <em>\"That part deserves a licensed professional — I can recommend the right doors, and I'll gladly handle the education side.\"</em></li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"13-what-we-still-need-from-dorina\">13. What We Still Need From Dorina</h2>\n<p>The plan proceeds without these, but each answer strengthens the protocol. None of them block launch.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Certificates and training:</strong> Does she hold <em>any</em> verifiable certificates relevant to this space (e.g., psychological first aid training, Mental Health First Aid, facilitation training, HR/wellbeing courses)? Exact names, issuers, and dates — so approved titles and bios can honestly reflect them, and nothing more.</li><li><strong>Employment status:</strong> Current confirmed role and employer (TiKiT vs. Sami Nahas Law Firm timeline), so public profiles are factually accurate and Section 12.8 applies correctly.</li><li><strong>Comfort line for facilitation:</strong> Which topics does she personally feel ready to facilitate (e.g., is a \"stress in the room\" discussion comfortable, or does she prefer strictly structured exercises at first)? The workshop portfolio will be tuned to her honest starting point.</li><li><strong>Referral network:</strong> Does she already know licensed psychologists, clinics, or organizations (including any connected to the MoPH workplace campaign partners) willing to receive referrals? Names and consent to be listed.</li><li><strong>Language of delivery:</strong> Preferred primary workshop language (Arabic, English, or mixed) and comfort writing in formal Arabic vs. Lebanese dialect for scripts.</li><li><strong>A designated support person:</strong> Who is her own point of contact for debriefing after difficult sessions? Facilitators need support structures too.</li><li><strong>Regional scope:</strong> Which areas of Lebanon can she physically serve, and is she open to online delivery (which changes the safety protocol slightly — e.g., crisis handover at distance)?</li><li><strong>Insurance and legal form:</strong> Does she operate as an individual or plan to register an activity/business? Professional liability considerations should be reviewed with a Lebanese legal/insurance advisor (this document is not legal advice).</li><li><strong>Approval preferences:</strong> Confirmation of how she wants to receive content for approval (format, frequency, language) so the no-publish-without-approval gate matches her workflow.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"14-sources\">14. Sources</h2>\n<p>This protocol's framing of workplace mental health education, literacy, and referral pathways is grounded in:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>World Health Organization — Mental health at work (fact sheet).</strong> Workplace risks, the case for worker mental health literacy and stress-management skills training, and the scale of the issue (an estimated 12 billion working days lost yearly to depression and anxiety). <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li><strong>World Health Organization — Mental health: strengthening our response.</strong> Mental health as a basic human right existing on a continuum; support through policy, manager training, and targeted interventions, with treatment remaining in qualified pathways. <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li><strong>Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme.</strong> National campaigns including \"Mental Health in the Workplace\" (ما تضيعوا الفرصة), anti-stigma and psychological first aid materials, and the national partner ecosystem. <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li><strong>World Health Organization — World Mental Health Day (October 10).</strong> Campaign themes including workplace mental health and psychological first aid. <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<hr>\n<p><em>This document is educational-operational guidance, not legal advice. Contact numbers and services must be re-verified periodically as described in Section 7.1.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"number-verification-rule\">Number verification rule</h2>\n<p>Embrace Lebanon publicly lists <strong>1564</strong> for its Lifeline on its website. Because crisis resources can change, Dorina must verify the number, operating hours, and any emergency-service fallback from official/current sources before printing handouts, publishing crisis posts, or delivering a workshop. If any number cannot be verified, remove it and use a generic instruction to contact local emergency services or a qualified professional.</p>","toc":[{"id":"dorina-salman-mental-health-management-authority-plan-lebano","text":"Dorina Salman — Mental Health Management Authority Plan (Lebanon)","lvl":3},{"id":"1-purpose-scope","text":"1. Purpose & Scope","lvl":2},{"id":"11-why-this-document-exists","text":"1.1 Why this document exists","lvl":3},{"id":"12-what-is-in-scope-dorina-may-do","text":"1.2 What is in scope (Dorina may do)","lvl":3},{"id":"13-what-is-out-of-scope-dorina-must-never-do","text":"1.3 What is out of scope (Dorina must never do)","lvl":3},{"id":"14-who-this-protocol-binds","text":"1.4 Who this protocol binds","lvl":3},{"id":"2-prohibited-claims","text":"2. Prohibited Claims","lvl":2},{"id":"3-titles-prohibited-and-approved","text":"3. Titles: Prohibited and Approved","lvl":2},{"id":"31-titles-dorina-cannot-use-in-any-language","text":"3.1 Titles Dorina cannot use (in any language)","lvl":3},{"id":"32-approved-title-options-choose-per-context","text":"3.2 Approved title options (choose per context)","lvl":3},{"id":"4-standard-educational-disclaimers-english-arabic","text":"4. Standard Educational Disclaimers (English & Arabic)","lvl":2},{"id":"41-full-disclaimer-for-handouts-workshop-slides-opening-slid","text":"4.1 Full disclaimer — for handouts, workshop slides (opening slide), proposals, landing pages","lvl":3},{"id":"42-short-disclaimer-for-linkedininstagram-bios-and-post-foot","text":"4.2 Short disclaimer — for LinkedIn/Instagram bios and post footers","lvl":3},{"id":"43-verbal-disclaimer-spoken-at-the-start-of-every-workshop","text":"4.3 Verbal disclaimer — spoken at the start of every workshop","lvl":3},{"id":"44-placement-rules","text":"4.4 Placement rules","lvl":3},{"id":"5-workshop-safety-rules","text":"5. Workshop Safety Rules","lvl":2},{"id":"6-participant-red-flags","text":"6. Participant Red Flags","lvl":2},{"id":"61-immediate-action-red-flags-respond-now-session-pauses-if-","text":"6.1 Immediate-action red flags (respond now, session pauses if needed)","lvl":3},{"id":"62-elevated-concern-red-flags-do-not-interrupt-the-session-a","text":"6.2 Elevated-concern red flags (do not interrupt the session; act at the next break or end)","lvl":3},{"id":"63-what-is-not-a-red-flag","text":"6.3 What is not a red flag","lvl":3},{"id":"7-crisis-referral-wording","text":"7. Crisis Referral Wording","lvl":2},{"id":"71-referral-resources-lebanon","text":"7.1 Referral resources (Lebanon)","lvl":3},{"id":"72-soft-referral-script-for-elevated-concern-private-convers","text":"7.2 Soft referral script — for elevated concern, private conversation","lvl":3},{"id":"73-crisis-script-when-someone-mentions-suicide-self-harm-or-","text":"7.3 Crisis script — when someone mentions suicide, self-harm, or harm to others","lvl":3},{"id":"8-dm-comment-response-scripts","text":"8. DM & Comment Response Scripts","lvl":2},{"id":"81-dm-someone-shares-personal-struggles-non-crisis","text":"8.1 DM: someone shares personal struggles (non-crisis)","lvl":3},{"id":"82-dm-crisis-language-mentions-of-suicide-or-self-harm","text":"8.2 DM: crisis language (mentions of suicide or self-harm)","lvl":3},{"id":"83-dm-asking-for-a-private-session","text":"8.3 DM: asking for a private \"session\"","lvl":3},{"id":"84-dm-asking-about-medication-or-diagnosis","text":"8.4 DM: asking about medication or diagnosis","lvl":3},{"id":"85-public-comment-someone-discloses-distress-under-a-post","text":"8.5 Public comment: someone discloses distress under a post","lvl":3},{"id":"86-public-comment-hostile-youre-not-even-qualified","text":"8.6 Public comment: hostile / \"you're not even qualified\"","lvl":3},{"id":"87-response-time-boundary","text":"8.7 Response-time boundary","lvl":3},{"id":"9-privacy-rules","text":"9. Privacy Rules","lvl":2},{"id":"10-when-to-stop-a-workshop-exercise","text":"10. When to Stop a Workshop Exercise","lvl":2},{"id":"11-content-review-checklist","text":"11. Content Review Checklist","lvl":2},{"id":"12-employer-workplace-boundaries","text":"12. Employer & Workplace Boundaries","lvl":2},{"id":"13-what-we-still-need-from-dorina","text":"13. What We Still Need From Dorina","lvl":2},{"id":"14-sources","text":"14. Sources","lvl":2},{"id":"number-verification-rule","text":"Number verification rule","lvl":2}],"words":4943,"mins":21},{"slug":"linkedin-authority-engine","file":"LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md","title":"LinkedIn Authority Engine","blurb":"Profile rewrite, network building, the posting system, conversion machinery, KPIs.","track":"Execution","n":6,"html":"<h1 id=\"linkedin-authority-engine-dorina-salman\">LinkedIn Authority Engine — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Turn Dorina's near-blank LinkedIn (~4 connections, ~4 followers, no recent posts) into her primary authority asset: a profile that converts visitors, a network that compounds, a posting system that runs on ~2–3 hours of her time per week, and an approval workflow in which nothing publishes without her sign-off.</p>\n<p>Starting from near-zero is an advantage: there is no contradictory history to clean up. Everything below builds the presence right the first time.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-profile-rewrite-copy-paste-ready-pending-fact-confirmation\">1. Profile rewrite (copy-paste ready, pending fact confirmation)</h2>\n<h3 id=\"11-name-headline\">1.1 Name &amp; headline</h3>\n<p><strong>Name:</strong> Dorina Salman (professional capitalization, consistent everywhere).</p>\n<p><strong>Headline (option A — recommended):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Workplace Wellbeing Educator | Mental Health Literacy Advocate | Helping teams in Lebanon prevent burnout with practical systems — not slogans | Workshops &amp; talks (AR/EN)</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Headline (option B — sharper problem focus):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>I help busy professionals and teams manage stress and prevent burnout — through education, boundaries, and better work systems | Workshop facilitator | Lebanon 🇱🇧 | AR/EN</p></blockquote>\n<p>Rules: no clinical terms, ever; the headline must state audience + outcome + method + offer; the (AR/EN) tag signals bilingual delivery to corporate buyers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"12-about-section-full-draft\">1.2 About section (full draft)</h3>\n<blockquote><p>Twelve years behind the executive desk taught me something no report ever could: <strong>workplaces rarely break people loudly. They break people quietly</strong> — one skipped lunch, one 7 pm \"quick favor,\" one blurred boundary at a time.</p>\n<p>They also taught me the reverse. The right systems protect people quietly, too: a guarded calendar, a visible workload, a team that can name stress without shame.</p>\n<p>That's the work I do now. I'm a workplace wellbeing educator and mental health literacy advocate. I translate guidance from authorities like the World Health Organization into desk-level habits, routines, and boundaries that working people in Lebanon can actually use.</p>\n<p>The need is not a feeling — it's documented. The WHO estimates that 12 billion working days are lost each year to depression and anxiety, costing US$1 trillion in productivity, and that 15% of working-age adults were living with a mental disorder in 2019. The WHO also says effective workplace actions exist — including mental health literacy, awareness, and stress-management skills. That is exactly what I teach.</p>\n<p><strong>What I offer:</strong> • Workshops and lunch-and-learns on burnout prevention, stress literacy, and emotional organization (Arabic / English) • The DESK Method (proposed) — Detect, Establish, Support, Keep: wellbeing built like a system, because I've spent a career building systems • Talks and internal campaigns for teams, aligned with the spirit of Lebanon's national workplace mental health efforts</p>\n<p><strong>What I don't do — on purpose:</strong> I'm not a therapist, psychologist, or counselor, and my work is not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. It's education and prevention. When someone needs professional care, I help point them to qualified pathways — that's what responsible wellbeing work looks like.</p>\n<p>📩 If you lead a team in Lebanon and want a practical, human, evidence-informed wellbeing session, send me a message with the word <strong>WORKSHOP</strong>.</p></blockquote>\n<p><em>(Statistics above cite the WHO Mental health at work fact sheet; keep them attributed exactly this way.)</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"13-experience-section\">1.3 Experience section</h3>\n<p><strong>Pending Dorina's confirmation of roles/dates (accuracy debt — see positioning doc §11).</strong> Structure once confirmed:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Current entry:</strong> [Administrator, TiKiT — dates TBC] — 2–3 lines connecting administration/marketing operations to workload systems and workplace communication.</li><li><strong>Sami Nahas Law Firm, Executive Assistant, Feb 2014 – [end date TBC or Present]:</strong> rewrite as an authority asset: &gt; Ran the operational heartbeat of a demanding legal practice for over a decade — calendars, deadlines, client communication, and the invisible systems that keep high-pressure work humane. This desk is where my work in workplace wellbeing began: I saw daily how structure protects people, and how its absence quietly costs them.</li><li><strong>New entry (from launch):</strong> <em>Workplace Wellbeing Educator — Independent</em> — \"Non-clinical workshops and educational content on stress literacy, burnout prevention, and emotional organization for teams in Lebanon. Educational work only; clear referral pathways for professional support.\"</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"14-education-licenses-skills-languages\">1.4 Education, licenses, skills, languages</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Education:</strong> Arab Open University – Lebanon, B.B.A., Business Administration and Management (as verified).</li><li><strong>Licenses &amp; certifications:</strong> only real ones, ever. When Dorina completes a recognized non-clinical course (e.g., psychological first aid–type training within the WHO/national programme ecosystem), it goes here with its exact official name.</li><li><strong>Skills (pin top 3):</strong> Workshop Facilitation, Workplace Wellbeing Education, Mental Health Literacy Advocacy. Supporting: Public Speaking, Content Development, Administrative Operations, Time &amp; Workload Management, Stakeholder Communication, Event Coordination.</li><li><strong>Languages:</strong> Arabic (native), English (professional), third language once confirmed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"15-featured-section-build-in-week-12\">1.5 Featured section (build in week 1–2)</h3>\n<ol><li>A one-page \"What I do / What I don't do\" graphic (boundary as brand).</li><li>The first pillar post that performs well.</li><li>Workshop one-pager (from <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>) once pilots are booked.</li><li>Later: testimonial graphic; World Mental Health Day campaign recap.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"16-visuals\">1.6 Visuals</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Photo:</strong> professional, warm, direct-to-camera headshot (open question — photography).</li><li><strong>Banner:</strong> calm-authority design carrying the positioning line: <em>\"Practical mental health management for working professionals — systems, not slogans.\"</em> + AR echo. Production prompt: <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code> §G1.</li><li><strong>Custom URL:</strong> claim <code>linkedin.com/in/dorinasalman</code> (or nearest available).</li><li><strong>Settings:</strong> Creator mode ON with topics: Workplace Wellbeing, Mental Health (literacy context), Burnout Prevention, Productivity, Leadership. Profile language: English primary; consider Arabic secondary profile.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-network-building-from-4-connections-to-1500\">2. Network building (from 4 connections to 1,500+)</h2>\n<h3 id=\"21-the-rules\">2.1 The rules</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Quality first:</strong> every connection should be a potential reader, buyer, partner, or amplifier. Targets: HR professionals in Lebanon, office managers/EAs, team leads, SME owners, nonprofit/humanitarian organization staff, university career/HR staff, journalists covering workplace topics.</li><li><strong>Volume discipline:</strong> 15–25 personalized invitations/week (safe, sustainable, non-spammy). Never mass-invite; never pitch in the invitation.</li><li><strong>Order of operations (first 6 weeks):</strong> (1) real-life network — colleagues, classmates, family friends in professional roles; (2) HR Association of Lebanon members and similar community members (the MoPH workplace campaign's partner ecosystem signals where these communities live); (3) engaged commenters on workplace wellbeing posts region-wide; (4) attendees/speakers of Lebanese business events.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"22-invitation-note-templates\">2.2 Invitation note templates</h3>\n<p><strong>To an HR professional (EN):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Hi [Name], I'm building an educational project on workplace wellbeing and burnout prevention for teams in Lebanon — non-clinical, very practical. As someone doing real HR work here, your perspective is exactly what I want to learn from. Glad to connect.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>To a fellow admin/EA (AR):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>مرحبا [الاسم]، أنا دورينا — اشتغلت أكثر من ١٢ سنة بالإدارة التنفيذية وعم أبني اليوم مشروع توعية عن الصحة النفسية ببيئة العمل بلبنان. حابة نتواصل ونتشارك الخبرة.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>To a business owner (EN):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Hi [Name], I share practical, evidence-informed content on preventing burnout in Lebanese teams — the WHO puts the global productivity cost of depression and anxiety at US$1 trillion a year, and most of the fixes are surprisingly practical. Connecting with leaders like you who care about their teams.</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"23-the-daily-20-minutes-engagement-routine\">2.3 The daily 20 minutes (engagement routine)</h3>\n<ul><li>10 min: comment substantively on 3–5 posts by HR leaders, business voices, and wellbeing-adjacent creators (comments are free authority).</li><li>5 min: reply to every comment on her own posts (replies double reach and build community).</li><li>5 min: accept/send invitations; answer DMs using scripts from <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-posting-system\">3. The posting system</h2>\n<h3 id=\"31-cadence-formats\">3.1 Cadence &amp; formats</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>3 posts/week — Monday, Wednesday, Friday</strong> (mornings, Beirut time; adjust with analytics after month 2).</li><li>Format rotation across the four pillars (defined in <code>POSITIONING_AND_BRAND_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> §7): text post with strong hook • carousel (systems/how-to) • quote/stat card (WHO-attributed) • story post • occasional poll • monthly long-form article from month 4.</li><li>Full month-by-month plan, themes, and written sample posts: <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"32-anatomy-of-an-authority-post-house-style\">3.2 Anatomy of an authority post (house style)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Hook line</strong> (first 2 lines decide everything): a tension, a number, or a quiet truth. <em>\"Your best employee is not fine. She's organized.\"</em></li><li><strong>Body:</strong> short lines, white space, one idea per post; systems shown as steps; statistics always attributed to WHO by name.</li><li><strong>Boundary-safe framing:</strong> education, never instruction to ignore professional help; no diagnosis language.</li><li><strong>Close:</strong> one question to the reader (comments fuel reach) or one soft CTA (max 1 per week: \"DM me WORKSHOP\").</li><li><strong>Signature + hashtags (3–5):</strong> #WorkplaceWellbeing #MentalHealthLiteracy #BurnoutPrevention #Lebanon + one post-specific.</li><li><strong>Arabic variant</strong> where the topic is stigma/advocacy-heavy (pillar 3 lands hardest in Arabic).</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"33-weekly-production-rhythm-dorinas-total-time-23-hrs\">3.3 Weekly production rhythm (Dorina's total time: ~2–3 hrs)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Step</th><th>Owner</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Draft next week's 3 posts + graphic drafts using <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code></td><td>Assistant/AI-assisted</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Friday</td><td><strong>Approval gate:</strong> Dorina reviews copy + visual; approves, edits, or rejects each</td><td>Dorina</td><td>30–45 min</td></tr><tr><td>Weekend</td><td>Revisions if requested; schedule approved posts</td><td>Assistant</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Mon/Wed/Fri</td><td>Posts go live <strong>only if approved</strong>; Dorina does the daily 20-minute engagement routine</td><td>Dorina</td><td>20 min/day</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The complete governance version of this workflow — roles, approval log, rejection loop, tool-agnostic scheduling, and the \"no autopost without Dorina\" rule — is <code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code>. That document is canonical for process; this section is the operating summary.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-conversion-machinery-profile-as-funnel\">4. Conversion machinery (profile as funnel)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Every element points one step down the ladder:</strong> headline → About; About → DM keyword \"WORKSHOP\"; Featured → workshop one-pager; posts → profile.</li><li><strong>DM handling:</strong> respond within 24h using the qualification script (<code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code> §DM scripts). If a message discloses personal distress rather than business interest, switch immediately to the referral response in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> — never advise, always signpost.</li><li><strong>Social proof loop:</strong> after each workshop, request a 2–3 line LinkedIn recommendation or a quotable testimonial (with consent) and post a recap within 72 hours.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-kpis-instrumentation\">5. KPIs &amp; instrumentation</h2>\n<p>Tracked monthly in a simple sheet (template row: month, connections, followers, posts published/planned, avg engagements, profile views, DMs, inquiries, workshops booked):</p>\n<ul><li>Connections: 400+ by day 90 → 1,500+ by month 12 • Followers: 500+ → 2,500+</li><li>Posting consistency ≥ 90% of plan • Engagements/post: 15+ → 80+ • Inbound inquiries: 1/mo → 6+/mo</li><li>Guardrails: 100% posts Dorina-approved; zero boundary violations; referral line present in profile at all times.</li></ul>\n<p>Review analytics on the first Friday of each month; shift posting times/formats based on data, never based on mood.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-we-still-need-from-dorina-linkedin-specific\">6. What we still need from Dorina (LinkedIn-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li>Role/dates confirmation for the Experience section (blocking item for §1.3 only).</li><li>Headline choice (A or B) and About-section approval, including her personal story beat.</li><li>Headshot + banner color approval.</li><li>Preferred approval channel and day for the Friday gate.</li><li>Confirmation of the third language before listing it.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-sources\">7. Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon MoPH — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<p><em>Educational and strategic material only — not medical, psychological, legal, or HR advice.</em></p>","toc":[{"id":"1-profile-rewrite-copy-paste-ready-pending-fact-confirmation","text":"1. Profile rewrite (copy-paste ready, pending fact confirmation)","lvl":2},{"id":"11-name-headline","text":"1.1 Name & headline","lvl":3},{"id":"12-about-section-full-draft","text":"1.2 About section (full draft)","lvl":3},{"id":"13-experience-section","text":"1.3 Experience section","lvl":3},{"id":"14-education-licenses-skills-languages","text":"1.4 Education, licenses, skills, languages","lvl":3},{"id":"15-featured-section-build-in-week-12","text":"1.5 Featured section (build in week 1–2)","lvl":3},{"id":"16-visuals","text":"1.6 Visuals","lvl":3},{"id":"2-network-building-from-4-connections-to-1500","text":"2. Network building (from 4 connections to 1,500+)","lvl":2},{"id":"21-the-rules","text":"2.1 The rules","lvl":3},{"id":"22-invitation-note-templates","text":"2.2 Invitation note templates","lvl":3},{"id":"23-the-daily-20-minutes-engagement-routine","text":"2.3 The daily 20 minutes (engagement routine)","lvl":3},{"id":"3-the-posting-system","text":"3. The posting system","lvl":2},{"id":"31-cadence-formats","text":"3.1 Cadence & formats","lvl":3},{"id":"32-anatomy-of-an-authority-post-house-style","text":"3.2 Anatomy of an authority post (house style)","lvl":3},{"id":"33-weekly-production-rhythm-dorinas-total-time-23-hrs","text":"3.3 Weekly production rhythm (Dorina's total time: ~2–3 hrs)","lvl":3},{"id":"4-conversion-machinery-profile-as-funnel","text":"4. Conversion machinery (profile as funnel)","lvl":2},{"id":"5-kpis-instrumentation","text":"5. KPIs & instrumentation","lvl":2},{"id":"6-what-we-still-need-from-dorina-linkedin-specific","text":"6. What we still need from Dorina (LinkedIn-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"7-sources","text":"7. Sources","lvl":2}],"words":1832,"mins":8},{"slug":"12-month-linkedin-content-calendar","file":"12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md","title":"12-Month Content Calendar","blurb":"Month-by-month themes, weekly cadence, sample posts, Lebanon seasonal hooks.","track":"Execution","n":7,"html":"<h1 id=\"12-month-linkedin-content-calendar-dorina-salman\">12-Month LinkedIn Content Calendar — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Period:</strong> September 2026 – August 2027 (shifts cleanly if launch moves) <strong>Cadence:</strong> 3 posts/week — Monday, Wednesday, Friday (Beirut mornings) <strong>Pillars:</strong> P1 Stress &amp; burnout literacy (30%) • P2 Systems &amp; boundaries / DESK Method (30%) • P3 Story &amp; advocacy (25%) • P4 Managers &amp; workplace culture (15%) <strong>Rules:</strong> every statistic attributed to WHO by name; no diagnosis/treatment language ever; max one CTA post per week; Arabic or bilingual versions for high-emotion advocacy posts; <strong>every post passes Dorina's approval gate before publishing</strong> (<code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>Annual goals this calendar serves:</strong> 1,500+ connections, 2,500+ followers, ≥90% posting consistency, 6+ inbound inquiries/month by month 12, 8–12 workshops booked from content + outreach.</p>\n<p>Generation prompts for every asset type in this calendar: <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code> §§B–D.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"seasonal-spine-lebanon-aware\">Seasonal spine (Lebanon-aware)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Moment</th><th>Timing</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Back-to-work/back-to-school reset</td><td>September</td><td>Launch energy; routines content</td></tr><tr><td><strong>World Mental Health Day — October 10 (WHO)</strong></td><td>October</td><td><strong>Annual flagship campaign</strong></td></tr><tr><td>End-of-year fatigue &amp; holidays</td><td>December</td><td>Rest, reflection, realistic closure</td></tr><tr><td>New work-year reset</td><td>January</td><td>Systems, boundaries, fresh starts</td></tr><tr><td>Ramadan (dates shift yearly — <strong>confirm each year</strong>)</td><td>~Feb–Mar 2027 window</td><td>Energy, routine, compassion at work; bilingual, culturally fluent</td></tr><tr><td>Spring pressure season (deadlines, exams, holidays cluster)</td><td>April–May</td><td>Workload visibility content</td></tr><tr><td>Summer heat, holidays, diaspora returning</td><td>July–August</td><td>Sustainable pace, guilt-free rest, recovery systems</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>(October 10 is sourced from WHO's World Mental Health Day campaign page. Other seasonal moments are calendar-common knowledge, not statistics; Ramadan dates must be confirmed annually.)</em></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-1-september-2026-the-introduction\">Month 1 — September 2026 · «The Introduction»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> Who I am, why workplace wellbeing, and the quiet cost of \"fine.\" <strong>Goal:</strong> Introduce the positioning; 150+ new connections; establish voice. No selling.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Wk</th><th>Mon (P3/P1)</th><th>Wed (P2)</th><th>Fri (P1/P4)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Launch post</strong> — my story: 12 years behind the desk (sample below)</td><td>The first DESK idea: your calendar is a wellbeing document</td><td>Stat post: WHO — 12 billion working days lost yearly to depression &amp; anxiety</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>\"Workplaces don't break people loudly\" — the quiet signals</td><td>15-minute Friday workload review (how-to carousel)</td><td>Poll: which drains your team most — workload, hours, or lack of control? (WHO risk list framing)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Why I say \"I'm not a therapist\" in my bio (boundary as trust)</td><td>Inbox boundaries: 3 rules that survived 12 years of executive inboxes</td><td>WHO: almost 60% of the world is in work — why workplace wellbeing is everyone's topic</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Arabic post: الصحة النفسية بالشغل مش رفاهية (workplace mental health isn't a luxury)</td><td>Meeting hygiene: protecting focus without drama</td><td>Recap post: what I learned in my first month of posting + what's coming</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Sample post (Week 1, Monday — launch):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>For 12 years, my job was to keep everything running.</p>\n<p>Calendars. Deadlines. Crises that never made it into anyone's job description.</p>\n<p>I was good at it. But I watched something over those years that I can't unsee: workplaces rarely break people loudly. They break people quietly — one skipped lunch, one 7 pm \"quick favor,\" one blurred boundary at a time.</p>\n<p>The World Health Organization estimates 12 billion working days are lost every year to depression and anxiety. That number isn't made in hospitals. It's made in offices like the ones I've spent my career in.</p>\n<p>So I'm starting something new — here, publicly. I'll be sharing practical, evidence-informed ways to manage stress, prevent burnout, and build work routines that protect people. Systems, not slogans.</p>\n<p>One thing I'll always be clear about: I'm not a therapist, and this isn't therapy. It's education — the part that comes <em>before</em> someone needs professional help, and the honesty to point to real help when it's needed.</p>\n<p>If you work in Lebanon and any of this sounds like your office — stay close.</p>\n<p>#WorkplaceWellbeing #MentalHealthLiteracy #BurnoutPrevention #Lebanon</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-2-october-2026-the-flagship-world-mental-health-day\">Month 2 — October 2026 · «The Flagship: World Mental Health Day»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> Workplace mental health literacy, anchored on <strong>October 10 (WHO World Mental Health Day)</strong>. <strong>Goal:</strong> First mini-campaign; 1 post shared by an HR community; open pilot-workshop offer.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Wk</th><th>Mon</th><th>Wed</th><th>Fri</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>WMHD is coming: why this October matters for Lebanese workplaces</td><td>Carousel: 5 workplace mental health risks named by the WHO (workload, pace, hours, control, culture)</td><td>Story: the colleague who \"was fine\" — early signals we all missed <em>(anonymized/composite, labeled as such)</em></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Oct 10 campaign post</strong> (bilingual; sample below)</td><td>What Lebanon already did: the national \"Mental Health in the Workplace\" campaign — ما تضيعوا الفرصة — and what it tells us</td><td><strong>CTA:</strong> I'm offering 3 free lunch-and-learn sessions to Lebanese teams this quarter — DM WORKSHOP</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>WHO: mental health is a basic human right — including at work</td><td>DESK carousel: Detect — 6 early overload signals you can notice without being a clinician</td><td>Manager post: WHO recommends manager training — here's the no-budget version: three questions to ask your team monthly</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Stigma post (Arabic-first): ليش منخاف نحكي؟ (why are we afraid to speak?)</td><td>Boundaries after hours: the \"end-of-day shutdown\" routine</td><td>Campaign recap + gratitude + first pilot announcement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Sample post (Oct 10 — bilingual flagship):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>اليوم هو اليوم العالمي للصحة النفسية. — Today is World Mental Health Day.</p>\n<p>The WHO tells us that 15% of working-age adults were living with a mental disorder in 2019 — and that effective workplace actions exist: awareness, literacy, manager training, stress-management skills.</p>\n<p>Lebanon has been here before, officially: our Ministry of Public Health ran a national workplace campaign with one unforgettable slogan — <strong>ما تضيعوا الفرصة. Don't miss the opportunity.</strong></p>\n<p>So here's my question to every manager and team in Lebanon today: What's one thing your workplace could start this month — not someday — to make work lighter to carry?</p>\n<p>I'll go first in the comments. 👇</p>\n<p><em>(Educational content — not therapy or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach out to a qualified professional or local emergency services.)</em></p>\n<p>#WorldMentalHealthDay #WorkplaceWellbeing #Lebanon #MentalHealthLiteracy</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-3-november-2026-burnout-properly-understood\">Month 3 — November 2026 · «Burnout, Properly Understood»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> Deep literacy month on burnout and chronic workplace stress. <strong>Goal:</strong> Deliver 2–3 free pilots booked in October; collect testimonials; 400+ connections milestone.</p>\n<p>Weekly threads: what burnout is and isn't (literacy, carefully non-diagnostic — \"if this feels heavy and persistent, that's a sign to talk to a professional\") • the workload-visibility system (DESK: Establish) • pilot-workshop behind-the-scenes post • WHO stat card (US$1 trillion productivity cost — the business case) • Arabic advocacy post on normalizing \"I need help\" • first testimonial share (with consent) • gratitude + November recap.</p>\n<p><strong>Fri Week 4 CTA:</strong> \"Companies: December berths for lunch-and-learns are open — DM WORKSHOP.\"</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-4-december-2026-ending-the-year-like-a-human\">Month 4 — December 2026 · «Ending the Year Like a Human»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> End-of-year fatigue, realistic closure, rest as a system. <strong>Goal:</strong> Seasonal resonance; first paid bookings for January; first long-form article.</p>\n<p>Threads: the \"end-of-year audit\" carousel (what drained you / what protected you) • saying no to December overload (scripts included) • <strong>long-form article:</strong> <em>What 12 Years Behind the Executive Desk Taught Me About Burnout</em> • rest is not a reward (advocacy, Arabic-first) • holiday boundary post for people whose work follows them home • year-one-quarter recap with numbers (transparency builds authority).</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-5-january-2027-the-reset-done-right\">Month 5 — January 2027 · «The Reset, Done Right»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> New work-year systems — anti-resolution, pro-structure. <strong>Goal:</strong> Paid workshop #1–2 delivered; 800+ connections; partnership conversations begin.</p>\n<p>Threads: why resolutions fail but systems survive • the DESK annual setup (carousel series, 2 parts) • \"protect your calendar before it fills itself\" • WHO framing: mental health exists on a continuum — check-ins beat overhauls • client-workshop recap post (with consent) • poll: your team's biggest January pressure • partnership announcement if signed.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-6-february-2027-support-is-a-skill\">Month 6 — February 2027 · «Support Is a Skill»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> DESK \"S\" month — help-seeking, peer support, and knowing the pathways. <strong>Goal:</strong> Cement the referral-responsible brand; deepen HR audience trust.</p>\n<p>Threads: help-seeking as a professional skill, not a weakness • what workplace support can and cannot do (boundary clarity) • Lebanon's National Mental Health Programme exists — what it means that our country has one (signposting, Arabic-first) • how to check on a colleague without playing therapist (literacy + referral) • manager post: making it safe to say \"I'm at capacity\" • testimonial/case recap.</p>\n<p><em>If Ramadan begins in this window (confirm dates), swap in the Ramadan module from Month 7.</em></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-7-march-2027-energy-routine-and-compassion-ramadan-awa\">Month 7 — March 2027 · «Energy, Routine, and Compassion» <em>(Ramadan-aware module)</em></h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> Working well through changed rhythms — designed for the fasting month if dates align (<strong>confirm annually</strong>). <strong>Goal:</strong> Culturally fluent authority; highest Arabic-content month; IG extension launches (see <code>INSTAGRAM_EXTENSION_ENGINE.md</code>).</p>\n<p>Threads (bilingual, Arabic-first): meetings and energy windows during fasting • workload compassion without productivity theater • the evening routine reset • rest without guilt • a manager's Ramadan checklist (culture pillar) • IG launch announcement post • month recap.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-8-april-2027-the-manager-month\">Month 8 — April 2027 · «The Manager Month»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> Pillar 4 takes the lead: managers and culture, grounded in WHO's manager-training recommendation. <strong>Goal:</strong> Sell the new manager-focused workshop; corporate inquiries 3+/month.</p>\n<p>Threads: the WHO says train managers — here's what that means in practice • carousel: 5 team habits that reduce invisible overload (from the WHO risk list) • what my law-firm years taught me about pressure rolling downhill • manager Q&amp;A post (collect questions, answer next week) • workshop offer CTA • spring pressure-season prep post.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-9-may-2027-systems-in-public\">Month 9 — May 2027 · «Systems in Public»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> DESK \"E/K\" showcase — the most practical month of the year. <strong>Goal:</strong> Demonstrate depth; 1,200+ connections; 6+ workshops cumulative.</p>\n<p>Threads: full DESK walkthrough carousel (flagship evergreen asset) • the weekly review, shown with real (sanitized) screenshots • boundary scripts that work in Lebanese office culture (bilingual) • leisure and movement as WHO-recommended stress interventions — the system version • attendee-question post • recap + testimonial.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-10-june-2027-proof-and-partnerships\">Month 10 — June 2027 · «Proof and Partnerships»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> Social proof season — recaps, testimonials, partner content. <strong>Goal:</strong> 3 partnerships in writing; half-day corporate package announced.</p>\n<p>Threads: what 8 workshops taught me about Lebanese workplaces (meta-post, high shareability) • partner co-post or event announcement • half-day program launch post • myth-busting carousel: \"wellbeing is a luxury\" vs the WHO business case • Arabic advocacy: الصحة النفسية حق (mental health is a right — WHO framing) • mid-year transparency recap with numbers.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-11-july-2027-summer-rest-as-strategy\">Month 11 — July 2027 · «Summer: Rest as Strategy»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> Sustainable pace, holidays, recovery — matched to Lebanese summer rhythm. <strong>Goal:</strong> Maintain consistency through slow season; bank evergreen content; plan October flagship.</p>\n<p>Threads: the case for real vacations (and how to hand over work cleanly — admin superpower content) • \"out of office\" scripts people are afraid to write • recovery is part of performance (WHO leisure/physical-activity framing) • lighter story posts; diaspora-summer observations • evergreen DESK reruns with new framing • poll + community post.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"month-12-august-2027-year-one-honestly\">Month 12 — August 2027 · «Year One, Honestly»</h2>\n<p><strong>Theme:</strong> Reflection, results, and the road to October. <strong>Goal:</strong> Position the October 2027 WMHD campaign; year-two runway; 1,500+ connections closed.</p>\n<p>Threads: year-one honest retrospective (numbers, misses, lessons — full transparency post) • the best-performing post, reborn • what Lebanese teams asked for most this year • World Mental Health Day 2027 teaser: this October, we go bigger (partner campaign announcement if secured) • gratitude post to the community • September pre-launch of year-two calendar.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"operating-notes\">Operating notes</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Batching:</strong> each month's posts are drafted in the last week of the prior month via <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code> §B, then pass the Friday approval gates week by week.</li><li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> national events or crises in Lebanon override the calendar — pause scheduled posts and let Dorina decide tone (sensitivity protocol in <code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code> §6).</li><li><strong>Recycling:</strong> from month 7, top posts are refreshed and repurposed to Instagram (<code>INSTAGRAM_EXTENSION_ENGINE.md</code>).</li><li><strong>Measurement:</strong> first Friday of each month = analytics review against the KPI table in <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code> §5.</li><li><strong>Composite stories:</strong> any \"colleague\" story is anonymized or composite and labeled as such — never a real identifiable person without written consent.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-calendar-specific\">What we still need from Dorina (calendar-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li>Approval of Month 1 drafts before launch (the only blocking item).</li><li>Ramadan 2027 exact dates confirmation when published, to place the Ramadan module correctly.</li><li>Comfort level for the transparency/recap posts (numbers public vs. qualitative only).</li><li>Any personal dates to avoid (leave, family commitments) so the calendar plans around them.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-2\">Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon MoPH — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day (October 10): <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<p><em>Educational and strategic material only — not medical, psychological, legal, or HR advice.</em></p>","toc":[{"id":"seasonal-spine-lebanon-aware","text":"Seasonal spine (Lebanon-aware)","lvl":2},{"id":"month-1-september-2026-the-introduction","text":"Month 1 — September 2026 · «The Introduction»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-2-october-2026-the-flagship-world-mental-health-day","text":"Month 2 — October 2026 · «The Flagship: World Mental Health Day»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-3-november-2026-burnout-properly-understood","text":"Month 3 — November 2026 · «Burnout, Properly Understood»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-4-december-2026-ending-the-year-like-a-human","text":"Month 4 — December 2026 · «Ending the Year Like a Human»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-5-january-2027-the-reset-done-right","text":"Month 5 — January 2027 · «The Reset, Done Right»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-6-february-2027-support-is-a-skill","text":"Month 6 — February 2027 · «Support Is a Skill»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-7-march-2027-energy-routine-and-compassion-ramadan-awa","text":"Month 7 — March 2027 · «Energy, Routine, and Compassion» (Ramadan-aware module)","lvl":2},{"id":"month-8-april-2027-the-manager-month","text":"Month 8 — April 2027 · «The Manager Month»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-9-may-2027-systems-in-public","text":"Month 9 — May 2027 · «Systems in Public»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-10-june-2027-proof-and-partnerships","text":"Month 10 — June 2027 · «Proof and Partnerships»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-11-july-2027-summer-rest-as-strategy","text":"Month 11 — July 2027 · «Summer: Rest as Strategy»","lvl":2},{"id":"month-12-august-2027-year-one-honestly","text":"Month 12 — August 2027 · «Year One, Honestly»","lvl":2},{"id":"operating-notes","text":"Operating notes","lvl":2},{"id":"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-calendar-specific","text":"What we still need from Dorina (calendar-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"sources-2","text":"Sources","lvl":2}],"words":2218,"mins":10},{"slug":"workshop-system-blueprint","file":"WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md","title":"Workshop System Blueprint","blurb":"The product ladder, session run sheets, facilitator guide, logistics, measurement.","track":"Delivery","n":8,"html":"<h1 id=\"workshop-system-blueprint-dorina-salman\">Workshop System Blueprint — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> This document is the complete operating system for Dorina's workshop business — the philosophy and safety frame, the full product ladder (W0–W5), minute-by-minute run sheets for the three workhorse formats, the facilitator guide, logistics and operations, the participant journey from invitation to testimonial, the closure system, and the measurement framework. It is designed so that Dorina can deliver her first pilot within weeks of approving the package, and scale to a retainer-based portfolio without ever crossing the non-clinical boundary. Exercise scripts and printable materials live in <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code>; how workshops are sold lives in <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>; where to sell them lives in <code>OPPORTUNITY_MAP_LEBANON.md</code>.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-workshop-philosophy-safety-frame\">1. Workshop Philosophy &amp; Safety Frame</h2>\n<h3 id=\"11-education-not-therapy-the-line-that-protects-everyone\">1.1 Education, not therapy — the line that protects everyone</h3>\n<p>Every workshop Dorina delivers is a <strong>class, not a clinic</strong>. She teaches skills — stress literacy, boundaries, workload structure, help-seeking as a competence — the way a first-aid trainer teaches bandaging: practical, universal, and explicitly not a substitute for professional care. This position is not a limitation; it is the product. WHO's guidance on mental health at work names worker mental health literacy and awareness training as a recommended workplace intervention precisely because it improves knowledge and reduces stigma — and that is exactly the lane Dorina occupies.</p>\n<p>Three sentences define the frame, and Dorina says versions of them out loud in every session:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>\"I am an educator, not a therapist.\"</strong> Nothing in the room is diagnosis, treatment, or assessment.</li><li><strong>\"We work on situations, not conditions.\"</strong> Participants discuss workload, routines, and habits — never symptoms, disorders, or medical histories.</li><li><strong>\"Part of this skill is knowing when to hand over.\"</strong> Help-seeking and referral to qualified professionals are taught as strengths, and the referral signpost appears in every session (see Section 7 and <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>).</li></ol>\n<p>In Arabic, the frame lands naturally as: <strong>\"أنا هون لعلّم مهارات، مش لعالج\"</strong> (<em>\"I'm here to teach skills, not to treat\"</em>) — warm, colloquial, and unambiguous.</p>\n<h3 id=\"12-the-desk-method-proposed-as-session-architecture\">1.2 The DESK Method (proposed) as session architecture</h3>\n<p>The DESK Method (proposed) is not only the content — it is the <strong>structure of every session</strong>. Whatever the format or length, the session moves through the same arc:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>DESK stage</th><th>In-session function</th><th>Typical share of time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>D — Detect</strong></td><td>Build stress literacy; participants notice and name their own signals without stigma</td><td>~25–30%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>E — Establish</strong></td><td>Convert awareness into structure: boundaries, routines, workload visibility</td><td>~30–35%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>S — Support</strong></td><td>Reframe help-seeking as a skill; clarify what workplace support is and is not; signpost referral pathways</td><td>~15–20%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>K — Keep</strong></td><td>Commit to sustaining habits: weekly reviews, rest as a system, one concrete next step</td><td>~15–20%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>This predictable arc means participants always leave with (a) something noticed, (b) something structured, (c) somewhere to turn, and (d) something to keep doing. It also means Dorina never improvises into risky territory: if a moment does not fit D, E, S, or K, it does not belong in the room.</p>\n<h3 id=\"13-the-container-concept\">1.3 The \"container\" concept</h3>\n<p>Lebanese corporate rooms mix hierarchy, humor, and healthy skepticism. The workshop works because Dorina builds a <strong>container</strong> — a predictable, low-pressure space — announced in the first five minutes of every session:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Predictability.</strong> The agenda is visible from minute one. Participants always know what comes next and when the session ends. No surprises, no ambush exercises.</li><li><strong>Opt-out rights.</strong> Every exercise is invitation, not obligation. The standing line: <em>\"Everything here is optional. Pass at any time — no explanation needed.\"</em> In Arabic: <strong>\"كل شي هون اختياري\"</strong> (<em>\"everything here is optional\"</em>).</li><li><strong>No forced sharing.</strong> Personal reflection stays on paper unless a participant chooses to speak. Pair and group discussions are framed around <strong>work situations, not private lives</strong>. Dorina never cold-calls a person to share something personal; she cold-calls only for opinions on the material.</li><li><strong>Confidentiality norm.</strong> \"What's said here about personal experience stays here; what's learned here leaves with you.\" This is stated, not assumed — especially important when managers and their direct reports are in the same room.</li><li><strong>The exit ramp.</strong> Anyone may step out at any time without comment. Dorina normalizes this in the opening (\"the door is not locked, and the coffee is not rationed\") so leaving the room is never conspicuous.</li></ul>\n<p>The container is also Dorina's professional protection: a room where sharing is optional and topics are situational is a room that stays educational.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-workshop-portfolio-product-ladder\">2. The Workshop Portfolio (Product Ladder)</h2>\n<p>The portfolio is a ladder, not a menu. W0 opens doors; W1 is the flagship revenue product; W2 sells to the budget-holder's own pain; W3 serves Dorina's tribe and builds her most loyal advocates; W4 removes geography; W5 converts one-off bookings into recurring revenue. All prices below are <strong>suggested ranges to validate for the Lebanese market</strong> — they are starting hypotheses for a price-sensitive, widely dollarized environment, not market facts. Quote in USD, invoice per local norms, and adjust after the first three paid engagements.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Product</th><th>Duration</th><th>Format</th><th>Group size</th><th>Suggested price (to validate)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>W0</td><td>The DESK Taster (lunch &amp; learn)</td><td>45–60 min</td><td>In-person or online</td><td>8–30</td><td>Free (pilot / door-opener)</td></tr><tr><td>W1</td><td>Mental Health Management at Work</td><td>Half day (3.5–4 h)</td><td>In-person preferred; hybrid possible</td><td>10–20</td><td>$500–$1,200 per session</td></tr><tr><td>W2</td><td>The Supportive Manager</td><td>2 h</td><td>In-person or online</td><td>6–15 managers</td><td>$300–$700 per session</td></tr><tr><td>W3</td><td>The Organized Calm (for admin professionals/EAs)</td><td>2.5–3 h</td><td>In-person or online</td><td>8–20</td><td>$250–$600 per session</td></tr><tr><td>W4</td><td>DESK Online (remote teams/diaspora)</td><td>90 min</td><td>Online live</td><td>8–25</td><td>$200–$500 per session</td></tr><tr><td>W5</td><td>The DESK Quarterly (refresher retainer)</td><td>60–90 min ×4/year</td><td>Hybrid</td><td>Existing clients</td><td>$600–$1,500 per year (4 sessions)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"w0-the-desk-taster-جلسة-تعارف-مع-طريقة-desk-an-introduction-\">W0 — The DESK Taster («جلسة تعارف مع طريقة DESK» — <em>an introduction session to the DESK Method</em>)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Duration:</strong> 45–60 minutes, ideally over lunch.</li><li><strong>Audience:</strong> Mixed staff at any SME, agency, law firm, or nonprofit/humanitarian organization — the door-opener for decision-makers to see Dorina work.</li><li><strong>Group size:</strong> 8–30. <strong>Format:</strong> In-person or online; deliberately low-tech (works with a flipchart if the projector fails).</li><li><strong>Learning outcomes:</strong> Participants can (1) name three early stress signals in themselves, (2) explain the difference between pressure and overload, (3) describe the four DESK stages, (4) leave with one micro-habit to try this week.</li><li><strong>DESK coverage:</strong> D in depth; E, S, K introduced as a map (\"this is what the full workshop builds\").</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> Free by design — this is the pilot format described in <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>. Cap free deliveries (suggested: 6–8 total, then only as paid-engagement previews) so \"free\" stays a strategy, not a habit.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"w1-mental-health-management-at-work-إدارة-الصحة-النفسية-في-ا\">W1 — Mental Health Management at Work («إدارة الصحة النفسية في العمل» — <em>managing mental health at work</em>)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Duration:</strong> Half day, 3.5–4 hours including breaks. <strong>The flagship.</strong></li><li><strong>Audience:</strong> Full teams (mixed levels) in SMEs, agencies, law firms, startups; nonprofit/humanitarian organization staff teams.</li><li><strong>Group size:</strong> 10–20 (above 20, interaction quality drops; propose two cohorts instead).</li><li><strong>Format:</strong> In-person strongly preferred (the exercises are tactile); hybrid possible with an in-room co-host handling the remote side.</li><li><strong>Learning outcomes:</strong> Participants can (1) map their personal stress signals and early-warning thresholds, (2) audit their real workload and make it visible, (3) use three ready-made boundary scripts adapted to Lebanese workplace culture, (4) describe what workplace support is and is not, and where to seek qualified help, (5) run a 15-minute weekly review to sustain all of the above.</li><li><strong>DESK coverage:</strong> Full method, all four stages, with take-home tools for each.</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> Suggested $500–$1,200 per session depending on organization size and customization (to validate).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"w2-the-supportive-manager-المدير-الداعم-the-supportive-manag\">W2 — The Supportive Manager («المدير الداعم» — <em>the supportive manager</em>)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Duration:</strong> 2 hours. <strong>Audience:</strong> People managers and team leads; HR business partners.</li><li><strong>Group size:</strong> 6–15 managers. <strong>Format:</strong> In-person or online.</li><li><strong>Rationale:</strong> WHO explicitly recommends <strong>manager training</strong> as a core workplace mental health action, and names workplace risks managers directly control — excessive workloads and work pace, understaffing, long or inflexible hours, lack of control over job design, and under-use of skills. W2 is Dorina's non-clinical translation of that recommendation.</li><li><strong>Learning outcomes:</strong> Managers can (1) spot team-level overload signals (missed deadlines patterns, withdrawal, uncharacteristic irritability — observed behavior, never inferred diagnosis), (2) redesign workload distribution using a simple visibility tool, (3) hold a supportive, strictly non-clinical one-to-one conversation using a listen–acknowledge–ask–signpost structure, (4) refer onward correctly: to HR, to EAP-type resources where they exist, or to qualified professional pathways — never playing counselor themselves.</li><li><strong>DESK coverage:</strong> D and E at team level; S in depth (the manager as signpost, not support-provider of last resort); K as management routine.</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> Suggested $300–$700 per session (to validate). Often sold as an add-on to W1 (\"train the team, equip the managers\").</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"w3-the-organized-calm-الهدوء-المنظّم-organized-calm-for-admi\">W3 — The Organized Calm («الهدوء المنظّم» — <em>organized calm</em>), for administrative professionals &amp; EAs</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Duration:</strong> 2.5–3 hours. <strong>Audience:</strong> Dorina's tribe — administrative professionals, executive assistants, office managers; the people who absorb everyone else's urgency.</li><li><strong>Group size:</strong> 8–20. <strong>Format:</strong> In-person or online; also suitable as a public (open-enrollment) session where individuals pay their own seat.</li><li><strong>Why it exists:</strong> This is where Dorina's 12+ years of lived credibility is strongest. Admin professionals sit at the intersection of everyone's deadlines with the least formal authority to push back — the exact \"lack of control\" risk WHO identifies. Nobody teaches this room better than someone who lived in it.</li><li><strong>Learning outcomes:</strong> Participants can (1) detect the specific overload pattern of support roles (invisible workload, interrupt-driven days), (2) make invisible work visible to their managers with a one-page tool, (3) deploy boundary scripts that respect hierarchy while protecting focus, (4) build a personal weekly reset that survives a chaotic office.</li><li><strong>DESK coverage:</strong> Full method through the lens of support roles; E is the deepest module.</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> Suggested $250–$600 per corporate session; for open-enrollment, suggested $25–$50 per individual seat (both to validate).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"w4-desk-online-desk-أونلاين\">W4 — DESK Online («DESK أونلاين»)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Duration:</strong> 90 minutes live online. <strong>Audience:</strong> Remote/distributed teams; diaspora and GCC-based Lebanese professionals; organizations avoiding travel or venue costs.</li><li><strong>Group size:</strong> 8–25. <strong>Format:</strong> Online, camera-on encouraged but never required (bandwidth realities in Lebanon make camera-optional the respectful default).</li><li><strong>Learning outcomes:</strong> A compressed W1: personal stress-signal mapping, one workload-visibility tool, one boundary script practiced in breakout pairs, help-seeking signposting, and the weekly review as the \"keep\" commitment.</li><li><strong>DESK coverage:</strong> All four stages, one tool each.</li><li><strong>Delivery notes:</strong> Built for connectivity constraints — slides pre-sent as PDF, all exercises workable on paper offline, session recorded only with client's written consent (and never the discussion segments). If Dorina's own power or connection drops, the pre-agreed fallback is a co-host or a rescheduled second half (state this in the contract).</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> Suggested $200–$500 per session (to validate); a natural product for diaspora-linked companies paying in fresh dollars.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"w5-the-desk-quarterly-refresher-retainer\">W5 — The DESK Quarterly (refresher retainer)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Duration:</strong> 60–90 minutes per quarter, four sessions per year. <strong>Audience:</strong> Organizations that have completed W1 (prerequisite).</li><li><strong>Format:</strong> Hybrid by default — alternate in-person and online across the year to manage cost and logistics.</li><li><strong>Structure:</strong> Each quarter revisits one DESK stage in depth with the team's own data: Q1 Detect (re-map signals; what changed?), Q2 Establish (boundary and workload tune-up), Q3 Support (help-seeking culture check), Q4 Keep (annual reset and rest planning — pairs naturally with the December/January arc in <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>).</li><li><strong>Why it matters commercially:</strong> W5 converts one-off workshop income into predictable recurring revenue and gives Dorina longitudinal outcome stories (with consent) — the raw material of the \"Referenced\" rung on the authority ladder.</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> Suggested $600–$1,500 per year for the four-session package (to validate) — priced so the annual retainer is clearly cheaper than four one-off bookings.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"21-suggested-bundles-to-validate-for-the-lebanese-market\">2.1 Suggested bundles (to validate for the Lebanese market)</h3>\n<p>Bundles simplify the buying decision and raise average engagement value without raising any single price:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Bundle</th><th>Contents</th><th>Logic</th><th>Suggested price (to validate)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>The Starter</strong></td><td>W0 (free) → W1 within 60 days</td><td>The default pilot path; W0 is quoted as \"included\" so the W1 proposal already contains it</td><td>W1 price only</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Whole-Team</strong></td><td>W1 for the team + W2 for its managers, delivered within the same month</td><td>Skills above and below the same problem; the most defensible outcome story</td><td>$700–$1,600 (to validate)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Annual System</strong></td><td>W1 + W2 + W5 quarterly retainer</td><td>The full DESK operating system for one organization; the flagship of proposals to HR</td><td>$1,500–$3,000/year (to validate)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The Admin Circle</strong></td><td>W3 delivered as a recurring open-enrollment session (e.g., quarterly public cohort)</td><td>Tribe-building and individual-payer revenue independent of corporate budgets</td><td>$25–$50/seat (to validate)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rules of thumb: never discount a single session — bundle instead; never quote the Annual System to a client who has not yet experienced W0 or W1 (it violates the authority ladder — they are not yet on the \"Invited/Paid\" rung for a retainer).</p>\n<h3 id=\"22-commercial-terms-to-standardize-suggested-to-validate\">2.2 Commercial terms to standardize (suggested, to validate)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Payment:</strong> 50% deposit on signature, 50% within 7 days of delivery; USD quotes with payment method agreed per client norms.</li><li><strong>Cancellation:</strong> free rescheduling once with ≥7 days' notice; ≤72 hours' notice forfeits 25% of the fee (waived for force-majeure events — a realistic clause in Lebanon, and offering it proactively builds trust).</li><li><strong>Headcount:</strong> price holds up to the stated cap; above cap, propose a second cohort rather than a degraded session.</li><li><strong>Scope clause (in every agreement, verbatim):</strong> \"Services are educational workshops on workplace wellbeing. They do not constitute therapy, counseling, diagnosis, assessment, or crisis services, and the facilitator will follow her published referral protocol for any personal disclosures.\"</li><li><strong>Materials license:</strong> handouts are for participants' personal use; organizational reuse or reproduction requires written agreement (protects the DESK Method (proposed) IP described in <code>POSITIONING_AND_BRAND_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>).</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-detailed-session-designs-run-sheets\">3. Detailed Session Designs (Run Sheets)</h2>\n<p>Full facilitation scripts and printable materials for every named exercise are in <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code>. The run sheets below give Dorina the skeleton she delivers from; timing assumes a punctual start (build in a soft 5-minute grace buffer for Lebanese corporate reality — start content on time, put housekeeping in minutes 0–5 so latecomers miss logistics, not learning).</p>\n<h3 id=\"31-w0-the-desk-taster-60-minute-version\">3.1 W0 — The DESK Taster (60-minute version)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Segment</th><th>Method</th><th>Materials</th><th>Facilitator notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0:00–0:05</td><td>Welcome &amp; container</td><td>Talk + slide</td><td>Agenda slide, disclaimer slide</td><td>State the three frame sentences (§1.1) and opt-out rights. Warm, brief, no autobiography yet.</td></tr><tr><td>0:05–0:12</td><td>Hook: \"the cost of pretending we're fine\"</td><td>Story + 2 facts</td><td>1 slide</td><td>Dorina's 60-second origin story (the admin desk where everyone's stress landed). Cite only: WHO's estimate of 12 billion working days lost yearly to depression and anxiety (~US$1 trillion in lost productivity), and that almost 60% of the world's population is in work — \"this is a workplace issue because the workplace is where we all are.\"</td></tr><tr><td>0:12–0:25</td><td>D — Detect: Stress Signal Map (lite)</td><td>Solo worksheet, 5 min + popcorn debrief</td><td>\"Stress Signal Map\" one-pager (see <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code>)</td><td>Participants mark their own early signals privately. Debrief asks for <em>categories</em> (\"who found a body signal? a sleep signal?\"), never personal detail.</td></tr><tr><td>0:25–0:35</td><td>E — Establish: one boundary micro-tool</td><td>Demo + pair try</td><td>Boundary script card (one script only)</td><td>Teach one script from the \"Boundary Scripts Lab\" (the \"not now, but when\" script). Pairs rehearse it on a fictional scenario Dorina provides.</td></tr><tr><td>0:35–0:45</td><td>S — Support: help-seeking in 8 minutes</td><td>Talk + slide</td><td>Referral signpost slide</td><td>What workplace support is (structure, flexibility, signposting) and is not (therapy from your manager). Show the referral signpost slide (§7.2). Mention Lebanon's National Mental Health Programme exists as a national resource ecosystem — as reference, never as affiliation.</td></tr><tr><td>0:45–0:52</td><td>K — Keep: one micro-habit</td><td>Solo commitment</td><td>Commitment card</td><td>Each participant writes one habit for the coming week. Voluntary shares only — two or three, time-boxed.</td></tr><tr><td>0:52–0:58</td><td>The map of the full method + close</td><td>Talk</td><td>DESK overview slide</td><td>\"Today you saw the doorway. The full workshop builds the house.\" One sentence on W1/W2/W3 — offer, don't push (the room is on the \"Seen/Followed\" rung; sell one rung up at most).</td></tr><tr><td>0:58–1:00</td><td>Feedback form + thanks</td><td>Paper/QR form</td><td>Feedback form (§8.1)</td><td>Collect forms in the room — response rates collapse afterward.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>(For the 45-minute version: cut the pair rehearsal to a demo, and compress S to 5 minutes.)</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"32-w1-mental-health-management-at-work-half-day-4-hours-with\">3.2 W1 — Mental Health Management at Work (half day, 4 hours with breaks)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Segment</th><th>Method</th><th>Materials</th><th>Facilitator notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0:00–0:10</td><td>Arrival, welcome, container</td><td>Talk + slides</td><td>Agenda, disclaimer, ground rules</td><td>Full container build (§1.3): agenda, opt-out rights, no forced sharing, confidentiality norm, exit ramp. Managers and reports in the same room? Name it: \"ranks stay at the door for four hours.\"</td></tr><tr><td>0:10–0:25</td><td>Framing: why this is a work skill</td><td>Story + evidence</td><td>3 slides</td><td>Dorina's story (fuller version). Evidence, strictly from the approved base: WHO's workplace risk list (excessive workload/pace, understaffing, long or inflexible hours, lack of control, under-used skills, poor conditions, culture issues); mental health as a continuum shaped by individual, family, community and structural factors — \"so today we work on the parts you can influence, and signpost the parts that need professionals.\"</td></tr><tr><td>0:25–0:55</td><td><strong>D1 — Stress Signal Map</strong> (full)</td><td>Solo worksheet → optional pairs → category debrief</td><td>\"Stress Signal Map\"</td><td>Full version: body, mood, behavior, and work-output signals; personal early-warning threshold. Pairs are opt-in; the debrief harvests patterns, not persons.</td></tr><tr><td>0:55–1:10</td><td>D2 — Naming without stigma</td><td>Facilitated discussion</td><td>Flipchart</td><td>How stress is talked about in Lebanese offices (humor, denial, \"شد حالك\" — <em>\"toughen up\"</em> — culture). Reframe: naming a signal early is competence, not weakness. This is the segment where WHO's finding that literacy training reduces stigma is doing its quiet work.</td></tr><tr><td>1:10–1:20</td><td><strong>Break</strong></td><td>—</td><td>Coffee</td><td>Dorina stays available but does not hover. This break is also the designated bridge window for any 1:1 the disclosure protocol requires (§4.4).</td></tr><tr><td>1:20–1:55</td><td><strong>E1 — Workload X-Ray</strong></td><td>Solo worksheet → trio discussion</td><td>\"Workload X-Ray\"</td><td>Participants map real current workload: visible vs. invisible tasks, interruption load, control level. Trios compare structures (not complaints — Dorina redirects venting into \"what does this show about the system?\").</td></tr><tr><td>1:55–2:30</td><td><strong>E2 — Boundary Scripts Lab</strong></td><td>Demo → pair rehearsal → volunteer replays</td><td>\"Boundary Scripts Lab\" cards</td><td>Three scripts: \"not now, but when,\" \"the visible plate\" (making workload visible before saying yes), and \"the respectful no\" adapted for hierarchy. Rehearsal in pairs on provided scenarios; volunteers replay for the room. Highest-energy segment — protect its full time.</td></tr><tr><td>2:30–2:45</td><td>E3 — <strong>White-Space Audit</strong></td><td>Solo worksheet</td><td>\"White-Space Audit\"</td><td>Where does recovery actually exist in the week? Participants find (or fail to find) their white space — sets up K.</td></tr><tr><td>2:45–2:55</td><td><strong>Break</strong></td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Second bridge window.</td></tr><tr><td>2:55–3:20</td><td><strong>S — Help-Seeking Compass</strong></td><td>Talk + solo worksheet</td><td>\"Help-Seeking Compass\"</td><td>Help-seeking as a skill with levels: self-management → peer/manager support → HR/organizational support → qualified professional care. What each level can and cannot do. Referral signpost slide; note the care gap WHO describes — many who need care don't reach it, which is exactly why knowing pathways matters. Strictly educational; no assessment of anyone's situation.</td></tr><tr><td>3:20–3:45</td><td><strong>K — The Friday 15 Weekly Review</strong></td><td>Teach + guided first run</td><td>\"The Friday 15 Weekly Review\" template</td><td>Participants run an abbreviated first review on the spot, using today's worksheets as input: signals noticed, boundaries to hold, one support action, one rest commitment. This is the habit that carries the workshop home.</td></tr><tr><td>3:45–3:55</td><td>Closure sequence</td><td>Commitment card + signpost + feedback</td><td>§7 materials</td><td>Full closure system (§7): commitment card, referral signposting slide restated, feedback form completed in-room, closing script (see <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code>).</td></tr><tr><td>3:55–4:00</td><td>Thanks &amp; what happens next</td><td>Talk</td><td>—</td><td>Preview the follow-up sequence (§6.3) so the emails land as promised, not as marketing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"33-w2-the-supportive-manager-2-hours\">3.3 W2 — The Supportive Manager (2 hours)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Segment</th><th>Method</th><th>Materials</th><th>Facilitator notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0:00–0:08</td><td>Welcome, container, and the manager's frame</td><td>Talk</td><td>Agenda + disclaimer slides</td><td>Add the manager-specific boundary to the frame: \"Your job after today is to notice, adjust, and signpost — never to counsel, probe, or diagnose. A manager who tries to be a therapist harms twice.\"</td></tr><tr><td>0:08–0:20</td><td>The case for manager action</td><td>Evidence + discussion</td><td>2 slides</td><td>WHO names manager training as a recommended action and lists workplace risks that sit largely in managers' hands: workload and pace, understaffing, inflexible hours, lack of control, under-use of skills, culture. Discussion: \"which of these do you actually control?\" (Usually more than they first admit.)</td></tr><tr><td>0:20–0:45</td><td>Spotting team overload</td><td>Mini-teach + <strong>Stress Signal Map</strong> (team edition)</td><td>\"Stress Signal Map\" (team-signals variant)</td><td>Observable team signals: pattern changes in output, communication withdrawal, rising error rates, uncharacteristic friction. Hard rule taught explicitly: managers observe <strong>behavior</strong>, never infer <strong>conditions</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>0:45–1:10</td><td>Workload design</td><td><strong>Workload X-Ray</strong> (manager edition)</td><td>\"Workload X-Ray\"</td><td>Managers map one real team member's load (anonymized) including invisible work. Discussion: rebalancing levers — sequencing, delegation, visibility rituals, protected focus time.</td></tr><tr><td>1:10–1:15</td><td>Micro-break</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Bridge window per §4.4.</td></tr><tr><td>1:15–1:40</td><td>The supportive conversation</td><td>Demo + trio practice</td><td>Conversation structure card (see <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code>)</td><td>The four-move structure: <strong>listen</strong> (without fixing), <strong>acknowledge</strong> (\"that sounds like a heavy load\"), <strong>ask</strong> (\"what would help from my side?\" — workload, flexibility, priorities), <strong>signpost</strong> (HR, organizational resources, qualified professional pathways). Trios rotate manager/employee/observer on scripted scenarios. Dorina interrupts any trio that drifts into amateur counseling — that interruption <em>is</em> the lesson.</td></tr><tr><td>1:40–1:50</td><td>Referring onward + red lines</td><td>Talk + Q&amp;A</td><td>Referral signpost slide; manager red-lines card</td><td>When to hand to HR; what the organization offers (customized per intake questionnaire, §5.2); the crisis-sign basics and the rule \"connect, don't counsel\" — full protocol in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>, which Dorina summarizes verbally, never improvises beyond.</td></tr><tr><td>1:50–2:00</td><td>K + closure</td><td>Commitment card + feedback</td><td>§7 materials</td><td>Each manager commits to one workload-design change and one supportive-conversation opportunity in the next two weeks. Closure sequence as standard.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-facilitator-guide\">4. Facilitator Guide</h2>\n<h3 id=\"41-preparation-checklist-run-t-7-days-t-1-day-t-0\">4.1 Preparation checklist (run T-7 days, T-1 day, T-0)</h3>\n<p><strong>T-7 days:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] Intake questionnaire (§5.2) returned and reviewed; customization notes written (sector language, team structure, known sensitivities).</li><li>[ ] Participant count confirmed; materials kit (§5.3) packed with 15% surplus handouts.</li><li>[ ] Room details confirmed: layout (cabaret/U-shape preferred; classroom rows discouraged), projector, flipchart, power situation, generator schedule if relevant.</li><li>[ ] Slides finalized; PDF backup exported to phone and USB.</li><li>[ ] Client reminded to send the participant invitation (§6.1) if not yet sent.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>T-1 day:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] Print run: handouts, commitment cards, referral cards, feedback forms, name tents.</li><li>[ ] Referral card contents re-verified against <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> — including the line \"[TO VERIFY before use: current national emotional-support helpline number — check the Ministry of Public Health / National Mental Health Programme site]\" replaced with the verified current number.</li><li>[ ] Confirm start time and arrival logistics with the client contact; confirm who opens the room.</li><li>[ ] Personal preparation: rehearse the opening five minutes aloud; sleep is part of the job.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>T-0 (arrive 45 minutes early):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>[ ] Room set: seating, materials at seats, flipchart tested, slide 1 up, music optional and low.</li><li>[ ] Identify the physical \"bridge space\" for any break-time 1:1 (a corner or side room within sight of others — never fully isolated).</li><li>[ ] Brief the client contact: agenda, break times, and \"if anyone leaves the room, no fuss.\"</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"42-room-energy-management\">4.2 Room &amp; energy management</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Open with warmth, not authority.</strong> Lebanese rooms give respect to competence shown, not claimed. Dorina's credential is her lived admin experience plus the quality of the material — she never needs to inflate it, and must never imply clinical credentials.</li><li><strong>Read the energy every 20 minutes.</strong> Post-lunch dip: switch to pair work. Restless energy: stand-up exercise. Heavy silence after a discussion: name it lightly (\"this one landed — take thirty seconds, then talk to your neighbor\").</li><li><strong>Physical movement is built in</strong> — the run sheets alternate solo/pair/plenary deliberately. Never lecture longer than 15 minutes without an interaction.</li><li><strong>Time is a promise.</strong> Ending on time is part of the container. If a segment runs long, cut content, not the break and never the closure sequence.</li><li><strong>Pre-planned flex cuts.</strong> Each run sheet has designated \"accordion\" segments so cutting is a decision made in advance, not under pressure: in W0, the pair rehearsal compresses to a demo; in W1, D2 (naming discussion) can shrink to 8 minutes and E3 (White-Space Audit) can move to homework with a one-minute setup; in W2, the workload-design discussion compresses before the conversation practice ever does — the practice segment is the session's spine.</li><li><strong>Hierarchy in the room.</strong> If a senior leader attends, greet them normally and give them no special role; if they dominate, the standard redirect applies (Dorina's calm evenhandedness with seniority is itself a demonstration of boundaries — participants notice). If leadership presence visibly freezes the room, switch more work to paper and pairs, where safety returns.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"43-bilingual-delivery-arabicenglish-code-switching\">4.3 Bilingual delivery (Arabic/English code-switching)</h3>\n<p>Lebanese corporate rooms code-switch constantly; fighting it loses the room. Working norms:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Slides and handouts in English</strong> (the default written register of Lebanese business), with key terms glossed in Arabic on the slide where the term carries emotional weight — e.g., burnout (<strong>الاحتراق الوظيفي</strong>), boundaries (<strong>الحدود</strong>), stress signals (<strong>إشارات التوتر</strong>).</li><li><strong>Spoken delivery mirrors the room.</strong> Open in whichever language the client contact used in emails, then match the participants. Stories and humor land best in Lebanese Arabic; frameworks and instructions are clearest in English. Dorina should switch deliberately, not apologetically.</li><li><strong>Emotional-register rule:</strong> stigma-sensitive moments (D2 naming segment, S help-seeking segment) benefit from Arabic — it removes the distancing effect English can create around personal topics. Technical tools (Workload X-Ray, weekly review) stay anchored in English terms so participants can reuse them in written work.</li><li><strong>Never translate live word-for-word</strong> — say it once, well, in the language that fits; repeat only headline instructions bilingually (\"خمس دقايق — five minutes, then we regroup\").</li><li>Ask in the intake questionnaire (§5.2) about language mix; nonprofit/humanitarian organization rooms may skew Arabic, agency rooms may skew English.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"44-handling-difficult-moments\">4.4 Handling difficult moments</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>What Dorina does</th><th>What she never does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Dominant participant</strong></td><td>Thank + redirect: \"Great point — let's hear a different desk.\" Assign them a role (timekeeper, scribe) at the next exercise. If persistent, a friendly private word at the break.</td><td>Publicly shut them down; let them convert the session into their platform.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Silence</strong></td><td>Wait a full 7 seconds (count silently — silence is thinking). Downgrade the ask: plenary question → pairs → written. Offer her own example first.</td><td>Fill every silence herself; cold-call someone for personal content.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Skepticism (\"this is soft stuff\")</strong></td><td>Welcome it: \"Healthy skepticism is a Detect signal working well.\" Answer with the operational frame — workload, hours, control are business design questions; WHO's working-days figure if numbers are wanted. Invite the skeptic to stress-test one tool.</td><td>Argue, plead, or over-claim outcomes; take it personally.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The room turns into a complaints session about management</strong></td><td>Acknowledge once, then structure: \"Real — and today's question is what's in <em>your</em> control; the manager version of this workshop exists too.\" (Genuinely note themes for the client debrief, anonymized.)</td><td>Join the complaining; promise to \"tell management\"; let it consume an exercise.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Someone cries or is visibly moved</strong></td><td>Normalize quietly (\"take your time — this stuff is real\"), offer the exit ramp without spotlight, continue the session's flow so the person isn't the center of attention, and apply §4.5 at the break.</td><td>Stop the whole room to process one person's emotion; probe for the cause.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"45-disclosure-protocol-summary-the-critical-page\">4.5 Disclosure protocol summary (the critical page)</h3>\n<p>Full protocol: <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>. The in-session summary Dorina memorizes:</p>\n<p>If a participant shares personal distress, mentions a mental health condition, or shows a possible crisis sign (in the room, in an exercise debrief, or on a worksheet):</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Acknowledge, briefly and warmly.</strong> \"Thank you for trusting us with that — that sounds genuinely hard.\" Dignity first, always.</li><li><strong>Don't explore.</strong> No follow-up questions about the experience, history, symptoms, or causes. Not in plenary, not in pairs, not \"just out of care.\" Exploration is clinical territory.</li><li><strong>Bridge to break-time 1:1.</strong> \"I'd like to check in with you at the break — is that okay?\" At the break, in the semi-visible bridge space: listen briefly, do not advise on the condition or situation, express care.</li><li><strong>Hand the referral card.</strong> The card (contents defined in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>) lists qualified pathway types and the verified helpline line. Frame: \"The strongest move in the whole DESK method is reaching the right person — this card is the map.\"</li><li><strong>Follow the protocol for anything beyond.</strong> Crisis signs, safety concerns, or requests for ongoing personal help all route through <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> — including when to inform the client contact and how to protect confidentiality while doing so.</li><li><strong>Never diagnose, assess, or advise on conditions.</strong> Not tentatively, not \"off the record,\" not when directly asked (\"do you think I have burnout?\" → \"I can't tell you that — and someone qualified genuinely can. Let me show you the pathway.\").</li></ol>\n<p>This protocol is rehearsed, not improvised. Dorina practices the bridge sentence aloud before every session until it is automatic.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-logistics-operations\">5. Logistics &amp; Operations</h2>\n<h3 id=\"51-booking-to-delivery-pipeline\">5.1 Booking-to-delivery pipeline</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Owner</th><th>Actions</th><th>Target timing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Inquiry</td><td>Dorina</td><td>Respond within 24h; send one-page portfolio PDF; propose a 20-minute discovery call</td><td>Day 0–1</td></tr><tr><td>2. Discovery call</td><td>Dorina</td><td>Understand need, audience, budget signal; recommend the right rung (often W0 first); state the non-clinical scope explicitly</td><td>Day 2–7</td></tr><tr><td>3. Proposal &amp; agreement</td><td>Dorina</td><td>One-page proposal: format, outcomes, price, logistics needs, scope-and-boundaries clause, cancellation terms, W4 connectivity-fallback clause</td><td>Within 3 days of call</td></tr><tr><td>4. Intake</td><td>Client contact</td><td>Intake questionnaire (§5.2) returned min. 7 days pre-delivery</td><td>T-14 to T-7</td></tr><tr><td>5. Customization &amp; prep</td><td>Dorina</td><td>§4.1 checklist; participant invitation copy (§6.1) sent to client to forward</td><td>T-7 to T-1</td></tr><tr><td>6. Delivery</td><td>Dorina</td><td>Run sheet + closure system</td><td>T-0</td></tr><tr><td>7. Follow-up sequence</td><td>Dorina</td><td>§6.3: 24h email → 1-week nudge → 1-month check-in + survey</td><td>T+1 to T+30</td></tr><tr><td>8. Debrief &amp; rebook</td><td>Dorina</td><td>Client debrief (anonymized themes + feedback summary); propose next rung (W1 after W0; W2 after W1; W5 after two engagements)</td><td>T+7 to T+30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Track every engagement in a simple spreadsheet (pipeline stage, dates, format, feedback scores, rebook status) — this feeds Section 8 directly. The workflow tooling that supports this is described in <code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"52-client-intake-questionnaire-the-actual-questions\">5.2 Client intake questionnaire (the actual questions)</h3>\n<p>Sent as a short form or document after agreement; answers drive customization.</p>\n<ol><li>What does your organization do, and what does a typical week look like for the team attending? (Sector, team size, rhythm of the work.)</li><li>Who exactly will attend — roles, seniority mix, and will managers and their direct reports be in the same room?</li><li>How many participants do you expect? (We recommend 10–20 for interactive formats.)</li><li>What prompted this workshop now? Was there a specific situation, season, or leadership decision behind it? (No names needed — context only.)</li><li>What would make this a clear success for you three months from now?</li><li>What language mix does the team naturally use in meetings — mostly Arabic, mostly English, or fluid switching?</li><li>Are there topics or recent events we should handle with extra care (e.g., recent layoffs, restructuring, a loss in the team)? We will not raise them; we simply avoid stepping on them blind.</li><li>What support does your organization currently offer employees (flexible hours, HR open door, any employee-assistance-type service, health coverage)? We reference only what actually exists.</li><li>Room and logistics: What space is available, what is the seating, is there a projector and flipchart, and what is the electricity/generator situation during our time slot?</li><li>For online/hybrid sessions: which platform does your team already use, and how reliable is participants' connectivity?</li><li>Who is our on-the-day contact, and who should receive the post-workshop debrief summary?</li><li>Anything else you want us to know before we design your session?</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Scope note appended to the form (verbatim):</strong> \"This workshop is educational — it teaches practical workplace wellbeing skills. It is not therapy, diagnosis, assessment, or crisis support, and it does not replace professional care. Our facilitator follows a strict referral protocol for any personal disclosures.\"</p>\n<h3 id=\"53-materials-kit-list\">5.3 Materials kit list</h3>\n<p><strong>Core kit (every session):</strong> printed handouts per run sheet (+15% surplus); commitment cards; referral cards (verified per §4.1); feedback forms + a QR-code version; name tents + markers; flipchart pads ×2 and working markers (test them — the eternal facilitator trap); masking tape; timer (phone in airplane mode); printed run sheet + facilitator notes; disclaimer/agenda slides as printed A4 backups; pens ×30; a small box for collected feedback forms.</p>\n<p><strong>Tech layer:</strong> laptop + charger; HDMI and USB-C adapters; USB stick with slides (PDF + editable); slides also on phone; portable speaker (optional); for W4/hybrid: headset, lighting, and the pre-sent PDF deck.</p>\n<p><strong>Low-tech fallback (power cuts are a planning assumption, not a surprise):</strong> the entire session must be deliverable with <strong>zero electricity</strong> — flipchart versions of every key slide pre-drawn on the pad's first pages; all exercises are paper-based by design; daylight-viable room requested in intake Q9. If power dies mid-session, the line is: \"Perfect — the DESK Method was designed for real Lebanese conditions. Flipchart it is.\" (This moment, handled with humor, routinely becomes the most-remembered part of the day.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"54-co-facilitation-rule-what-needs-a-licensed-professional-w\">5.4 Co-facilitation rule: what needs a licensed professional, what gets declined</h3>\n<p>Dorina delivers alone anything within the educational frame: stress literacy, boundaries, workload structure, routines, help-seeking as a skill, manager conversation structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Requires a licensed mental health professional as co-facilitator (Dorina may organize and co-host, never solo-deliver):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Any session marketed around a condition (anxiety, depression, trauma, grief) rather than workplace skills.</li><li>Psychological first aid training or anything derived from clinical protocols.</li><li>Sessions for teams in the immediate aftermath of a critical incident (death of a colleague, serious accident, security event).</li><li>Any format involving screening, assessment, or individual evaluation of participants.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Declined and referred out entirely (with a warm referral to qualified providers):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Requests for individual counseling, \"coaching\" of a struggling named employee, or mediation of an acute interpersonal/mental-health situation.</li><li>Crisis response or debriefing requests.</li><li>Anything a client insists on framing as treatment or therapy, even after scope clarification.</li></ul>\n<p>Declining well <em>builds</em> authority: \"That's outside what an educator should ethically do — here is the kind of professional you need, and here is what I <em>can</em> do around it once the acute phase has passed.\" The referral-partner shortlist logic lives in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> and <code>OPPORTUNITY_MAP_LEBANON.md</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-participant-journey\">6. Participant Journey</h2>\n<h3 id=\"61-before-invitation-copy-snippet-client-forwards-to-partici\">6.1 Before — invitation copy snippet (client forwards to participants)</h3>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Subject: [Date] — a working session on managing pressure well (not a lecture)</strong></p>\n<p>Hi team — on [date] we're hosting <strong>[workshop name]</strong> with workplace wellbeing educator <strong>Dorina Salman</strong>. It's a practical, hands-on session: you'll map your own stress signals, get ready-to-use scripts for protecting your focus, and leave with a 15-minute weekly habit that keeps it all alive.</p>\n<p>Three things to know going in: everything is <strong>optional</strong> — nobody is ever required to share anything personal; this is <strong>education, not therapy</strong> — we work on workload and habits, not private matters; and it <strong>ends on time</strong>. Bring a pen and your real calendar. See you there.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Expectation-setting is part of safety: participants who arrive knowing the container exists relax into it faster.</p>\n<h3 id=\"62-during\">6.2 During</h3>\n<p>The run sheets (§3) and closure system (§7) define the in-session journey. Two participant-experience rules sit above everything: every participant leaves with <strong>paper in hand</strong> (their own completed worksheets — the workshop's tangible residue), and every participant hears the referral signpost <strong>twice</strong> (S segment + closure) without it ever feeling like an alarm.</p>\n<h3 id=\"63-after-the-follow-up-system\">6.3 After — the follow-up system</h3>\n<p><strong>T+24h — Thank-you email (Dorina sends to the client contact for forwarding, or directly where emails were collected with consent):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Subject: Your DESK toolkit — and one small ask</strong></p>\n<p>Hi everyone — thank you for the energy you brought yesterday. A few things, briefly:</p>\n<p><strong>Your tools:</strong> attached is the PDF pack of everything we used — the Stress Signal Map, the Workload X-Ray, the boundary scripts, and The Friday 15 Weekly Review template. Print the weekly review; it works better on paper.</p>\n<p><strong>Your commitment:</strong> you each wrote one habit on your commitment card. That card is the whole workshop in one sentence — keep it where you'll see it on Friday.</p>\n<p><strong>The signpost, one more time:</strong> what we did together was education, not therapy. If anything in your life needs more than a habit — that's normal, that's human, and reaching a qualified professional is the strongest move in the entire method. The referral card from your pack has the pathways.</p>\n<p>It was a privilege to spend the time with you. — Dorina</p>\n<p><em>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>T+7 days — one-habit nudge (short email or client-channel message):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Subject: Friday is coming — 15 minutes</strong></p>\n<p>Quick nudge from your workshop: this Friday, run <strong>The Friday 15</strong> — 15 minutes, four questions: What signals did I notice this week? Which boundary held, which slipped? Do I need to ask for anything? What's my rest plan for the weekend? That's it. One week of doing it beats a hundred slides about it.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>T+30 days — check-in + feedback survey.</strong> A short warm note (\"one month on — two minutes of your honesty helps me make this better for the next team\") linking to the survey:</p>\n<ol><li>One month later, which single tool or idea from the workshop have you actually used? (Open text)</li><li>On a scale of 1–5, how much do you agree: \"I notice my own overload signals earlier than I did before the workshop.\"</li><li>On a scale of 1–5: \"I have used at least one boundary or workload tool from the session in real work.\"</li><li>Have you run The Friday 15 Weekly Review at least once? (Yes, more than once / Yes, once / Not yet)</li><li>What has gotten in the way of using what you learned? (Open text)</li><li>On a scale of 0–10, how likely are you to recommend this workshop to a colleague or another team?</li><li>What should be added, cut, or changed? (Open text)</li><li>Optional: If any part of this experience helped you in a way you'd be willing to have quoted (anonymously or with your name — your choice, and you can withdraw it anytime), tell us here. Leaving this blank is completely fine.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"64-from-outcomes-to-testimonials-and-case-summaries\">6.4 From outcomes to testimonials and case summaries</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Consent is explicit, written, and revocable.</strong> Only survey Q8 responses (or direct written offers) become testimonials, and only after Dorina confirms in writing how the person wants to be credited. No invented or \"reconstructed\" quotes, ever.</li><li><strong>Case summaries are patterns, not people.</strong> After each engagement, Dorina writes a five-line internal summary: sector type, format, headline feedback scores, one anonymized theme, one iteration note. With client permission, an anonymized version (\"a 15-person team at a Beirut services firm\") can appear in proposals and in LinkedIn content per <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code>.</li><li><strong>Nothing disclosed in a session is ever content.</strong> Personal moments from the room — even anonymized, even moving — do not appear in marketing. Workshop <em>design</em> stories are shareable; participant <em>lives</em> are not.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"65-the-client-debrief-t7-to-t14-the-meeting-that-earns-the-r\">6.5 The client debrief (T+7 to T+14) — the meeting that earns the rebooking</h3>\n<p>A 30-minute call or short written summary to the client contact, with a fixed agenda:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>What we did:</strong> format delivered, attendance, and the session's arc in three sentences.</li><li><strong>How it landed:</strong> headline feedback scores (value, safety, recommendation) against the quality bar in Section 8 — shared transparently, including anything below bar and what Dorina will change.</li><li><strong>What the room told us (anonymized):</strong> two or three workload/culture <em>themes</em> — never individuals, never disclosures. Where relevant, map themes to the WHO workplace risk categories (workload and pace, hours and flexibility, control, skills use, culture) so the client sees their situation in a recognized framework rather than as criticism.</li><li><strong>What the organization could do next:</strong> one or two low-cost structural suggestions within the client's control (e.g., a workload-visibility ritual, protected focus blocks) — offered as options, not audit findings.</li><li><strong>The natural next rung:</strong> the single most logical follow-on (W2 after W1; W5 after two engagements; the 1-month survey as the checkpoint) — proposed once, without pressure.</li></ol>\n<p>The debrief is where a workshop becomes a relationship. It is also the ethical firewall in action: the client receives patterns and scores, never names or stories.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-closure-system-how-every-workshop-ends\">7. Closure System — How Every Workshop Ends</h2>\n<p>The last ten minutes are identical in shape across W0–W5. Predictable endings are part of the container, and this sequence is where safety, habit, and business quietly converge.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>The commitment card.</strong> Each participant writes one specific habit (\"I will…\") on a physical card, keeps it, and — if willing — photographs it as their own reminder. Voluntary shares only, two or three, time-boxed. The card template is in <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code>.</li><li><strong>The referral signposting slide.</strong> Always the penultimate slide, always delivered in the same calm tone: the levels of support, the reminder that professional care is the right tool for some things, the referral card in their pack, and the verified helpline line — \"[TO VERIFY before use: current national emotional-support helpline number — check the Ministry of Public Health / National Mental Health Programme site]\" replaced before first use. Never rushed, never skipped, never dramatic.</li><li><strong>The feedback form.</strong> Completed <strong>in the room</strong> (paper or QR), before goodbyes — this single discipline is the difference between real measurement and vibes.</li><li><strong>The closing script.</strong> Dorina's 60-second close — the \"keep the card, use the Friday 15, and know the pathway\" arc — is scripted verbatim in <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code> so quality never depends on end-of-day energy. It ends with thanks, on time, in whichever language the room's heart was in — usually Arabic: <strong>\"اعتنوا بحالكن — هيدي مش رفاهية، هيدي شغل\"</strong> (<em>\"take care of yourselves — that's not a luxury, that's part of the work\"</em>).</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-measurement\">8. Measurement</h2>\n<h3 id=\"81-in-room-feedback-form-every-session\">8.1 In-room feedback form (every session)</h3>\n<ol><li>Overall, how valuable was this session? (1–5)</li><li>\"I can name my own early stress signals.\" (1–5, agree)</li><li>\"I know one concrete thing I will do differently this week.\" (1–5, agree)</li><li>\"The facilitator created a comfortable, pressure-free space.\" (1–5, agree)</li><li>How likely are you to recommend this session? (0–10)</li><li>The most useful part was… / One thing to improve is… (open)</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"82-the-metrics-that-actually-matter\">8.2 The metrics that actually matter</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Definition</th><th>Quality bar (first year, to calibrate)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Session value score</td><td>Mean of Q1</td><td>≥ 4.2/5</td></tr><tr><td>Safety score</td><td>Mean of Q4</td><td>≥ 4.5/5 — this one is non-negotiable; below 4.5 triggers immediate review of container delivery</td></tr><tr><td>Recommendation score</td><td>Mean of Q5 (0–10)</td><td>≥ 8</td></tr><tr><td>Behavior echo</td><td>% of 1-month survey respondents reporting they used ≥1 tool (survey Q3 ≥ 4, or Q4 = yes)</td><td>≥ 40% of respondents</td></tr><tr><td>Pilot-to-paid conversion</td><td>% of W0 deliveries that produce a paid booking (any W1–W5) within 90 days</td><td>≥ 1 in 3</td></tr><tr><td>Rebooking rate</td><td>% of paying clients who book again (any format, incl. W5) within 12 months</td><td>≥ 30%</td></tr><tr><td>Referral bookings</td><td>% of new inquiries citing a past client or participant</td><td>Track from month 1; this becomes the leading indicator of the \"Trusted → Invited\" transition</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"83-the-iteration-loop-simple-ruthless-monthly\">8.3 The iteration loop (simple, ruthless, monthly)</h3>\n<p>After every session: same-evening, Dorina writes three lines — <em>what landed, what dragged, what I change next time</em> — and updates the run sheet within 48 hours (one change at a time, so cause and effect stay visible). Monthly: review the metrics table; any metric below bar for two consecutive engagements gets a named fix in the next delivery. Quarterly: retire the weakest exercise variant, promote the strongest, and re-validate prices against actual close rates. The quality bar is deliberately modest in year one — the goal is a truthful baseline, then compounding.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"9-how-workshops-connect-to-the-rest-of-the-system\">9. How Workshops Connect to the Rest of the System</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Selling:</strong> the funnel that fills W0 seats — LinkedIn authority content, the lead magnet, the pilot-outreach campaign to HR professionals and people managers — is specified in <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>. The rule of the authority ladder applies to rooms as much as feeds: a W0 room is being moved from <em>Seen</em> to <em>Trusted</em>; the only sale made inside it is the idea of the next conversation.</li><li><strong>Where:</strong> target segments, sectors, and institution types to approach — SMEs, agencies, law firms, nonprofit/humanitarian organizations/development organizations, business associations, and diaspora-linked companies — are mapped in <code>OPPORTUNITY_MAP_LEBANON.md</code>, including the ecosystem context of Lebanon's National Mental Health Programme (a reference point and potential future partner to approach — never a claimed affiliation). Note that the NMHP has itself run a national \"Mental Health in the Workplace\" campaign, which is useful evidence in proposals that this topic has national-level standing in Lebanon.</li><li><strong>Materials:</strong> every named exercise, handout, card, and script referenced above is fully written in <code>WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md</code>.</li><li><strong>Safety:</strong> the complete disclosure, crisis-sign, and referral protocol — including the referral card contents and partner-verification checklist — is <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>, which governs every session in this blueprint.</li><li><strong>Calendar leverage:</strong> October's World Mental Health Day (October 10) — whose past themes include workplace mental health — is the natural flagship window for W0 pilots and W1 launches; the campaign choreography is in <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code> and <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-workshop-specific\">What we still need from Dorina (workshop-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Availability envelope:</strong> How many evenings/half-days per month can she realistically dedicate to delivery while employed — and is her current employer's policy on outside professional activity confirmed in writing?</li><li><strong>Facilitation baseline:</strong> Has she facilitated or presented to groups before (team trainings, inductions, meetings), and would she like one or two rehearsal runs of W0 with friends/family before the first real pilot?</li><li><strong>Referral shortlist:</strong> Names of 2–3 licensed mental health professionals (or clinics/organization types) she is comfortable approaching for the referral card and for co-facilitation under §5.4 — to be verified per <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</li><li><strong>First-pilot targets:</strong> Which 3–5 organizations in her existing network (former law-firm contacts, current professional circle, university alumni ties) could plausibly host the first free W0 sessions?</li><li><strong>Pricing comfort:</strong> Her reaction to the suggested ranges in Section 2 — and any real quotes she encounters from the Lebanese training market so we can calibrate against actual data rather than assumptions.</li><li><strong>Delivery kit status:</strong> Does she have reliable access to a laptop, printing, and transport to client sites — and should we budget for a starter materials kit?</li><li><strong>Online platform preference:</strong> Which video platform she is most fluent in for W4, and her home connectivity/power reliability for hosting live sessions (this determines whether W4 needs a co-host fallback from day one).</li><li><strong>Arabic naming check:</strong> Her preference among the suggested Arabic workshop names in Section 2 — she is the native judge of what sounds natural in a Lebanese corporate room.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"sources-3\">Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme (NMHP): <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"final-review-addendum-preconditions-before-public-delivery-2\">Final Review Addendum — Preconditions Before Public Delivery</h2>\n<p>Before Dorina publishes the profile positioning or delivers paid workshops, complete these checks:</p>\n<ul><li>Confirm exact employment history and current employer restrictions; do not publish any dates or titles that Dorina has not verified.</li><li>Complete at least one non-clinical mental-health-literacy or Psychological First Aid training and one basic facilitation practice cycle before paid delivery.</li><li>Confirm local public/professional liability insurance options and obtain local legal review for disclaimers, privacy boundaries, workshop terms, and employer/moonlighting issues.</li><li>Verify current Lebanon crisis/referral contacts and operating hours before every printed handout or live workshop. Embrace Lebanon publicly lists 1564 for its Lifeline, but all details must be re-checked close to use.</li><li>Budget honestly: content drafting may take 2–3 hours/week, but the full engine including engagement, approvals, outreach, workshop preparation, delivery, and review can require 6–10+ hours/week during launch periods.</li><li>Track safeguarding metrics alongside growth: referral signposts made, boundary escalations, stopped exercises, post-workshop debrief notes, and whether the protocol was followed.</li></ul>","toc":[{"id":"1-workshop-philosophy-safety-frame","text":"1. Workshop Philosophy & Safety Frame","lvl":2},{"id":"11-education-not-therapy-the-line-that-protects-everyone","text":"1.1 Education, not therapy — the line that protects everyone","lvl":3},{"id":"12-the-desk-method-proposed-as-session-architecture","text":"1.2 The DESK Method (proposed) as session architecture","lvl":3},{"id":"13-the-container-concept","text":"1.3 The \"container\" concept","lvl":3},{"id":"2-the-workshop-portfolio-product-ladder","text":"2. The Workshop Portfolio (Product Ladder)","lvl":2},{"id":"w0-the-desk-taster-جلسة-تعارف-مع-طريقة-desk-an-introduction-","text":"W0 — The DESK Taster («جلسة تعارف مع طريقة DESK» — an introduction session to the DESK Method)","lvl":3},{"id":"w1-mental-health-management-at-work-إدارة-الصحة-النفسية-في-ا","text":"W1 — Mental Health Management at Work («إدارة الصحة النفسية في العمل» — managing mental health at work)","lvl":3},{"id":"w2-the-supportive-manager-المدير-الداعم-the-supportive-manag","text":"W2 — The Supportive Manager («المدير الداعم» — the supportive manager)","lvl":3},{"id":"w3-the-organized-calm-الهدوء-المنظّم-organized-calm-for-admi","text":"W3 — The Organized Calm («الهدوء المنظّم» — organized calm), for administrative professionals & EAs","lvl":3},{"id":"w4-desk-online-desk-أونلاين","text":"W4 — DESK Online («DESK أونلاين»)","lvl":3},{"id":"w5-the-desk-quarterly-refresher-retainer","text":"W5 — The DESK Quarterly (refresher retainer)","lvl":3},{"id":"21-suggested-bundles-to-validate-for-the-lebanese-market","text":"2.1 Suggested bundles (to validate for the Lebanese market)","lvl":3},{"id":"22-commercial-terms-to-standardize-suggested-to-validate","text":"2.2 Commercial terms to standardize (suggested, to validate)","lvl":3},{"id":"3-detailed-session-designs-run-sheets","text":"3. Detailed Session Designs (Run Sheets)","lvl":2},{"id":"31-w0-the-desk-taster-60-minute-version","text":"3.1 W0 — The DESK Taster (60-minute version)","lvl":3},{"id":"32-w1-mental-health-management-at-work-half-day-4-hours-with","text":"3.2 W1 — Mental Health Management at Work (half day, 4 hours with breaks)","lvl":3},{"id":"33-w2-the-supportive-manager-2-hours","text":"3.3 W2 — The Supportive Manager (2 hours)","lvl":3},{"id":"4-facilitator-guide","text":"4. Facilitator Guide","lvl":2},{"id":"41-preparation-checklist-run-t-7-days-t-1-day-t-0","text":"4.1 Preparation checklist (run T-7 days, T-1 day, T-0)","lvl":3},{"id":"42-room-energy-management","text":"4.2 Room & energy management","lvl":3},{"id":"43-bilingual-delivery-arabicenglish-code-switching","text":"4.3 Bilingual delivery (Arabic/English code-switching)","lvl":3},{"id":"44-handling-difficult-moments","text":"4.4 Handling difficult moments","lvl":3},{"id":"45-disclosure-protocol-summary-the-critical-page","text":"4.5 Disclosure protocol summary (the critical page)","lvl":3},{"id":"5-logistics-operations","text":"5. Logistics & Operations","lvl":2},{"id":"51-booking-to-delivery-pipeline","text":"5.1 Booking-to-delivery pipeline","lvl":3},{"id":"52-client-intake-questionnaire-the-actual-questions","text":"5.2 Client intake questionnaire (the actual questions)","lvl":3},{"id":"53-materials-kit-list","text":"5.3 Materials kit list","lvl":3},{"id":"54-co-facilitation-rule-what-needs-a-licensed-professional-w","text":"5.4 Co-facilitation rule: what needs a licensed professional, what gets declined","lvl":3},{"id":"6-participant-journey","text":"6. Participant Journey","lvl":2},{"id":"61-before-invitation-copy-snippet-client-forwards-to-partici","text":"6.1 Before — invitation copy snippet (client forwards to participants)","lvl":3},{"id":"62-during","text":"6.2 During","lvl":3},{"id":"63-after-the-follow-up-system","text":"6.3 After — the follow-up system","lvl":3},{"id":"64-from-outcomes-to-testimonials-and-case-summaries","text":"6.4 From outcomes to testimonials and case summaries","lvl":3},{"id":"65-the-client-debrief-t7-to-t14-the-meeting-that-earns-the-r","text":"6.5 The client debrief (T+7 to T+14) — the meeting that earns the rebooking","lvl":3},{"id":"7-closure-system-how-every-workshop-ends","text":"7. Closure System — How Every Workshop Ends","lvl":2},{"id":"8-measurement","text":"8. Measurement","lvl":2},{"id":"81-in-room-feedback-form-every-session","text":"8.1 In-room feedback form (every session)","lvl":3},{"id":"82-the-metrics-that-actually-matter","text":"8.2 The metrics that actually matter","lvl":3},{"id":"83-the-iteration-loop-simple-ruthless-monthly","text":"8.3 The iteration loop (simple, ruthless, monthly)","lvl":3},{"id":"9-how-workshops-connect-to-the-rest-of-the-system","text":"9. How Workshops Connect to the Rest of the System","lvl":2},{"id":"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-workshop-specific","text":"What we still need from Dorina (workshop-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"sources-3","text":"Sources","lvl":2},{"id":"final-review-addendum-preconditions-before-public-delivery-2","text":"Final Review Addendum — Preconditions Before Public Delivery","lvl":2}],"words":8460,"mins":37},{"slug":"workshop-exercises-and-handouts","file":"WORKSHOP_EXERCISES_AND_HANDOUTS.md","title":"Workshop Exercises & Handouts","blurb":"Seven core exercises, verbatim scripts, printable handout masters, safety rules.","track":"Delivery","n":9,"html":"<h1 id=\"workshop-exercises-and-handouts-dorina-salman\">Workshop Exercises and Handouts — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> This is the complete, ready-to-print facilitation toolkit for Dorina's workplace-wellbeing workshops. It contains verbatim opening and closing scripts, seven fully written core exercises (facilitator cards plus participant handout text), two manager-session add-ons, and all printable handout masters. It is the hands-on companion to <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code> (which defines the session formats that use these exercises) and operates under the safety rules of <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>. Everything here is educational and non-clinical: Dorina teaches practical mental health <em>management</em> — stress literacy, boundaries, workload structure, and help-seeking as a skill — and never diagnoses, treats, or assesses anyone.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-how-to-use-this-toolkit\">1. How to Use This Toolkit</h2>\n<p>Each exercise is self-contained: a <strong>facilitator card</strong> (what Dorina does and says) and a <strong>participant handout</strong> (full text, ready to lay out and print). Pick exercises according to the session format defined in <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>; the DESK Method (proposed) is the spine that connects them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"11-exercise-desk-mapping\">1.1 Exercise ↔ DESK mapping</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Exercise</th><th>DESK letter</th><th>Core skill taught</th><th>Time</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Stress Signal Map</td><td><strong>D — Detect</strong></td><td>Noticing early overload signals without stigma</td><td>20 min</td><td>All-staff sessions</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Workload X-Ray</td><td><strong>E — Establish</strong></td><td>Making invisible workload visible</td><td>25 min</td><td>All-staff and team sessions</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Boundary Scripts Lab</td><td><strong>E — Establish</strong></td><td>Saying no respectfully, in Arabic and English</td><td>30 min</td><td>All-staff sessions</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>White-Space Audit</td><td><strong>E / K</strong></td><td>Finding and protecting recovery gaps</td><td>20 min</td><td>All-staff and manager sessions</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Help-Seeking Compass</td><td><strong>S — Support</strong></td><td>Mapping support options; help-seeking as strength</td><td>20 min</td><td>All-staff sessions</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>The Friday 15 Weekly Review</td><td><strong>K — Keep</strong></td><td>A sustainable weekly self-check ritual</td><td>15 min teach + take-home</td><td>Closing block of any session</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Energy Ledger</td><td><strong>K — Keep</strong></td><td>Tracking drains and gains; leisure and movement habits</td><td>10 min teach + one-week take-home</td><td>Closing block; follow-up sessions</td></tr><tr><td>M1</td><td>The Supportive Conversation</td><td><strong>S — Support</strong></td><td>Manager listening and signposting skills</td><td>35 min</td><td>Manager session (W2) only</td></tr><tr><td>M2</td><td>Team Workload Weather Report</td><td><strong>D / E</strong></td><td>A recurring team check-in on load, not feelings</td><td>15 min teach</td><td>Manager session (W2) only</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"12-safety-rules-that-apply-to-every-exercise-non-negotiable\">1.2 Safety rules that apply to every exercise (non-negotiable)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Participation is always voluntary.</strong> Every exercise is an invitation, never a requirement.</li><li><strong>The pass rule.</strong> Anyone may say \"pass\" on any question, pairing, or share-back — at any point, without giving a reason, and without being asked why. Dorina states this rule aloud before every exercise that involves speaking.</li><li><strong>No personal-trauma prompts.</strong> No exercise ever asks participants to share personal health history, past crises, family matters, or emotionally painful memories. All prompts stay at the level of <em>work situations, workload, habits, and routines</em>.</li><li><strong>The facilitator never interprets anyone's feelings.</strong> Dorina reflects back what people <em>say about their work</em>, never what she thinks they <em>feel or \"really\" mean</em>. No labeling, no analysis, no armchair diagnosis — ever.</li><li><strong>Nothing here is a screening tool.</strong> No exercise produces a score, a category, or a \"result\" about anyone's mental state. Signals of overload are discussed as normal human responses to work conditions, not symptoms.</li><li><strong>Disclosure protocol.</strong> If a participant discloses distress or asks for personal help, Dorina follows the disclosure and referral steps in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> — acknowledge, keep it private, signpost to qualified help, never probe.</li><li><strong>The footer disclaimer block (Section 8) appears on every printed handout.</strong> No exceptions.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-opening-script-verbatim-2-minutes\">2. Opening Script (Verbatim, ~2 Minutes)</h2>\n<p>Dorina delivers this at the start of every session, before any exercise. Read it as written until it is memorized; the safety beats are not optional.</p>\n<blockquote><p>\"Good morning, everyone — welcome, and thank you for giving this hour to yourselves.</p>\n<p>My name is Dorina Salman. I spent more than twelve years in administrative and executive-assistant roles, so I know from the inside what deadlines, inboxes, and 'quick favors' at 7 p.m. actually feel like. Today I'm here as a <strong>workplace wellbeing educator</strong>. Let me be very clear about what that means: <strong>I am an educator, not a therapist.</strong> I don't diagnose anyone, I don't treat anyone, and I won't ask anyone to share anything personal about their health. What I <em>will</em> do is teach practical, learnable skills for managing stress, workload, and boundaries at work.</p>\n<p>This session is education, not therapy. We'll talk about work — workload, routines, boundaries, and how to ask for support — not about anyone's private life or medical history.</p>\n<p>Three ground rules keep this room safe. <strong>One:</strong> everything is voluntary — every exercise is an invitation. <strong>Two:</strong> you can say 'pass' at any moment, on anything, and no one — including me — will ask you why. <strong>Three:</strong> what colleagues share here stays here.</p>\n<p>One more important thing. If anything we discuss today touches something you're personally struggling with, please talk to a qualified professional — I'll show you at the end exactly where to find that kind of support, and there's a card in your handout pack with the details. Asking for that help is a skill and a strength, and it belongs with people trained to give it.</p>\n<p>That's the frame. Let's begin.\"</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"arabic-rendering-of-the-key-safety-lines-deliver-in-arabic-w\">Arabic rendering of the key safety lines (deliver in Arabic when the room prefers it)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>English</th><th>Arabic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>I am an educator, not a therapist.</td><td>أنا مثقِّفة في مجال الرفاه المهني، ولستُ معالِجة نفسية.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>This session is education, not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support.</td><td>هذه الجلسة تثقيفية — ليست علاجاً ولا تشخيصاً ولا دعماً في الأزمات.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Everything is voluntary; you can say \"pass\" at any time, without giving any reason.</td><td>المشاركة طوعية بالكامل؛ يمكنكم قول «باس» في أي وقت، دون إبداء أي سبب.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>We talk about work — not about anyone's private life or health history.</td><td>نتحدث عن العمل — لا عن الحياة الخاصة أو التاريخ الصحي لأي شخص.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>If you are struggling, please reach a qualified professional — I will show you where.</td><td>إذا كنتم تمرّون بضائقة، يرجى التواصل مع اختصاصي مؤهَّل — وسأدلّكم على المكان المناسب.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-ground-rules-slide-poster-text\">3. Ground Rules — Slide / Poster Text</h2>\n<p>Print or project this exactly. One slide, both languages side by side.</p>\n<p><strong>English:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Our Ground Rules</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Everything is an invitation — participation is voluntary.</li><li>\"Pass\" is a complete sentence. No explanations needed, none requested.</li><li>We talk about work situations — not private lives or health histories.</li><li>What is shared in this room stays in this room.</li><li>No one interprets anyone else's feelings — including the facilitator.</li><li>This is education, not therapy. Qualified support is signposted on your referral card.</li></ol></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Arabic:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>قواعدنا في هذه الجلسة</strong> ١. كل نشاط هو دعوة — المشاركة طوعية. ٢. كلمة «باس» جملة كاملة. لا حاجة لأي تفسير، ولن يطلبه أحد. ٣. نتحدث عن مواقف العمل — لا عن الحياة الخاصة أو التاريخ الصحي. ٤. ما يُقال في هذه القاعة يبقى في هذه القاعة. ٥. لا أحد يفسِّر مشاعر غيره — بما في ذلك الميسِّرة. ٦. هذه جلسة تثقيفية، وليست علاجاً. جهات الدعم المؤهَّلة مذكورة على بطاقة الإحالة الخاصة بكم.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-the-seven-core-exercises\">4. The Seven Core Exercises</h2>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"41-exercise-1-stress-signal-map-d-detect\">4.1 Exercise 1 — Stress Signal Map (D — Detect)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Build stress literacy: participants learn to notice their own early overload signals at work — in body, behavior, and thinking — and to name them without stigma.</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letter</td><td>D — Detect</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>20 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Any; works from 6 to 60</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 4.1, pens; flipchart with three columns labeled Body / Behavior / Thinking</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (2 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"The World Health Organization recommends mental health literacy for workers — plain knowledge that improves understanding and reduces stigma. That's what this exercise is: literacy, not diagnosis. Overload has early signals, the way a car has a fuel light. We're going to learn to read our own dashboard. This is a map of signals — it is not a checklist of symptoms, it produces no score, and it says nothing about anyone's health.\"</em></li><li><strong>Demonstrate (3 min).</strong> On the flipchart, offer neutral, generic examples in each column. Say: <em>\"For example — Body: tense shoulders by mid-afternoon. Behavior: re-reading the same email three times, skipping lunch to 'save time'. Thinking: everything starts to feel equally urgent.\"</em> Use only work-flavored, everyday examples.</li><li><strong>Individual work (7 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"On your handout, privately, note two or three of your own early signals in each column. This paper is yours — no one will collect it, and no one will read it.\"</em></li><li><strong>Optional pair share (4 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"If you'd like, tell a partner ONE signal you're comfortable sharing — something work-related, like 'I stop taking my lunch break.' And remember the pass rule: staying silent is completely fine.\"</em></li><li><strong>Debrief (4 min).</strong> Ask the debrief questions below to the room; take only volunteered answers.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"What surprised you about your own list?\"</li><li>\"Which signal shows up <em>earliest</em> for you — the fuel light, not the empty tank?\"</li><li>\"What's one thing you could do the same day you notice your earliest signal?\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Never turn this into a symptom inventory: no counting signals, no thresholds, no \"if you ticked more than X.\" The map has no score by design.</li><li>Never comment on any individual's signals (\"that sounds like anxiety\" is forbidden). Reflect the work situation only.</li><li>If someone shares something clearly personal or distressing, thank them briefly, do not probe, and follow <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</li><li>Do not go around the room; volunteers only.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> No flipchart? Draw the three columns on any whiteboard or dictate them; participants use blank paper folded in thirds.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 4.1):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"my-stress-signal-map\">My Stress Signal Map</h3>\n<p><em>Private working sheet — yours to keep. No one collects or reads this.</em></p>\n<p>Everyone's system sends early signals when work load runs hot — like a fuel light on a dashboard. Learning to read your own signals early is a skill the WHO calls mental health literacy: it improves understanding and reduces stigma. <strong>These are signals of overload, not symptoms of illness. This sheet has no score and diagnoses nothing.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>My early signals at work</th><th>Examples (generic)</th><th>Mine</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Body</strong></td><td>Tight shoulders; headaches by 4 p.m.; running on coffee</td><td>____________________</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Behavior</strong></td><td>Skipping lunch; re-reading emails; snapping at small requests; working late \"just this once,\" every week</td><td>____________________</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thinking</strong></td><td>Everything feels urgent; hard to start simple tasks; replaying work conversations at night</td><td>____________________</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>My earliest signal — the fuel light, not the empty tank:</strong> ____________________</p>\n<p><strong>One small action I can take the same day I notice it:</strong> ____________________</p>\n<p><em>A useful frame:</em> signals usually point at <strong>conditions</strong> — workload, pace, hours, control — more than at you. When a signal repeats, the question is not \"what's wrong with me?\" but \"what in my week needs adjusting?\" The next exercises show you how.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"42-exercise-2-workload-x-ray-e-establish\">4.2 Exercise 2 — Workload X-Ray (E — Establish)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Make invisible workload visible: participants inventory one real week of tasks, then examine it through the WHO's workplace risk lens (workload, pace, hours, control).</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letter</td><td>E — Establish</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>25 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Any; individual work with optional pairs</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 4.2, pens</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (3 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"The WHO names specific workplace risks to mental health: excessive workloads and work pace, understaffing, long or inflexible hours, and lack of control over your work. Notice something — those are all</em> conditions*, not character flaws. But conditions are hard to fix while they're invisible. Administrative work especially runs on invisible tasks: the follow-ups, the reminders, the 'can you just.' Today we X-ray one week.\"*</li><li><strong>Task inventory (8 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Column one: list everything you actually did last week — including the invisible items. The five-minute favors. The chasing. The things nobody sees. Don't rank, just dump.\"</em></li><li><strong>Apply the risk lens (7 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Now mark each task with one or more letters. W if the sheer amount is the issue. P if the speed demanded is the issue. H if it lands outside reasonable hours. C if you had no say in when or how it arrived. Some tasks get no letter — that's fine, that's healthy work.\"</em></li><li><strong>Find the pattern (4 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Look at your letters. Which letter appears most? That's your week's dominant pressure — and each pressure has a different fix. Too much W is a prioritization and delegation conversation. Lots of H is a boundaries conversation — which is exactly our next exercise.\"</em></li><li><strong>Debrief (3 min).</strong> Volunteers only.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"How much of your list would your manager be surprised to see?\"</li><li>\"Which letter dominated — and did that surprise you?\"</li><li>\"What's ONE item you could make visible to someone this week — not complain about, just make visible?\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Keep the frame on conditions, not coping. This exercise never asks how people <em>feel</em> about tasks — only what the tasks <em>are</em>.</li><li>Do not let the debrief become a complaint session about named managers or colleagues; redirect to patterns (\"lots of H-tasks\") rather than people.</li><li>Do not collect the sheets.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> Blank paper with a hand-drawn two-column table; letters written in the margin.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 4.2):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"workload-x-ray\">Workload X-Ray</h3>\n<p><em>Private working sheet — make one real week visible.</em></p>\n<p>The WHO identifies specific workplace risks to mental health: excessive <strong>W</strong>orkload, unsustainable <strong>P</strong>ace, long or inflexible <strong>H</strong>ours, and lack of <strong>C</strong>ontrol. These are conditions of work — and conditions can be adjusted, but only once they are visible.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Inventory.</strong> List everything you did last week, especially the invisible tasks (follow-ups, reminders, \"quick favors,\" fixing others' formatting, being the office memory).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task (visible or invisible)</th><th>Risk letters (W / P / H / C)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Read your X-ray.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Most frequent letter this week: ______</li><li><strong>W dominates →</strong> the conversation to have is about priorities: \"Which of these should come first?\"</li><li><strong>P dominates →</strong> the conversation is about sequencing: \"What's the real deadline on each?\"</li><li><strong>H dominates →</strong> the conversation is about boundaries — see the Boundary Scripts handout.</li><li><strong>C dominates →</strong> the conversation is about planning: \"Can I get these requests earlier / batched?\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>One item I will make visible to my manager or team this week:</strong> ____________________</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"43-exercise-3-boundary-scripts-lab-e-establish\">4.3 Exercise 3 — Boundary Scripts Lab (E — Establish)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Participants write and rehearse respectful, culturally natural boundary sentences for real Lebanese workplace situations — in Arabic and English — so the words are ready before the moment arrives.</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letter</td><td>E — Establish</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Pairs; any room size</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 4.3, pens</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (4 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"A boundary is not a wall and it is not a fight — it's a clear sentence, said kindly, that protects your capacity to keep doing good work. In our workplace culture, warmth matters: we don't say a cold 'no.' The formula we'll practice is</em> <strong>acknowledge + limit + alternative</strong>: <em>I hear you, here's my limit, here's what I</em> can <em>do. The magic is preparing the sentence before you need it — because at 7 p.m., tired, on the spot, nobody improvises well.\"</em></li><li><strong>Model (4 min).</strong> Read example script 1 (below) aloud in both languages. Point out the three parts.</li><li><strong>Write (8 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Pick the scenario on your handout closest to your real life — or write your own work scenario. Draft your sentence in whichever language you'd actually use. Use the formula.\"</em></li><li><strong>Pair practice (10 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"In pairs: one of you makes the request, the other delivers the boundary sentence. Then swap. Requester — be realistic, not cruel. And the pass rule applies: you can write without practicing aloud.\"</em></li><li><strong>Debrief (4 min).</strong></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"How did the sentence feel out loud compared to in your head?\"</li><li>\"Which of the three parts — acknowledge, limit, alternative — is hardest for you?\"</li><li>\"Where will you keep your sentence so it's ready when you need it?\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Scenarios stay professional: workload, hours, scope. Never set up scenarios involving harassment, discrimination, or abusive behavior — those need HR and formal channels, not a phrasing exercise; say so if asked, and point to workplace policy.</li><li>Do not force anyone to role-play; writing alone is a complete version of this exercise.</li><li>Do not critique anyone's delivery style, accent, or tone.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> No handout? Write the three-part formula on the board and dictate the four scenarios.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 4.3):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"boundary-scripts-lab\">Boundary Scripts Lab</h3>\n<p><em>The formula: Acknowledge + Limit + Alternative — كلمة طيّبة، حدّ واضح، وبديل عملي (a kind word, a clear limit, a practical alternative).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Script 1 — Declining the 7 p.m. \"quick favor\"</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>EN:</strong> \"I appreciate that this is important — and I've finished for today. I'll make it the first thing I do tomorrow morning, and you'll have it by 10.\"</li><li><strong>AR:</strong> «بقدّر إنّو الموضوع مهم — بس أنا خلّصت دوامي اليوم. بكرا الصبح أوّل شي رح اشتغل عليه، وبيكون عندك قبل العشرة.»</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Script 2 — Protecting your lunch break</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>EN:</strong> \"I have a commitment from 1:00 to 1:30. I'm fully available right after — shall we say 1:30?\"</li><li><strong>AR:</strong> «عندي التزام من الوحدة للوحدة ونص. بعدها فاضية تماماً — منحكي الساعة وحدة ونص؟»</li></ul>\n<p><em>(Your lunch IS a commitment. You don't have to say to whom.)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Script 3 — Renegotiating a deadline</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>EN:</strong> \"I want to give this the quality it deserves. With my current list, I can deliver it Thursday — or, if Wednesday is essential, tell me which of my other tasks moves.\"</li><li><strong>AR:</strong> «بدّي قدّم هالشغل بالجودة يلّي بيستاهلها. مع المهام يلّي عندي حالياً، فيني سلّمه الخميس — وإذا الأربعا ضروري، خبّرني أيّا مهمة تانية منأجّلها.»</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Script 4 — Saying no to scope creep</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>EN:</strong> \"Happy to help with that. Since it's outside what we agreed, let's decide together what comes off my plate to make room for it.\"</li><li><strong>AR:</strong> «بكل سرور بساعد. بس بما إنّو هالشي خارج يلّي اتفقنا عليه، خلّينا نقرّر سوا شو منأجّل من مهامي حتى يصير في مجال.»</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Now write yours:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>My real scenario: ____________________</li><li>Acknowledge: ____________________</li><li>Limit: ____________________</li><li>Alternative: ____________________</li></ul>\n<p><em>Keep your sentence where you'll find it in the moment — a sticky note, your phone, your desk drawer. Boundaries are not walls; they are the maintenance schedule that keeps you reliable.</em></p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"44-exercise-4-white-space-audit-e-k\">4.4 Exercise 4 — White-Space Audit (E / K)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Participants audit a real week of their calendar to find recovery gaps (\"white space\"), then protect at least two of them deliberately.</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letters</td><td>E — Establish / K — Keep</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>20 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Any; individual work</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 4.4, pens; participants' own calendars (phone or paper)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (3 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Rest is not what's left over after work — it's a system you design. The WHO points to long or inflexible hours as a genuine workplace risk, and to leisure and physical activity as part of the answer. White space is any gap in your week where nothing is scheduled and nothing is owed. Most of us have some — and we let it get eaten, five minutes at a time.\"</em></li><li><strong>Map (7 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Open last week in your calendar. On the handout grid, shade the blocks that were truly free — no meetings, no tasks, no 'catching up.' Be honest: scrolling through work emails on the sofa is not white space.\"</em></li><li><strong>Diagnose (4 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Now answer the three questions on the sheet: how much white space survived, what usually eats it, and when it disappears — is it always evenings? Always the same day?\"</em></li><li><strong>Protect (4 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Choose two blocks in the coming week and book them with yourself, by name — 'walk,' 'lunch away from the desk,' 'nothing.' A named block survives; a blank one gets taken.\"</em></li><li><strong>Debrief (2 min).</strong> One volunteered insight or two.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"What ate most of your white space — others' requests, or your own habits?\"</li><li>\"What will you <em>call</em> your protected blocks? Named blocks survive.\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Never comment on how little white space anyone has; no comparisons between participants.</li><li>Keep it practical, not moral — no guilt framing about rest (\"you <em>should</em>…\").</li><li>If someone's schedule includes caregiving or a second job, acknowledge that white space can be small and still count; never suggest their life design is wrong.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> Hand-draw a 7-day × 3-band (morning / afternoon / evening) grid on blank paper.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 4.4):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"white-space-audit\">White-Space Audit</h3>\n<p><em>White space = time where nothing is scheduled and nothing is owed. It is where recovery happens.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Map last week.</strong> Shade only the blocks that were genuinely free (no work, no \"catching up,\" no half-working on the sofa).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Mon</th><th>Tue</th><th>Wed</th><th>Thu</th><th>Fri</th><th>Sat</th><th>Sun</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Morning</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Afternoon</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Evening</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Diagnose.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Blocks of true white space last week: ______</li><li>What usually eats my white space: ____________________</li><li>When it disappears most (day/time pattern): ____________________</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Protect two blocks this coming week.</strong> Give each a name — named blocks survive.</p>\n<ul><li>Block 1: Day ______ Time ______ Name: ____________________</li><li>Block 2: Day ______ Time ______ Name: ____________________</li></ul>\n<p><em>Good names: \"walk,\" \"lunch away from my desk,\" \"coffee with no phone,\" \"nothing — on purpose.\" Rest as a system, not a leftover — that is the K in DESK.</em></p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"45-exercise-5-help-seeking-compass-s-support\">4.5 Exercise 5 — Help-Seeking Compass (S — Support)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Participants map their own support options in three rings — self-management, workplace support, professional help — learn what each ring is <em>for</em>, and leave knowing where qualified help lives. Frames help-seeking explicitly as a skill and a strength.</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letter</td><td>S — Support</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>20 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Any; individual work, no sharing required</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 4.5, pens; referral signposting cards (Section 7.2) ready to distribute</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (4 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Help-seeking is a skill — you can be good at it, and today we practice the first step: knowing your map before you need it. Three rings. Ring one, self-management: the routines and skills in this workshop. Ring two, workplace support: a trusted colleague or a manager — good for workload adjustments, priorities, and not carrying things alone. Ring three, professional support: qualified practitioners — the right address when distress is persistent, heavy, or beyond what routines and conversations at work can carry. The WHO is clear that mental health is a basic human right and that real care belongs in qualified pathways. Moving to ring three is not failure. It's the same skill as calling an accountant for taxes: the strong move is knowing which problems deserve a professional.\"</em></li><li><strong>Individual mapping (8 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Fill in your own compass. Ring two: write initials, not names, of one or two people at work you could actually talk to about workload. Ring three: you don't need to know a specific professional today — write where you would</em> look. <em>In Lebanon, the Ministry of Public Health's National Mental Health Programme publishes awareness and support materials, and your referral card points there.\"</em></li><li><strong>Distribute the referral card (3 min).</strong> Hand out Section 7.2 cards. Say: <em>\"This card lives in your wallet or on your phone. You may never need it. Someone near you might — and you'll be the person who knew where to point.\"</em></li><li><strong>Debrief (5 min).</strong> Volunteers only; keep answers at the level of the <em>map</em>, not personal situations.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"Which ring was easiest to fill in? Which was emptiest?\"</li><li>\"What would make it easier to use ring two — to actually raise workload with someone?\"</li><li>\"Ring three: where would you look first, now that you've seen the card?\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Never ask anyone whether they <em>need</em> ring three, have used it, or plan to. The map is hypothetical and private.</li><li>Never recommend a specific named practitioner; signpost only to qualified pathways as on the referral card.</li><li>If a participant asks \"do you think I need therapy?\", the answer is verbatim: <em>\"That's exactly the kind of question a qualified professional can answer with you — and asking it is a strength. Your card shows where to start.\"</em> Then follow <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> Draw three concentric circles on blank paper.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 4.5):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"my-help-seeking-compass\">My Help-Seeking Compass</h3>\n<p><em>Private sheet. Help-seeking is a skill — and knowing your map before you need it is the first move. طلب المساعدة مهارة وقوة، مش ضعف — asking for help is a skill and a strength, not a weakness.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ring 1 — Self-management (what I do myself).</strong> For: everyday stress, workload structure, routines, rest, boundaries. My three most reliable tools (from today or my own life): 1) ______________ 2) ______________ 3) ______________</p>\n<p><strong>Ring 2 — Workplace support (who I can talk to at work).</strong> For: workload adjustments, priorities, making the invisible visible, not carrying things alone. <em>Not for:</em> diagnosis or treatment — colleagues and managers are allies, not clinicians. One or two people (initials only): ______________ What I would ask for (an adjustment, not a rescue): ____________________</p>\n<p><strong>Ring 3 — Professional support (qualified help).</strong> For: distress that is persistent, heavy, or bigger than routines and work conversations can carry. Mental health is a basic human right, and qualified care is where real treatment belongs. Where I would look first: the Ministry of Public Health's <strong>National Mental Health Programme</strong> publishes awareness and support material — see your referral card, which also lists the support helpline and emergency guidance.</p>\n<p><strong>The rule of the compass:</strong> you never need to be in crisis to use ring 3, and using it never means rings 1 and 2 failed. Strong professionals use other professionals.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"46-exercise-6-the-friday-15-weekly-review-k-keep\">4.6 Exercise 6 — The Friday 15 Weekly Review (K — Keep)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Install a 15-minute weekly self-review ritual: look back at load, flag next week's risks, plan white space, and name one ask-for-help item. This is the maintenance habit that keeps the whole DESK Method running.</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letter</td><td>K — Keep</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>15 minutes to teach and run once live; then weekly take-home</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Any; individual</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 4.6 (the template — print several copies per person), pens</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (3 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Everything you learned today decays without maintenance — that's not a flaw in you, it's how habits work. The fix is a standing appointment with yourself: fifteen minutes, once a week, ideally Friday before you close the laptop. Four questions, every week, same order. Weekly reviews are how you catch the fuel light in review rather than on the highway.\"</em></li><li><strong>Run it live (9 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Let's do this week's review right now, together, silently. Question one: what was this week's load actually like — heavier, lighter, or as planned? Two minutes.\"</em> Continue through all four questions, timing each: <em>\"Question two: looking at next week, where's the risk — which day, which task, which letter from your X-ray? … Question three: where's your white space next week — name two blocks. … Question four: what is ONE thing you'll ask someone for — help, clarity, a deadline, a priority call? One ask. Small is fine.\"</em></li><li><strong>Anchor it (3 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Now open your calendar and create the recurring appointment: Friday, fifteen minutes, titled 'Friday 15.' If Friday doesn't fit your week, pick your own day — the ritual matters more than the day.\"</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"Which of the four questions was hardest to answer?\"</li><li>\"What will realistically try to steal this slot — and what's your boundary sentence for it?\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The review looks at <em>load and plans</em>, never mood-tracking or symptom-tracking. Do not add rating scales of any kind.</li><li>Never ask participants to share their answers; the live run is silent.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> The four questions on an index card taped inside a notebook.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 4.6 — print multiple copies):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"the-friday-15-weekly-review-template\">The Friday 15 — Weekly Review Template</h3>\n<p><em>15 minutes. Same four questions, every week. Week of: ____________</em></p>\n<p><strong>1. LOOK BACK — this week's load (4 min).</strong> Heavier / lighter / as planned? What made it so? Which invisible tasks appeared? ____________________</p>\n<p><strong>2. FLAG — next week's risk (4 min).</strong> Which day looks heaviest? Which task carries a W / P / H / C risk? What would make it lighter <em>if I raised it now</em>? ____________________</p>\n<p><strong>3. PROTECT — white space (3 min).</strong> Two named blocks for next week: Block 1: ______________ Block 2: ______________</p>\n<p><strong>4. ASK — one ask-for-help item (4 min).</strong> One thing I will ask someone for next week (clarity, a priority call, a hand, a deadline shift) — and who: ____________________</p>\n<p><em>Done. Close the laptop. The review is the system; the system is what keeps you well-resourced — that's the K in DESK.</em></p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"47-exercise-7-energy-ledger-k-keep\">4.7 Exercise 7 — Energy Ledger (K — Keep)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Over one week, participants track what drains and what restores their energy — with deliberate attention to leisure and physical activity, which the WHO includes among recommended individual interventions — and then adjust one habit.</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letter</td><td>K — Keep</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>10 minutes to teach; one-week take-home; 10-minute debrief if a follow-up session exists</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Any; individual</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 4.7 (the printable ledger), pens</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (4 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Think of energy like a simple ledger — entries in, entries out. This is not a mood diary and not a health record: you're logging</em> activities <em>and their effect on your energy, the way you'd track spending. The WHO's recommended actions for individuals include stress-management skills and leisure and physical activity — so the ledger deliberately asks about movement and genuine leisure, because those are the deposits most of us skip first when work gets loud.\"</em></li><li><strong>Teach the ledger (4 min).</strong> Walk through the columns on the handout. Say: <em>\"Each evening, sixty seconds: two or three entries. A drain is anything that left you flatter — a 90-minute meeting with no break, an inbox marathon. A gain is anything that left you fuller — a real lunch, a walk, coffee with a friend, prayer or quiet time, sport, music. Mark gains that involved movement with an M and genuine leisure with an L. No ratings, no scores — just entries.\"</em></li><li><strong>Set the contract (2 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"One week. Then, on your next Friday 15, read your ledger and make exactly one adjustment: one drain to shrink or one gain to repeat on purpose. One. Small and done beats grand and abandoned.\"</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions (for a follow-up session, volunteers only):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"What was your most reliable gain — and was it an M or an L?\"</li><li>\"What drain surprised you by being on the list at all?\"</li><li>\"What one adjustment did you choose?\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The ledger logs activities and energy, never emotions, symptoms, sleep quality scores, or anything resembling a clinical diary. Do not add numeric scales.</li><li>In debrief, never analyze anyone's ledger or draw conclusions about their state; ask, don't interpret.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> Any notebook page with two columns, \"− drains / + gains,\" dated per day.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 4.7 — the printable ledger):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"energy-ledger-one-week\">Energy Ledger — One Week</h3>\n<p><em>Sixty seconds each evening. Log activities, not feelings. This is a habit tool, not a health record — no scores, no ratings.</em> <em>Mark gains: <strong>M</strong> = involved physical movement, <strong>L</strong> = genuine leisure (something done purely for enjoyment).</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>− Drains (what flattened my energy)</th><th>+ Gains (what restored it)</th><th>M/L</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mon</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Tue</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Wed</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Thu</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Fri</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Sat</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Sun</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>End-of-week reading (do this during your Friday 15):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>My most reliable gain this week: ____________________</li><li>A drain I hadn't noticed before: ____________________</li><li><strong>My ONE adjustment for next week</strong> (shrink one drain <em>or</em> deliberately repeat one gain): ____________________</li></ul>\n<p><em>Why M and L matter: leisure and physical activity are among the WHO-recommended ways individuals protect their wellbeing at work. They are deposits, not rewards — you don't have to earn them first.</em></p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-manager-session-add-on-exercises-w2-only\">5. Manager-Session Add-On Exercises (W2 Only)</h2>\n<p>These two exercises are for the manager session defined in <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>. They train managers in <em>support and structure</em> — never in assessment. The WHO recommends manager training precisely so that managers can respond supportively and adjust conditions; nothing in W2 teaches managers to evaluate anyone's mental state.</p>\n<h3 id=\"51-m1-the-supportive-conversation-s-support\">5.1 M1 — The Supportive Conversation (S — Support)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Managers rehearse a strictly bounded supportive conversation: listen, acknowledge, ask what support <em>at work</em> would help, offer workplace adjustments, signpost professional resources. They also learn what a manager must never do.</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letter</td><td>S — Support</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>35 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Trios (speaker / listener / observer), rotating</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 5.1 (the script skeleton), scenario slips describing <em>work situations only</em> (e.g., \"a team member says the workload has become impossible since the departure of a colleague\")</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (6 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"When a team member tells you they're struggling with work, you have one job, and it is not diagnosis. Your job is support and structure: listen, acknowledge, ask what would help at work, adjust what you can, and signpost professional resources for everything beyond work. You are a manager, not a clinician — and staying in your lane is not coldness, it's respect. The skeleton on your handout has five moves. In practice you will follow them in order, and there are two hard rules: never probe symptoms, never probe personal life.\"</em></li><li><strong>Model (5 min).</strong> Read the five moves aloud with the sample lines from Handout 5.1.</li><li><strong>Trio practice (18 min).</strong> Two rotations of ~8 minutes: \"team member\" reads a scenario slip and improvises <em>work details only</em>; \"manager\" runs the skeleton; observer checks each move off and notes any probing. Say before starting: <em>\"Team members: keep it about work — that's the scenario and the rule. Managers: if you catch yourself asking 'why' about anything personal, stop and return to move three.\"</em></li><li><strong>Debrief (6 min).</strong></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"Which move felt least natural — and which will matter most in real life?\"</li><li>\"Observers: where did the pull toward probing show up?\"</li><li>\"What adjustment did you offer — and was it something you can actually deliver?\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Scenario slips must contain only workplace situations. No scenarios involving disclosed conditions, crises, family circumstances, or grief.</li><li>If managers ask \"but what if someone discloses something serious?\", the answer is: acknowledge with care, do not probe, keep it private, signpost the referral card, and follow the organization's own policy plus <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>. A manager's role in a serious disclosure is a warm handover, not an intervention.</li><li>Ban the phrases \"What's wrong with you?\", \"Are you depressed?\", and any symptom question, explicitly, during the frame.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> Write the five moves on the board; scenarios dictated.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 5.1):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"the-supportive-conversation-script-skeleton-for-managers\">The Supportive Conversation — Script Skeleton for Managers</h3>\n<p><em>Your role: support and structure. Not diagnosis, not counseling, not investigation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Move 1 — LISTEN.</strong> Let them finish. Don't fix yet. Sample line: \"Thank you for telling me. Take your time — I'm listening.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Move 2 — ACKNOWLEDGE.</strong> Reflect the <em>work situation</em>, without interpreting feelings. Sample line: \"So since the handover, you're carrying two roles' worth of deadlines. That's a real load.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Move 3 — ASK WHAT WOULD HELP AT WORK.</strong> This is the pivot question — memorize it. Sample line: <strong>\"What support at work would make a difference right now?\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Move 4 — OFFER WORKPLACE ADJUSTMENTS.</strong> Offer only what you can deliver: reprioritization, a deadline shift, redistribution, clearer scope, a regular check-in on workload. Sample line: \"Here's what I can do this week: X moves to next sprint, and we'll review the load together every Monday.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Move 5 — SIGNPOST.</strong> Normalize professional resources without implying they need them. Sample line: \"And separately from work — the referral card from the wellbeing session lists where qualified support is available, for any of us, anytime.\"</p>\n<p><strong>The two hard rules:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Never probe symptoms.</strong> No \"are you anxious/depressed,\" no questions about sleep, mood, or health.</li><li><strong>Never probe personal life.</strong> If they volunteer something personal: acknowledge kindly, keep it private, return to what you can adjust at work, signpost.</li></ol>\n<p><em>A supportive manager changes working conditions and opens doors. Qualified professionals do the rest.</em></p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"52-m2-team-workload-weather-report-d-e\">5.2 M2 — Team Workload Weather Report (D / E)</h3>\n<p><strong>Facilitator card</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Give managers a recurring 5-minute team ritual that surfaces workload pressure early — using weather language about the <em>work</em>, never questions about feelings.</td></tr><tr><td>DESK letters</td><td>D — Detect / E — Establish</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>15 minutes to teach</td></tr><tr><td>Group size</td><td>Manager cohort; then used in their own team meetings</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Handout 5.2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step-by-step facilitation script:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Frame (4 min).</strong> Say: <em>\"Overload is easiest to fix early, and hardest to see from above. This ritual makes it visible in one round of a normal team meeting. The genius of weather language is that it describes the</em> workload*, not the person: 'my week is stormy' is information about conditions, not a disclosure about mental state. Nobody is asked how they feel — ever.\"*</li><li><strong>Teach the ritual (6 min).</strong> Walk through Handout 5.2: the question, the scale, the two rules, the manager's follow-up.</li><li><strong>Practice one round (5 min).</strong> Run a live round with the managers about <em>their own</em> current week, then model the follow-up: <em>\"Two storms in the room — let's look at what can move.\"</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Debrief questions:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>\"What will you do the first time someone reports 'storm' three weeks in a row?\" (Answer to land: adjust conditions and, if appropriate, have a Supportive Conversation — moves 1–5.)</li><li>\"How will you keep this from becoming a performance ritual where everyone says 'sunny'?\" (Answer to land: the manager reports honestly first, and storms visibly lead to help, never to blame.)</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safety notes / what NOT to do:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The question is always about <em>workload</em>, never \"how are you feeling?\" Do not let teams drift the ritual into a mood check-in.</li><li>Weather reports are never recorded in performance systems or used in evaluations — state this to teams explicitly.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Low-tech variant:</strong> Verbal round only; no artifacts needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Participant handout — full text (Handout 5.2):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"team-workload-weather-report-a-5-minute-team-ritual\">Team Workload Weather Report — a 5-minute team ritual</h3>\n<p><strong>The question (same every week):</strong> \"One word on your <em>workload</em> this week — sunny, cloudy, or stormy?\"</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sunny</strong> — load is manageable; normal week.</li><li><strong>Cloudy</strong> — load is heavy; watch it; small adjustments welcome.</li><li><strong>Stormy</strong> — load is over capacity; something needs to move <em>this week</em>.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>The two rules:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>It's about the workload, never the person. Nobody asks \"why,\" and nobody asks about feelings.</li><li>Storms trigger help, never blame. A storm report is a flare, and flares get answered.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>The manager's follow-up:</strong> every storm gets a same-week workload conversation using the Supportive Conversation skeleton — starting with: \"What support at work would make a difference right now?\"</p>\n<p><strong>The manager's honesty rule:</strong> the manager reports first, truthfully. Teams copy what leaders model.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-closing-script-verbatim-3-minutes\">6. Closing Script (Verbatim, ~3 Minutes)</h2>\n<p>Delivered at the end of every session, with commitment cards (Section 7.4) and feedback forms (Section 7.3) ready to hand out.</p>\n<blockquote><p>\"Before we finish, let's make today portable.</p>\n<p>On the card in front of you there are three lines: <strong>one habit, one boundary, one support.</strong> One habit from today you'll actually start — maybe the Friday 15, maybe the Energy Ledger. One boundary sentence you'll keep ready — you wrote it in the Boundary Lab. And one support: a person, a ring on your compass, or simply keeping the referral card where you can find it. Take ninety seconds and write your three. This card is yours — nobody collects it. Put it somewhere your future self will find it: your wallet, your desk drawer, a photo on your phone.</p>\n<p>[Pause ~90 seconds.]</p>\n<p>One final word — the most important one. Everything we did today is education: skills for managing stress, workload, and boundaries. It is not therapy, and it never replaces it. If you — or someone you care about — are struggling, reaching a qualified professional is the strong move, and your referral card shows where to start. In an emergency, contact local emergency services immediately. Asking for help is a skill and a strength.</p>\n<p>Thank you for your openness, your questions, and your ninety seconds of honesty on that card. Before you leave, please take two minutes for the feedback form — it shapes what these sessions become. Take care of your white space this week. Thank you.\"</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"arabic-rendering-of-the-key-closing-lines\">Arabic rendering of the key closing lines</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>Arabic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One habit, one boundary, one support.</td><td>عادة واحدة، حدّ واحد، ودعم واحد.</td></tr><tr><td>Everything today is education — it is not therapy, and it never replaces it.</td><td>كل ما قمنا به اليوم تثقيفي — ليس علاجاً، ولا يحلّ محلّه أبداً.</td></tr><tr><td>Asking for help is a skill and a strength.</td><td>طلب المساعدة مهارة وقوة.</td></tr><tr><td>In an emergency, contact local emergency services immediately.</td><td>في الحالات الطارئة، اتصلوا بخدمات الطوارئ المحلية فوراً.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-printable-handout-masters\">7. Printable Handout Masters</h2>\n<h3 id=\"71-take-home-one-pager-the-desk-method-on-one-page\">7.1 Take-home one-pager — \"The DESK Method on One Page\"</h3>\n<blockquote><h1 id=\"the-desk-method-proposed-on-one-page\">The DESK Method (proposed) — on one page</h1>\n<p><em>Practical skills for managing stress and workload at work. Educational, not clinical.</em></p>\n<p>Almost 60% of the world's population is in work — and the WHO estimates 12 billion working days are lost every year to depression and anxiety. The workplace conditions that put wellbeing at risk are known: excessive workload and pace, long or inflexible hours, lack of control, under-used skills, and culture problems. The skills that protect you are learnable. That is what DESK is.</p>\n<p><strong>D — Detect.</strong> Know your early overload signals — body, behavior, thinking. They are a fuel light, not a diagnosis. Read your dashboard early. <em>(Tool: Stress Signal Map.)</em> <strong>E — Establish.</strong> Make workload visible, then set structure around it: boundary sentences prepared in advance (acknowledge + limit + alternative), protected white space, communication hygiene. <em>(Tools: Workload X-Ray, Boundary Scripts, White-Space Audit.)</em> <strong>S — Support.</strong> Help-seeking is a skill. Three rings: self-management → workplace support (adjustments, not rescue) → professional help (the right address for persistent or heavy distress — using it is strength, and mental health is a basic human right). <em>(Tool: Help-Seeking Compass + referral card.)</em> <strong>K — Keep.</strong> Habits decay without maintenance. Fifteen minutes weekly: review load, flag risks, protect white space, name one ask. Log your energy drains and gains — and treat leisure and movement as deposits, not rewards. <em>(Tools: Friday 15, Energy Ledger.)</em></p>\n<p><strong>The one-sentence version:</strong> notice early, structure deliberately, ask skillfully, maintain weekly. بالعربية: لاحِظ باكراً، نظِّم بوعي، اطلب المساعدة بمهارة، وحافِظ على عاداتك أسبوعياً.</p></blockquote>\n<p><em>(Print with the Section 8 footer block.)</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"72-referral-signposting-card-wallet-size-two-sided-en-ar\">7.2 Referral signposting card (wallet-size, two-sided EN / AR)</h3>\n<p><strong>Side A — English:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Where to find qualified support</strong> This card is educational signposting — carrying it is wisdom, not weakness.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Professional support in Lebanon:</strong> the Ministry of Public Health runs the <strong>National Mental Health Programme (NMHP)</strong>, which publishes awareness, anti-stigma, and psychological first aid materials: moph.gov.lb → National Mental Health Program.</li><li><strong>Emotional-support helpline:</strong> [TO VERIFY before use: current national emotional-support helpline number — check the Ministry of Public Health / National Mental Health Programme site]</li><li><strong>In an emergency:</strong> contact local emergency services immediately.</li><li><strong>At work:</strong> you can always ask your manager for workload support — that conversation is about adjustments, and you never owe anyone personal details.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Side B — Arabic:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>أين تجدون الدعم المؤهَّل</strong> هذه البطاقة للإرشاد والتثقيف — حملُها حكمة، لا ضعف.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>الدعم المهني في لبنان:</strong> وزارة الصحة العامة لديها <strong>البرنامج الوطني للصحة النفسية</strong>، وينشر مواد توعية ومواد لمكافحة الوصمة ومواد للإسعاف النفسي الأوّلي: moph.gov.lb ← البرنامج الوطني للصحة النفسية.</li><li><strong>خط الدعم النفسي:</strong> [للتحقّق قبل الاستخدام: رقم الخط الوطني الحالي للدعم النفسي — راجعوا موقع وزارة الصحة العامة / البرنامج الوطني للصحة النفسية]</li><li><strong>في الحالات الطارئة:</strong> اتصلوا بخدمات الطوارئ المحلية فوراً.</li><li><strong>في العمل:</strong> يمكنكم دائماً طلب دعم متعلّق بعبء العمل من مديركم — هذا الحديث عن التعديلات، ولا يُلزمكم بمشاركة أي تفاصيل شخصية.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<p><em>(Print with the Section 8 footer block, small type.)</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"73-feedback-form-non-clinical-asks-about-the-session-never-a\">7.3 Feedback form (non-clinical — asks about the session, never about the participant's state)</h3>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"session-feedback-2-minutes-anonymous\">Session Feedback — 2 minutes, anonymous</h3>\n<p><em>Your answers shape future sessions. No names.</em></p>\n<ol><li>Which exercise was most useful to you today? (circle) Stress Signal Map / Workload X-Ray / Boundary Scripts / White-Space Audit / Help-Seeking Compass / Friday 15 / Energy Ledger</li><li>Which ONE thing from today will you actually use in the next two weeks? ____________________</li><li>Was anything unclear, rushed, or not useful? What, and why? ____________________</li><li>How was the balance of Arabic and English for you? (circle) More Arabic, please / Just right / More English, please</li><li>Did the session respect your comfort and privacy throughout? (circle) Yes / Mostly / No — and if you wish, tell us more: ____________________</li><li>What work topic should a future session cover? (e.g., email overload, meetings, deadlines, remote work) ____________________</li><li>Would you recommend this session to a colleague? (circle) Yes / Maybe / No</li><li>Anything else you'd like the facilitator to know: ____________________</li></ol></blockquote>\n<p><em>(Print with the Section 8 footer block.)</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"74-commitment-card-business-card-or-a6-size\">7.4 Commitment card (business-card or A6 size)</h3>\n<blockquote><h3 id=\"my-card-عادة،-حدّ،-دعم\">My card — عادة، حدّ، دعم</h3>\n<p><strong>One habit</strong> I'm starting: ____________________ <strong>One boundary</strong> sentence I'll keep ready: ____________________ <strong>One support</strong> I'm keeping in reach: ____________________ Date: __________ <em>Review me at your next Friday 15.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p><em>(Print with the Section 8 footer block, small type.)</em></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-footer-disclaimer-block-required-on-every-printed-handout\">8. Footer Disclaimer Block — Required on Every Printed Handout</h2>\n<p>Place this block, verbatim, at the foot of every printed page in this toolkit (small type is fine; omission is not). For wallet cards, the first line alone plus the emergency sentence is the minimum.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</p>\n<p>محتوى تثقيفي حول الرفاه في مكان العمل — ليس علاجاً ولا تشخيصاً ولا دعماً في الأزمات. إذا كنتم تمرّون بضائقة، يرجى التواصل مع اختصاصي مؤهَّل؛ وفي الحالات الطارئة، اتصلوا بخدمات الطوارئ المحلية.</p>\n<p>Facilitated by Dorina Salman — Workplace Wellbeing Educator | Mental Health Literacy Advocate. Independent educational work; not affiliated with or endorsed by WHO, the Ministry of Public Health, or any organization referenced for signposting.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-exercises-specific\">What we still need from Dorina (exercises-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Arabic register check.</strong> The Arabic boundary scripts (4.3) are written in Lebanese-inflected colloquial; the safety lines and poster are in Modern Standard Arabic. Dorina should read all Arabic aloud and adjust wording to her natural voice before first delivery — her ear is the final authority.</li><li><strong>Helpline verification.</strong> Before any card is printed, replace every [TO VERIFY] placeholder with the current national emotional-support helpline number, confirmed on the MoPH/NMHP site on the day of printing. Re-verify each print run.</li><li><strong>Her own examples.</strong> Each exercise lands harder with one real, non-confidential anecdote from her 12+ admin years (e.g., an invisible-workload story for the X-Ray). She should prepare one per exercise — reviewed against the confidentiality rules in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> and with no identifiable employers or colleagues.</li><li><strong>Scenario slips for M1.</strong> Confirm 6–8 workload-only scenario slips drawn from realistic Lebanese office life; Dorina to draft, we refine.</li><li><strong>Print preferences.</strong> Confirm paper sizes (A4 handouts, A6 commitment card, wallet-size referral card assumed), whether handouts are single bilingual sheets or separate EN/AR versions, and any logo/visual identity once her brand assets exist.</li><li><strong>Timing rehearsal.</strong> Run the opening and closing scripts aloud twice and report actual timings so <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code> agendas can be trimmed to fit.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"sources-4\">Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a> (workforce share, working days lost to depression and anxiety, workplace risk factors, recommended actions including literacy, stress-management skills, leisure and physical activity, and manager training).</li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a> (mental health as a basic human right; the continuum view; care belongs in qualified pathways).</li><li>Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a> (NMHP existence and published awareness, anti-stigma, and psychological first aid materials — used for signposting only; no affiliation claimed).</li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a> (October 10; past themes include workplace mental health).</li></ol>","toc":[{"id":"1-how-to-use-this-toolkit","text":"1. How to Use This Toolkit","lvl":2},{"id":"11-exercise-desk-mapping","text":"1.1 Exercise ↔ DESK mapping","lvl":3},{"id":"12-safety-rules-that-apply-to-every-exercise-non-negotiable","text":"1.2 Safety rules that apply to every exercise (non-negotiable)","lvl":3},{"id":"2-opening-script-verbatim-2-minutes","text":"2. Opening Script (Verbatim, ~2 Minutes)","lvl":2},{"id":"arabic-rendering-of-the-key-safety-lines-deliver-in-arabic-w","text":"Arabic rendering of the key safety lines (deliver in Arabic when the room prefers it)","lvl":3},{"id":"3-ground-rules-slide-poster-text","text":"3. Ground Rules — Slide / Poster Text","lvl":2},{"id":"4-the-seven-core-exercises","text":"4. The Seven Core Exercises","lvl":2},{"id":"41-exercise-1-stress-signal-map-d-detect","text":"4.1 Exercise 1 — Stress Signal Map (D — Detect)","lvl":3},{"id":"42-exercise-2-workload-x-ray-e-establish","text":"4.2 Exercise 2 — Workload X-Ray (E — Establish)","lvl":3},{"id":"43-exercise-3-boundary-scripts-lab-e-establish","text":"4.3 Exercise 3 — Boundary Scripts Lab (E — Establish)","lvl":3},{"id":"44-exercise-4-white-space-audit-e-k","text":"4.4 Exercise 4 — White-Space Audit (E / K)","lvl":3},{"id":"45-exercise-5-help-seeking-compass-s-support","text":"4.5 Exercise 5 — Help-Seeking Compass (S — Support)","lvl":3},{"id":"46-exercise-6-the-friday-15-weekly-review-k-keep","text":"4.6 Exercise 6 — The Friday 15 Weekly Review (K — Keep)","lvl":3},{"id":"47-exercise-7-energy-ledger-k-keep","text":"4.7 Exercise 7 — Energy Ledger (K — Keep)","lvl":3},{"id":"5-manager-session-add-on-exercises-w2-only","text":"5. Manager-Session Add-On Exercises (W2 Only)","lvl":2},{"id":"51-m1-the-supportive-conversation-s-support","text":"5.1 M1 — The Supportive Conversation (S — Support)","lvl":3},{"id":"52-m2-team-workload-weather-report-d-e","text":"5.2 M2 — Team Workload Weather Report (D / E)","lvl":3},{"id":"6-closing-script-verbatim-3-minutes","text":"6. Closing Script (Verbatim, ~3 Minutes)","lvl":2},{"id":"arabic-rendering-of-the-key-closing-lines","text":"Arabic rendering of the key closing lines","lvl":3},{"id":"7-printable-handout-masters","text":"7. Printable Handout Masters","lvl":2},{"id":"71-take-home-one-pager-the-desk-method-on-one-page","text":"7.1 Take-home one-pager — \"The DESK Method on One Page\"","lvl":3},{"id":"72-referral-signposting-card-wallet-size-two-sided-en-ar","text":"7.2 Referral signposting card (wallet-size, two-sided EN / AR)","lvl":3},{"id":"73-feedback-form-non-clinical-asks-about-the-session-never-a","text":"7.3 Feedback form (non-clinical — asks about the session, never about the participant's state)","lvl":3},{"id":"74-commitment-card-business-card-or-a6-size","text":"7.4 Commitment card (business-card or A6 size)","lvl":3},{"id":"8-footer-disclaimer-block-required-on-every-printed-handout","text":"8. Footer Disclaimer Block — Required on Every Printed Handout","lvl":2},{"id":"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-exercises-specific","text":"What we still need from Dorina (exercises-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"sources-4","text":"Sources","lvl":2}],"words":8823,"mins":38},{"slug":"marketing-funnel-and-campaigns","file":"MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md","title":"Marketing Funnel & Campaigns","blurb":"Funnel architecture, offer ladder, landing copy, DM scripts, launch plan, partnerships.","track":"Execution","n":10,"html":"<h1 id=\"marketing-funnel-campaigns-dorina-salman\">Marketing Funnel &amp; Campaigns — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> This document is the complete go-to-market system that converts Dorina's LinkedIn authority into booked workshops. It maps the Authority Ladder to a working funnel, gives fully written copy for every conversion asset — landing page, DMs, emails, proposals — and lays out the launch plan (September–December 2026), the seasonal campaign modules, the partnership strategy, the post-event nurture engine, and the metrics that keep the pipeline honest. Everything here inherits the fixed positioning: Dorina is a <strong>Workplace Wellbeing Educator | Mental Health Literacy Advocate</strong> — strictly non-clinical. We sell education, skills, and structure. We never sell treatment, and we never promise health outcomes. Every asset in this document passes through the approval workflow in <code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code> before it touches the public.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-funnel-architecture-authority-ladder-funnel-stages\">1. Funnel Architecture — Authority Ladder → Funnel Stages</h2>\n<p>The funnel is the Authority Ladder made operational. The core motion is simple and repeats forever:</p>\n<p><strong>Educational content → profile visit → free resource or W0 pilot → W1/W2 paid booking → W5 retainer → referral.</strong></p>\n<p>The single most important rule, inherited from the package strategy: <strong>never sell above the rung the audience is on.</strong> Months 1–2 (September–October 2026) sell nothing — they teach. The first paid conversations happen only after pilots have run.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Ladder rung</th><th>Funnel stage</th><th>What the person does</th><th>Dorina's move</th><th>Primary asset</th><th>Success signal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Seen</strong></td><td>Awareness</td><td>Sees a DESK Method (proposed) post in feed</td><td>Publish 3×/week per <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>; comment on HR/manager posts</td><td>LinkedIn posts</td><td>Impressions, profile visits</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Followed</strong></td><td>Interest</td><td>Follows / connects; reads more</td><td>Consistent teaching; optimized profile (see <code>LINKEDIN_PROFILE_OPTIMIZATION.md</code> if in package)</td><td>Profile + featured section</td><td>Follower growth, saves</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trusted</strong></td><td>Trust</td><td>Downloads DESK one-pager; comments repeatedly</td><td>Deliver the free resource fast; reply to every comment personally</td><td>DESK one-pager (free)</td><td>Downloads, DM replies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Invited</strong></td><td>Conversation</td><td>Accepts a call or replies to a DM</td><td>20-minute discovery conversation — listen, don't pitch</td><td>DM scripts (§5), discovery call notes</td><td>Booked conversations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Invited → Paid</strong></td><td>Pilot</td><td>Hosts a free W0 lunch &amp; learn</td><td>Run W0 flawlessly per <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>; capture feedback</td><td>W0 + feedback form</td><td>W0 delivered, decision-maker in room</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Paid</strong></td><td>Paid booking</td><td>Books W1/W2/W3/W4</td><td>Proposal email (§5.5), pre-call, delivery, summary report</td><td>Proposal template, workshop kit</td><td>Signed booking</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Paid → Referenced</strong></td><td>Retained</td><td>Signs W5 quarterly refresher</td><td>Nurture engine (§9); rebooking triggers on calendar moments</td><td>Nurture emails, newsletter</td><td>Retainer signed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Referenced</strong></td><td>Advocacy</td><td>Refers, hosts again, provides a quote</td><td>Referral ask (§5.7); case summary (with written permission only)</td><td>Referral script, permissioned case notes</td><td>Warm intros received</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Leak-plugging principle:</strong> at every stage there is exactly one next step, always smaller than a sale. A post never asks for a booking; it asks for a comment or a download. A download never asks for money; it opens a conversation. A conversation asks only for a free W0. The W0 earns the right to propose W1. This is slow by design — from ~4 connections, trust is the entire asset.</p>\n<p><strong>Content-to-funnel bridge — the CTA ladder.</strong> Every public asset carries the CTA of its rung, never a higher one:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Asset type</th><th>Allowed CTA</th><th>Never</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ordinary LinkedIn post (teaching)</td><td>\"What's your experience?\" / \"Save this for Monday\"</td><td>Any booking or price mention</td></tr><tr><td>Monthly anchor post</td><td>\"Comment or DM for the DESK one-pager\"</td><td>Direct workshop pitch</td></tr><tr><td>Open-session announcement (WMHD, campaigns)</td><td>\"Register — it's free and educational\"</td><td>Upsell language inside the invite</td></tr><tr><td>One-pager PDF (last line)</td><td>\"For workshops: message Dorina on LinkedIn\"</td><td>Prices on the free asset</td></tr><tr><td>DM after engagement</td><td>Offer the one-pager or a W0 conversation</td><td>Proposal or price in a first message</td></tr><tr><td>Proposal email</td><td>Book a date</td><td>New offers not previously discussed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two funnel-instrumentation notes. First, LinkedIn is rented land: the goal of every quarter is to move the most engaged people onto owned channels — the email list behind the one-pager download and the quarterly newsletter (§9). Second, every download and every conversation gets a source note in the tracker (§10), so that by December Dorina knows which content themes actually produce conversations, not just impressions.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-segment-playbooks\">2. Segment Playbooks</h2>\n<p>Five segments, five playbooks. The WHO business-case line is the only place statistics appear in outbound messages — one line, sourced, never embellished.</p>\n<h3 id=\"21-hr-professionals-people-managers-lebanon-the-buyers\">2.1 HR professionals &amp; people managers (Lebanon) — <strong>the buyers</strong></h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The pain, in their words:</strong> \"Everyone is stretched. People are quietly exhausted, good staff leave without warning, and when I raise wellbeing the answer is 'we have no budget.' I need something practical I can actually run — not a poster campaign.\"</li><li><strong>WHO-grounded business case line:</strong> \"The WHO estimates around 12 billion working days are lost every year to depression and anxiety — roughly US$1 trillion in lost productivity — and its recommended workplace actions are exactly what a workshop can deliver: manager training and mental health literacy for staff.\"</li><li><strong>Entry offer:</strong> free W0 lunch &amp; learn (45–60 min) for one team, plus the DESK one-pager they can circulate internally.</li><li><strong>Channel:</strong> LinkedIn DM after engagement (§5.1), then email with the one-pager PDF.</li><li><strong>Proof they need:</strong> a structured agenda they can show their leadership, the non-clinical boundary stated plainly (this de-risks it for them), and evidence Dorina understands office reality — her 12+ years in administration is the proof. After the first pilots: a one-line summary of what a previous session covered (no client names without permission).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"22-sme-owners-lebanon-the-business-case-segment\">2.2 SME owners (Lebanon) — <strong>the business-case segment</strong></h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The pain, in their words:</strong> \"I can't afford turnover and I can't afford sick days. But I also can't afford a big consultancy programme. If I spend a day of my team's time, I need it to be useful the same week.\"</li><li><strong>WHO business case line:</strong> \"Almost 60% of the world's population is in work, and the WHO names excessive workload, long hours, and lack of control as key workplace risks — the exact things a half-day of practical structure addresses.\"</li><li><strong>Entry offer:</strong> W0 for the owner plus their team leads, framed as \"one lunch hour, zero cost, you judge the value yourself.\"</li><li><strong>Channel:</strong> warm introduction where possible (owners buy on trust); otherwise LinkedIn DM referencing a specific post they engaged with; coworking-space partner events (§8).</li><li><strong>Proof they need:</strong> price certainty (a clear range up front), minimal time cost, hybrid/low-tech delivery options (electricity and connectivity constraints are real — Dorina should always offer a projector-free, printout-based version), and a same-day takeaway (the DESK worksheet).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"23-administrative-professionals-eas-office-managers-the-trib\">2.3 Administrative professionals / EAs / office managers — <strong>the tribe</strong></h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The pain, in their words:</strong> \"I hold everyone else's calendar, everyone else's stress, and everyone else's deadlines. Nobody asks how I'm managing mine. Saying no feels impossible in my role.\"</li><li><strong>WHO business case line (peer framing, softer):</strong> \"The WHO lists workload, pace, and lack of control over one's work among the main workplace risks to mental health — a description of the average admin desk.\"</li><li><strong>Entry offer:</strong> free content first (this segment is community, not revenue): the DESK one-pager, plus early invitations to the free WMHD open online session. Paid path is W3 — pitched to <em>their employers</em>, often via the tribe member championing it internally.</li><li><strong>Channel:</strong> LinkedIn content and comments (Dorina is one of them — peer voice, not vendor voice), plus DMs that never sell.</li><li><strong>Proof they need:</strong> authenticity. Dorina's own story — 12+ years at the admin desk, including a law-firm environment — is the entire proof. This segment converts to advocates who bring her into their companies.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"24-nonprofithumanitarian-organization-development-sector-sta\">2.4 nonprofit/humanitarian organization / development-sector staff — <strong>the mission-aligned segment</strong></h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The pain, in their words:</strong> \"Our teams carry heavy work in a heavy context. We talk about staff care in every proposal, but the actual budget line is small and the time is smaller.\"</li><li><strong>WHO business case line:</strong> \"The WHO frames mental health as a basic human right and recommends workplace mental health literacy and stress-management skills as core interventions — a natural fit for organisations already committed to duty of care.\"</li><li><strong>Entry offer:</strong> W0 or a discounted W2 for managers, framed as staff-care programming; W4 online for field-dispersed teams.</li><li><strong>Channel:</strong> email (this sector lives in email), referencing shared values and the national conversation the MoPH's National Mental Health Programme has opened around workplace mental health — always as ecosystem context, never as claimed affiliation.</li><li><strong>Proof they need:</strong> boundary clarity above all (nonprofit/humanitarian organizations are rightly careful about anyone near \"mental health\" without clinical credentials — the non-clinical educator framing, stated first, is what opens the door), Arabic/English bilingual delivery, and referral-pathway literacy (the \"S — Support\" pillar of DESK: help-seeking as a skill, signposting to qualified professionals).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"25-diaspora-gcc-based-professionals-remote-teams-the-online-\">2.5 Diaspora &amp; GCC-based professionals / remote teams — <strong>the online segment</strong></h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The pain, in their words:</strong> \"I work across time zones, my team is scattered, and 'wellbeing' at my company is a webinar nobody attends. I want something short, live, and actually built for remote work.\"</li><li><strong>WHO business case line:</strong> \"The WHO names long or inflexible hours and lack of control among key workplace risks — remote work amplifies both, which is why the session focuses on boundaries and routine design.\"</li><li><strong>Entry offer:</strong> the free WMHD open online session (§6) as the taster; W4 (90-minute online) as the paid step.</li><li><strong>Channel:</strong> LinkedIn only (content travels borderlessly); posts tagged to remote-work themes; diaspora networks and Lebanese professional groups online.</li><li><strong>Proof they need:</strong> a smooth online experience (test-run tech, recordings where agreed, materials delivered digitally), scheduling flexibility across time zones, and USD invoicing simplicity.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-offer-ladder-packaging\">3. Offer Ladder &amp; Packaging</h2>\n<p>All prices below are <strong>suggested ranges to validate for the Lebanese market</strong> — USD-denominated per dollarized pricing norms, deliberately wide, to be tested in the first ten sales conversations and adjusted. Workshop content and delivery specs live in <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>; this section covers packaging and money only.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Offer</th><th>Suggested price (to validate)</th><th>Included</th><th>Upgrade path</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Free — content</strong></td><td>LinkedIn posts, DESK Method (proposed) one-pager download</td><td>$0</td><td>The one-pager: DESK on one page, 3 self-check prompts, standing disclaimer, contact line</td><td>→ conversation → W0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Free — W0 pilot</strong></td><td>45–60 min lunch &amp; learn taster</td><td>$0 (limited; criteria below)</td><td>15-min pre-call, session, DESK one-pager for all attendees, 48h feedback summary to the host</td><td>→ W1 or W2 within 30 days</td></tr><tr><td><strong>W1 — flagship</strong></td><td>Half-day \"Mental Health Management at Work\" (DESK core)</td><td>$600–$1,200 per session</td><td>30-min pre-call + short needs form, printed workbooks, half-day delivery (hybrid/low-tech option standard), attendee one-pagers, 1-page summary report to HR within 1 week, 1-month check-in</td><td>→ W2 for managers, → W5</td></tr><tr><td><strong>W2 — managers</strong></td><td>2-hour session for people managers</td><td>$350–$700 per session</td><td>Pre-call, manager-specific materials (spotting workload risk, boundary-respecting management, signposting — never diagnosing), summary report</td><td>→ W1 for whole staff, → W5</td></tr><tr><td><strong>W3 — admin tribe</strong></td><td>Session for admin professionals / EAs / office managers</td><td>$300–$600 per session</td><td>Pre-call, admin-specific DESK application (calendar boundaries, workload visibility, saying no upward), materials, summary report</td><td>→ W1, → W5</td></tr><tr><td><strong>W4 — online</strong></td><td>90-min live online session for remote/diaspora teams</td><td>$250–$500 per session</td><td>Tech test-run, digital workbook, live delivery, digital one-pager pack, summary email</td><td>→ repeat W4 quarterly, → W5</td></tr><tr><td><strong>W5 — retainer</strong></td><td>Quarterly refresher programme</td><td>$900–$2,000 per year (4 sessions + touchpoints)</td><td>Quarterly 60–90 min refreshers aligned to calendar moments (WMHD, January reset, Ramadan-aware, summer), quarterly literacy newsletter for staff, priority scheduling</td><td>→ renewal, → referrals, → case reference (with permission)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>W0 qualification criteria — who gets the free pilot (capacity: ~10 in launch quarter, then 2/month):</strong></p>\n<ol><li>A decision-maker (HR lead, owner, or department head) commits to attend in person.</li><li>Minimum 8 attendees, maximum 25.</li><li>The host agrees to a 15-minute pre-call and a 10-minute debrief within one week.</li><li>The organisation plausibly fits a paid tier (team size and sector), OR strategic value is explicit (e.g., a partner community, a flagship-name pilot).</li><li>One W0 per organisation. A second free session is never offered — the debrief conversation proposes W1/W2 instead.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Packaging notes:</strong> every paid tier includes the pre-call, materials, and a written summary — this is what separates a professional programme from \"a talk.\" Bundle discount to test: W2 + W1 booked together at ~15% off combined. Nonprofit consideration: a stated nonprofit/humanitarian organization rate (e.g., lower end of range) is cleaner than ad-hoc discounting.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-landing-page-one-pager-copy-full-copy-doubles-as-pdf-for-e\">4. Landing Page / One-Pager Copy (Full Copy — Doubles as PDF for Email)</h2>\n<p><em>Use as a simple one-page site (e.g., a lightweight page builder) and as a designed PDF attached to outreach emails. Copy below is final draft for Dorina's approval.</em></p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"practical-mental-health-skills-for-real-workplaces\">Practical mental health skills for real workplaces.</h3>\n<p><strong>Workshops that teach your team to manage stress, workload, and boundaries — in plain language, in Arabic or English, with tools they'll use the same week.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>The problem, honestly stated.</strong> The World Health Organization estimates that around 12 billion working days are lost every year to depression and anxiety — about US$1 trillion in lost productivity. And the risks it names are ordinary office realities: excessive workloads and pace, understaffing, long or inflexible hours, lack of control over one's work, under-used skills, and workplace culture that makes it hard to speak up. None of this is exotic. All of it is manageable — if people are taught how.</p>\n<p><strong>The approach: the DESK Method (proposed).</strong> A four-part framework for practical mental health management at work — built at an admin desk, not in a lecture hall:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>D — Detect.</strong> Stress literacy: recognising your early signals before they become your normal.</li><li><strong>E — Establish.</strong> Boundaries, routines, and workload visibility — structure that protects energy.</li><li><strong>S — Support.</strong> Help-seeking as a professional skill, and knowing the referral pathways when someone needs more than a workshop.</li><li><strong>K — Keep.</strong> Sustaining the habits: weekly reviews, real rest, leisure and physical activity.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What a workshop includes.</strong> Every engagement starts with a pre-call so the session fits your team, not a template. You get: a live, interactive session (in person, online, or hybrid — including a low-tech, printout-based option for venues where power or connectivity is unreliable), a DESK one-pager for every attendee, and a one-page summary report for HR within a week. Formats run from a free 45-minute lunch &amp; learn taster to a half-day flagship, a 2-hour manager session, and quarterly refresher programmes.</p>\n<p><strong>Who's teaching.</strong> Dorina Salman spent 12+ years as an executive assistant and administrator — including in a law-firm environment — holding calendars, deadlines, and other people's pressure. She teaches workplace wellbeing from lived office experience, in Arabic and English. <strong>She is not a therapist, and she'll be the first to tell you so.</strong> These are educational workshops in stress literacy, boundaries, and workload structure — and part of what they teach is when and how to reach a qualified professional.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>FAQ</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Is this therapy?</strong> No — and that's a feature, not a disclaimer. These are educational workshops: skills, structure, and literacy. Dorina does not diagnose, treat, or counsel. One module (DESK's \"Support\" pillar) specifically teaches teams how to seek qualified professional help when it's needed.</li><li><strong>Do you do this in Arabic?</strong> Yes. Sessions run in Arabic, English, or a natural mix (المزيج اللبناني الطبيعي — the natural Lebanese blend), whatever suits your team. All handouts are available in both languages.</li><li><strong>Will you tell us if someone on the team is struggling?</strong> No. Sessions are educational and group-based; nobody is assessed, and nothing personal shared in a session is reported. If an individual needs support, the session equips them — and their manager — to find qualified help.</li><li><strong>What does it cost?</strong> The lunch &amp; learn taster is free for qualifying teams. Paid formats range from a 90-minute online session to a half-day flagship — ask for the current rate card, and expect a straight answer.</li><li><strong>Our power and internet are unreliable — can this still work?</strong> Yes. Every format has a hybrid or fully low-tech version: printed materials, no projector required. This is designed for Lebanon.</li><li><strong>Can you prove it will reduce sick days or anxiety?</strong> No — and be wary of anyone who says yes. What a workshop reliably delivers is what the WHO recommends workplaces provide: mental health literacy, stress-management skills, and manager awareness. Skills, not medical outcomes.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Book the free taster.</strong> One lunch hour. Zero cost. Your team leaves with the DESK one-pager and one habit to try this week. <strong>→ Message Dorina Salman on LinkedIn or email [Dorina's business email — TO CONFIRM].</strong></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-outreach-dm-scripts-full-copy\">5. Outreach &amp; DM Scripts (Full Copy)</h2>\n<p>House rules for all outreach: personalize the first line every time (a template opener is visible from orbit); one message, one ask; never send a pitch to someone who hasn't engaged or been warmly introduced; log every send in the pipeline tracker (§10). Arabic variants are provided for the two most-used scripts (5.1 and 5.3) — Dorina adapts register to the relationship (Lebanese professional Arabic tends toward a warm formal mix).</p>\n<h3 id=\"51-cold-but-warm-linkedin-dm-to-an-hr-manager-who-engaged-wi\">5.1 Cold-but-warm LinkedIn DM — to an HR manager who engaged with a post</h3>\n<p><strong>EN:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Hi [Name] — thank you for your comment on my post about [specific topic, e.g., workload visibility]. Your point about [their actual point] stayed with me.</p>\n<p>I run short educational workshops on practical mental health management at work — stress literacy, boundaries, workload structure. Nothing clinical; I'm an educator, not a therapist, and I'm careful about that line.</p>\n<p>I'm offering a small number of free 45-minute lunch &amp; learn sessions this quarter so HR teams can judge the format for themselves. If that could be useful at [Company], I'd be glad to send a one-page outline. Either way — thank you for engaging with the topic. It matters.</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>AR variant (gloss: same structure — thank, position with the non-clinical line, soft W0 offer, graceful close):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>مرحبا [الاسم] — شكراً على تعليقك على منشوري عن [الموضوع]. ملاحظتك عن [نقطتهم] خلّتني فكّر.</p>\n<p>أنا بقدّم ورش عمل تثقيفية قصيرة عن الإدارة العملية للصحة النفسية في الشغل — الوعي بالضغط، الحدود، وتنظيم عبء العمل. ما في شي علاجي أو سريري؛ أنا مثقِّفة، مش معالجة، وواضحة بهالخط.</p>\n<p>عم قدّم عدد محدود من جلسات \"غداء وتعلّم\" مجانية (٤٥ دقيقة) هالفصل ليقدر فريق الموارد البشرية يحكم على الصيغة بنفسه. إذا ممكن تفيد بـ[الشركة]، بكون سعيدة إبعتلك ملخص من صفحة واحدة. وبكل الأحوال — شكراً لاهتمامك بالموضوع.</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"52-follow-up-after-no-reply-one-graceful-then-stop\">5.2 Follow-up after no reply — one, graceful, then stop</h3>\n<p><strong>EN:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Hi [Name] — just floating this back up once, in case it arrived at a busy moment (I know how those weeks go). If a free lunch &amp; learn on practical workplace wellbeing isn't a fit right now, no reply needed — I'll keep sharing the useful material on my page either way. Wishing you a light week.</p></blockquote>\n<p><em>Rule: exactly one follow-up, 7–10 days later, then the contact moves to \"nurture via content\" in the tracker. No third message.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"53-pilot-invitation-dm-to-a-qualified-w0-candidate\">5.3 Pilot invitation DM — to a qualified W0 candidate</h3>\n<p><strong>EN:</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>Hi [Name] — following our exchange, I'd love to offer [Company] one of my free 45-minute lunch &amp; learn sessions: \"Mental Health Management at Work — the DESK Method.\" It's educational, practical, and light: how to detect stress early, set workable boundaries, make workload visible, and keep good habits going.</p>\n<p>What I'd need from you: a room (or a link), 8–25 people, and 15 minutes on a call beforehand so I tailor it to your team. What you get: the session, a one-page DESK handout for everyone, and a short feedback summary afterwards. No cost, and no obligation beyond that hour.</p>\n<p>Would a lunchtime in the next 2–3 weeks work?</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>AR variant (gloss: same offer, needs, deliverables, and time-bound ask):</strong></p>\n<blockquote><p>مرحبا [الاسم] — بعد حديثنا، حابة قدّم لـ[الشركة] وحدة من جلساتي المجانية \"غداء وتعلّم\" (٤٥ دقيقة): \"إدارة الصحة النفسية في الشغل — طريقة DESK\". الجلسة تثقيفية وعملية وخفيفة: كيف نكتشف الضغط بكير، نحط حدود واقعية، نخلّي عبء العمل مرئي، ونحافظ على العادات الجيدة.</p>\n<p>يلي بحتاجه منكم: قاعة (أو رابط)، من ٨ لـ٢٥ شخص، و١٥ دقيقة مكالمة قبل الجلسة لأكيّفها على فريقكم. يلي بتحصلوا عليه: الجلسة، ورقة DESK لكل مشارك، وملخص قصير بعدها. بدون أي كلفة أو التزام.</p>\n<p>بيناسبكم وقت غداء خلال الأسبوعين-الثلاثة الجايين؟</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"54-partnership-outreach-email-to-an-hr-community-professiona\">5.4 Partnership outreach email — to an HR community / professional association</h3>\n<p><strong>Subject:</strong> A free member session on workplace mental health literacy — no strings</p>\n<blockquote><p>Dear [Name / Committee],</p>\n<p>My name is Dorina Salman. I'm a workplace wellbeing educator based in Lebanon, with 12+ years of administrative and executive-assistant experience before moving into this work. I teach practical, non-clinical mental health management at work — stress literacy, boundaries, workload structure, and help-seeking as a skill — through a framework called the DESK Method.</p>\n<p>The WHO's recommended workplace actions — manager training and mental health literacy for workers — align closely with what professional communities like yours are best placed to spread, and Lebanon's national conversation on workplace mental health (led by the Ministry of Public Health's National Mental Health Programme) has opened real appetite for it. I'd like to contribute to that conversation through your community, not around it.</p>\n<p><strong>What I'm offering your members, free:</strong> a 45–60 minute educational session (in person or online, Arabic/English) on practical mental health management at work, plus a co-branded one-page DESK literacy handout your association can distribute. No selling from the stage; my only ask is a brief introduction and permission to share my contact details with interested members afterwards.</p>\n<p>Would a 20-minute call in the next two weeks suit you to explore whether this fits your events calendar?</p>\n<p>With respect and thanks, Dorina Salman Workplace Wellbeing Educator | Mental Health Literacy Advocate [LinkedIn URL] · [email]</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"55-workshop-proposal-email-post-conversation-with-structure\">5.5 Workshop proposal email — post-conversation, with structure</h3>\n<p><strong>Subject:</strong> Proposal: [W1/W2] workplace wellbeing workshop for [Company] — [month]</p>\n<blockquote><p>Dear [Name],</p>\n<p>Thank you for [the conversation / hosting the lunch &amp; learn]. As promised, here is a short proposal — one page, no annexes.</p>\n<p><strong>1. What you told me.</strong> [2–3 bullets in their words: e.g., \"team leads absorbing overflow workload,\" \"no shared language for raising pressure,\" \"wellbeing initiatives that fade after a week.\"]</p>\n<p><strong>2. What I propose.</strong> [Workshop tier + duration], \"Mental Health Management at Work\" built on the DESK Method: Detect (stress literacy) · Establish (boundaries, routines, workload visibility) · Support (help-seeking as a skill, referral pathways) · Keep (sustaining habits). Delivery in [Arabic/English/mixed], [in person / online / hybrid], with a full low-tech option if the venue needs it.</p>\n<p><strong>3. What's included.</strong> A 30-minute pre-call and short needs form; all participant materials; the live session; a one-page summary report to you within one week; a one-month check-in.</p>\n<p><strong>4. What this is and isn't.</strong> This is an educational programme in workplace mental health literacy and self-management skills — the kind of worker literacy and stress-management training the WHO recommends for workplaces. It is not therapy, assessment, or clinical intervention, and I don't promise health outcomes — I promise well-taught, usable skills.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Investment.</strong> [Amount] USD, inclusive of materials and follow-up. Valid for 30 days.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Next step.</strong> Reply with a preferred date and I'll send the pre-call agenda within 48 hours.</p>\n<p>Warm regards, Dorina Salman</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"56-post-w0-conversion-message-pilot-to-paid-sent-with-the-fe\">5.6 Post-W0 conversion message — pilot to paid, sent with the feedback summary</h3>\n<blockquote><p>Hi [Name] — attached is the feedback summary from Tuesday's session, as promised. A few things stood out: [1–2 genuine, specific observations from the room — e.g., \"the workload-visibility exercise generated the most discussion,\" \"several attendees asked for a manager-focused version\"].</p>\n<p>That last point is worth acting on. The natural next step most teams take after the taster is [W1: the half-day flagship, where the team actually builds their DESK routines rather than just meeting the ideas / W2: the 2-hour manager session, so team leads can support what the team started]. I've held [two dates in month] provisionally.</p>\n<p>Shall I send the one-page proposal?</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"57-referral-ask-to-a-happy-client-1-month-after-delivery\">5.7 Referral ask — to a happy client, ~1 month after delivery</h3>\n<blockquote><p>Hi [Name] — a month on from the workshop, I hope some of the DESK habits have stuck ([specific callback, e.g., \"I still think about your team's 'red-flag list' — one of the best I've seen\"]).</p>\n<p>A small ask, and please feel free to say no: I grow this work almost entirely through introductions. Is there one HR colleague or business owner in your network who'd value a free lunch &amp; learn like the one we started with? A one-line intro is plenty — I'll take it respectfully from there.</p>\n<p>And separately: if you'd be open to a one-sentence quote about the session for my materials, I'd be honoured — only with your written OK, and only worded in a way you fully approve.</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"58-objection-handling-the-six-objections-dorina-will-hear-an\">5.8 Objection handling — the six objections Dorina will hear, and the honest responses</h3>\n<p>These are conversational responses, not scripts to recite. The pattern is always the same: agree with the legitimate part, reframe with one fact or one boundary, offer the smallest next step.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Objection</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>\"We have no budget for wellbeing.\"</strong></td><td>\"Completely fair — most Lebanese SMEs don't have a wellbeing line. That's exactly why the taster is free: you spend one lunch hour, not one dollar, and judge for yourself whether the paid formats earn a place in next quarter's budget. And when we do talk numbers, they're SME numbers, not consultancy numbers.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>\"Our people are too busy for this.\"</strong></td><td>\"That's usually the strongest sign it's needed — the WHO lists excessive workload and pace among the main workplace risks to mental health. The taster is 45 minutes over lunch, and the half-day format is designed to pay back its time in workload structure the same week. But I'd never push a session onto a team in crisis-delivery mode; let's pick a calmer window.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>\"Isn't this a therapist's job?\"</strong></td><td>\"A therapist's job is treatment — and I don't touch that; I'm an educator, not a clinician, and I say so on every slide. My job is the layer before: literacy, boundaries, workload structure, and teaching people how and when to reach a qualified professional. Those are workplace skills, and the WHO specifically recommends workplaces build them.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>\"We tried a wellbeing talk before; nothing changed.\"</strong></td><td>\"One-off talks usually don't change anything — I agree. That's why every session ends with specific habits, a one-page tool, and a follow-up sequence, and why the format I most recommend long-term is the quarterly refresher, not the single event. Skills stick with repetition, not applause.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>\"Can you guarantee results — less stress, fewer sick days?\"</strong></td><td>\"No, and I'd be careful with anyone who guarantees health outcomes. What I guarantee is the education: your team will leave with a shared language for stress, concrete boundary and workload tools, and knowledge of where real help lives. What you do with those skills is where results come from.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>\"Why you? You were an administrator.\"</strong></td><td>\"That's exactly why. I spent 12+ years at the desk where everyone else's pressure lands — including in a law firm. I teach what I had to learn to survive it, in the language of people who actually sit in these offices, in Arabic or English. I'm not translating a textbook; I'm handing over a working system.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-launch-plan-septemberdecember-2026\">6. Launch Plan — September–December 2026</h2>\n<p>Three phases, aligned to the calendar in <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code> (Month 1 = September 2026, «The Introduction»).</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 1 — Pre-launch (September 2026).</strong> Nothing is sold. Objectives: profile fully live; 12 posts banked and approved; the <strong>Warm 20</strong> list built — 20 named people Dorina genuinely knows or can reach in one hop (former law-firm colleagues, AOU alumni, current professional contacts, admin peers), each with a personal, no-pitch reconnection message. The DESK one-pager is designed and ready. W0 logistics rehearsed once with a friendly audience.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 2 — Soft launch (October 2026) — the World Mental Health Day flagship week.</strong> This is the campaign centerpiece of the whole year. WMHD is October 10; its history of workplace-related themes makes it the single most natural moment for Dorina to plant her flag. The week delivers: a 5-post series, one <strong>free open online session</strong> (a public W0-style 45–60 min session, registration via LinkedIn), and <strong>W0 pilot offers to 10 named target companies</strong> drawn from September's warm conversations and post engagers. Still no paid selling in public — the paid conversation happens privately, after pilots.</p>\n<p><strong>WMHD flagship week — day-by-day (October 5–11, 2026):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Public action</th><th>Private action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mon Oct 5</td><td>Series post 1 — \"D — Detect\": stress literacy, the early-signal checklist</td><td>Final registration push to warm list; confirm session tech + low-bandwidth fallback</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Oct 6</td><td>Series post 2 — \"E — Establish\": one boundary that survives a Lebanese workweek</td><td>First 5 W0 offer DMs (§5.3) to target companies</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Oct 7</td><td>Series post 3 — \"S — Support\": help-seeking is a skill, not a weakness; signpost to the MoPH/NMHP public resources and [TO VERIFY before use: current national emotional-support helpline number — check the Ministry of Public Health / National Mental Health Programme site]</td><td>Remaining 5 W0 offer DMs</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Oct 8</td><td>Series post 4 — \"K — Keep\": the weekly review, rest, and why the WHO counts leisure and physical activity as stress-management interventions</td><td>Personal reminders to registrants</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Oct 9</td><td>Announcement post: tomorrow is World Mental Health Day; final open-session call</td><td>Session dry-run</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Oct 10</td><td><strong>World Mental Health Day.</strong> Capstone post (the full DESK story + why this day matters — WMHD's past themes include workplace mental health). <strong>Free open online session delivered.</strong></td><td>One-pager sent to every attendee same day</td></tr><tr><td>Sun Oct 11</td><td>Gratitude post: what the session's participants raised (themes only, no names)</td><td>Attendee thank-you messages; flag hottest conversations in tracker</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Phase 3 — Post-launch (November–December 2026).</strong> Convert: every delivered W0 gets the §5.6 message within 72 hours of its feedback summary. December runs the <strong>year-end closure campaign</strong> (content theme: closing the year without carrying it home — DESK's \"Keep\" pillar), whose commercial function is quiet: booking January W1/W2 slots and planting the \"start the year with the reset workshop\" seed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"week-by-week-table-septemberdecember-2026\">Week-by-week table, September–December 2026</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Dates (approx.)</th><th>Focus</th><th>Actions</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td>Sep 1–6</td><td>Foundation</td><td>Profile live; first 3 posts publish; one-pager final</td><td>Profile complete; 12 posts banked</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td>Sep 7–13</td><td>Warm 20 (part 1)</td><td>10 personal reconnection messages (no pitch); daily commenting on HR/manager posts</td><td>10 sent; 5 replies</td></tr><tr><td>S3</td><td>Sep 14–20</td><td>Warm 20 (part 2)</td><td>Next 10 messages; first one-pager offers to engaged commenters</td><td>20 sent total; 10 conversations open</td></tr><tr><td>S4</td><td>Sep 21–27</td><td>W0 rehearsal</td><td>Full dry-run of W0 with friendly audience; fix timings; build 10-company target list for October</td><td>Rehearsal done; list of 10 named companies</td></tr><tr><td>S5</td><td>Sep 28–Oct 4</td><td>WMHD ramp</td><td>Teaser posts; open-session registration live; confirm online-session tech (with low-bandwidth fallback)</td><td>20+ registrations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>O1</strong></td><td><strong>Oct 5–11</strong></td><td><strong>WMHD flagship week</strong></td><td>5-post series peaking Oct 10; free open online session delivered; W0 offer DMs (§5.3) to all 10 target companies</td><td>Session delivered; 10 offers out</td></tr><tr><td>O2</td><td>Oct 12–18</td><td>Pilot booking</td><td>Follow-ups (§5.2) to non-responders; schedule accepted W0s; thank-you + one-pager to open-session attendees</td><td>4–6 W0s scheduled</td></tr><tr><td>O3</td><td>Oct 19–25</td><td>First pilots</td><td>Deliver first 2 W0s; feedback summaries within 48h</td><td>2 delivered</td></tr><tr><td>O4</td><td>Oct 26–Nov 1</td><td>Pilots continue</td><td>Deliver next W0s; first §5.6 conversion messages</td><td>2 more delivered; 2 conversion messages sent</td></tr><tr><td>N1</td><td>Nov 2–8</td><td>Convert</td><td>Proposal emails (§5.5) to warm pilots; keep 3×/week content</td><td>2 proposals out</td></tr><tr><td>N2</td><td>Nov 9–15</td><td>Convert + pipeline</td><td>Remaining W0s delivered; partnership email (§5.4) to first professional community</td><td>All launch W0s done; 1 partner conversation</td></tr><tr><td>N3</td><td>Nov 16–22</td><td>First paid delivery window</td><td>Aim: first paid W1 or W2 delivered or firmly booked</td><td><strong>First paid booking</strong></td></tr><tr><td>N4</td><td>Nov 23–29</td><td>Review</td><td>Mid-launch metrics review (§10); adjust price ranges based on real conversations</td><td>Reviewed rate card</td></tr><tr><td>D1</td><td>Nov 30–Dec 6</td><td>Closure campaign opens</td><td>December content theme launches; December/January booking push in private channels</td><td>2 January slots proposed</td></tr><tr><td>D2</td><td>Dec 7–13</td><td>Nurture</td><td>1-month check-ins to October pilots; referral asks (§5.7) to happiest hosts</td><td>2 referral asks sent</td></tr><tr><td>D3</td><td>Dec 14–20</td><td>Year-end</td><td>\"Close the year well\" content peak; quarterly newsletter issue #1 (§9)</td><td>Newsletter sent</td></tr><tr><td>D4</td><td>Dec 21–31</td><td>Rest + plan</td><td>Reduced posting; Dorina's own DESK \"Keep\" week; January campaign pre-approved</td><td>Q1 2027 plan signed off</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Year-one realism check:</strong> the target for launch quarter is modest and honest — 6–10 W0s delivered, 1–3 paid bookings, 1 partnership conversation opened. From ~4 connections, that is success.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-seasonal-campaign-modules\">7. Seasonal Campaign Modules</h2>\n<p>Each module aligns with the corresponding month in <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>; the funnel role of each is defined here.</p>\n<h3 id=\"71-october-world-mental-health-day-flagship-oct-2026-repeats\">7.1 October — World Mental Health Day flagship (Oct 2026, repeats Oct 2027)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Goal:</strong> maximum visibility + fill the W0 pipeline (10 offers out; 4–6 booked).</li><li><strong>Hook:</strong> \"October 10 is World Mental Health Day — and the workplace is where most adults spend most of their waking hours. This week, one practical skill a day.\" (WMHD's past themes include workplace mental health — the alignment is natural, not forced.)</li><li><strong>Assets needed:</strong> 5-post series (one per DESK letter + a capstone), open-session slide deck + low-bandwidth fallback plan, registration link, DESK one-pager (EN/AR), W0 offer DM, 10-company target list.</li><li><strong>Offer attached:</strong> free open online session (public); W0 pilots (private, targeted).</li><li><strong>KPI:</strong> open-session registrations and attendance; W0 offers accepted; profile-visit spike week-over-week.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"72-january-reset-done-right-jan-2027\">7.2 January — \"Reset, Done Right\" (Jan 2027)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Goal:</strong> convert year-start motivation into W1 bookings; re-engage December's warm pipeline.</li><li><strong>Hook:</strong> \"New year's resolutions fail because they're goals without structure. A reset that lasts is a routine, not a promise. Here's how to build one at work.\" (Anti-hype framing — no transformation promises.)</li><li><strong>Assets needed:</strong> 3–4 post mini-series on routines and workload structure (DESK \"E\" and \"K\"), a simple \"January workload reset\" worksheet as the month's free download, §5.5 proposal ready with January/February dates.</li><li><strong>Offer attached:</strong> W1 flagship, positioned as the team's structured start-of-year session; W2 for managers planning the year.</li><li><strong>KPI:</strong> worksheet downloads; proposals sent; W1 bookings for Q1.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"73-ramadan-aware-campaign-around-februaryfebruarymarch-2027-\">7.3 Ramadan-aware campaign (around February–February–March 2027 — dates shift by moon sighting; never state exact dates)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Goal:</strong> trust and cultural credibility; retention touchpoint for W5 clients; light W4 pipeline.</li><li><strong>Hook:</strong> \"Ramadan changes the shape of the workday — energy, hours, routines. That's not a problem to push through; it's a rhythm to plan for, with compassion for yourself and your colleagues.\" Framing is energy, routines, and compassion — inclusive of fasting and non-fasting colleagues alike, never prescriptive about observance.</li><li><strong>Assets needed:</strong> 2–3 posts on routine adaptation and considerate scheduling; a short \"Ramadan workday rhythm\" one-pager (EN/AR); a W5-client refresher session variant on adjusted routines.</li><li><strong>Offer attached:</strong> nothing sold publicly during the month — this campaign is deliberately generous. Privately: the W5 quarterly refresher lands here for retainer clients; W4 offered to remote teams wanting a short seasonal session.</li><li><strong>KPI:</strong> engagement quality (comments, shares, DMs), W5 refreshers delivered, inbound enquiries in the following month.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"74-summer-rest-as-strategy-july-2027\">7.4 Summer — \"Rest as Strategy\" (July 2027)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Goal:</strong> sustain presence at low effort (Dorina rests too — modelling the message); seed September restart bookings.</li><li><strong>Hook:</strong> \"Rest isn't the reward for finishing the work. It's part of the work. The WHO lists leisure and physical activity among recommended stress-management interventions — your summer plan is a professional document.\"</li><li><strong>Assets needed:</strong> 2 posts/week (reduced cadence), a \"handover-and-switch-off checklist\" free download, an August \"book your September session\" private note to the full warm list.</li><li><strong>Offer attached:</strong> early-bird September/October bookings (soft, private); W5 renewal conversations for year-one retainer clients.</li><li><strong>KPI:</strong> checklist downloads; September bookings confirmed before August ends; W5 renewals.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-partnership-strategy\">8. Partnership Strategy</h2>\n<p>Four tiers plus one sensitive channel. Universal rules: partners are approached as <strong>ecosystems Dorina contributes to, never claims</strong> — no implied affiliation, partnership, or endorsement by WHO, MoPH/NMHP, the HR Association of Lebanon, or any named organisation unless a written agreement exists. Every pitch leads with what the partner's audience gains.</p>\n<p><strong>The pitch framing (all tiers):</strong> \"I bring your community a free, high-quality educational session on workplace mental health literacy — a topic the WHO recommends every workplace address and that Lebanon's national programme has put on the agenda. You provide the audience and the room; I provide the expertise, bilingual delivery, and a co-branded handout your members keep. No selling from your stage.\"</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Who</th><th>What Dorina offers them</th><th>What she asks for</th><th>First move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1. Professional communities</strong></td><td>HR associations and HR professional groups in Lebanon (e.g., the HR Association of Lebanon — approached respectfully as a potential partner, nothing more)</td><td>Free member session (W0-format); co-branded DESK literacy one-pager; a written article for their newsletter</td><td>Stage time, an introduction, permission to follow up with interested members</td><td>§5.4 email, ideally preceded by 4–6 weeks of genuine engagement with their public content</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. NMHP-ecosystem nonprofit/humanitarian organizations</strong></td><td>Organisations active in Lebanon's mental-health space (the NMHP's workplace campaign lists partners such as ABAAD, ACTED, ESA, IMC, Médecins du Monde, SKOUN — named here as ecosystem context only)</td><td>Free staff-facing session for <em>their own teams</em>; a bilingual literacy handout; a clearly non-clinical educator who signposts to qualified services rather than competing with them</td><td>Nothing at first. Relationship, alignment, and — over time — being a known, trusted name in the ecosystem</td><td>Attend public events; engage genuinely; offer the free staff session with the boundary stated up front</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Coworking spaces &amp; business communities</strong></td><td>Coworking hubs, entrepreneur networks, SME/business communities in Beirut and beyond</td><td>A free member lunch &amp; learn (their events calendar gets quality content at zero cost); a member-benefit rate for resident companies</td><td>Hosting + promotion to members; intro to 2–3 resident companies afterwards</td><td>Short DM/email to the community manager: one paragraph, the one-pager attached</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. Universities &amp; alumni networks</strong></td><td>Arab Open University – Lebanon alumni network (Dorina's own), university career services</td><td>A free session for graduating students / young alumni on managing the first years of working life; an alumni-story angle</td><td>Access to the young-professional audience (the 25–40 tribe pipeline); credibility by association with her own institution</td><td>Warm email to alumni office identifying herself as a graduate; offer the free session for their next event</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5. Current employer's client network</strong> <em>(handle with care)</em></td><td>The marketing company where Dorina currently works, and — only with explicit consent — its clients</td><td>Value-add for the employer: their administrator offers a professional development perk to clients</td><td>Explicit written consent from her employer first; clear separation between her role and her practice; zero use of employer resources or client data without permission</td><td>A transparent conversation with her manager <em>before anything else</em> — see the employment-boundary guidance in the package's positioning documents. If consent isn't clear and enthusiastic, this channel stays closed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Sequencing:</strong> launch quarter opens Tier 3 (fastest yes) and Tier 4 (warmest door); Tier 1 in November after there's a delivery track record to point to; Tier 2 is a year-long relationship investment, not a Q1 conversion play; Tier 5 only after the employer conversation, whenever Dorina judges it right.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"9-post-event-nurture-engine\">9. Post-Event Nurture Engine</h2>\n<p>Every workshop — free or paid — triggers the same sequence. Delivery logistics for the first two steps are specified in <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>; this section defines the marketing function of each touch.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>24-hour thank-you (to the host).</strong> The thank-you plus feedback summary commitment (per <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>). Marketing function: professionalism proof and the anchor for the §5.6 conversion message that follows for W0s.</li><li><strong>1-week value email (to the host, forwardable to attendees).</strong> No ask. Function: keeps DESK alive in the office and gives the host something shareable that carries Dorina's name deeper into the organisation. Full copy:</li></ol>\n<p>   <strong>Subject:</strong> Three things from last week's session your team can use today</p>\n<p>   &gt; Hi [Name],    &gt;    &gt; One week on from our session — no request in this email, just the three things I'd most want your team to keep from it:    &gt;    &gt; <strong>1. [Session-specific skill — e.g., the two-minute morning stress check from \"Detect.\"]</strong> The habit that matters is noticing early, not analysing deeply.    &gt; <strong>2. [Session-specific tool — e.g., the workload-visibility list from \"Establish.\"]</strong> One shared list beats ten private worries.    &gt; <strong>3. [Session-specific principle — e.g., help-seeking as a professional skill from \"Support.\"]</strong> Knowing where qualified support lives is a competence, not a confession.    &gt;    &gt; The DESK one-pager is attached again — feel free to forward this whole email to the team.    &gt;    &gt; Warmly,    &gt; Dorina    &gt;    &gt; <em>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</em></p>\n<ol><li><strong>1-month check-in (to the host).</strong> Function: reopens the conversation exactly when the post-workshop glow needs reinforcement — the natural W5 pitch moment, and the trigger point for the §5.7 referral ask where the relationship warrants it. Full copy:</li></ol>\n<p>   <strong>Subject:</strong> One month on — two quick questions</p>\n<p>   &gt; Hi [Name],    &gt;    &gt; It's been about a month since the workshop, which is exactly when new habits either settle or slide. Two honest questions, thirty seconds each:    &gt;    &gt; 1. <strong>What stuck?</strong> (Anything the team still does or references.)    &gt; 2. <strong>What faded?</strong> (No judgment — this tells me what needs reinforcement.)    &gt;    &gt; If the answer to the second question is \"most of it,\" that's normal — skills hold through repetition, which is what the quarterly refresher format exists for. Happy to tell you how that works if useful; equally happy just to hear how the team is doing.    &gt;    &gt; Warmly,    &gt; Dorina</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Quarterly literacy newsletter.</strong> Working name suggestion: <strong>«On the DESK | على المكتب»</strong> (gloss: \"on the desk\" — the framework and the workplace in one phrase). Rubric per issue, one page maximum: (a) one DESK skill explained in ~150 words; (b) one workplace scenario and how to handle it (anonymized composite, never a real client story without permission); (c) one seasonal note tied to the calendar; (d) one curated pointer to a credible public resource (WHO or MoPH/NMHP public materials — linked, never claimed as affiliation); (e) standing disclaimer + unsubscribe. Audience: every past host, attendee who opted in, and warm contact. Function: the low-cost drumbeat that keeps Dorina \"Referenced\" between bookings. Consent-based list only.</li><li><strong>Rebooking triggers tied to calendar moments.</strong> Three standing prompts in the pipeline tracker, fired as personal emails (never mass sends): <strong>August →</strong> \"World Mental Health Day is in October — shall we reserve your team's session now?\" (to all past clients); <strong>December →</strong> \"January reset session — your team started this year with DESK; want to start the next one the same way?\"; <strong>~6 weeks before Ramadan →</strong> \"A short refresher on adjusted routines for the season — shall I schedule your team's?\" (W5 clients get this automatically; past W1/W2 clients get it as an offer).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>The engine's arithmetic:</strong> a client touched five times a year with value rebooks without being \"sold.\" W5 exists so that rebooking becomes a subscription instead of a decision.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"10-metrics-pipeline-hygiene\">10. Metrics &amp; Pipeline Hygiene</h2>\n<h3 id=\"101-kpi-set-per-funnel-stage-realistic-year-one-targets-from\">10.1 KPI set per funnel stage — realistic year-one targets (from ~4 connections)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Funnel stage</th><th>Metric</th><th>Q1 target (Sep–Nov 2026)</th><th>Year-one target (by Aug 2027)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Seen</td><td>LinkedIn followers/connections</td><td>150+</td><td>1,000+</td></tr><tr><td>Seen</td><td>Avg. impressions per post</td><td>300+</td><td>1,500+</td></tr><tr><td>Followed → Trusted</td><td>One-pager downloads / resource requests</td><td>25+</td><td>200+</td></tr><tr><td>Trusted → Invited</td><td>Meaningful conversations opened (DM threads or calls)</td><td>20</td><td>100+</td></tr><tr><td>Invited → Pilot</td><td>W0 sessions delivered</td><td>6–10</td><td>18–24</td></tr><tr><td>Pilot → Paid</td><td>Paid bookings (W1–W4)</td><td>1–3</td><td>10–15</td></tr><tr><td>Paid → Retained</td><td>W5 retainers signed</td><td>0 (too early)</td><td>2–4</td></tr><tr><td>Retained → Referenced</td><td>Warm referrals received; permissioned quotes</td><td>1–2</td><td>8+ referrals, 5+ quotes</td></tr><tr><td>Health check</td><td>W0 → paid conversion rate</td><td>—</td><td>40–50% of qualified W0s</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Targets are planning instruments, not promises — reviewed and reset at the November review (week N4) and quarterly thereafter. If W0 → paid conversion runs below ~30%, the problem is usually W0 qualification (wrong rooms), not the workshop; tighten §3's criteria before touching price.</p>\n<h3 id=\"102-pipeline-tracker-spec-one-spreadsheet-one-row-per-opport\">10.2 Pipeline tracker spec — one spreadsheet, one row per opportunity</h3>\n<p>Columns: <strong>Date added · Contact name · Role · Organisation · Segment (HR / SME / Admin / nonprofit/humanitarian organization / Diaspora) · Source (post engagement / Warm 20 / referral / partner / open session) · Funnel stage (Seen / Conversation / W0 offered / W0 scheduled / W0 delivered / Proposal sent / Booked / Delivered / Retained / Closed–not now) · Last touch date · Next action · Next action date · Offer discussed · Value (USD, if proposed) · Notes.</strong></p>\n<p>Hygiene rules: every row has a next action and a date, or it moves to \"Closed–not now\" with a revisit month; \"Closed–not now\" is a parking lot, not a graveyard — WMHD and January triggers sweep it; nothing lives only in LinkedIn DMs — if it matters, it's a row.</p>\n<h3 id=\"103-weekly-30-minute-marketing-review-ritual-fixed-slot-eg-f\">10.3 Weekly 30-minute marketing review ritual (fixed slot, e.g., Friday)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Minutes 0–5:</strong> last week's numbers into the tracker (posts published, impressions, new conversations, downloads).</li><li><strong>Minutes 5–15:</strong> walk every open pipeline row; execute or reschedule each overdue next action. No row skipped.</li><li><strong>Minutes 15–20:</strong> reply debt — clear every unanswered comment and DM.</li><li><strong>Minutes 20–25:</strong> next week — confirm the 3 scheduled posts (per approval workflow), pick the week's single outreach priority (never more than one campaign push at a time).</li><li><strong>Minutes 25–30:</strong> one-line journal — what worked, what didn't, one adjustment. Monthly, these lines become the review input for <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code> adjustments.</li></ol>\n<p>This ritual is itself DESK's \"Keep\" pillar applied to marketing: a weekly review that sustains the system. If Dorina runs nothing else in a chaotic week, she runs this half hour.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-marketing-specific\">What we still need from Dorina (marketing-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Business email address</strong> for the landing page, proposals, and newsletter sender identity (a dedicated address, not her employer's).</li><li><strong>The Warm 20 list</strong> — 20 real names she can reconnect with in September (former law-firm colleagues, AOU contacts, admin peers, current professional network).</li><li><strong>The 10-company WMHD target list</strong> — organisations she can plausibly reach for October W0 offers, with the connecting thread for each.</li><li><strong>Employer conversation outcome</strong> — whether her current employer consents to (a) her public practice generally and (b) any approach to its client network (Tier 5 stays closed until this is explicit).</li><li><strong>Price validation input</strong> — her read on the suggested USD ranges after her first five sales conversations, so the rate card can be locked.</li><li><strong>Approval of all copy in this document</strong> — especially the landing page (§4), the two Arabic DM variants (§5.1, §5.3), and the newsletter name «On the DESK | على المكتب» — per <code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code>.</li><li><strong>Delivery constraints</strong> — days/times she can realistically deliver workshops around her current job, which caps monthly W0 + paid capacity and therefore the pipeline targets.</li><li><strong>Photography/design assets</strong> — a professional headshot and simple brand basics (colors, logo if any) for the one-pager PDF and newsletter template.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"sources-5\">Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a> (workforce share, working days lost, productivity cost, workplace risk factors, recommended actions: manager training, worker literacy, stress-management and leisure/physical-activity interventions)</li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a> (mental health as a basic human right; workplace support mechanisms)</li><li>Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a> (national workplace mental health campaign «ما تضيعوا الفرصة / Don't miss the opportunity»; ecosystem partners — referenced as context only; no affiliation claimed)</li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a> (October 10; past themes include workplace mental health)</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"final-review-addendum-preconditions-before-public-delivery-3\">Final Review Addendum — Preconditions Before Public Delivery</h2>\n<p>Before Dorina publishes the profile positioning or delivers paid workshops, complete these checks:</p>\n<ul><li>Confirm exact employment history and current employer restrictions; do not publish any dates or titles that Dorina has not verified.</li><li>Complete at least one non-clinical mental-health-literacy or Psychological First Aid training and one basic facilitation practice cycle before paid delivery.</li><li>Confirm local public/professional liability insurance options and obtain local legal review for disclaimers, privacy boundaries, workshop terms, and employer/moonlighting issues.</li><li>Verify current Lebanon crisis/referral contacts and operating hours before every printed handout or live workshop. Embrace Lebanon publicly lists 1564 for its Lifeline, but all details must be re-checked close to use.</li><li>Budget honestly: content drafting may take 2–3 hours/week, but the full engine including engagement, approvals, outreach, workshop preparation, delivery, and review can require 6–10+ hours/week during launch periods.</li><li>Track safeguarding metrics alongside growth: referral signposts made, boundary escalations, stopped exercises, post-workshop debrief notes, and whether the protocol was followed.</li></ul>","toc":[{"id":"1-funnel-architecture-authority-ladder-funnel-stages","text":"1. Funnel Architecture — Authority Ladder → Funnel Stages","lvl":2},{"id":"2-segment-playbooks","text":"2. Segment Playbooks","lvl":2},{"id":"21-hr-professionals-people-managers-lebanon-the-buyers","text":"2.1 HR professionals & people managers (Lebanon) — the buyers","lvl":3},{"id":"22-sme-owners-lebanon-the-business-case-segment","text":"2.2 SME owners (Lebanon) — the business-case segment","lvl":3},{"id":"23-administrative-professionals-eas-office-managers-the-trib","text":"2.3 Administrative professionals / EAs / office managers — the tribe","lvl":3},{"id":"24-nonprofithumanitarian-organization-development-sector-sta","text":"2.4 nonprofit/humanitarian organization / development-sector staff — the mission-aligned segment","lvl":3},{"id":"25-diaspora-gcc-based-professionals-remote-teams-the-online-","text":"2.5 Diaspora & GCC-based professionals / remote teams — the online segment","lvl":3},{"id":"3-offer-ladder-packaging","text":"3. Offer Ladder & Packaging","lvl":2},{"id":"4-landing-page-one-pager-copy-full-copy-doubles-as-pdf-for-e","text":"4. Landing Page / One-Pager Copy (Full Copy — Doubles as PDF for Email)","lvl":2},{"id":"practical-mental-health-skills-for-real-workplaces","text":"Practical mental health skills for real workplaces.","lvl":3},{"id":"5-outreach-dm-scripts-full-copy","text":"5. Outreach & DM Scripts (Full Copy)","lvl":2},{"id":"51-cold-but-warm-linkedin-dm-to-an-hr-manager-who-engaged-wi","text":"5.1 Cold-but-warm LinkedIn DM — to an HR manager who engaged with a post","lvl":3},{"id":"52-follow-up-after-no-reply-one-graceful-then-stop","text":"5.2 Follow-up after no reply — one, graceful, then stop","lvl":3},{"id":"53-pilot-invitation-dm-to-a-qualified-w0-candidate","text":"5.3 Pilot invitation DM — to a qualified W0 candidate","lvl":3},{"id":"54-partnership-outreach-email-to-an-hr-community-professiona","text":"5.4 Partnership outreach email — to an HR community / professional association","lvl":3},{"id":"55-workshop-proposal-email-post-conversation-with-structure","text":"5.5 Workshop proposal email — post-conversation, with structure","lvl":3},{"id":"56-post-w0-conversion-message-pilot-to-paid-sent-with-the-fe","text":"5.6 Post-W0 conversion message — pilot to paid, sent with the feedback summary","lvl":3},{"id":"57-referral-ask-to-a-happy-client-1-month-after-delivery","text":"5.7 Referral ask — to a happy client, ~1 month after delivery","lvl":3},{"id":"58-objection-handling-the-six-objections-dorina-will-hear-an","text":"5.8 Objection handling — the six objections Dorina will hear, and the honest responses","lvl":3},{"id":"6-launch-plan-septemberdecember-2026","text":"6. Launch Plan — September–December 2026","lvl":2},{"id":"week-by-week-table-septemberdecember-2026","text":"Week-by-week table, September–December 2026","lvl":3},{"id":"7-seasonal-campaign-modules","text":"7. Seasonal Campaign Modules","lvl":2},{"id":"71-october-world-mental-health-day-flagship-oct-2026-repeats","text":"7.1 October — World Mental Health Day flagship (Oct 2026, repeats Oct 2027)","lvl":3},{"id":"72-january-reset-done-right-jan-2027","text":"7.2 January — \"Reset, Done Right\" (Jan 2027)","lvl":3},{"id":"73-ramadan-aware-campaign-around-februaryfebruarymarch-2027-","text":"7.3 Ramadan-aware campaign (around February–February–March 2027 — dates shift by moon sighting; never state exact dates)","lvl":3},{"id":"74-summer-rest-as-strategy-july-2027","text":"7.4 Summer — \"Rest as Strategy\" (July 2027)","lvl":3},{"id":"8-partnership-strategy","text":"8. Partnership Strategy","lvl":2},{"id":"9-post-event-nurture-engine","text":"9. Post-Event Nurture Engine","lvl":2},{"id":"10-metrics-pipeline-hygiene","text":"10. Metrics & Pipeline Hygiene","lvl":2},{"id":"101-kpi-set-per-funnel-stage-realistic-year-one-targets-from","text":"10.1 KPI set per funnel stage — realistic year-one targets (from ~4 connections)","lvl":3},{"id":"102-pipeline-tracker-spec-one-spreadsheet-one-row-per-opport","text":"10.2 Pipeline tracker spec — one spreadsheet, one row per opportunity","lvl":3},{"id":"103-weekly-30-minute-marketing-review-ritual-fixed-slot-eg-f","text":"10.3 Weekly 30-minute marketing review ritual (fixed slot, e.g., Friday)","lvl":3},{"id":"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-marketing-specific","text":"What we still need from Dorina (marketing-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"sources-5","text":"Sources","lvl":2},{"id":"final-review-addendum-preconditions-before-public-delivery-3","text":"Final Review Addendum — Preconditions Before Public Delivery","lvl":2}],"words":8590,"mins":37},{"slug":"opportunity-map-lebanon","file":"OPPORTUNITY_MAP_LEBANON.md","title":"Lebanon Opportunity Map","blurb":"Sector map, seasonal calendar, partnership ecosystem, the 90-day shortlist.","track":"Strategy","n":11,"html":"<h1 id=\"opportunity-map-lebanon-diaspora-dorina-salman\">Opportunity Map: Lebanon &amp; Diaspora — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> This document maps where, when, and through whom Dorina's authority as a <strong>Workplace Wellbeing Educator | Mental Health Literacy Advocate</strong> converts into real opportunities — paid workshops, partnerships, speaking slots, and retainer relationships — across the Lebanese market and the diaspora/GCC remote market. It is the \"territory\" companion to the \"vehicle\" documents: the offers live in <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>, the conversion machinery in <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>, the visibility engine in <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>, and the guardrails in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>. Everything here is strictly non-clinical: education on stress literacy, burnout prevention, boundaries, routines, workload structure, and help-seeking — never therapy, diagnosis, or treatment.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-how-to-read-this-map\">1. How to read this map</h2>\n<p>An <strong>opportunity</strong> in this strategy is not a sector or a contact — it is an intersection of four things:</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Opportunity = Audience pain × Access route × Seasonal moment × Offer fit</strong></p></blockquote>\n<ul><li><strong>Audience pain</strong> — a workplace stressor the audience already feels and names (deadlines, workload, long hours, unclear roles). The WHO's list of workplace risks to mental health — excessive workloads and work pace, understaffing, long or inflexible hours, lack of control over job design, under-use of skills, poor physical conditions, and negative workplace culture — is our anchor vocabulary. If a sector's daily reality maps onto that list, wellbeing education resonates there.</li><li><strong>Access route</strong> — a realistic path to the decision-maker: a warm contact, a community, an alumni network, a public event. No access route, no opportunity — however large the sector.</li><li><strong>Seasonal moment</strong> — the month when the door is naturally open (World Mental Health Day on 10 October, January reset, pre-summer fatigue). Section 3 maps these.</li><li><strong>Offer fit</strong> — the right first workshop from the W0–W5 portfolio in <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>. Almost every sector enters through <strong>W0 (free lunch &amp; learn taster)</strong> because it removes budget risk; the sector map notes where a different entry makes sense.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"scoring-rubric-used-consistently-in-section-2\">Scoring rubric (used consistently in Section 2)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Low</th><th>Medium</th><th>High</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Access</strong></td><td>Can Dorina realistically reach the decision-maker in the next 6 months?</td><td>No warm path; cold outreach only</td><td>One-step introduction plausible (community, alumni, ex-colleague)</td><td>Existing relationship or insider status</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fit</strong></td><td>Does the DESK Method (proposed) and non-clinical positioning match what this buyer needs?</td><td>Buyer likely wants clinical services or something else</td><td>Fit with adaptation</td><td>Direct fit; her story is native to this world</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Effort</strong></td><td>How much preparation, customization, and logistics per engagement?</td><td>High effort (procurement, long cycles, heavy tailoring)</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Light (standard workshop lands as-is)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Revenue potential</strong></td><td>Realistic 12-month paid ceiling, assuming pilots convert per <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>?</td><td>Free/goodwill mostly</td><td>Occasional paid W1–W3</td><td>Repeat business, W5 retainer plausible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Reading rule:</strong> a sector with High Access and High Fit but Low Revenue (e.g., some nonprofit/humanitarian organization or community work) is still valuable — it builds proof, testimonials, and referrals that unlock High-Revenue sectors later. In year one, <strong>Access beats Revenue</strong> as the sorting key. Note that on the Effort row, \"High\" effort is a <em>cost</em>, not a virtue — read High-Effort sectors as slower bets.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-sector-map\">2. Sector map</h2>\n<p>Ten sectors, each assessed against the rubric. All Lebanon-market specifics (which firms, how many, what they pay) are deliberately absent — they are <strong>to verify</strong> during the 90-day shortlist in Section 7. Sector <em>types</em> below are suggestions to explore, not asserted market facts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2a-law-firms-professional-services-her-home-turf\">2a. Law firms &amp; professional services — her home turf</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why wellbeing education resonates:</strong> legal and professional-services work concentrates several WHO-listed risks in one place: excessive workloads and pace, long and inflexible hours, and a culture where admitting strain can feel career-limiting. Dorina spent 12+ years inside a law-firm environment as an executive assistant — she does not need to imagine this pain; she administered around it daily.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> direct, personal outreach to former colleagues, office managers, and partners she worked with or alongside. This is the only sector where she can open with \"you know my work.\"</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W0</strong> taster for the whole office, positioned as \"workload, energy, and focus for high-pressure teams,\" converting to <strong>W1</strong> (flagship half-day) or <strong>W3</strong> (admin-professionals session for the support staff she once was).</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> managing partner or office manager/practice manager; in larger firms, an HR lead (to verify per firm).</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> billable-hours culture makes half-days hard to schedule (offer W2's 2-hour format as fallback); confidentiality-minded firms may hesitate to discuss \"mental health\" openly (lead with the workload/focus framing).</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>High</strong> · Fit <strong>High</strong> · Effort <strong>High (light lift)</strong> · Revenue <strong>Medium</strong></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2b-marketing-creative-media-agencies-her-current-world\">2b. Marketing, creative &amp; media agencies — her current world</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> deadline culture, client-driven pace, always-on communication, and understaffed teams — a near-verbatim match to the WHO workplace risk list (excessive pace, long hours, lack of control over deliverables). Burnout language is already normalized in agency talk, lowering stigma barriers.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> her current employer (see the constraint in Section 6 — employer blessing first), then the agency's client and peer network, then agency-community events (to verify locally).</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W0</strong> internally as a rehearsal-with-witnesses; then <strong>W2</strong> (manager session) sold to agency leadership — agency managers are young, promoted fast, and rarely trained to spot team strain, which is exactly what WHO recommends manager training for.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> founder/managing director in smaller agencies; HR or operations lead in larger ones (to verify).</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> conflict-of-interest optics with her employer (resolve in writing before any external agency outreach); agencies are cost-sensitive — keep pilots free or modular per <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>.</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>High</strong> · Fit <strong>High</strong> · Effort <strong>High (light lift)</strong> · Revenue <strong>Medium</strong></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2c-banks-financial-institutions\">2c. Banks &amp; financial institutions</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> high-pressure targets, compliance load, long hours, and — as a planning assumption — years of sector stress in Lebanon's financial context have left workforces fatigued. WHO's recommended organizational actions (manager training, mental-health literacy, stress-management skills) map directly onto what an HR department in this sector could commission.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> HR and training departments, reached via HR communities (Section 4, Tier 1) rather than cold-calling branches. Banks buy from vendors with references — so this is a <strong>year-one-seeding, year-two-selling</strong> sector.</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W0</strong> for one department as a pilot, explicitly framed as literacy/education; <strong>W5</strong> quarterly retainer is the long-term prize, since banks prefer structured recurring programs.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> HR director or learning &amp; development manager (to verify per institution).</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> procurement processes, vendor-registration requirements, and preference for established training houses; slow decision cycles.</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>Low</strong> · Fit <strong>Medium</strong> · Effort <strong>Low (heavy lift)</strong> · Revenue <strong>High</strong></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2d-nonprofithumanitarian-organizations-humanitariandevelopme\">2d. nonprofit/humanitarian organizations &amp; humanitarian/development organizations</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> humanitarian and development work is structurally high-stress — emergency tempo, funding uncertainty, exposure to others' distress, understaffing. Critically, this sector is <em>adjacent to the national ecosystem</em>: Lebanon's Ministry of Public Health runs a National Mental Health Programme (NMHP) which has run a national \"Mental Health in the Workplace\" campaign (\"Don't miss the opportunity / ما تضيعوا الفرصة\") with publicly listed partners including WHO, ABAAD, ACTED, ESA, the HR Association of Lebanon, IMC, Médecins du Monde, and SKOUN. That public list is a map of organizations that already care about workplace mental health. Dorina claims <strong>no affiliation</strong> with any of them — but their existence proves the conversation is nationally live.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> HR/staff-care focal points in nonprofit/humanitarian organizations, reached through professional networks and public sector events; offering <em>staff wellbeing education</em> (for their own teams), not program delivery to beneficiaries.</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W0</strong> for an nonprofit/humanitarian organization's admin and operations staff; <strong>W4</strong> (online 90-min) works well for nonprofit/humanitarian organizations with field-distributed teams.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> HR manager, staff-care/duty-of-care focal point, or country operations lead (titles vary — to verify).</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> many nonprofit/humanitarian organizations have in-house psychosocial expertise and will scrutinize scope — which is actually an asset: her clearly non-clinical, education-only positioning (with <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> shareable on request) is exactly what a careful nonprofit/humanitarian organization wants to see. Budgets may favor free/low-cost engagements initially.</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>Medium</strong> · Fit <strong>High</strong> · Effort <strong>Medium</strong> · Revenue <strong>Low–Medium</strong> (but High referral and credibility value)</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2e-hospitals-clinics-administrative-and-non-clinical-staff-o\">2e. Hospitals &amp; clinics — administrative and non-clinical staff ONLY</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Scope statement, explicit:</strong> Dorina serves the <strong>administrative workforce</strong> of healthcare institutions — receptionists, billing and records staff, schedulers, office managers, HR teams. She does <strong>not</strong> train, advise, or support clinical staff on clinical matters, and she does not deliver anything resembling clinical supervision or staff counseling. This boundary goes in writing in every healthcare proposal.</li><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> hospital admin staff absorb the pressure of the clinical environment — pace, understaffing, emotionally charged interactions at the front desk — without the professional support structures clinicians may have. They are her tribe wearing a different badge.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> hospital HR/administration departments, framed precisely: \"workplace wellbeing education for your non-clinical teams.\"</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W3</strong> (admin-professionals session) is the natural first offer here — more specific than W0 and instantly signals she understands this workforce.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> HR director or director of administration (to verify per institution).</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> healthcare institutions are highly sensitive to scope-of-practice questions — any ambiguity about \"mental health\" services will stall the deal. Lead every conversation with the non-clinical scope statement and the standing disclaimer.</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>Low–Medium</strong> · Fit <strong>High (for admin staff)</strong> · Effort <strong>Medium</strong> · Revenue <strong>Medium</strong></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2f-universities-schools-staff-wellbeing-the-aou-network\">2f. Universities &amp; schools — staff wellbeing + the AOU network</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> administrative and academic-support staff in education face term-time surges, exam-season pressure, and under-recognized workloads — WHO's \"under-use of skills\" and workload-spike risks in institutional form. Separately, universities host exactly the audience (young professionals and soon-to-be professionals) her LinkedIn content serves.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> <strong>Arab Open University – Lebanon alumni relations</strong> — Dorina is a B.B.A. graduate and this is a real network she can activate: alumni newsletters, guest-talk slots, career-services events. Other universities and schools follow once she has an AOU talk as proof.</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> a <strong>guest talk</strong> (adapted W0 content) for alumni or staff; <strong>W1</strong> for administrative staff development days; <strong>W4</strong> for alumni abroad.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> alumni-relations office, HR/staff-development office, or student-affairs teams for career-adjacent talks (to verify).</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> academic calendars book far ahead (approach in September for winter/spring slots); talks are usually unpaid — treat them as authority assets feeding the funnel in <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>.</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>High (AOU) / Medium (others)</strong> · Fit <strong>High</strong> · Effort <strong>High (light lift)</strong> · Revenue <strong>Low</strong> (High visibility value)</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2g-tech-startups-remote-first-companies\">2g. Tech startups &amp; remote-first companies</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> startup culture normalizes overwork; remote work blurs boundaries between work and rest — directly the WHO risks of excessive pace, long/inflexible hours, and culture problems. Founders are often receptive to \"sustainable performance\" framing, and small headcounts mean one yes from one founder is a full engagement.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> startup community events, coworking-space programming (see 2i), and LinkedIn itself — this audience actually reads it. Explore local startup hubs and entrepreneurship-support organizations as venue categories (specific names to verify).</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W4</strong> (online 90-min) fits remote-first teams natively; <strong>W0</strong> in-person for co-located startups.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> founder or head of people/operations — often the same person.</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> cash-sensitive; churn (a startup client may not exist next year); preference for free content. Counter with low-priced W4 and community bundles.</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>Medium</strong> · Fit <strong>High</strong> · Effort <strong>High (light lift)</strong> · Revenue <strong>Medium</strong></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2h-smes-family-businesses\">2h. SMEs &amp; family businesses</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> in small and family-run firms there is no HR department to catch strain — the owner is HR. Understaffing and role overload (WHO-listed risks) are structural: everyone does three jobs. A practical, jargon-free session on workload structure and boundaries speaks their language, especially when framed as <em>keeping good people</em> rather than \"mental health.\"</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> business networks, chambers-of-commerce-type bodies, and owner communities (categories to explore, names to verify); referrals from her law-firm network, since law firms serve SME owners.</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W2</strong> adapted for owners (\"how to spot strain in your team before people quit\"), or <strong>W0</strong> at a business-community event reaching many owners at once.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> the owner. One conversation, one decision — the shortest sales cycle on this map.</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> \"wellbeing\" can sound like a luxury to a stretched owner; extreme price sensitivity as a planning assumption. Lead with retention and productivity framing (WHO: an estimated 12 billion working days are lost every year to depression and anxiety, at about US$1 trillion in lost productivity — the global-scale version of the owner's local problem).</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>Medium</strong> · Fit <strong>Medium–High</strong> · Effort <strong>High (light lift)</strong> · Revenue <strong>Medium</strong></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2i-coworking-spaces-business-communities-professional-syndic\">2i. Coworking spaces, business communities &amp; professional syndicates/associations</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> these are not end-buyers so much as <strong>multipliers</strong> — one session in a coworking space or association event puts Dorina in front of dozens of decision-makers from sectors 2a–2h simultaneously. Community managers constantly need quality free programming; she constantly needs stages. The trade is natural.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> direct proposal to community/events managers: \"a free 60-minute lunch &amp; learn on workload, energy, and focus for your members.\" Professional associations (HR bodies, admin-professional groups, sector syndicates — categories to verify) work the same way at higher credibility.</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W0</strong>, always — it is literally built for this. Each event feeds email capture and follow-up per <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> community manager, events coordinator, or association board/committee member.</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> free-programming expectations (fine in year one — this is the seeding channel); crowded event calendars around popular dates — book the October World Mental Health Day window early.</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>High</strong> · Fit <strong>High</strong> · Effort <strong>High (light lift)</strong> · Revenue <strong>Low direct / High indirect</strong></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"2j-diaspora-gcc-remote-market-w4-online\">2j. Diaspora &amp; GCC remote market — W4 online</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why it resonates:</strong> as a planning assumption, Lebanon has a significant diaspora, including many professionals in GCC countries, often working demanding schedules far from family support systems. They share her language, cultural context, and workplace idioms; a bilingual Arabic/English educator who <em>gets</em> both the Lebanese office and the expat grind is a differentiated offer. GCC-based employers and Lebanese-owned businesses abroad also budget in stronger currencies — a pricing advantage.</li><li><strong>Realistic entry point:</strong> LinkedIn (the diaspora professional's default network), diaspora professional groups and alumni chapters (AOU alumni abroad — to verify what chapters exist), and referrals from Lebanon-based clients whose sister companies operate in the GCC.</li><li><strong>First workshop:</strong> <strong>W4</strong> (online 90-min) exclusively at first — it was designed for this market. Time-zone friendliness with the GCC is workable from Lebanon.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker:</strong> individual professionals (paid tickets or free-then-nurture), community organizers, and eventually GCC-based HR contacts.</li><li><strong>Obstacles:</strong> zero physical presence; connectivity on Dorina's side must be redundant (see Section 6); building trust remotely takes longer — the <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code> is the primary trust engine here.</li><li><strong>Scores:</strong> Access <strong>Medium</strong> · Fit <strong>High</strong> · Effort <strong>Medium</strong> · Revenue <strong>Medium–High over time</strong></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"sector-map-at-a-glance\">Sector map at a glance</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sector</th><th>Access</th><th>Fit</th><th>Effort (lift)</th><th>Revenue</th><th>First offer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law firms &amp; professional services</td><td>High</td><td>High</td><td>Light</td><td>Medium</td><td>W0 → W1/W3</td></tr><tr><td>Marketing/creative agencies</td><td>High</td><td>High</td><td>Light</td><td>Medium</td><td>W0 → W2</td></tr><tr><td>Banks &amp; financial institutions</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium</td><td>Heavy</td><td>High</td><td>W0 pilot → W5</td></tr><tr><td>nonprofit/humanitarian organizations &amp; humanitarian orgs</td><td>Medium</td><td>High</td><td>Medium</td><td>Low–Med</td><td>W0 / W4</td></tr><tr><td>Hospitals — admin staff only</td><td>Low–Med</td><td>High</td><td>Medium</td><td>Medium</td><td>W3</td></tr><tr><td>Universities &amp; schools (AOU first)</td><td>High/Med</td><td>High</td><td>Light</td><td>Low</td><td>Guest talk → W1</td></tr><tr><td>Tech startups &amp; remote-first</td><td>Medium</td><td>High</td><td>Light</td><td>Medium</td><td>W4 / W0</td></tr><tr><td>SMEs &amp; family businesses</td><td>Medium</td><td>Med–High</td><td>Light</td><td>Medium</td><td>W2 / W0</td></tr><tr><td>Coworking &amp; associations</td><td>High</td><td>High</td><td>Light</td><td>Low direct</td><td>W0</td></tr><tr><td>Diaspora &amp; GCC</td><td>Medium</td><td>High</td><td>Medium</td><td>Med–High</td><td>W4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Year-one priority order:</strong> 2a and 2b (insider access) → 2i and 2f (multipliers and stages) → 2g, 2h, 2d (first paid conversions) → 2j (parallel online track) → 2e and 2c (patient, high-proof plays).</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-seasonal-opportunity-calendar-sept-2026-aug-2027\">3. Seasonal opportunity calendar — Sept 2026 → Aug 2027</h2>\n<p>Aligned month-by-month with <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>. Workplace mood entries are planning assumptions about rhythm, not asserted facts; religious-calendar timing shifts by moon sighting and is never stated as exact dates.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Workplace mood / context in Lebanon</th><th>The opportunity move</th><th>Offer to lead with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Sept 2026</strong></td><td>Back-to-work and back-to-school energy; teams re-planning after summer; budget conversations beginning in some firms</td><td><strong>Seed, don't sell.</strong> Activate law-firm and agency contacts; book October stages now (coworking spaces, communities, AOU) — everyone plans World Mental Health Day content in September</td><td>W0 offered free for October delivery</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Oct 2026</strong></td><td><strong>World Mental Health Day (10 October)</strong> — the one month the whole market is primed for this topic</td><td><strong>Flagship conversion window.</strong> Deliver 2–4 free W0 sessions on public stages; publish the October flagship content; capture every attendee into follow-up per <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code></td><td>W0 everywhere → W1 proposals to warm rooms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nov 2026</strong></td><td>Post-campaign momentum; Q4 pressure building; year-end deliverable crunch starting</td><td>Convert October warmth: send W1/W2/W3 proposals within two weeks of each W0; pitch \"end the year without burning out your team\" to managers</td><td>W2 (2-hour manager session)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dec 2026</strong></td><td>Year-end closure, holidays, reduced availability, fatigue peak</td><td><strong>Low outreach month by design</strong> (calendar closure theme). One or two gentle \"end-of-year reset\" W4 online sessions for those who can't switch off; plan Q1</td><td>W4 (light, reflective format)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Jan 2027</strong></td><td>New-year reset mindset; resolutions; fresh training budgets in some organizations (to verify per client)</td><td><strong>January reset campaign.</strong> \"Start the year with a workload structure that lasts\" — re-approach every warm contact from Q4; propose W5 retainer starts aligned to the new year</td><td>W1 + W5 retainer conversations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Feb 2027</strong></td><td>Steady work rhythm; short month; planning season for spring events</td><td>Deepen sectors: hospital-admin (W3) and SME-owner (W2) outreach; book spring university slots; first diaspora W4 paid cohort</td><td>W3 / W2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mar 2027</strong></td><td><strong>Ramadan-aware month</strong> (timing shifts by moon sighting — never state exact dates): adjusted hours, energy rhythms, iftar schedules for observing colleagues</td><td>Match the calendar's Ramadan-aware content: \"energy and focus during shifted schedules\" — a genuinely useful, culturally fluent angle. Offer short-format sessions only; respect reduced availability</td><td>W4 or compact W0 (\"energy rhythms\" edition)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Apr 2027</strong></td><td>Post-Ramadan/Eid-period return to full rhythm (timing shifts); spring energy; Q2 planning</td><td>Re-accelerate: deliver spring university talks; nonprofit/humanitarian organization staff-wellbeing pitches for new program cycles; follow up on Q1 pilots for conversion</td><td>W1 conversions; guest talks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>May 2027</strong></td><td>Pre-summer push; teams racing to close projects before summer slowdown</td><td>\"Finish strong without burning out\" campaign; last big in-person delivery window before summer; propose W5 retainers that start in September</td><td>W1 / W2; W5 pre-sold for autumn</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Jun 2027</strong></td><td>Wind-down beginning; exams season affects working parents; event calendars thinning</td><td>Close open proposals; deliver committed sessions early in the month; shift energy toward the online/diaspora track</td><td>W4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Jul 2027</strong></td><td>Summer/rest month (calendar theme); vacations; diaspora visiting Lebanon — a unique in-person window with GCC-based professionals</td><td><strong>Rest + one asymmetric bet:</strong> if diaspora relationships have matured, host one in-person meetup or session while diaspora professionals are in the country; otherwise genuinely rest and document year-one lessons</td><td>Light community event; no hard selling</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aug 2027</strong></td><td>Continued slowdown; September planning begins late-month</td><td>Build the Sept 2027 pipeline with a full year of proof: testimonials, photos, delivered-session count; renew W5 retainers; plan year two</td><td>Year-two W5 renewals and September bookings</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The single most important row is October 2026.</strong> WHO's World Mental Health Day (10 October) gives every HR manager, community organizer, and company page a reason to program this topic. Dorina's job in September is to make sure that when they look for someone local, bilingual, practical, and affordable — she is already in their inbox.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-partnership-ecosystem-map\">4. Partnership &amp; ecosystem map</h2>\n<p>Five tiers, ordered by how directly they convert. For every tier: what Dorina gives, what she gets, the first-touch move, and the risk to avoid. <strong>Standing rule for all tiers:</strong> Dorina never claims affiliation, partnership, or endorsement by any named organization. Named organizations below are ecosystem actors and <em>potential</em> partners to approach respectfully.</p>\n<h3 id=\"tier-1-professional-communities-hr-focused-associations-and-\">Tier 1 — Professional communities (HR-focused associations and communities)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Why this tier leads:</strong> HR professionals are the buyers. Notably, the HR Association of Lebanon appears publicly on the MoPH page as a partner of the NMHP's national \"Mental Health in the Workplace\" campaign — which signals that HR communities in Lebanon are already engaged with this exact topic. That makes HR communities a natural ally <em>category</em> (approach respectfully; no affiliation claims; specific communities and their current activity to verify).</li><li><strong>Give:</strong> free, high-quality W0 content for their member events; a bilingual speaker who shows up prepared; co-created literacy content their members can use.</li><li><strong>Get:</strong> direct access to a room full of buyers; credibility by association with professional bodies; recurring speaking slots.</li><li><strong>First-touch move:</strong> a short, respectful message to the community's events lead: \"I deliver a free 60-minute educational session on workplace wellbeing for professional communities — non-clinical, practical, bilingual. Could this fit one of your upcoming member events?\"</li><li><strong>Risk to avoid:</strong> implying the association endorses her or that she is affiliated with any national campaign. She is an independent educator offering member value, full stop.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"tier-2-nmhp-ecosystem-nonprofithumanitarian-organizations\">Tier 2 — NMHP-ecosystem nonprofit/humanitarian organizations</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Context:</strong> the Ministry of Public Health's National Mental Health Programme publishes awareness campaigns, anti-stigma material, and psychological first aid material, and its workplace campaign's partners are listed publicly (including ABAAD, ACTED, ESA, IMC, Médecins du Monde, SKOUN, alongside WHO and the HR Association of Lebanon). That public list is intelligence: it shows which organizations have invested in workplace mental health in Lebanon.</li><li><strong>Give:</strong> what a non-clinical educator can legitimately offer such organizations — volunteer workplace-literacy content (e.g., plain-language explainers for their staff-facing channels), facilitation of explicitly <em>non-clinical</em> segments at their events (logistics-savvy, bilingual, reliable), and amplification of their public anti-stigma materials to her audience.</li><li><strong>Get:</strong> ecosystem legitimacy; learning from organizations that navigate this space professionally; referral pathways for her own referral protocol (see <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>); possible future invitations to workplace-focused initiatives.</li><li><strong>First-touch move:</strong> attend their public events first; engage genuinely with their published campaigns on LinkedIn; only then send a modest offer of volunteer support in her defined lane. Ecosystem relationships are earned slowly.</li><li><strong>Risk to avoid:</strong> two failure modes — (1) overclaiming: presenting herself as connected to the NMHP or any partner; (2) scope drift: accepting a role in anything clinical or crisis-adjacent because an nonprofit/humanitarian organization asked. Both are declined per Section 8.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"tier-3-business-enablers-chambers-business-networks-coworkin\">Tier 3 — Business enablers (chambers, business networks, coworking spaces)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Give:</strong> free member programming (W0), a professional-development angle that differentiates the enabler's event calendar, and cross-promotion to her growing audience.</li><li><strong>Get:</strong> stages, SME-owner and startup-founder audiences, venue partnerships (a coworking space hosting her paid W1 in exchange for member discounts — structure to negotiate), and local business credibility.</li><li><strong>First-touch move:</strong> identify three business-community venues (categories: coworking spaces, business networks, chamber-type bodies — specific names to verify), visit in person where possible, and propose a free October session tied to World Mental Health Day.</li><li><strong>Risk to avoid:</strong> becoming permanent free entertainment. After two free sessions with any single enabler, the conversation moves to paid formats or venue-partnership terms per <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"tier-4-academia-aou-alumni-relations-and-guest-talks\">Tier 4 — Academia (AOU alumni relations and guest talks)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Give:</strong> an alumna success story the university can showcase; free guest talks for students and alumni on career-relevant wellbeing (workload, boundaries, sustainable early-career habits); willingness to appear in alumni communications.</li><li><strong>Get:</strong> an institutional stage with built-in trust; access to the AOU alumni network (her one <em>real</em>, activatable institutional network); photos and recordings that become authority assets; young-professional audience growth.</li><li><strong>First-touch move:</strong> email the AOU – Lebanon alumni-relations office as a graduate: brief bio, the positioning line, and an offer of a free talk for an alumni or student event — proposing the October window or a January \"new year, sustainable habits\" angle.</li><li><strong>Risk to avoid:</strong> letting talks drift toward student mental-health support, which is a wellbeing-services domain owned by professionals. Her talks are about <em>workplace</em> skills and habits; anything personal gets the referral protocol response.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"tier-5-media-podcasts-business-press-wellbeing-columns\">Tier 5 — Media (podcasts, business press wellbeing columns)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Give:</strong> an articulate, bilingual guest with a fresh angle — the executive assistant who became a workplace-wellbeing educator — and ready-made, evidence-anchored talking points (the WHO working-days-lost figure lands well in business media).</li><li><strong>Get:</strong> borrowed audiences, authority-by-platform, quotable assets for the LinkedIn calendar, and SEO-relevant mentions.</li><li><strong>First-touch move:</strong> list regional business podcasts and workplace/HR-adjacent media (specific outlets to verify); pitch a tight, specific episode idea (\"what admin professionals see about burnout that managers miss\") rather than a generic \"have me on.\"</li><li><strong>Risk to avoid:</strong> being framed as a mental-health <em>expert</em> in the clinical sense. She supplies her own framing in every pitch — \"workplace wellbeing educator, non-clinical\" — and gently corrects introductions that overstate it.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-her-unfair-advantages\">5. Her unfair advantages</h2>\n<p>Each advantage below maps to a concrete opportunity on this map — none is decorative.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>12+ years of admin insider knowledge — she IS the tribe.</strong> Every admin professional, EA, and office manager instantly recognizes her as one of their own. This makes <strong>W3</strong> the most defensible product in the portfolio (few competitors can teach admin-specific workload management with lived authority) and makes sectors 2a and 2e unusually receptive. Concrete play: W3 positioned to law firms and hospital admin departments as \"built by an EA, for the people who hold the office together.\"</li><li><strong>Bilingual Arabic/English delivery.</strong> She can run a session in English for a multinational-style agency, in Arabic for a family business, and mixed for everyone in between — and serve the diaspora (2j) in their own code-switching idiom. Concrete play: every proposal states \"delivered in Arabic, English, or both — your choice,\" a line most training vendors cannot honestly write.</li><li><strong>Marketing-company environment — content-production adjacency.</strong> She currently works inside a company whose business is content and campaigns (details unconfirmed — to be clarified with her). Even at the exposure level, she absorbs how professional content gets made, which compounds the <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code> execution. Concrete play: production polish on workshop materials and LinkedIn assets that outclasses typical solo trainers.</li><li><strong>The law-firm network.</strong> Lawyers and their clients form a referral web that touches SMEs (2h), banks' legal departments (2c), and other professional-services firms (2a). Concrete play: her first three W0 sessions and her first paid W1 most plausibly come from this network; every law-firm session ends with \"who else should hear this?\"</li><li><strong>From-zero authenticity as a story asset.</strong> Rebuilding public presence from near zero is not a weakness to hide — it is the narrative spine of the LinkedIn calendar: a mid-career professional building expertise in public, documenting the journey honestly. Audiences trust builders more than arrivers. Concrete play: the \"building in public\" thread makes early small wins (first talk, first workshop, first testimonial) into content, converting progress itself into authority.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-constraints-realities-panel\">6. Constraints &amp; realities panel</h2>\n<p>Stated honestly, as planning assumptions — the strategy is designed <em>around</em> these, not in denial of them.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Constraint (planning assumption)</th><th>Reality check</th><th>Strategy response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Economic pressure &amp; budget sensitivity</strong></td><td>Organizations in Lebanon operate under sustained economic pressure; training budgets are scrutinized; service pricing is commonly dollarized</td><td>Favor free pilots (W0) and modular, right-sized pricing per <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code>; sell productivity and retention, not luxury; keep W4 online pricing accessible; let GCC/diaspora revenue (2j) subsidize patience at home</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Power &amp; connectivity variability</strong></td><td>Electricity and internet reliability vary by location and provider</td><td>Hybrid-ready and low-tech delivery per <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code>: printed kits, no-projector fallback for every in-person session; for W4, backup power and a secondary connection on Dorina's side, plus recorded fallback for attendees</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stigma around mental-health topics</strong></td><td>Some buyers and attendees are wary of \"mental health\" framing at work</td><td>Lead with \"workload, energy, and focus\" language; introduce literacy vocabulary once the room is safe. National momentum helps: the NMHP's public anti-stigma work and its workplace campaign show the conversation is legitimized at the national level — Dorina rides that wave without claiming any part of it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Her employment status</strong></td><td>She is currently employed as an administrator at a marketing company (details unconfirmed); side activities can create conflicts</td><td><strong>Get clarity before public launch:</strong> employer awareness and, ideally, blessing; check any contractual restrictions; consider offering W0 internally first — turning the employer into the first reference instead of a risk; schedule external delivery outside working hours or via agreed arrangements. This is a gating item in Section 7</td></tr><tr><td><strong>From-zero network</strong></td><td>Public presence is being rebuilt from near zero; there is no waiting audience</td><td><strong>The first 90 days are seeding, not selling.</strong> Success in Sept–Nov 2026 is measured in conversations, stages, and list growth — not revenue. Premature selling to a cold market would burn the few warm contacts she has</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-clinical scope pressure</strong></td><td>Once visible, she will receive requests that drift toward counseling territory</td><td>Every engagement is qualified against Section 8; the graceful-decline script and <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> exist precisely for this</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-the-90-day-opportunity-shortlist-septnov-2026\">7. The 90-day opportunity shortlist — Sept–Nov 2026</h2>\n<p>Prioritized, concrete, and small enough to actually happen alongside a full-time job. Owner is Dorina unless noted; \"Success signal\" defines done.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Move</th><th>Timing</th><th>Effort</th><th>Success signal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Employer clarity conversation:</strong> confirm in writing (email suffices) that external workshop activity outside work hours is acceptable; explore offering W0 internally as a pilot — with the employer's blessing</td><td>Sept, week 1–2</td><td>Low</td><td>Written green light; internal W0 scheduled or explicitly parked</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>List 20 warm HR/decision-maker contacts</strong> from the law-firm and agency networks (ex-colleagues, office managers, partners, client contacts) into a simple tracking sheet</td><td>Sept, week 1–2</td><td>Low</td><td>20 names with role, org type, warmth rating, next step</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Personal reactivation messages to the top 10</strong> — not a pitch: a genuine reconnection note plus one line on the new direction (\"I'm building an education practice on workplace wellbeing — non-clinical, very practical\")</td><td>Sept, weeks 2–4</td><td>Medium</td><td>6+ replies; 3+ calls or coffees booked</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>AOU alumni-relations outreach:</strong> email the alumni office proposing a free guest talk (October World Mental Health Day angle, or a January-reset alternative)</td><td>Sept, week 2</td><td>Low</td><td>A reply and a slot under discussion</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Identify 3 coworking/business-community venues</strong> (names to verify on the ground) and propose a free October W0 tied to World Mental Health Day</td><td>Sept, weeks 2–3</td><td>Medium</td><td>At least 1 confirmed October stage</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Locate and approach HR-professional communities</strong> (noting the HR Association of Lebanon's publicly listed NMHP-campaign involvement as evidence this community cares about the topic — approach respectfully, no affiliation claims; current activity to verify) with a free member-session offer</td><td>Sept–Oct</td><td>Medium</td><td>1 conversation with a community events lead</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>October delivery sprint:</strong> deliver 2–4 free W0 sessions (employer-internal, coworking, community, or alumni), each with sign-up capture per <code>MARKETING_FUNNEL_AND_CAMPAIGNS.md</code></td><td>Oct</td><td>High</td><td>2+ sessions delivered; 30+ contacts captured; 2+ testimonials collected</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>World Mental Health Day content flagship:</strong> execute the October plan in <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>, anchored to WHO's 10 October observance</td><td>Oct, first two weeks</td><td>Medium</td><td>Flagship posts published; best-performing post identified for reuse</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><strong>Two-week conversion follow-up:</strong> every W0 host and every engaged attendee organization receives a tailored W1/W2/W3 proposal within 14 days of the session</td><td>Oct–Nov</td><td>Medium</td><td>3+ proposals sent; 1+ serious paid conversation</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><strong>Map 5 nonprofit/humanitarian organization HR/staff-care contacts</strong> (drawing on the publicly listed NMHP-campaign partner categories as a signal of which organization types engage with workplace mental health) for Q1 2027 outreach — research and list only, no pitching yet</td><td>Nov</td><td>Medium</td><td>5 named organizations with the right contact role identified</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><strong>Diaspora beachhead:</strong> schedule one free W4 online session for a diaspora/GCC audience in late November or January; recruit via LinkedIn and any AOU-alumni-abroad channels (to verify)</td><td>Nov</td><td>Medium</td><td>Date set; 15+ registrations</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><strong>90-day review:</strong> score every sector touched against the Section 1 rubric with real data; pick the two conversion bets for Dec–Feb; update this map</td><td>Nov, last week</td><td>Low</td><td>A one-page review; two named priority sectors for Q1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Sequencing logic:</strong> items 1–2 gate everything (employer clarity and the contact base); 3–6 build September's stages; 7–9 are the October engine and November harvest; 10–12 set up Q1 2027.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-opportunity-qualification-checklist\">8. Opportunity qualification checklist</h2>\n<p>Before saying <strong>yes</strong> to any engagement — paid or free — run this checklist. A single hard \"no\" on items 1–3 means decline or renegotiate.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Audience fit.</strong> Is the room made of working professionals attending in a workplace/professional-development context? (Not: patients, therapy-seeking groups, minors, or clinical populations.)</li><li><strong>Non-clinical scope confirmed in writing.</strong> Does the written agreement/email trail describe the session as <em>education on workplace wellbeing</em> — and has the host seen the standing disclaimer? If the host's promotional copy says \"therapy,\" \"counseling,\" \"healing,\" or \"mental health support session,\" it gets corrected before Dorina appears under it.</li><li><strong>Referral protocol shareable.</strong> Can she share <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> (or its client-facing summary) with the host, including the helpline placeholder — [TO VERIFY before use: current national emotional-support helpline number — check the Ministry of Public Health / National Mental Health Programme site] — and is the host comfortable with it? A host who resists the safety framing is a red flag.</li><li><strong>Decision-maker engaged.</strong> Is the person who can approve payment, scheduling, and follow-on work in the conversation — not only an enthusiastic intermediary?</li><li><strong>Logistics feasible.</strong> Venue, timing outside employment conflicts, power/connectivity fallback for the format, room setup, and (for W4) platform tested? Per <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code> checklists.</li><li><strong>Strategic value if unpaid.</strong> If free: does it deliver at least two of — a target-sector audience, a testimonial opportunity, content assets, a referral path? Free work with none of these is politely declined; her calendar is the scarcest asset she owns.</li><li><strong>Story safety.</strong> Nothing about the engagement pressures her to present as a clinician, share attendee disclosures, or handle personal crises in the room.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"graceful-decline-script-out-of-scope-requests\">Graceful-decline script (out-of-scope requests)</h3>\n<p>For requests that drift clinical — \"can you run a group therapy-style session,\" \"our employee is struggling, can you talk to her,\" \"can you assess our team's mental health\":</p>\n<blockquote><p>\"Thank you for trusting me with this — it tells me you genuinely care about your people. What you're describing needs a qualified mental-health professional, and that's outside my scope: I'm an educator, not a clinician, and your team deserves the right kind of support. What I <em>can</em> do is [the in-scope alternative: a workshop on stress literacy and workload structure / a session that teaches teams how and when to seek help]. And I can point you toward finding qualified professional support — my referral protocol covers exactly this.\"</p></blockquote>\n<p>Then follow <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>: refer, don't rescue; document the exchange; never name-drop specific clinicians as \"partners\" without an actual agreement. Arabic soft opener where useful: <strong>\"هيدا الموضوع بيستاهل شخص مختصّ\"</strong> — <em>\"this matter deserves a specialist\"</em> — which declines while honoring the request.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-opportunity-specific\">What we still need from Dorina (opportunity-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Employer situation, precisely:</strong> company name, role scope, contract clauses on outside work, and her read on whether an internal W0 pilot is welcome — this gates shortlist items 1 and 7.</li><li><strong>Her law-firm network inventory:</strong> which firms, which people, current warmth of each relationship, and any contacts now in HR, management, or SME-owner roles.</li><li><strong>AOU specifics:</strong> graduation year, campus, any existing alumni-office contact, and whether she knows of alumni chapters abroad (for the diaspora play).</li><li><strong>Delivery constraints:</strong> which days/evenings are realistically available for external sessions; comfort level presenting in Arabic vs. English vs. mixed; access to a reliable-power, reliable-internet location for W4 delivery.</li><li><strong>Geographic range:</strong> which areas of Lebanon she can serve in person without unreasonable travel cost, and whether she has any GCC contacts (relatives, former colleagues, classmates) for the diaspora beachhead.</li><li><strong>Existing assets:</strong> any references, letters, or testimonials from the law-firm years that can be refreshed; any professional photos; any prior public speaking, however small.</li><li><strong>Risk appetite and capacity:</strong> honest hours-per-week available for the 90-day shortlist, so we cut it to fit reality rather than pretending.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"sources-6\">Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a> (workplace risk factors; ~60% of the world's population in work; 15% of working-age adults estimated to have a mental disorder in 2019; ~12 billion working days lost per year to depression and anxiety ≈ US$1 trillion/year in lost productivity; recommended actions including manager training, literacy/awareness, and stress-management skills).</li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a> (mental health as a basic human right; the continuum shaped by individual, family, community, and structural factors; the care gap; workplace support via legislation, policies, manager training, and targeted interventions).</li><li>Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a> (NMHP under MoPH; awareness campaigns, anti-stigma and psychological first aid materials; national \"Mental Health in the Workplace\" campaign, slogan \"Don't miss the opportunity / ما تضيعوا الفرصة\", with publicly listed partners including WHO, ABAAD, ACTED, ESA, HR Association of Lebanon, IMC, Médecins du Monde, SKOUN).</li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a> (observed 10 October).</li></ol>\n<p><em>All Lebanon-market specifics beyond these sources (organization activity, venues, communities, budgets, salaries) are planning assumptions or items marked \"to verify\" — they must be confirmed on the ground before being used in client-facing materials. No affiliation, partnership, or endorsement by any organization named in this document is claimed or implied.</em></p>","toc":[{"id":"1-how-to-read-this-map","text":"1. How to read this map","lvl":2},{"id":"scoring-rubric-used-consistently-in-section-2","text":"Scoring rubric (used consistently in Section 2)","lvl":3},{"id":"2-sector-map","text":"2. Sector map","lvl":2},{"id":"2a-law-firms-professional-services-her-home-turf","text":"2a. Law firms & professional services — her home turf","lvl":3},{"id":"2b-marketing-creative-media-agencies-her-current-world","text":"2b. Marketing, creative & media agencies — her current world","lvl":3},{"id":"2c-banks-financial-institutions","text":"2c. Banks & financial institutions","lvl":3},{"id":"2d-nonprofithumanitarian-organizations-humanitariandevelopme","text":"2d. nonprofit/humanitarian organizations & humanitarian/development organizations","lvl":3},{"id":"2e-hospitals-clinics-administrative-and-non-clinical-staff-o","text":"2e. Hospitals & clinics — administrative and non-clinical staff ONLY","lvl":3},{"id":"2f-universities-schools-staff-wellbeing-the-aou-network","text":"2f. Universities & schools — staff wellbeing + the AOU network","lvl":3},{"id":"2g-tech-startups-remote-first-companies","text":"2g. Tech startups & remote-first companies","lvl":3},{"id":"2h-smes-family-businesses","text":"2h. SMEs & family businesses","lvl":3},{"id":"2i-coworking-spaces-business-communities-professional-syndic","text":"2i. Coworking spaces, business communities & professional syndicates/associations","lvl":3},{"id":"2j-diaspora-gcc-remote-market-w4-online","text":"2j. Diaspora & GCC remote market — W4 online","lvl":3},{"id":"sector-map-at-a-glance","text":"Sector map at a glance","lvl":3},{"id":"3-seasonal-opportunity-calendar-sept-2026-aug-2027","text":"3. Seasonal opportunity calendar — Sept 2026 → Aug 2027","lvl":2},{"id":"4-partnership-ecosystem-map","text":"4. Partnership & ecosystem map","lvl":2},{"id":"tier-1-professional-communities-hr-focused-associations-and-","text":"Tier 1 — Professional communities (HR-focused associations and communities)","lvl":3},{"id":"tier-2-nmhp-ecosystem-nonprofithumanitarian-organizations","text":"Tier 2 — NMHP-ecosystem nonprofit/humanitarian organizations","lvl":3},{"id":"tier-3-business-enablers-chambers-business-networks-coworkin","text":"Tier 3 — Business enablers (chambers, business networks, coworking spaces)","lvl":3},{"id":"tier-4-academia-aou-alumni-relations-and-guest-talks","text":"Tier 4 — Academia (AOU alumni relations and guest talks)","lvl":3},{"id":"tier-5-media-podcasts-business-press-wellbeing-columns","text":"Tier 5 — Media (podcasts, business press wellbeing columns)","lvl":3},{"id":"5-her-unfair-advantages","text":"5. Her unfair advantages","lvl":2},{"id":"6-constraints-realities-panel","text":"6. Constraints & realities panel","lvl":2},{"id":"7-the-90-day-opportunity-shortlist-septnov-2026","text":"7. The 90-day opportunity shortlist — Sept–Nov 2026","lvl":2},{"id":"8-opportunity-qualification-checklist","text":"8. Opportunity qualification checklist","lvl":2},{"id":"graceful-decline-script-out-of-scope-requests","text":"Graceful-decline script (out-of-scope requests)","lvl":3},{"id":"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-opportunity-specific","text":"What we still need from Dorina (opportunity-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"sources-6","text":"Sources","lvl":2}],"words":6498,"mins":28},{"slug":"content-approval-and-automation-workflow","file":"CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md","title":"Approval & Automation Workflow","blurb":"Eleven statuses, two approval gates, weekly rhythm, audit log, emergency procedures.","track":"Governance","n":12,"html":"<h1 id=\"content-approval-automation-workflow-dorina-salman\">Content Approval &amp; Automation Workflow — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> This document is the operating system for Dorina's content engine: a pipeline that lets drafting, design, and scheduling scale — with human help or AI assistance — while Dorina retains 100% control over everything that reaches the public. It defines the statuses, gates, checklists, rhythms, tools, logs, and emergency procedures that make three LinkedIn posts a week sustainable for a busy professional without ever compromising the non-clinical boundary or her voice. The absolute law of this workflow: <strong>nothing is published, scheduled-to-publish, or sent externally without Dorina's explicit approval of BOTH the final text AND the final graphic.</strong></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-operating-principles\">1. Operating Principles</h2>\n<p>Five principles govern every decision in this workflow. When in doubt, resolve any conflict in the order listed — safety and approval always beat speed.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Approval-first, always.</strong> Automation and assistants accelerate <em>drafting, formatting, and scheduling of approved items only</em>. No tool, assistant, or \"time-saving shortcut\" ever publishes, schedules-to-publish, or sends anything externally that Dorina has not explicitly approved in both its final text and its final graphic. There is no exception for \"small edits\", \"urgent posts\", or \"it's basically the same as last week's\".</li><li><strong>Draft fast, approve deliberately.</strong> Speed lives upstream. Ideas can be generated in bulk, drafts can be produced in minutes with AI assistance (see <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code>), and visuals can be templated. The two approval gates are the only deliberately slow points in the pipeline — and that is by design. A post that misses its slot is a minor inconvenience; a post that misrepresents Dorina's non-clinical positioning is a reputational and ethical problem.</li><li><strong>One calendar is the single source of truth.</strong> <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code> (and its live working copy in the shared spreadsheet or board) is the only authoritative record of what is planned, for when, and in what status. If a post is not on the calendar, it does not exist. If two records disagree, the calendar wins and the discrepancy is fixed the same day.</li><li><strong>Everything is auditable.</strong> Every item carries an ID from idea to archive. Every approval is dated and attributable. Every published post can be traced back to who drafted it, when the safety check ran, when Dorina approved text and visual, and when it went live. The approval log (Section 7) is not bureaucracy — it is Dorina's protection and her learning record.</li><li><strong>Safety gates are non-negotiable and cannot be skipped — even when late.</strong> The automated boundary/fact check (P26 in <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code>) plus a human read happen on every item, every time. If the pipeline is running behind, the correct response is to publish later or skip a slot — never to bypass a gate. A missed Tuesday post has zero long-term cost. A boundary breach can undo months of authority-building.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>How to use this document:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Read Sections 1–4 in full before the first post moves through the pipeline; they are the constitution.</li><li>Sections 5–7 are the day-to-day manual — print or pin the weekly rhythm table and the two gate checklists.</li><li>Sections 8–9 are the \"break glass\" pages: skim them now, know where they are, and rehearse the STOP protocol once (Section 9.1) before any scheduling tool is adopted.</li><li>Section 10 is for the monthly and quarterly sessions only.</li><li>Wherever this document and a sibling document overlap, the more restrictive rule wins; where safety is concerned, <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> always wins.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-roles-permissions\">2. Roles &amp; Permissions</h2>\n<p>The workflow defines four roles. At launch, Dorina plus one assistant (human, or Dorina working with her AI assistant) can hold all of them; the roles still matter because they define <em>which hat is being worn</em> at each step, and which combinations are never acceptable.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Held by</th><th>May do</th><th>May NEVER do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Approver &amp; Voice Owner</strong></td><td>Dorina, and only Dorina</td><td>Approve/reject text (Gate 1); approve/reject visuals (Gate 2); own the publish/schedule decision; edit anything at any stage; veto anything at any stage</td><td>Delegate approval; pre-approve unseen future content (\"approve the whole month sight unseen\" is not approval)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Content Assistant</strong></td><td>A human assistant, or Dorina/assistant working with an AI assistant</td><td>Capture ideas; write briefs; draft posts using <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code>; run the automated safety check; format approved text; load approved items into the scheduling tool; maintain the calendar and approval log</td><td>Approve anything; edit text <em>after</em> Gate 1 approval without sending it back through Gate 1; touch the publish button on unapproved content; reply to sensitive/urgent inbound messages (Section 8)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Designer</strong></td><td>A human designer, or an AI-assisted design step operated by the Content Assistant</td><td>Produce visuals from approved text only; apply the brand look; write alt-text drafts</td><td>Start design before Gate 1 text approval (wasted work and version-drift risk); alter approved wording to \"fit the layout\" without triggering a Gate 1 re-check</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Safety Gate</strong></td><td>Automated check (P26 boundary/fact compliance checker in <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code>) <strong>plus</strong> one human eye</td><td>Flag boundary violations, clinical drift, unsourced statistics, missing disclaimers; block progression to Gate 1 until issues are resolved</td><td>Approve. A pass from the safety gate is a <em>precondition</em> for Dorina's review, never a substitute for it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Acceptable role combinations:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Dorina may draft AND approve her own content. Approving her own words is the normal case, not a conflict — the safety gate still runs on everything, including Dorina's own drafts, because everyone (including the expert) occasionally drifts.</li><li>One assistant may hold Content Assistant + Designer + safety-gate <em>operator</em> (running P26 and doing the human read) simultaneously.</li><li><strong>An assistant may never approve.</strong> Not \"approve small ones\", not \"approve reposts\", not \"approve while Dorina is travelling\". If Dorina is unavailable, content waits. The calendar is built with enough lead time (Section 5) that a week of unavailability never creates pressure to break this rule.</li><li>An AI assistant may hold no role beyond producing drafts, formats, checks, and summaries <em>for a human to act on</em>. AI output is always input to a human step, never a step of its own.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-pipeline\">3. The Pipeline</h2>\n<p>Every content item moves through eleven statuses. The two bold statuses are <strong>Dorina's gates</strong> — the only two points where an item acquires the right to move toward the public.</p>\n<p><strong>Happy path:</strong> IDEA → BRIEFED → DRAFTED → SAFETY-CHECKED → <strong>TEXT-APPROVED (Gate 1)</strong> → DESIGNED → <strong>VISUAL-APPROVED (Gate 2)</strong> → SCHEDULED → PUBLISHED → MEASURED → ARCHIVED</p>\n<p><strong>Rework loops:</strong> From Gate 1 or Gate 2, Dorina may return an item to <strong>REVISION-REQUESTED</strong>, with a note saying what to fix and which status it returns to.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Status</th><th>Who acts</th><th>\"Done\" means</th><th>Max dwell time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>IDEA</strong></td><td>Anyone (Dorina, assistant, AI-suggested from the calendar's monthly themes)</td><td>Idea captured in the calendar/board with one line, a pillar tag (stress &amp; burnout literacy / systems &amp; boundaries / story &amp; advocacy / workplace culture &amp; managers), and a DESK Method (proposed) stage if relevant</td><td>Open-ended; ideas can sit in the backlog indefinitely, but the backlog is pruned monthly</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>BRIEFED</strong></td><td>Content Assistant</td><td>Brief written: target post date, pillar, format (per <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code>), key message, CTA rung, language(s) (EN / AR / bilingual), which prompt from <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code> will be used</td><td>2 weeks before target post date</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>DRAFTED</strong></td><td>Content Assistant (AI-assisted drafting allowed and encouraged)</td><td>Full text drafted in final language(s), disclaimer included where the brief requires it, hashtags and CTA written</td><td>10 days before target post date</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>SAFETY-CHECKED</strong></td><td>Safety Gate: P26 automated check + human read by the Content Assistant</td><td>P26 run and result logged (pass, or issues fixed and re-run to pass); human eye confirms no clinical language, no statistics outside the four approved sources, no forbidden claims</td><td>Within 24 hours of DRAFTED</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>TEXT-APPROVED (Gate 1)</strong></td><td><strong>Dorina</strong></td><td>Dorina has read the full final text (including Arabic text, hashtags, CTA, and disclaimer) against the Gate 1 checklist (Section 4) and explicitly approved it in writing (a dated \"Approved\" in the calendar/log). Or: REVISION-REQUESTED with a note → back to DRAFTED</td><td>Dorina reviews within her Sunday batch (Section 5); items should reach this gate ≥7 days before target date</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>DESIGNED</strong></td><td>Designer (human or AI-assisted step)</td><td>Visual produced from the <em>approved</em> text, in the brand look, correct dimensions for LinkedIn, alt-text drafted</td><td>5 days before target post date</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>VISUAL-APPROVED (Gate 2)</strong></td><td><strong>Dorina</strong></td><td>Dorina has seen the final graphic at actual size (including a phone-screen check) against the Gate 2 checklist (Section 4) and explicitly approved it in writing. Or: REVISION-REQUESTED with a note → back to DESIGNED (or to DRAFTED if the text itself needs changing — which then re-triggers Gate 1)</td><td>Same Sunday batch; ≥3 days before target date</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>SCHEDULED</strong></td><td>Content Assistant</td><td>The doubly-approved item (text + visual, exactly as approved, byte-for-byte) is loaded into the scheduling tool or the manual-posting checklist, with date/time per <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code> posting windows</td><td>Loaded within 48 hours of Gate 2 approval</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><strong>PUBLISHED</strong></td><td>Scheduling tool fires, or assistant posts manually; assistant verifies the post is live and correct within 2 hours of the slot</td><td>Live post matches the approved version exactly; URL captured in the log</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><strong>MEASURED</strong></td><td>Content Assistant</td><td>KPIs per <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code> recorded at day 7 (impressions, engagements, comments, profile actions as available)</td><td>Day 7 after publishing</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><strong>ARCHIVED</strong></td><td>Content Assistant</td><td>Final text, final visual, metrics, and log entry stored in the shared folder archive; strong performers tagged for the reuse/repurposing pool</td><td>End of the month</td></tr><tr><td>—</td><td><strong>REVISION-REQUESTED</strong></td><td>Content Assistant / Designer act on Dorina's note</td><td>The specific issues Dorina flagged are fixed; the item re-enters at the status Dorina specified (DRAFTED items re-run the safety check before returning to Gate 1)</td><td>72 hours per rework loop</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Three rules that keep the pipeline honest:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>No skipping.</strong> An item may never jump a status. In particular: nothing enters DESIGNED without a dated Gate 1 approval, and nothing enters SCHEDULED without a dated Gate 2 approval.</li><li><strong>Any change resets the gate.</strong> If text changes after Gate 1 — even one word, even \"just the hashtag\" — the item goes back through SAFETY-CHECKED and Gate 1. If a visual changes after Gate 2, it goes back through Gate 2. Approval attaches to an exact version, not to an item in general.</li><li><strong>Dwell-time breaches degrade gracefully.</strong> If an item misses a dwell target, the fallback is: move it to a later slot, or promote a ready evergreen item from the approved reserve (Section 5). The fallback is never \"compress the gates\".</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"a-worked-example-one-post-end-to-end\">A worked example — one post, end to end</h3>\n<p>To make the pipeline concrete, here is a typical item's journey (dates illustrative):</p>\n<ol><li><strong>IDEA (Sept 1).</strong> The calendar's September theme includes a systems &amp; boundaries post. The assistant logs <code>DS-2026-09-004</code>: \"The meeting-free hour — one small boundary that survives a busy week\", pillar: systems &amp; boundaries, DESK stage: Establish.</li><li><strong>BRIEFED (Sept 3).</strong> Brief written: target date Tuesday Sept 15, bilingual (EN body, AR pull-quote on the visual), text-plus-graphic format, CTA rung: soft (\"What's the smallest boundary that has actually held for you?\"), drafting prompt selected from <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code>.</li><li><strong>DRAFTED (Sept 5).</strong> The assistant produces the draft with AI assistance, including hashtags and the Arabic pull-quote. No statistics are needed for this post, so no source citations arise; the disclaimer is not required per the brief (a pure how-to post, no distress content).</li><li><strong>SAFETY-CHECKED (Sept 6).</strong> P26 is run: PASS. The assistant's human read confirms no clinical drift; result and date logged.</li><li><strong>TEXT-APPROVED — Gate 1 (Sunday Sept 6).</strong> In the batch session, Dorina reads the full text against the Gate 1 checklist. She tightens one sentence herself (owner-edits at the gate are fine — the approved version is what she signs off). \"TEXT-APPROVED — 06 Sep — DS\" goes into the log.</li><li><strong>DESIGNED (Sept 8).</strong> The designer builds the graphic from the approved text: the Arabic pull-quote, brand colors, alt-text drafted. File saved as <code>DS-2026-09-004_APPROVED-pending-visual</code>.</li><li><strong>VISUAL-APPROVED — Gate 2 (Sunday Sept 13).</strong> Dorina checks the graphic at phone size, verifies her name in both scripts and the exact match of the pull-quote to the approved text. \"VISUAL-APPROVED — 13 Sep — DS\" logged.</li><li><strong>SCHEDULED (Sept 13).</strong> The assistant loads the doubly-approved item for Tuesday Sept 15, 8:30 a.m. per the posting windows in <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code>.</li><li><strong>PUBLISHED (Sept 15).</strong> The post goes live; the assistant verifies it within two hours and pastes the URL into the log.</li><li><strong>MEASURED (Sept 22).</strong> Day-7 KPIs recorded. The post over-performs on comments; the assistant tags it \"strong — boundary micro-habit angle\" for the retro.</li><li><strong>ARCHIVED (end of Sept).</strong> Final text, final visual, metrics, and notes stored under the item ID; the angle is flagged for the repurposing pool.</li></ol>\n<p>Total Dorina time on this item: roughly ten minutes across two Sunday sessions. That is the whole point of the pipeline — her attention is spent only at the two moments where it is irreplaceable.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-approval-checklists-use-verbatim-at-each-gate\">4. Approval Checklists (Use Verbatim at Each Gate)</h2>\n<p>These are Dorina's working checklists. They should be pasted into the calendar/board as checklist fields so each approval is a matter of ticking through, not remembering.</p>\n<h3 id=\"41-gate-1-text-approval-checklist-dorina-before-any-design-w\">4.1 Gate 1 — Text Approval Checklist (Dorina, before any design work starts)</h3>\n<p>Approve only when every line is a yes:</p>\n<ul><li>[ ] <strong>Voice match.</strong> This sounds like me — warm, direct, practical, educator-not-lecturer. I would say this sentence out loud to a room of Lebanese professionals.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Pillar fit.</strong> The post clearly belongs to one of my four pillars (stress &amp; burnout literacy, systems &amp; boundaries, story &amp; advocacy, workplace culture &amp; managers) and, where relevant, maps cleanly to a DESK Method (proposed) stage (Detect, Establish, Support, Keep) without forcing it.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Boundary compliance.</strong> Zero clinical positioning. No language from the prohibited list in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>: nothing that diagnoses, treats, promises therapeutic outcomes, or frames me as a therapist/psychologist/counselor/clinician. Educator and advocate only.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Facts from the four sources only.</strong> Every statistic and factual claim traces to the WHO mental health at work fact sheet, the WHO \"strengthening our response\" fact sheet, the Lebanon MoPH National Mental Health Programme page, or the WHO World Mental Health Day page — and is stated accurately. If a number appears and I cannot name its source from those four, the post fails.</li><li>[ ] <strong>P26 safety check logged as PASS</strong>, dated, before this text reached me. (If there is no logged pass, I stop here and send it back — not because the text is necessarily wrong, but because the gate order is the system.)</li><li>[ ] <strong>Arabic accuracy and register</strong> (for Arabic or bilingual posts). The Arabic is correct, natural, and in the right register for Lebanese professional readers — not a stiff literal translation of the English. Names, terms, and the DESK Method (proposed) rendering are consistent with previous approved posts.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Disclaimer present where needed.</strong> Any post touching personal struggle, symptoms, or distress carries the standing short-form disclaimer: <em>\"Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.\"</em> Pure how-to/culture posts may omit it per the rules in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</li><li>[ ] <strong>CTA matches the authority-ladder rung.</strong> The ask is appropriate to where my audience currently is on the ladder in <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code> — no premature selling, no mismatch between a soft educational post and a hard commercial ask.</li><li>[ ] <strong>No forbidden claims.</strong> No outcome guarantees (\"this will eliminate your burnout\"), no cure/fix framing, no implied clinical results, no exaggeration of my credentials, and no unnamed-but-identifiable stories about real workplaces or people without consent.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Practical read-through.</strong> Hook works in the first two lines; length and formatting suit LinkedIn; hashtags are the approved set; the target date and time still make sense.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> write \"TEXT-APPROVED — [date] — [initials]\" in the log, or \"REVISION-REQUESTED — [date] — [what to fix]\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"42-gate-2-visual-approval-checklist-dorina-before-anything-i\">4.2 Gate 2 — Visual Approval Checklist (Dorina, before anything is scheduled)</h3>\n<p>Approve only when every line is a yes:</p>\n<ul><li>[ ] <strong>My name is spelled correctly</strong> in English (Dorina Salman) and, where it appears, in Arabic — checked character by character, in every place it appears on the graphic.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Text-on-image matches the approved text exactly.</strong> Any quote, statistic, headline, or phrase on the graphic is identical to the Gate 1–approved wording. No paraphrasing by the design step. (If the designer needed to shorten a line, the shortened line came back through me.)</li><li>[ ] <strong>Readable on mobile.</strong> I have viewed the graphic at phone size. Every word is legible without zooming; Arabic text renders correctly (right-to-left, correct letter joining, no broken glyphs).</li><li>[ ] <strong>Brand look.</strong> Colors, fonts, and layout match my brand kit; the visual would sit comfortably next to my last ten posts in the profile grid.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Alt-text written</strong> and accurate — it describes the image and includes any text shown on it, in the post's language(s).</li><li>[ ] <strong>Dignified imagery — no stocky clichés.</strong> No head-clutching, no dark-storm-cloud-over-silhouette, no dramatic despair imagery. My visual language is calm, respectful, and hopeful: real workplaces, clean typography, light, steady colors. People experiencing stress are portrayed (if at all) with dignity, never as broken.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Disclaimer/attribution placement</strong> (where the format requires it) is present and legible but not dominating.</li><li>[ ] <strong>Technical check.</strong> Correct dimensions for the LinkedIn format, no compression artifacts, file named with the item ID.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> write \"VISUAL-APPROVED — [date] — [initials]\" in the log, or \"REVISION-REQUESTED — [date] — [what to fix] — [returns to DESIGNED or DRAFTED]\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"43-quick-reference-the-most-common-rejection-reasons-train-t\">4.3 Quick reference — the most common rejection reasons (train the upstream steps on these)</h3>\n<p>These lists exist so the assistant and designer can self-check before an item ever reaches Dorina, keeping her gate time short.</p>\n<p><strong>Most common Gate 1 rejections to pre-empt:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Sounds like generic wellbeing content — could have been written by anyone; missing Dorina's specificity, Lebanese workplace context, or DESK Method (proposed) anchoring.</li><li>A statistic slipped in from outside the four approved sources (often via AI drafting — this is exactly what P26 exists to catch, and why the human read backs it up).</li><li>Clinical drift in single words: \"symptoms\", \"treat\", \"heal\", \"diagnose\", \"therapy-adjacent\" phrasing used loosely. See the prohibited list in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</li><li>CTA too heavy for the post's rung — an educational post ending in a hard commercial ask.</li><li>Arabic register off — technically correct but stiff, or a term rendered inconsistently with earlier posts.</li><li>Missing disclaimer on a post that brushes personal struggle.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Most common Gate 2 rejections to pre-empt:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Text on the graphic paraphrases (rather than reproduces) the approved wording.</li><li>Arabic rendering broken by the design tool: disconnected letters, wrong direction, flipped punctuation.</li><li>Type too small for a phone screen, or low contrast against the background.</li><li>A stock image that dramatizes distress — the head-in-hands cliché in any variation.</li><li>Alt-text missing, or written for the wrong language version.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-weekly-operating-rhythm\">5. Weekly Operating Rhythm</h2>\n<p>This rhythm assumes a busy professional with roughly 2.5–3 focused hours per week for content operations. The pipeline's lead times (Section 3) exist precisely so this rhythm never becomes frantic.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Duration</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Sunday</strong></td><td>60–90 min</td><td><strong>Batch approval session.</strong> Dorina reviews next week's three posts: runs Gate 1 on any texts waiting, runs Gate 2 on any visuals waiting, writes revision notes for anything not approved. Target output: all three of next week's posts doubly approved and handed to SCHEDULED. Then a 10-minute look one week further ahead to unblock briefs and drafts early.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mon–Fri (daily)</strong></td><td>15 min</td><td><strong>Engagement window</strong> per <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code>: reply to routine comments on recent posts, engage with target-audience content, note any inbound message that needs triage (Section 8). This is the one daily touch; everything else is batched.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Friday</strong></td><td>15 min</td><td><strong>Metrics glance.</strong> Content Assistant (or Dorina) records day-7 KPIs for posts due for measurement, flags anything unusual (a breakout post, an underperformer, a comment thread that needs Dorina personally). No deep analysis — that is monthly.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Monthly</strong> (first Sunday, extending the batch session)</td><td>90 min</td><td><strong>Calendar planning session</strong> using <code>12_MONTH_LINKEDIN_CONTENT_CALENDAR.md</code>: confirm next month's themes and post briefs, prune the idea backlog, review the monthly retro (Section 10), adjust briefs based on what the metrics said.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quarterly</strong></td><td>Half a day</td><td><strong>Strategy review</strong>: pillars balance vs the 30/30/25/15 target, authority-ladder progress, KPI trends vs <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code> targets, whether to change tooling level (Section 6), and a read-through of this workflow document for updates (Section 10).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The evergreen reserve.</strong> The Content Assistant maintains 3–5 fully doubly-approved, non-time-sensitive posts as a reserve. When travel, illness, or a heavy work week breaks the rhythm, the reserve fills slots without any pressure to rush a gate. The reserve is replenished during normal Sunday sessions, never as an emergency scramble. Reserve items are re-glanced by Dorina before use if they are more than a quarter old (context can shift).</p>\n<p><strong>If Dorina cannot do the Sunday session:</strong> the week's unapproved items simply do not publish. Reserve items (already approved) may run instead, or slots go quiet. An empty slot is always acceptable; a bypassed gate never is.</p>\n<h3 id=\"suggested-sunday-session-agenda-75-minutes\">Suggested Sunday session agenda (75 minutes)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Activity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0–5</td><td>Open the calendar/board; confirm which items are waiting at each gate; scheduling-tool reconciliation check if at Level 3 (queue vs approval log, line by line)</td></tr><tr><td>5–35</td><td><strong>Gate 1 pass:</strong> read each waiting text in full against the checklist; approve, owner-edit-and-approve, or write a revision note. Three posts typically take 8–10 minutes each</td></tr><tr><td>35–55</td><td><strong>Gate 2 pass:</strong> review each waiting visual at desktop and phone size against the checklist; approve or write a revision note</td></tr><tr><td>55–65</td><td>Confirm next week's slots are fully covered (doubly-approved or reserve-backed); flag any gaps to the assistant</td></tr><tr><td>65–75</td><td>Look one week further ahead: unblock briefs, answer the assistant's open questions, top up the evergreen reserve if a slot in the session remains</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The session works best as a protected, recurring calendar block with notifications off. If it routinely overruns 90 minutes, that is a signal to raise at the next quarterly review — usually it means drafts are arriving at the gate under-cooked, which is a prompt-library or briefing problem, not a Dorina problem.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-tooling-patterns-tools-agnostic\">6. Tooling Patterns (Tools-Agnostic)</h2>\n<p>The workflow runs identically at three implementation levels. Start at Level 1; move up only when a migration trigger fires. No tool is ever named here on purpose — any product in each generic category works, and switching products never changes the workflow.</p>\n<h3 id=\"level-1-minimal-shared-spreadsheet-cloud-folder-manual-posti\">Level 1 — Minimal: shared spreadsheet + cloud folder + manual posting</h3>\n<p>The right starting point for Dorina + one assistant. Total tool cost: usually zero.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A shared spreadsheet</strong> is the calendar, the pipeline tracker, and the approval log in one. One row per content item, with these columns:</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item ID</td><td>Sequential, e.g., <code>DS-2026-09-001</code></td></tr><tr><td>Target date &amp; slot</td><td>Per the calendar</td></tr><tr><td>Pillar</td><td>One of the four pillars</td></tr><tr><td>DESK stage</td><td>Detect / Establish / Support / Keep / n/a</td></tr><tr><td>Language</td><td>EN / AR / bilingual</td></tr><tr><td>Format &amp; CTA rung</td><td>Per <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>One of the eleven pipeline statuses (data-validated dropdown)</td></tr><tr><td>Drafter</td><td>Who/what produced the draft</td></tr><tr><td>Safety check (P26)</td><td>PASS + date, or issue summary</td></tr><tr><td>Text approved</td><td>Date + Dorina's initials (blank until Gate 1)</td></tr><tr><td>Visual approved</td><td>Date + Dorina's initials (blank until Gate 2)</td></tr><tr><td>Scheduled for</td><td>Confirmed date/time</td></tr><tr><td>Published URL</td><td>Filled after go-live verification</td></tr><tr><td>Day-7 metrics</td><td>Impressions / engagements / comments / profile actions</td></tr><tr><td>Notes</td><td>Revision notes, learnings, incident references</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>A shared cloud folder</strong> holds one subfolder per item ID containing the brief, draft versions, the approved final text (clearly named <code>APPROVED-TEXT</code>), design files, and the approved final graphic (<code>APPROVED-VISUAL</code>). Only files with the <code>APPROVED-</code> prefix may ever be posted.</li><li><strong>Manual posting</strong> follows this exact checklist every time (pin it wherever the assistant works):</li></ul>\n<ol><li>Open the item's row in the spreadsheet; confirm both approval dates are present. If either is blank, stop — do not post, message Dorina.</li><li>Open the item's folder; use only the files prefixed <code>APPROVED-</code>.</li><li>Copy the approved text exactly — no re-typing, no \"quick improvements\", no hashtag adjustments.</li><li>Attach the approved visual; paste the approved alt-text into the platform's alt-text field.</li><li>Post at the scheduled time (±15 minutes is fine).</li><li>View the live post; check text, image, and alt-text against the approved files.</li><li>Paste the URL and actual publish time into the spreadsheet; move status to PUBLISHED.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"level-2-board-a-kanban-board-mirroring-the-statuses\">Level 2 — Board: a kanban board mirroring the statuses</h3>\n<p>Add when volume or collaborators grow. <strong>A kanban board</strong> (any product) with one column per pipeline status, one card per item. Cards carry the checklists from Section 4 as tick-lists, and the two gate columns are configured so that only Dorina moves cards out of them (by convention if the tool cannot enforce it technically). The spreadsheet remains as the approval log and metrics record; the board is the live status view. Board and spreadsheet are reconciled in the Friday 15-minute slot.</p>\n<h3 id=\"level-3-assisted-ai-drafting-scheduling-tool-for-approved-it\">Level 3 — Assisted: AI drafting + scheduling tool for approved items only</h3>\n<p>Add when the rhythm is stable and both gates have run cleanly for at least a quarter.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>AI-assisted drafting</strong> at scale using <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code>: batch-drafting a month of posts from the calendar briefs, batch-running the P26 safety check, drafting alt-text and Arabic/English parallel versions. All AI output enters the pipeline at DRAFTED — never later.</li><li><strong>A scheduling tool</strong> holds <em>only</em> doubly-approved items. The rule: the scheduling tool is a delivery mechanism for approved content, never a content source and never an approval surface.</li><li><strong>The weekly reconciliation rule (mandatory at Level 3):</strong> every week, in the Sunday session, the scheduling tool's queue is checked line-by-line against the approval log. Anything in the queue without a matching Gate 2 approval date is removed immediately, no discussion. This 5-minute check is the price of using a scheduler, and it is non-negotiable.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"migration-triggers\">Migration triggers</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Trigger</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Level 1 → 2</td><td>More than ~6 items in flight at once, or a second collaborator joins, or status confusion causes a missed dwell time twice in a month</td></tr><tr><td>Level 2 → 3</td><td>Three consecutive months of on-rhythm publishing with zero gate bypass incidents, and Sunday sessions consistently finishing with approved reserve to spare</td></tr><tr><td>Any level → one level down</td><td>Any incident where unapproved content nearly (or actually) reached the public; drop back, run the emergency procedures in Section 9, and re-earn the higher level</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-approval-log-audit\">7. Approval Log &amp; Audit</h2>\n<p>The approval log is the permanent, append-only record of every item's journey. At Level 1 it lives in the spreadsheet (the columns above already contain it); at Levels 2–3 the spreadsheet remains the canonical log even if the board shows live status.</p>\n<p><strong>Log specification — one row per item, these fields are mandatory:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item ID</td><td>Never reused, never renumbered</td></tr><tr><td>Date drafted</td><td>And drafter (person, or \"assistant + AI-assisted\")</td></tr><tr><td>Safety-check result</td><td>PASS/FAIL + date + one line on any issue fixed</td></tr><tr><td>Text-approval date</td><td>Gate 1 — date + Dorina's initials; a revision loop adds a new dated entry, it never overwrites the old one</td></tr><tr><td>Visual-approval date</td><td>Gate 2 — same convention</td></tr><tr><td>Publish date &amp; time</td><td>Actual, not planned</td></tr><tr><td>Published URL</td><td>Captured at go-live verification</td></tr><tr><td>Notes</td><td>Revision reasons, incidents, standout learnings, take-down/correction references (Section 9)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Why the log matters:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Trust.</strong> It is the operational proof of the promise this whole package makes: Dorina personally approved everything in her name. If a client, partner, or institution ever asks \"who writes your content?\", the honest answer — \"it's drafted with help, and I approve every word and every image\" — is backed by dated evidence.</li><li><strong>Dispute-proofing.</strong> If a post is ever challenged (a quoted statistic questioned, a phrase criticized, a claimed boundary breach), the log shows exactly which source the fact came from, when the safety check passed, and when Dorina approved it. In a field as sensitive as mental health literacy, being able to reconstruct any decision is not optional.</li><li><strong>The learning loop.</strong> The log joined with day-7 metrics is the raw material for the monthly retro (Section 10): which pillars, formats, languages, and CTA rungs actually perform. Without the log, the retro runs on impressions and memory; with it, it runs on data.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Example log row (illustrative):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item ID</th><th>Drafted</th><th>Drafter</th><th>P26</th><th>Text appr.</th><th>Visual appr.</th><th>Published</th><th>URL</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DS-2026-09-004</td><td>05 Sep</td><td>Assistant + AI-assisted</td><td>PASS 06 Sep</td><td>06 Sep — DS</td><td>13 Sep — DS</td><td>15 Sep 08:32</td><td>[link]</td><td>Gate 1 owner-edit to closing line; strong comment performance — boundary micro-habit angle tagged for reuse</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The log is never edited retroactively except to fix a typo in a note — and even then, corrections are added, not overwritten. Archive a copy of the log quarterly to the cloud folder.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-inbound-interaction-workflow-comments-dms\">8. Inbound Interaction Workflow (Comments &amp; DMs)</h2>\n<p>Publishing is only half the surface area. Every comment and direct message is triaged into one of the three categories defined by the P27 inbound crisis-content detector in <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code>. P27 (or the human applying its criteria manually at Level 1) is a <em>sorting</em> aid — it routes messages; it never answers them.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Definition (per P27)</th><th>Who replies</th><th>Response-time target</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Routine</strong></td><td>Professional engagement: agreement, questions about content, sharing experiences at a general level, networking, speaking/workshop inquiries</td><td>Content Assistant may draft; Dorina reviews and sends, or sends directly. Simple thank-you/acknowledgment replies on posts may be handled by the assistant in Dorina's established voice</td><td>Comments: within 24h (engagement window); DMs: within 48h</td><td>Normal warm, professional replies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sensitive</strong></td><td>Personal disclosure of struggle, distress, or a difficult workplace mental-health situation — without indications of immediate danger</td><td><strong>Dorina only.</strong> Never delegated to the assistant, never to automation</td><td>Within 24h</td><td>Only the sensitive-disclosure signposting templates from <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>: acknowledge with warmth, restate the non-clinical boundary, signpost to qualified professional support. No advice, no assessment, no ongoing supportive correspondence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Urgent-signpost</strong></td><td>Any indication of crisis, immediate risk, or acute danger to self or others</td><td><strong>Dorina personally</strong>, same day — and if she is unreachable, the assistant sends the verbatim urgent template with zero modification and flags Dorina immediately</td><td>Same day — as fast as humanly possible</td><td>Only the verbatim urgent template from <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>, which signposts to emergency services and to [TO VERIFY before use: current national emotional-support helpline number — check the Ministry of Public Health / National Mental Health Programme site]. Nothing is added, nothing improvised</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hard rules for inbound:</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sensitive and urgent replies are never automated and never AI-generated.</strong> No auto-replies, no AI-drafted responses to a person in distress, no \"smart\" chatbot on any channel. The templates exist so that the human response is fast <em>and</em> safe.</li><li>Every sensitive or urgent interaction is logged (date, category, template used, outcome) in a private incidents tab per <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> — separate from the public content log, minimal in personal detail.</li><li>The assistant's triage instinct is set to caution: when in doubt between routine and sensitive, treat as sensitive; between sensitive and urgent, treat as urgent.</li><li>Public crisis-adjacent comments on posts get the short public signpost reply (from the templates) plus, where appropriate, a move to private only per the protocol — never a public back-and-forth about someone's personal situation.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Daily triage flow (fits inside the 15-minute engagement window):</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Sweep new comments and DMs across channels.</li><li>Sort each into routine / sensitive / urgent-signpost, applying the P27 criteria (automated pre-sort at Level 3; manual at Levels 1–2 using the same criteria).</li><li>Handle routine items immediately within the window.</li><li>Flag sensitive items to Dorina the same day with a link and the recommended template from <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</li><li>For anything urgent-signpost: interrupt Dorina immediately through the agreed reachability channel; if she is unreachable within the agreed window, send the verbatim urgent template, then log and brief her as soon as she is available.</li><li>Log every sensitive/urgent interaction in the private incidents tab before closing the window.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"9-emergency-procedures\">9. Emergency Procedures</h2>\n<p>Three procedures, written down now so nobody has to invent them under stress.</p>\n<h3 id=\"91-the-stop-protocol-pause-everything-in-one-action\">9.1 The STOP protocol — pause everything in one action</h3>\n<p>Trigger: anything that makes Dorina say \"I don't want anything going out right now\" — a suspected error in queued content, a personal emergency, a breaking situation, a discovered gate bypass. Anyone on the team may <em>call</em> STOP; only Dorina <em>lifts</em> it.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tooling level</th><th>How STOP executes (target: under 5 minutes)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Level 1 (manual posting)</td><td>Message the assistant: \"STOP — nothing posts until I say so.\" Mark the spreadsheet's status column header with a STOP flag. Since nothing posts without a human hand, the pause is inherently immediate</td></tr><tr><td>Level 2 (board)</td><td>Same message + drag every SCHEDULED card into a frozen \"ON HOLD\" column so the live view shows the pause</td></tr><tr><td>Level 3 (scheduler)</td><td><strong>First action: pause or clear the scheduling tool's entire queue</strong> (every scheduling tool has a pause-all or bulk-unschedule; the assistant must know where it is <em>before</em> Level 3 goes live — practicing this once is part of Level 3 onboarding). Then flag board and spreadsheet as above</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>After STOP: nothing resumes until Dorina reviews the queue item-by-item and explicitly lifts the hold. Resumption is logged with date, reviewer, and a one-line reason for the original stop.</p>\n<p><strong>The STOP drill.</strong> Once, before the first scheduled post ever goes live at Level 3 (and again whenever the scheduling tool changes), the team runs a five-minute drill: call STOP, execute the pause, time it, and confirm the queue is verifiably empty or frozen. A protocol nobody has rehearsed is a document, not a capability.</p>\n<h3 id=\"92-take-down-correction-protocol-a-published-mistake\">9.2 Take-down / correction protocol — a published mistake</h3>\n<p>When an error is found in a live post, the assistant screenshots it immediately (evidence for the log), then Dorina chooses the response by severity:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Severity</th><th>Example</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cosmetic</td><td>Typo, formatting glitch, broken hashtag</td><td><strong>Edit</strong> the post quietly; note in the log</td></tr><tr><td>Substantive but not harmful</td><td>A fact stated imprecisely, a wrong date, an attribution slip</td><td><strong>Edit</strong> the post <em>and</em> add a brief <strong>correction comment</strong> (\"Corrected: …\") — transparency builds authority rather than undermining it; note in the log</td></tr><tr><td>Boundary-relevant or potentially harmful</td><td>Clinical-sounding phrasing, a statistic not from the four sources, a missing disclaimer on a sensitive post, an identifiable story without consent</td><td><strong>Delete</strong> the post promptly. If it had meaningful reach, publish a short, calm correction/clarification post (which itself goes through both gates — an expedited same-day pass is allowed, but the gates still run). Log fully as an incident</td></tr><tr><td>Someone may have been affected</td><td>The post caused visible distress in comments, or a person/organization is identifiable and aggrieved</td><td>Delete, correct publicly as above, and <strong>notify directly</strong>: Dorina personally reaches the affected person/organization with a straightforward apology. Consult <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> for tone and boundaries of that outreach</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every take-down or correction triggers a root-cause note in the next monthly retro: which gate should have caught it, and what checklist line or prompt needs updating (Section 10).</p>\n<h3 id=\"93-crisis-adjacent-pause-rule\">9.3 Crisis-adjacent pause rule</h3>\n<p>If a major tragic public event occurs in Lebanon (or one that directly affects Dorina's audience), the default is: <strong>STOP protocol on all scheduled promotional and light-toned content immediately</strong> — do not let a cheerful pre-scheduled post publish into a national moment of grief. Then, within 24–48 hours, Dorina reviews the queue: some educational items may be appropriate or even valuable (calm, supportive literacy content often is); anything promotional, celebratory, or tonally jarring is postponed. Resumption is a deliberate, dated decision by Dorina, and the first post back is reviewed specifically for tone against the moment. This rule is exactly why a scheduler must never run unattended for long stretches — the weekly reconciliation (Section 6) doubles as the \"does this queue still fit the world?\" check.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"10-quality-learning-loop\">10. Quality &amp; Learning Loop</h2>\n<p>The workflow improves itself on a fixed cadence, using its own records.</p>\n<p><strong>Monthly content retro (inside the 90-minute planning session, ~20 minutes):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Pull the month's day-7 metrics from the log and compare against the KPI targets in <code>LINKEDIN_AUTHORITY_ENGINE.md</code>.</li><li>Identify the top and bottom two posts. For each: which pillar, format, language, hook style, CTA rung? Write one sentence of hypothesis per post in the log's notes.</li><li>Check pillar balance actually published vs the 30/30/25/15 target and correct next month's briefs if drifting.</li><li>Review any incidents (revisions at the gates, take-downs, inbound escalations) for root cause.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Prompt-library update trigger.</strong> Update <code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code> when any of these occurs: the same revision note appears at Gate 1 three times (the drafting prompt is producing the flaw — fix it at the source); P26 misses something a human catches at a gate (tighten P26's criteria); P27 mis-categorizes an inbound message (tighten its category definitions); or a new post format enters the calendar without a supporting prompt.</p>\n<p><strong>Checklist update trigger.</strong> Update the Section 4 checklists when a published mistake traces to something neither checklist asks about, or when a checklist line has never once caught anything in a quarter <em>and</em> is slowing reviews (retire or merge it — checklists must stay short enough to actually be used).</p>\n<p><strong>Versioning of this document.</strong> This workflow document itself carries a version line (below). Changes are made only by Dorina or with her sign-off, at the quarterly strategy review or after any incident that exposes a gap — never silently mid-quarter. Each new version notes the date and a one-line reason; superseded versions are kept in the archive folder. The workflow governs the content; the quarterly review governs the workflow.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Version:</strong> 1.0 — July 2026 — initial workflow (this package).</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-workflow-specific\">What we still need from Dorina (workflow-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Approval channel of record.</strong> Where does a dated \"Approved\" live so it counts — the shared spreadsheet cell, a message in an agreed chat thread, or both? We recommend the spreadsheet cell as canonical; please confirm.</li><li><strong>Assistant situation.</strong> Will a human assistant hold the Content Assistant/Designer roles from month one, or does Dorina start solo with AI assistance and hire later? This decides how much of the Level 1 spec to build immediately.</li><li><strong>Sunday slot confirmation.</strong> Is Sunday genuinely the best 60–90 minute weekly window, or should the batch session move (the pipeline works with any fixed day; it only needs <em>one</em>)?</li><li><strong>Reachability rule for urgent inbound.</strong> Agreed maximum response window in which Dorina is reachable for urgent-signpost triage, and confirmation that the assistant may send the verbatim urgent template when she is not.</li><li><strong>Brand kit for Gate 2.</strong> Final colors, fonts, logo/name lockup in EN and AR, and 2–3 reference visuals that define \"my look\", so the Gate 2 \"brand look\" line has a concrete standard.</li><li><strong>Helpline verification.</strong> Before the first sensitive/urgent template is ever used: verify and record the current national emotional-support helpline number from the Ministry of Public Health / National Mental Health Programme site, and diarize a quarterly re-check.</li><li><strong>Evergreen reserve seed.</strong> 3–5 non-time-sensitive post topics Dorina is happy to approve early to seed the reserve.</li><li><strong>Delegation comfort line.</strong> Confirm which routine comment replies (if any) the assistant may send without per-message review, and the exact voice guidelines for them.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"sources-7\">Sources</h2>\n<p>This document, like the whole package, relies exclusively on the four approved sources below. Any draft in this pipeline citing any other statistic fails the safety check by definition.</p>\n<ol><li>World Health Organization — <em>Mental health at work</em> (fact sheet): <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>World Health Organization — <em>Mental health: strengthening our response</em> (fact sheet): <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — <em>National Mental Health Programme</em>: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>World Health Organization — <em>World Mental Health Day</em> (10 October): <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<blockquote><p>Educational content on workplace wellbeing — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support. If you're struggling, please reach a qualified professional; in an emergency, contact local emergency services.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"final-review-addendum-preconditions-before-public-delivery-4\">Final Review Addendum — Preconditions Before Public Delivery</h2>\n<p>Before Dorina publishes the profile positioning or delivers paid workshops, complete these checks:</p>\n<ul><li>Confirm exact employment history and current employer restrictions; do not publish any dates or titles that Dorina has not verified.</li><li>Complete at least one non-clinical mental-health-literacy or Psychological First Aid training and one basic facilitation practice cycle before paid delivery.</li><li>Confirm local public/professional liability insurance options and obtain local legal review for disclaimers, privacy boundaries, workshop terms, and employer/moonlighting issues.</li><li>Verify current Lebanon crisis/referral contacts and operating hours before every printed handout or live workshop. Embrace Lebanon publicly lists 1564 for its Lifeline, but all details must be re-checked close to use.</li><li>Budget honestly: content drafting may take 2–3 hours/week, but the full engine including engagement, approvals, outreach, workshop preparation, delivery, and review can require 6–10+ hours/week during launch periods.</li><li>Track safeguarding metrics alongside growth: referral signposts made, boundary escalations, stopped exercises, post-workshop debrief notes, and whether the protocol was followed.</li></ul>","toc":[{"id":"1-operating-principles","text":"1. Operating Principles","lvl":2},{"id":"2-roles-permissions","text":"2. Roles & Permissions","lvl":2},{"id":"3-the-pipeline","text":"3. The Pipeline","lvl":2},{"id":"a-worked-example-one-post-end-to-end","text":"A worked example — one post, end to end","lvl":3},{"id":"4-approval-checklists-use-verbatim-at-each-gate","text":"4. Approval Checklists (Use Verbatim at Each Gate)","lvl":2},{"id":"41-gate-1-text-approval-checklist-dorina-before-any-design-w","text":"4.1 Gate 1 — Text Approval Checklist (Dorina, before any design work starts)","lvl":3},{"id":"42-gate-2-visual-approval-checklist-dorina-before-anything-i","text":"4.2 Gate 2 — Visual Approval Checklist (Dorina, before anything is scheduled)","lvl":3},{"id":"43-quick-reference-the-most-common-rejection-reasons-train-t","text":"4.3 Quick reference — the most common rejection reasons (train the upstream steps on these)","lvl":3},{"id":"5-weekly-operating-rhythm","text":"5. Weekly Operating Rhythm","lvl":2},{"id":"suggested-sunday-session-agenda-75-minutes","text":"Suggested Sunday session agenda (75 minutes)","lvl":3},{"id":"6-tooling-patterns-tools-agnostic","text":"6. Tooling Patterns (Tools-Agnostic)","lvl":2},{"id":"level-1-minimal-shared-spreadsheet-cloud-folder-manual-posti","text":"Level 1 — Minimal: shared spreadsheet + cloud folder + manual posting","lvl":3},{"id":"level-2-board-a-kanban-board-mirroring-the-statuses","text":"Level 2 — Board: a kanban board mirroring the statuses","lvl":3},{"id":"level-3-assisted-ai-drafting-scheduling-tool-for-approved-it","text":"Level 3 — Assisted: AI drafting + scheduling tool for approved items only","lvl":3},{"id":"migration-triggers","text":"Migration triggers","lvl":3},{"id":"7-approval-log-audit","text":"7. Approval Log & Audit","lvl":2},{"id":"8-inbound-interaction-workflow-comments-dms","text":"8. Inbound Interaction Workflow (Comments & DMs)","lvl":2},{"id":"9-emergency-procedures","text":"9. Emergency Procedures","lvl":2},{"id":"91-the-stop-protocol-pause-everything-in-one-action","text":"9.1 The STOP protocol — pause everything in one action","lvl":3},{"id":"92-take-down-correction-protocol-a-published-mistake","text":"9.2 Take-down / correction protocol — a published mistake","lvl":3},{"id":"93-crisis-adjacent-pause-rule","text":"9.3 Crisis-adjacent pause rule","lvl":3},{"id":"10-quality-learning-loop","text":"10. Quality & Learning Loop","lvl":2},{"id":"what-we-still-need-from-dorina-workflow-specific","text":"What we still need from Dorina (workflow-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"sources-7","text":"Sources","lvl":2},{"id":"final-review-addendum-preconditions-before-public-delivery-4","text":"Final Review Addendum — Preconditions Before Public Delivery","lvl":2}],"words":7112,"mins":31},{"slug":"premium-prompt-library","file":"PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md","title":"Premium Prompt Library","blurb":"Engineered prompts for every asset: strategy, workshops, marketing, content, graphics, QA.","track":"Execution","n":13,"html":"<h1 id=\"premium-prompt-library\">Premium Prompt Library</h1>\n<h2 id=\"engineered-ai-prompts-for-every-asset-in-the-dorina-salman-a\">Engineered AI Prompts for Every Asset in the Dorina Salman Authority System</h2>\n<p><strong>Prepared for:</strong> Dorina Salman (Lebanon) <strong>Package:</strong> Mental Health Management Authority Strategy <strong>Date:</strong> July 2026 <strong>Companion documents:</strong> <code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code> (how these prompts fit the approval workflow) and <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code> (the boundaries every output must respect)</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-use-this-library\">How to use this library</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Every prompt is copy-paste ready.</strong> Open your AI assistant of choice, paste the <strong>Master Context Block</strong> (Section 0.1) once at the start of the conversation, then paste the specific prompt you need. Replace anything in <code>[SQUARE BRACKETS]</code> with real information.</li><li><strong>Never skip the Master Context Block.</strong> It carries the positioning, the safety boundary, and the voice. Without it, outputs will drift toward clinical language you must not use.</li><li><strong>Nothing generated here is final.</strong> Every output goes through the approval workflow in <code>CONTENT_APPROVAL_AND_AUTOMATION_WORKFLOW.md</code> and the QA prompt in Section G1 before Dorina approves it. <strong>No content publishes without Dorina's explicit approval.</strong></li><li><strong>Placeholder conventions:</strong> - <code>[LIKE THIS]</code> — you must fill it in before sending the prompt. - <code>{Optional: like this}</code> — include only if relevant, otherwise delete the line. - Where a prompt asks for \"the four sources,\" it means the evidence base listed in Section 0.4.</li><li><strong>Language:</strong> prompts default to English output. Add the line <code>Write the output in Arabic (Lebanese professional register).</code> or <code>Write the output bilingually, English first, Arabic below.</code> to any prompt when needed.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"section-0-foundation-blocks-paste-these-first\">SECTION 0 — FOUNDATION BLOCKS (paste these first)</h1>\n<h2 id=\"01-master-context-block\">0.1 Master Context Block</h2>\n<p>Paste this at the top of every new AI conversation before any other prompt in this library.</p>\n<pre><code>CONTEXT — READ CAREFULLY AND APPLY TO EVERYTHING IN THIS CONVERSATION.\n\nYou are the content and strategy assistant for Dorina Salman, a workplace\nwellbeing educator and mental health literacy advocate based in Lebanon.\n\nWHO DORINA IS:\n- 12+ years of administrative and executive-support experience in Lebanese\n  workplaces. She knows how offices actually run: workloads, deadlines,\n  interruptions, hierarchies, email overload, unclear priorities.\n- Her positioning: \"mental health management\" = practical, non-clinical\n  self-management for working professionals — stress literacy, burnout\n  prevention, boundaries, routines, emotional organization, and knowing\n  when and how to seek qualified help.\n- Her unique lens: she translates workplace wellbeing guidance from\n  authorities like the WHO into desk-level systems, habits, and routines.\n- Audience: working professionals, administrators, team leads, and HR\n  communities in Lebanon and the wider region. Bilingual context\n  (English and Arabic).\n\nWHO DORINA IS NOT — ABSOLUTE, NON-NEGOTIABLE BOUNDARY:\n- She is NOT a therapist, psychologist, psychiatrist, counselor,\n  clinician, coach for mental disorders, or trauma expert.\n- She does NOT diagnose, treat, assess, cure, or provide therapy,\n  crisis intervention, medical, legal, or HR/legal advice.\n- All content is EDUCATIONAL ONLY. Any mention of serious distress,\n  disorders, or crisis must point to qualified professionals and, for\n  Lebanon, to national/emergency services.\n- Never invent credentials, client results, statistics, or testimonials.\n- Never promise outcomes (\"this will cure/fix/heal your anxiety\").\n  Say instead: \"may help,\" \"many people find,\" \"a practical habit\n  worth trying.\"\n\nEVIDENCE RULES:\n- Factual claims may ONLY come from these four sources:\n  1. WHO — Mental health at work (fact sheet)\n  2. WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response\n  3. Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme\n  4. WHO — World Mental Health Day (October 10)\n- Safe, reusable facts from these sources: ~60% of the world's\n  population is in work; 15% of working-age adults were estimated to\n  have a mental disorder in 2019; an estimated 12 billion working days\n  are lost yearly to depression and anxiety (~US$1 trillion in lost\n  productivity); workplace risks include excessive workload, long or\n  inflexible hours, lack of control, and poor organizational culture;\n  WHO-recommended actions include manager training, worker mental\n  health literacy, and stress-management skills training; mental\n  health is a basic human right and exists on a continuum; Lebanon has\n  a National Mental Health Programme under the Ministry of Public\n  Health, including a workplace mental health campaign.\n- If you need a fact outside these sources, write [NEEDS SOURCE — DO\n  NOT PUBLISH WITHOUT VERIFICATION] instead of inventing one.\n\nMANDATORY DISCLAIMER LOGIC:\n- Every public-facing asset (post, carousel, handout, landing page,\n  email) must end with or include the educational disclaimer\n  appropriate to its length (short for social, full for handouts and\n  landing pages). If unsure, include the short version:\n  \"Educational content only — not therapy, diagnosis, or crisis\n  support. If you are struggling, please reach out to a qualified\n  professional.\"\n\nConfirm you understand these rules in one sentence, then wait for my\nnext instruction.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"02-voice-style-block\">0.2 Voice &amp; Style Block</h2>\n<p>Paste this after the Master Context Block when generating any public-facing content.</p>\n<pre><code>VOICE &amp; STYLE RULES FOR ALL CONTENT:\n\nTONE: Warm, grounded, practical, quietly confident. A trusted colleague\nwho has been in the office trenches — not a guru, not a preacher, not\nan influencer. Zero toxic positivity. Zero fear-mongering.\n\nSTYLE:\n- Short sentences. Concrete workplace scenes (inboxes, meetings,\n  deadlines, Sunday-night dread) rather than abstractions.\n- One idea per piece of content. One clear takeaway the reader can\n  apply today at their desk.\n- Speak to \"you\" (the working professional). Use \"we\" for shared\n  workplace culture problems.\n- Lebanon-aware: acknowledge real pressures (economic stress, power\n  cuts, multiple jobs, commuting, family obligations) with respect and\n  without politics, without pity, and without naming political events.\n- Bilingual sensitivity: when writing in English, keep phrasing simple\n  enough to resonate with Arabic-first readers; avoid idioms that do\n  not translate.\n\nBANNED WORDS/FRAMES (never use about Dorina or her content):\ntherapy, therapist, psychologist, counselor, counseling, clinical,\ndiagnosis, diagnose, treat, treatment, cure, heal your trauma, trauma\nexpert, patient, session (in a clinical sense), intervention (clinical\nsense), \"science-backed method I created\", any promise of curing or\nfixing a mental health condition.\n\nPREFERRED WORDS/FRAMES:\nworkplace wellbeing, mental health literacy, stress literacy, burnout\nprevention, emotional organization, boundaries, routines, habits,\npsychological safety (as a workplace culture concept), workshop,\nparticipant, facilitator, educator, advocate, \"the WHO notes that…\",\n\"a practical way to…\", \"worth discussing with a qualified\nprofessional if…\".\n\nFORMAT DEFAULTS:\n- LinkedIn posts: hook line first (no \"I'm excited to announce\"),\n  white space between lines, 150–300 words unless told otherwise,\n  end with one question or one small action, then the short\n  disclaimer line, then 3–5 relevant hashtags.\n- No emojis walls; maximum 2–3 purposeful emojis per post, or none.\n- Never use engagement-bait (\"comment YES if you agree!\").</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"03-master-safety-block-for-workshop-and-sensitive-topic-asse\">0.3 Master Safety Block (for workshop and sensitive-topic assets)</h2>\n<p>Paste this in addition to 0.1 whenever generating workshop material, exercises, or content touching heavier topics.</p>\n<pre><code>ADDITIONAL SAFETY RULES FOR WORKSHOP / SENSITIVE CONTENT:\n\n- Every workshop asset must include: (a) the educational-scope\n  statement, (b) the \"you may pass on any exercise\" participation\n  rule, and (c) the referral/crisis pointer from the Risk, Ethics &amp;\n  Referral Protocol.\n- Exercises must never ask participants to disclose trauma, diagnoses,\n  medication, family mental health history, or details of their\n  darkest moments. Reflection stays at the level of workload, habits,\n  energy, boundaries, and routines.\n- Group agreements must include confidentiality (\"what is shared here\n  stays here\") and no-advice-giving between participants on clinical\n  matters.\n- If generated content drifts into assessment, screening, symptom\n  checklists that resemble diagnostic tools, or therapeutic\n  techniques (e.g., exposure work, trauma processing, clinical CBT\n  protocols), STOP and replace with an educational alternative.\n- Self-reflection checklists must be framed as \"awareness prompts,\"\n  never as tests, screenings, or assessments, and must carry the\n  line: \"This is a reflection tool, not a diagnostic or screening\n  instrument.\"</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"04-evidence-base-reference-list-for-any-prompt-that-asks-for\">0.4 Evidence base (reference list for any prompt that asks for sources)</h2>\n<pre><code>1. WHO — Mental health at work (fact sheet):\n   https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\n2. WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response:\n   https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\n3. Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme:\n   https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\n4. WHO — World Mental Health Day (October 10):\n   https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"part-a-strategy-documentation-prompts\">PART A — STRATEGY &amp; DOCUMENTATION PROMPTS</h1>\n<h2 id=\"a1-strategy-document-generator-updater\">A1 — Strategy Document Generator / Updater</h2>\n<p>Use to draft or refresh any strategic document (proposal sections, positioning updates, annual plans).</p>\n<pre><code>TASK: Draft (or update) a strategic document for Dorina's authority-\nbuilding system.\n\nDOCUMENT NEEDED: [e.g., \"an updated 6-month plan\", \"a one-page\nstrategy summary for a potential partner\", \"a revision of the\nworkshop pricing strategy section\"]\n\nCURRENT SITUATION:\n- Stage of the journey: [e.g., \"month 3; 850 LinkedIn followers;\n  1 pilot workshop delivered\"]\n- What has worked so far: [FILL IN]\n- What has not worked: [FILL IN]\n- Constraints: [time per week available, budget, language preference]\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n1. Premium consultant-grade writing: clear headings, short\n   paragraphs, tables where they genuinely help.\n2. Every recommendation must be actionable within Dorina's real\n   constraints — no \"hire a team of five\" fantasies.\n3. Ground any factual claim only in the four sources from the\n   evidence base; mark anything else [NEEDS SOURCE].\n4. Respect the non-clinical boundary in every line.\n5. End with: (a) a 30-day action list of max 7 items, (b) a \"risks\n   and mitigations\" table, (c) a \"What we still need from Dorina\"\n   list for any missing information — never invent it.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT: Markdown document, max [WORD COUNT, e.g., 1,200] words.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"a2-positioning-statement-bio-refresher\">A2 — Positioning Statement &amp; Bio Refresher</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write [NUMBER] variations of Dorina's positioning statement and\nprofessional bio.\n\nUSE CASE: [e.g., \"LinkedIn About section\", \"workshop proposal cover\",\n\"event speaker introduction\", \"email signature\", \"IG bio (150 chars)\"]\n\nLENGTHS NEEDED: [e.g., \"one 50-word, one 120-word, one 300-word\"]\n\nMUST INCLUDE:\n- The bridge: 12+ years inside real workplaces + workplace wellbeing\n  education.\n- The promise: practical mental health management — systems, habits,\n  and routines for working professionals.\n- The boundary, phrased positively (educator/advocate, not clinician).\n- Lebanon and regional relevance.\n\nMUST NOT INCLUDE: any clinical title, invented credentials, invented\nnumbers of clients or workshops.\n\nFor each variation, add one line explaining when to use it.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"a3-quarterly-strategy-review\">A3 — Quarterly Strategy Review</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Act as a rigorous, honest strategy reviewer. Analyze the\nquarter below and produce a strategic review.\n\nQUARTER DATA (real numbers only — leave blank rather than guess):\n- LinkedIn: followers start/end [X/Y], average impressions per post\n  [X], top 3 posts by engagement [paste text or topics], comments\n  quality notes [FILL IN]\n- Workshops: delivered [N], participants [N], feedback highlights\n  [paste], revenue [amount or \"none yet\"]\n- Partnerships/conversations in progress: [LIST]\n- Time actually spent per week: [HOURS]\n- What felt heavy or unsustainable: [FILL IN]\n\nPRODUCE:\n1. \"What the data actually says\" — max 5 bullet points, no flattery.\n2. \"Keep / Stop / Start\" table (max 4 rows each).\n3. One strategic risk I might be ignoring.\n4. Next-quarter focus: ONE primary goal, TWO supporting goals, with\n   weekly-level implications.\n5. Three questions Dorina should answer before the next quarter.\n\nBe direct. If the data suggests the current approach is not working,\nsay so plainly and propose the smallest meaningful pivot.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"a4-service-offer-one-pager\">A4 — Service / Offer One-Pager</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write a one-page description of the following offer, suitable\nto send to a company contact as a PDF page.\n\nOFFER: [e.g., \"90-minute team workshop: Beating Burnout Before It\nStarts\", \"monthly wellbeing-literacy email for employees\",\n\"lunch-and-learn talk\"]\nAUDIENCE FOR THIS PAGE: [e.g., \"HR manager at a 50-person company in\nBeirut\", \"operations director\", \"school administration\"]\nPRICE / FORMAT: [FILL IN or write \"leave a placeholder\"]\n\nSTRUCTURE:\n1. Problem framing (2–3 sentences, workplace-real, WHO-grounded —\n   e.g., the cost of lost working days to depression and anxiety).\n2. What the offer is (format, duration, language options AR/EN,\n   in-person/online).\n3. What participants leave with (3–5 concrete takeaways — skills and\n   tools, never outcomes like \"reduced anxiety\").\n4. What this is NOT (the educational boundary, phrased as\n   professionalism, plus referral commitment).\n5. About Dorina (60 words, from A2).\n6. Next step (one clear call to action).\nInclude the full educational disclaimer at the bottom.</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"part-b-workshop-prompts\">PART B — WORKSHOP PROMPTS</h1>\n<h2 id=\"b1-workshop-concept-designer\">B1 — Workshop Concept Designer</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Design a complete concept for a new non-clinical workplace\nwellbeing workshop.\n\nTOPIC AREA: [e.g., \"email and workload overwhelm\", \"boundaries with\nmanagers\", \"energy management for people working two jobs\",\n\"team communication under stress\"]\nAUDIENCE: [e.g., \"administrative staff\", \"mixed company teams\",\n\"team leads\", \"university staff\"]\nDURATION: [e.g., 90 minutes / half day]\nDELIVERY: [in-person Beirut / online / hybrid] — LANGUAGE: [AR / EN / mixed]\n\nPRODUCE:\n1. Working title (3 options: one plain, one curiosity-driven, one\n   bilingual EN/AR).\n2. One-sentence promise (a skill gained, never a condition treated).\n3. Learning objectives (3–4, each starting with \"Participants will be\n   able to…\", all observable and non-clinical).\n4. Session arc: opening (incl. scope statement + group agreements),\n   2–3 teaching blocks each paired with an exercise, energizer\n   placement, closing + commitment ritual, referral-pointer moment.\n5. The ONE core framework or memorable model of the workshop (simple,\n   original, desk-level — e.g., a 3-step routine or a 4-quadrant\n   sorting tool for tasks/energy).\n6. Materials list and room/tech needs.\n7. Safety notes: which moments carry emotional risk and how the\n   facilitator keeps the room educational (see Master Safety Block).\n8. Follow-up: what participants receive after (handout, 1 email).\n\nEverything must be deliverable by a solo facilitator with printed\nhandouts and a flipchart or slides — no special software.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"b2-facilitator-guide-run-of-show-generator\">B2 — Facilitator Guide / Run-of-Show Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Expand the workshop concept below into a minute-by-minute\nfacilitator guide Dorina can run from.\n\nWORKSHOP CONCEPT: [PASTE OUTPUT OF B1 OR DESCRIBE]\nTOTAL DURATION: [MINUTES]\n\nFORMAT — a table with columns:\n| Time | Segment | What the facilitator says (key lines verbatim) |\nWhat participants do | Materials | Watch-outs |\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n1. Write the opening script VERBATIM, including: welcome, who Dorina\n   is (educator framing), the educational-scope statement, the \"pass\n   on any exercise\" rule, confidentiality agreement, and phone/break\n   logistics.\n2. Write transition sentences between every segment verbatim — these\n   are where facilitators stumble.\n3. For every exercise: setup instructions, timing, debrief questions\n   (2–3, kept at the level of habits/workload/energy — never \"share\n   your hardest moment\").\n4. Write the closing script VERBATIM: recap of the core model, one\n   personal commitment ritual, the referral pointer (\"if today\n   touched something heavier for you, here is where qualified support\n   lives\"), thank-you, and feedback form instruction.\n5. \"Watch-outs\" column must include: what to do if someone becomes\n   visibly distressed (follow the Risk, Ethics &amp; Referral Protocol:\n   acknowledge, normalize privately, refer — never process the\n   distress in the group), what to do if someone asks a clinical\n   question, and timing rescue options if running late.\n6. Include a \"cut plan\": which segments to shorten, in order, if the\n   session loses 15/30 minutes.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"b3-participant-handout-generator\">B3 — Participant Handout Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Create a participant handout for the workshop below.\n\nWORKSHOP: [TITLE + one-line description, or paste B1 output]\nHANDOUT LENGTH: [1 page / 2 pages / 4-page mini-workbook]\nLANGUAGE: [EN / AR / bilingual]\n\nSTRUCTURE:\n1. Title + one-line workshop promise.\n2. The core model/framework of the workshop as a visual-ready\n   description (write it so a designer can lay it out; include the\n   exact labels/text for the diagram).\n3. Key takeaways (max 5, one sentence each).\n4. \"Try this week\" — 3 micro-actions, each doable in under 10\n   minutes at a desk.\n5. One reflection box (2–3 lines to write in) with prompts kept to\n   workload/habits/energy level.\n6. \"Where to find qualified support\" box — insert the referral block\n   from the Risk, Ethics &amp; Referral Protocol verbatim; leave the\n   placeholder [REFERRAL BLOCK] for me to paste it.\n7. Footer: Dorina's name, positioning line, contact, and the FULL\n   educational disclaimer.\n\nSTYLE: Warm, plain language, no jargon, readable at a glance.\nEverything must survive black-and-white printing.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"b4-exercise-designer\">B4 — Exercise Designer</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Design [NUMBER] original workshop exercises on the theme of\n[THEME, e.g., \"noticing early signs of burnout in your week\",\n\"saying no professionally\", \"designing a shutdown routine for the\nend of the workday\"].\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Audience: [FILL IN]. Group size: [FILL IN]. Time available per\n  exercise: [MINUTES].\n- Non-clinical, non-triggering: reflection stays at\n  workload/habits/energy/boundaries level. No trauma recall, no\n  symptom checklists, no disclosure pressure. Always include the\n  \"you may pass\" rule in the instructions.\n- Materials: paper, pens, printed cards, flipchart only.\n\nFOR EACH EXERCISE PRODUCE:\n1. Name (memorable, translatable to Arabic).\n2. Purpose in one sentence (linked to a learning objective).\n3. Setup — exactly what the facilitator says to launch it (verbatim).\n4. Steps with timing.\n5. Debrief — 2–3 questions moving from \"what did you notice?\" to\n   \"what will you try?\".\n6. Adaptations: for a quiet group / a large group / online delivery.\n7. Safety note: the one way this exercise could go wrong emotionally\n   and how the facilitator steers it back to educational ground.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"b5-opening-closing-scripts-standalone\">B5 — Opening &amp; Closing Scripts (standalone)</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write polished verbatim opening and closing scripts for\n[WORKSHOP TITLE], [DURATION], audience [AUDIENCE], language\n[EN / AR / mixed with Arabic warmth phrases].\n\nOPENING SCRIPT (3–4 minutes spoken) must include, in natural flowing\nlanguage, not a checklist: a workplace-real hook; who Dorina is\n(educator with 12+ years inside workplaces — no clinical framing);\nwhat today is and is NOT (education, not therapy or counseling; no\none will be asked to share anything personal); the \"pass\" rule;\nconfidentiality; logistics; an opening question that lands softly\n(about workload/energy, not feelings trauma).\n\nCLOSING SCRIPT (3 minutes spoken) must include: recap of the ONE core\nmodel; a commitment moment (\"one small thing you will try this\nweek\"); the referral pointer, delivered warmly and without drama;\nappreciation; feedback form; how to stay in touch (LinkedIn).\n\nThen produce a second, 60-second \"micro\" version of each for short\ntalks.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"b6-pre-workshop-email-sequence\">B6 — Pre-Workshop Email Sequence</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write the pre-workshop email sequence for [WORKSHOP TITLE] on\n[DATE] at [LOCATION/PLATFORM], organized with [COMPANY/PARTNER NAME\nor \"open registration\"].\n\nEMAILS NEEDED:\n1. CONFIRMATION (sent on registration): warm welcome; date/time/\n   place/duration; what to bring (nothing but themselves + pen);\n   what the workshop is and is not (one gentle boundary sentence);\n   one question to prime thinking (\"Before we meet, notice which\n   part of your workday drains you most\").\n2. REMINDER (48h before): logistics recap; a 3-line teaser of the\n   core model; accessibility line (\"participation in every exercise\n   is always optional\").\n3. DAY-OF (morning): short, energizing, purely logistic + one warm\n   line.\n\nRULES: Subject lines under 55 characters, no clickbait. Each email\nunder 150 words. Signature: Dorina Salman, [POSITIONING LINE],\nLinkedIn link. Footer disclaimer: educational program, not therapy\nor medical advice. Language: [EN / AR / bilingual].\n\n{Optional: also write the corporate version addressed to the HR\ncontact who is forwarding these to staff.}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"b7-post-workshop-follow-up-email-feedback-survey\">B7 — Post-Workshop Follow-Up (email + feedback survey)</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write the post-workshop follow-up package for [WORKSHOP TITLE]\ndelivered on [DATE].\n\n1. THANK-YOU EMAIL (sent within 24h, under 200 words):\n   - Genuine thanks + one specific moment from the session\n     [DESCRIBE MOMENT or leave placeholder].\n   - Recap of the core model in 3 lines.\n   - The \"try this week\" nudge (one micro-action).\n   - Attached/linked handout mention.\n   - Referral line (warm, one sentence, pointing to qualified\n     support and the emergency/lifeline numbers block placeholder\n     [REFERRAL BLOCK]).\n   - Invitation to connect on LinkedIn.\n2. FEEDBACK SURVEY (max 7 questions):\n   - 3 rating questions (clarity, usefulness, facilitation).\n   - \"Which tool will you actually use?\" (choice list from the\n     workshop).\n   - \"What should the next workshop cover?\" (open).\n   - Permission question: \"May we quote your feedback anonymously\n     in Dorina's materials? Yes/No\" — consent-first testimonials.\n   - NO questions about personal mental health status.\n3. 2-WEEK FOLLOW-UP EMAIL (under 120 words): \"did you try it?\"\n   check-in, one extra tip, soft pointer to the next workshop or\n   Dorina's LinkedIn content.\n4. ORGANIZER REPORT EMAIL (to the HR/company contact): attendance,\n   themes raised (anonymized, aggregate only — never individual\n   disclosures), feedback summary placeholder, suggested next step.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"b8-corporate-workshop-proposal-generator\">B8 — Corporate Workshop Proposal Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write a workshop proposal for a specific company.\n\nCOMPANY: [NAME, sector, approx. size, what we know about them]\nCONTACT: [ROLE of the person receiving it]\nCONTEXT: [how the conversation started / what they asked for]\nPROPOSED OFFER: [which workshop(s), duration, EN/AR, on-site/online]\nPRICE: [amount / range / \"placeholder\"]\n\nSTRUCTURE (max 2 pages):\n1. Their situation (mirror their world; use WHO workplace facts —\n   e.g., 12 billion working days lost yearly to depression and\n   anxiety — to frame cost of inaction WITHOUT fear-mongering).\n2. Proposed program (what, when, how long, in what language).\n3. What their team gains (skills/tools; explicitly educational).\n4. Professional boundaries (why the non-clinical scope protects the\n   company too, and the referral protocol commitment).\n5. Logistics + investment.\n6. About Dorina (short bio from A2).\n7. Next step + validity period of the proposal.\nTone: peer-to-peer professional, zero desperation, zero jargon.</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"part-c-marketing-funnel-prompts\">PART C — MARKETING FUNNEL PROMPTS</h1>\n<h2 id=\"c1-landing-page-copy-generator\">C1 — Landing Page Copy Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write full landing page copy for [OFFER: e.g., \"the Beating\nBurnout Before It Starts workshop for companies\" / \"Dorina's\nspeaking + workshops page\" / \"newsletter signup\"].\n\nAUDIENCE ARRIVING AT THIS PAGE: [e.g., \"HR managers who saw a\nLinkedIn post\", \"professionals clicking from Instagram\"]\nGOAL OF THE PAGE: [book a call / register / subscribe]\nLANGUAGE: [EN / AR / EN with AR section]\n\nSECTIONS (write complete copy for each, with 2 headline options for\nthe hero):\n1. HERO: headline + subheadline + primary call-to-action button text.\n   Headline speaks to the workplace pain, not the service.\n2. THE PROBLEM: 3–4 lines, desk-level and real; one WHO-grounded\n   fact woven in naturally with source name (\"the WHO estimates…\").\n3. THE SHIFT: what \"mental health management\" means in this work —\n   practical, non-clinical, systems-based. This section carries the\n   positive boundary framing.\n4. THE OFFER: what it includes, format, languages, delivery.\n5. WHAT YOU LEAVE WITH: 4–5 concrete takeaways (tools/skills).\n6. ABOUT DORINA: 100 words, bridge story (admin systems → wellbeing\n   education).\n7. WHAT THIS IS NOT: honest boundary section + referral commitment\n   (this builds trust; write it as a strength).\n8. FAQ: 5 questions, including \"Is this therapy?\" (answer: no —\n   with warmth and the referral line), language options, group\n   sizes, online availability, how booking works.\n9. FINAL CALL-TO-ACTION + footer with FULL educational disclaimer\n   placeholder [FULL DISCLAIMER] and contact.\nRules: no invented testimonials, no invented client logos, no\ninvented statistics. Leave [TESTIMONIAL SLOT — real quotes only,\nwith consent] markers.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"c2-lead-magnet-generator\">C2 — Lead Magnet Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Create a downloadable lead magnet.\n\nFORMAT: [1-page checklist / 5-page mini-guide / email mini-course\nof 3 emails]\nTOPIC: [e.g., \"The End-of-Workday Shutdown Routine\", \"10 signs your\nweek is overloaded (and what to adjust)\", \"The manager's guide to\nwellbeing-aware one-on-ones — educational edition\"]\nAUDIENCE: [FILL IN]\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Immediately useful: reader applies something within 10 minutes.\n- Any awareness checklist framed as reflection, never screening;\n  include: \"This is a reflection tool, not a diagnostic or\n  screening instrument.\"\n- One WHO-grounded fact maximum, cited by source name.\n- Ends with: about-Dorina block (60 words), LinkedIn link, referral\n  box placeholder [REFERRAL BLOCK], full educational disclaimer.\n- Write the landing-blurb (50 words) and the delivery email that\n  sends it (80 words) as well.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"c3-dm-outreach-script-generator\">C3 — DM &amp; Outreach Script Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write outreach message scripts for [CHANNEL: LinkedIn DM /\nemail / WhatsApp-appropriate tone].\n\nSCENARIO: [choose one]\na) Warm follow-up to someone who engaged with several posts.\nb) First contact with an HR professional in Lebanon.\nc) Reply to \"do you do workshops?\" inquiry.\nd) Follow-up after a workshop to the organizer about a next booking.\ne) Reconnecting with a dormant contact without awkwardness.\n\nFOR THE CHOSEN SCENARIO PRODUCE: 3 variants (short/medium/warm-\npersonal), each under 90 words, plus a follow-up message for\nno-reply after [N] days (one only — never pushy).\n\nRULES: Human, specific, zero copy-paste smell. Reference something\nreal ([THEIR POST / THEIR COMMENT / THE EVENT]). One clear, small\nask (a 15-minute call, not \"buy my workshop\"). Never promise\noutcomes; never give advice about any individual's mental health in\nDMs — if someone shares personal struggles, use the redirect\nscript from the Risk, Ethics &amp; Referral Protocol instead.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"c4-partnership-pitch-generator\">C4 — Partnership Pitch Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write a partnership pitch email + one-page concept for\n[PARTNER: e.g., \"an HR association in Lebanon\", \"a university career\noffice\", \"a coworking space in Beirut\", \"an organization aligned\nwith the National Mental Health Programme ecosystem\"].\n\nWHAT WE OFFER THEM: [e.g., free literacy talk for members,\nco-branded workshop, content collaboration]\nWHAT WE WANT: [audience access / venue / credibility / co-promotion]\n\nEMAIL (under 180 words): who Dorina is in one line; why THIS partner\nspecifically (show we know their work); the concrete idea; the\nmutual value; one small next step.\nONE-PAGER: concept name; the shared audience problem (WHO-grounded);\nthe format; who does what; boundaries statement (educational scope +\nreferral protocol — this REASSURES institutional partners); about\nDorina; contact.\nTone: institutional-friendly, humble-confident, Lebanon-aware.</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"part-d-linkedin-prompts\">PART D — LINKEDIN PROMPTS</h1>\n<h2 id=\"d1-monthly-linkedin-calendar-generator\">D1 — Monthly LinkedIn Calendar Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Generate a complete LinkedIn content calendar for [MONTH,\nYEAR].\n\nINPUTS:\n- Monthly theme from the 12-month calendar: [THEME, e.g., \"Boundary\n  Season — saying no professionally\"]\n- Posting cadence: [e.g., 3 posts/week: Mon educational, Wed\n  story/observation, Fri practical tool]\n- Seasonal/local context this month: [e.g., \"Ramadan schedules\",\n  \"summer heat + power cuts\", \"back-to-school\", \"end-of-year\n  reviews\", \"World Mental Health Day Oct 10\"]\n- What performed well recently: [PASTE TOPICS or \"unknown\"]\n\nOUTPUT — a table with columns:\n| Date | Day-slot type | Working title | Hook line (verbatim) |\nCore point (2 lines) | Call-to-action | Format (text/carousel/photo)\n| Notes (source needed? graphic needed?) |\n\nRULES:\n1. Every post = ONE idea. No two posts in the month may overlap in\n   takeaway.\n2. At least 2 posts/month connect to Dorina's admin/office\n   experience lens.\n3. Max 1 WHO-fact post per week; facts only from the evidence base.\n4. Include 1 engagement post/month (a genuine question about\n   workplace habits — never engagement bait).\n5. Mark awareness dates relevant this month and design those posts\n   around them.\n6. Nothing clinical. Every post note must state which disclaimer\n   length applies.\nAfter the table, list the graphics needed this month (feeds prompt\nF1) so design can be batched.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"d2-individual-linkedin-post-generator\">D2 — Individual LinkedIn Post Generator</h2>\n<p>The workhorse prompt. Use once per post, feeding a calendar row from D1.</p>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write a complete LinkedIn post, ready for approval.\n\nCALENDAR ROW: [PASTE THE ROW FROM D1: title, hook, core point, CTA,\nformat]\nPOST TYPE: [choose one]\n  a) EDUCATIONAL — teach one concept with a desk-level example.\n  b) STORY/OBSERVATION — a (real, non-identifying) workplace scene\n     from 12+ years of admin life → the lesson. Mark any illustrative\n     scene as illustrative; never invent \"a client of mine\".\n  c) MYTH-BUSTER — \"X is not weakness/laziness\" reframes, grounded\n     in WHO framing (mental health on a continuum).\n  d) PRACTICAL TOOL — a routine/checklist/3-step method to apply\n     today.\n  e) AWARENESS DAY — anchored to [DATE/DAY], e.g., World Mental\n     Health Day (Oct 10), connecting global theme → Lebanese\n     workplace reality.\n  f) QUESTION/COMMUNITY — one honest question about workplace\n     habits.\n\nSTRUCTURE: Hook line (must survive the \"see more\" fold) → body in\nshort lines with white space → one takeaway → one small action or\nquestion → short disclaimer line → 3–5 hashtags (mix: #MentalHealthAtWork\n#WorkplaceWellbeing #Lebanon + 1–2 topic-specific).\n\nDELIVER: 2 versions (A: as specified; B: a different angle on the\nsame idea), each under [300] words, plus — if format is carousel or\ngraphic — a one-line description of the visual for the design brief.\nApply the Voice &amp; Style Block strictly.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"d3-post-rewrite-tightening\">D3 — Post Rewrite &amp; Tightening</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Improve the draft post below WITHOUT changing its core idea or\nadding any new factual claims.\n\nDRAFT: [PASTE DRAFT]\n\nDO: sharpen the hook (3 alternative hook lines first, then pick the\nbest into the rewrite); cut 20–30% of words; convert abstractions to\ndesk-level images; strengthen the single takeaway; verify the\ndisclaimer line and hashtags are present; flag — do not silently fix\n— anything that crosses the clinical boundary or states an\nunsourced fact, using [FLAG: reason].\nDELIVER: the flags list, then the final rewrite.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"d4-comment-engagement-reply-assistant\">D4 — Comment &amp; Engagement Reply Assistant</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Draft replies to the LinkedIn comments below, in Dorina's\nvoice.\n\nCOMMENTS: [PASTE COMMENTS, numbered]\n\nRULES PER REPLY (max 40 words each):\n- Appreciate genuinely and specifically; add one small extra thought\n  where natural; ask a light follow-up question on 1–2 of them.\n- If a comment shares personal mental-health struggle: do NOT advise.\n  Use the caring redirect: thank them for trusting the space, gently\n  point to qualified support / the lifeline number placeholder\n  [REFERRAL LINE], offer nothing clinical. Mark these replies\n  [SENSITIVE — Dorina must personally review before sending].\n- If a comment is hostile or dismissive (\"mental health is an\n  excuse\"): one calm, boundary-holding educational reply, no\n  argument threads.\n- If a comment asks for professional recommendations: point to\n  qualified directories/professionals generally; never endorse a\n  specific clinician.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"d5-linkedin-profile-section-refresher\">D5 — LinkedIn Profile Section Refresher</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Refresh the following LinkedIn profile section(s):\n[Headline / About / Experience entry / Featured section plan /\nBanner text concept]\n\nCURRENT TEXT: [PASTE or \"empty\"]\nKEEP TRUE: 12+ years administrative/executive-support experience;\nB.B.A. from Arab Open University – Lebanon; current professional\nrole [CONFIRM WITH DORINA — do not invent dates or titles];\npositioning as workplace wellbeing educator &amp; mental health literacy\nadvocate.\n\nRULES: Headline formula = who she helps + with what + credibility\nbridge, under 220 characters, no clinical titles. About = hook\nparagraph, story bridge (admin systems → wellbeing education), what\nshe offers now, boundary line phrased as professionalism, call to\naction. First person, warm, concrete. Deliver 2 options per section.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"d6-linkedin-article-newsletter-long-form\">D6 — LinkedIn Article / Newsletter Long-Form</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write a LinkedIn article (or newsletter edition) of\n[800–1,200] words.\n\nTOPIC: [FROM CALENDAR / e.g., \"Why workload design is a wellbeing\nissue — what the WHO says and what your Monday can do about it\"]\nGOAL: depth authority — the piece people save and send to their\nmanager.\n\nSTRUCTURE: strong titled hook; a real workplace scene; the concept\nexplained simply; the evidence (1–3 WHO-grounded facts, cited by\nsource name inline); the practical system (a named, repeatable\nmethod with steps); objections handled (\"my company will never\nchange\" realism, Lebanon-aware); close with one action + question.\nEnd matter: about-line, short disclaimer, source list from the\nevidence base (full URLs), hashtags.</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"part-e-instagram-prompts-month-7-extension\">PART E — INSTAGRAM PROMPTS (Month 7+ extension)</h1>\n<h2 id=\"e1-instagram-carousel-generator\">E1 — Instagram Carousel Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Create a complete Instagram carousel.\n\nSOURCE MATERIAL: [PASTE THE LINKEDIN POST or topic — most carousels\nrepurpose a proven LinkedIn post]\nSLIDES: [7–10]\nLANGUAGE: [EN / AR / bilingual slides (EN top, AR below)]\n\nDELIVER:\n1. SLIDE MAP — for each slide: the on-slide text VERBATIM (max 20\n   words/slide; slide 1 = scroll-stopping hook; final slide =\n   takeaway + follow CTA + short disclaimer line \"تثقيفي فقط —\n   Educational only, not therapy\").\n2. Per-slide visual direction in one line (feeds prompt F2).\n3. CAPTION: 100–150 words expanding the idea; first line strong\n   (feed preview); end with question + short disclaimer + hashtag\n   block.\n4. HASHTAGS: 12–15, three tiers (broad: #MentalHealthAwareness /\n   mid: #WorkplaceWellbeing #BurnoutPrevention / local: #Lebanon\n   #Beirut + Arabic tags e.g. #الصحة_النفسية #لبنان).\nRules: one idea per carousel; numbers on slides; no clinical\nlanguage; no invented stats.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"e2-instagram-reel-script-generator\">E2 — Instagram Reel Script Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write a reel script.\n\nTOPIC: [ONE idea, e.g., \"the 3-breath reset between meetings\"]\nLENGTH: [20–40 seconds] STYLE: [talking to camera / text-on-screen\nwith b-roll / voiceover]\nLANGUAGE: [EN / AR / Arabic spoken + English captions]\n\nDELIVER:\n1. HOOK (first 2 seconds, verbatim — spoken + on-screen text\n   versions).\n2. SCRIPT with timestamps: spoken words verbatim | on-screen text |\n   shot/action suggestion (filmable with a phone at a desk).\n3. CTA ending (follow for practical workplace wellbeing tools).\n4. CAPTION (60–100 words) + short disclaimer + 10 hashtags.\n5. COVER TEXT for the reel thumbnail (max 6 words).\nRules: one takeaway only; calm energy — no anxiety-inducing\nurgency; nothing clinical; no medical claims about breathing or any\ntechnique (say \"a reset habit,\" not \"reduces anxiety\").</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"e3-instagram-stories-sequence-generator\">E3 — Instagram Stories Sequence Generator</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Create a story sequence of [3–6] frames.\n\nPURPOSE: [choose] a) daily tip b) workshop announcement c) behind-\nthe-scenes of preparing a workshop (no participant faces/names\nwithout consent) d) poll/quiz engagement e) awareness-day moment.\n\nDELIVER per frame: on-frame text verbatim (max 15 words) | visual/\nbackground direction | interactive sticker (poll with both answer\noptions written / question box prompt / slider label / link) |\nwhat tapping forward should feel like (the narrative thread).\nRules: polls ask about habits and workdays (\"Do you take a real\nlunch break?\"), NEVER about symptoms or conditions (\"Are you\ndepressed?\" is prohibited). Last frame: soft CTA + where the full\ncontent lives. Announcement sequences include date/time/language\nand \"educational workshop\" labeling.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"e4-caption-hashtag-refresher\">E4 — Caption &amp; Hashtag Refresher</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write [N] caption variants for the finished visual described\nbelow, plus a hashtag set.\n\nVISUAL: [DESCRIBE or paste carousel slide map]\nGOAL: [saves / shares / registrations / discussion]\nLANGUAGE: [EN / AR / bilingual]\nRules: first line must work as a hook in feed preview; 60–120\nwords; one question; short disclaimer line; hashtags per the E1\nthree-tier system; no banned words.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"e5-linkedin-instagram-repurposing-engine\">E5 — LinkedIn → Instagram Repurposing Engine</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Repurpose the LinkedIn post below into an Instagram package.\n\nLINKEDIN POST: [PASTE — choose posts that performed well]\nPERFORMANCE NOTE: [e.g., \"highest comments this month; people\nresponded to the 'shutdown routine' idea\"]\n\nDELIVER:\n1. Recommended primary format for IG (carousel / reel / single\n   graphic) with one-line reasoning.\n2. The full asset via the matching prompt structure (E1 or E2).\n3. A story frame (E3-style) teasing it.\n4. What to CHANGE for IG audience (younger, more visual, more\n   Arabic-first): tone shifts, examples swapped, hook rewritten.\n5. What must NOT change: the takeaway, the boundary language, the\n   disclaimer.</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"part-f-graphics-design-brief-prompts\">PART F — GRAPHICS &amp; DESIGN BRIEF PROMPTS</h1>\n<h2 id=\"f1-graphic-design-brief-generator-single-asset\">F1 — Graphic Design Brief Generator (single asset)</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Write a complete design brief a designer (or Dorina using a\ndesign tool) can execute without asking questions.\n\nASSET: [e.g., \"LinkedIn quote graphic for the Oct 10 post\",\n\"workshop announcement square\", \"handout header\"]\nCONTENT (final approved text only — graphics are made AFTER text\napproval): [PASTE EXACT TEXT]\nPLATFORM + SIZE: [LinkedIn 1200×1200 / IG 1080×1350 / A4 print]\n\nBRIEF MUST SPECIFY:\n1. Purpose + the ONE thing a viewer should retain.\n2. Exact text placement hierarchy (headline / body / attribution /\n   disclaimer line — the disclaimer must be legible, not hidden).\n3. Brand look: calm, premium, trustworthy — [PALETTE: e.g., deep\n   teal + warm sand + off-white; NO red-alarm colors, no cliché\n   head-with-puzzle-piece or dark-storm imagery; imagery shows\n   real work life: desks, calendars, coffee, hands, plants].\n4. Typography direction: clean sans-serif; Arabic text set in a\n   proper Arabic typeface, never mirrored Latin; bilingual layouts\n   give Arabic equal visual dignity (right-aligned, correct\n   direction).\n5. Logo/name placement: \"Dorina Salman — [POSITIONING LINE]\".\n6. Accessibility: contrast ratio readable on mobile; minimum text\n   size; alt-text suggestion for the post (write it).\n7. File deliverables + export formats.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"f2-carousel-visual-specification\">F2 — Carousel Visual Specification</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Convert the approved carousel slide map below into a\nslide-by-slide visual spec.\n\nSLIDE MAP: [PASTE E1 OUTPUT — approved text only]\n\nPER SLIDE SPECIFY: layout (text position, size hierarchy) |\nbackground treatment | icon/illustration suggestion (simple, line-\nstyle, workplace-real; no clinical imagery — no brains, pills,\nstethoscopes, therapy couches) | slide number placement |\nprogress/continuity device (consistent color strip or motif) |\nswipe cue on slide 1.\nGLOBAL: palette + fonts consistent with the brand look in F1; the\nfinal slide includes the disclaimer line and handle; export\n1080×1350 PNG.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"f3-brand-template-system-brief\">F3 — Brand Template System Brief</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Define a reusable template system so every future graphic is\nconsistent and fast.\n\nTEMPLATES NEEDED: LinkedIn text-post graphic; LinkedIn carousel\nmaster (cover + content + closing slide); IG carousel master; IG\nstory master (tip + announcement variants); workshop announcement;\nhandout page (A4, print-safe); email header.\nFOR EACH: fixed elements (logo/name position, disclaimer slot,\npalette, fonts incl. Arabic typeface) vs. variable elements (text\nzones, image zone); character limits per text zone (write them);\none usage rule (\"never…\") to prevent drift.\nAlso produce: a one-paragraph brand-look description Dorina can\npaste into any design tool's AI generator, and a checklist the\ndesigner runs before export (spelling incl. Arabic diacritics,\ndisclaimer present, contrast, correct size per platform).</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"part-g-governance-qa-analytics-planning-prompts\">PART G — GOVERNANCE, QA, ANALYTICS &amp; PLANNING PROMPTS</h1>\n<h2 id=\"g1-approval-qa-reviewer-run-on-every-asset-before-dorina-app\">G1 — Approval QA Reviewer (run on EVERY asset before Dorina approves)</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: You are the pre-approval quality and safety reviewer. Audit\nthe asset below against every rule. Be strict; your job is to catch\nproblems, not to be agreeable.\n\nASSET TYPE: [post / carousel / email / handout / landing page /\nproposal]\nASSET: [PASTE FULL TEXT + description of visual if any]\n\nRUN THIS CHECKLIST AND REPORT PASS/FAIL PER ITEM WITH THE EXACT\nPROBLEM LINE QUOTED:\n1. BOUNDARY: zero clinical positioning; no banned words (therapy,\n   diagnose, treat, cure, patient, clinical, trauma expert…); no\n   implied credentials.\n2. CLAIMS: every factual claim traces to the four evidence-base\n   sources; no invented statistics, testimonials, or client\n   references; no outcome promises (\"will reduce your anxiety\").\n3. SAFETY: appropriate disclaimer present for the asset length;\n   referral pointer present where required (handouts, workshop\n   emails, landing pages); no content that could pressure\n   disclosure or resemble screening/diagnosis.\n4. VOICE: matches the Voice &amp; Style Block; hook works; one idea\n   only; no engagement bait; Lebanon-appropriate.\n5. MECHANICS: spelling (EN + AR), links, dates, names, hashtags,\n   platform length limits, alt-text present for graphics.\n6. LEGAL/ETHICS: consent confirmed for any quote/photo; no\n   identifiable third-party stories; no politics.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVE-READY / FIX-FIRST (with the fixes listed as exact\nrewrites) / REJECT (with reason).</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"g2-compliance-red-team\">G2 — Compliance Red-Team</h2>\n<p>Run monthly on a sample of published content, and on any high-stakes asset (landing page, proposal, lead magnet).</p>\n<pre><code>TASK: Act as a hostile reviewer with three hats, in sequence:\n1. A licensed clinician looking for scope-of-practice violations —\n   anything that could be read as therapy, diagnosis, or treatment\n   advice.\n2. A skeptical journalist looking for unsupported claims, invented\n   authority, or misleading framing.\n3. A distressed reader in crisis — trace what they would experience:\n   does every path lead them to qualified help quickly and warmly?\n\nASSETS TO REVIEW: [PASTE 3–5 RECENT ASSETS]\n\nFor each hat: list every issue found with severity (critical /\nmoderate / minor), the exact quoted line, and the corrected\nwording. End with a \"patterns to fix at the system level\" note if\nthe same issue appears more than once.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"g3-monthly-analytics-review\">G3 — Monthly Analytics Review</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Analyze last month's content performance and produce decisions,\nnot just observations.\n\nDATA (real numbers only; leave unknowns blank):\nLINKEDIN — followers start/end: [X/Y]; posts published: [N]; per-post:\n[paste table or list: topic | format | impressions | reactions |\ncomments | shares | profile views if known]\n{Optional INSTAGRAM — same structure + saves and story replies}\nBUSINESS SIGNALS — DMs/inquiries: [N + nature]; workshop leads: [N];\nnewsletter signups: [N]\n\nPRODUCE:\n1. Top 3 and bottom 3 posts, with a HYPOTHESIS for each (topic?\n   hook? format? day? — distinguish reach problems from resonance\n   problems).\n2. Format scoreboard (text vs. carousel vs. photo) on\n   engagement-per-impression, not raw reach.\n3. Audience signal: what are comments/DMs telling us people\n   actually struggle with? (aggregate themes only, no individuals)\n4. THREE decisions for next month: one thing to double, one to\n   change, one to drop. Justify each from the data above only.\n5. KPI tracker line vs. the 12-month calendar goals: on/off track,\n   and the single highest-leverage correction.\n6. Feed-forward note for the D1 calendar prompt next month\n   (write the exact \"What performed well recently\" line to paste).</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"g4-repurposing-engine-content-multiplication\">G4 — Repurposing Engine (content multiplication)</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Multiply the proven asset below into a repurposing package.\n\nSOURCE ASSET: [PASTE — a post/talk/workshop segment that performed\nwell] WHY IT WORKED: [one line from analytics]\n\nDELIVER:\n1. 3 NEW LinkedIn angles on the same core idea (different hook,\n   different audience entry point: the overloaded admin / the team\n   lead / the person working two jobs) — hooks + 2-line outlines\n   each, no duplication of the original's framing.\n2. 1 Instagram carousel slide map (E1 format).\n3. 1 reel concept (hook + 3 beats).\n4. 1 newsletter/article outline going deeper (D6 feed).\n5. 1 workshop micro-moment: how this idea becomes a 5-minute\n   exercise (B4 feed).\n6. A \"shelf date\": when this idea can be reposted fresh (min. 8\n   weeks) and what to update then.\nRule: the takeaway and boundary language stay identical across all\nderivatives; only the doorway changes.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"g5-monthly-planning-session-the-operating-rhythm\">G5 — Monthly Planning Session (the operating rhythm)</h2>\n<p>Run this once a month; it chains the other prompts together.</p>\n<pre><code>TASK: Facilitate my monthly planning session. Work through these\nsteps IN ORDER, waiting for my input at each step:\n\nSTEP 1 — REVIEW: Ask me for last month's analytics summary (output\nof G3). Reflect back the 3 decisions made there and confirm they\nshape this month.\nSTEP 2 — CONTEXT SCAN: Ask me for this month's realities: Lebanon\nseasonal context, awareness dates in range (check: World Mental\nHealth Day Oct 10; other dates from the 12-month calendar),\nworkshops scheduled, my available hours/week, energy level (be\nhonest — the plan must fit real capacity; a plan I can't keep\ndamages authority more than a lighter one).\nSTEP 3 — THEME: Propose the monthly theme from the 12-month\ncalendar, adjusted for Steps 1–2. One theme, one sentence.\nSTEP 4 — CALENDAR: Generate the D1 monthly calendar using\neverything above.\nSTEP 5 — BATCH LIST: Convert the calendar into a production\nchecklist: drafts needed (D2 runs), graphics needed (F1/F2 briefs),\nemails needed (B6/B7), each with a suggested batch day.\nSTEP 6 — APPROVAL SLOTS: Propose 2 fixed weekly review slots where\nDorina approves the coming week's content (nothing publishes\nwithout her sign-off), sized to the actual volume.\nSTEP 7 — ONE GROWTH ACTION: Propose exactly one non-content growth\naction this month (a partnership pitch C4, an outreach batch C3, a\nproposal B8) — the smallest one with the highest leverage.\nOutput the final plan as a single one-page summary I can pin.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"g6-awareness-day-campaign-planner\">G6 — Awareness-Day Campaign Planner</h2>\n<pre><code>TASK: Plan a focused campaign around [AWARENESS DATE, e.g., World\nMental Health Day — October 10].\n\nKNOWN GROUNDING: World Mental Health Day is October 10 (WHO). Past\nWHO campaign themes have included mental health in the workplace.\nLebanon's National Mental Health Programme has run national\nworkplace campaigns (e.g., \"Don't miss the opportunity / ما تضيعوا\nالفرصة\" with partners including WHO and others).\nTHIS YEAR'S OFFICIAL THEME: [FILL IN ONLY IF VERIFIED — otherwise\nbuild theme-agnostic and mark [VERIFY THEME BEFORE PUBLISHING]]\n\nDELIVER a 2-week arc (1 week before → day → 3 days after):\n1. Narrative arc in one sentence (what the audience should feel/\n   know/do by the end).\n2. Post-by-post plan (D1 table format): teaser, education,\n   Lebanon-context piece honoring the national programme's work,\n   day-of flagship post, personal-commitment post, wrap-up with\n   referral resources.\n3. One community action (e.g., inviting others to share one\n   workplace habit — participation, never disclosure of conditions).\n4. Optional workshop/webinar tie-in and its announcement copy.\n5. Graphics batch list (F1 feeds).\nRules: the day honors people living with mental health conditions —\ntone is dignity, not marketing; any campaign hashtags verified\nbefore use [VERIFY]; referral resources appear in every day-of\nasset.</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h1 id=\"appendix\">APPENDIX</h1>\n<h2 id=\"a-prompt-chaining-map-which-prompt-feeds-which\">A. Prompt-chaining map (which prompt feeds which)</h2>\n<pre><code>G5 Monthly Planning\n ├── G3 Analytics Review  (feeds Step 1)\n ├── D1 Monthly Calendar  (Step 4)\n │     └── D2 Individual Posts ──► G1 QA ──► Dorina approval ──► F1/F2 Graphics ──► G1 QA (visual) ──► Dorina approval ──► publish\n ├── B6/B7 Workshop emails (if workshops scheduled)\n └── C3/C4/B8 One growth action\n\nProven post ──► G4 Repurposing ──► E1/E2/E3 Instagram assets ──► G1 QA ──► approval\nQuarterly: A3 Strategy Review; Monthly: G2 Red-Team sample audit.</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"b-non-negotiables-summary\">B. Non-negotiables (summary)</h2>\n<ol><li>Master Context Block opens every conversation.</li><li>Every asset passes G1 QA before Dorina sees it; <strong>Dorina approves everything before it publishes</strong> — text first, then the final graphic.</li><li>No clinical language, no invented facts, no outcome promises — ever.</li><li>Disclaimers and referral blocks come verbatim from <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>; prompts reference them as <code>[REFERRAL BLOCK]</code> / <code>[FULL DISCLAIMER]</code> placeholders.</li><li>When information is missing, outputs say so — they never invent it.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-evidence-base\">C. Evidence base</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work (fact sheet): <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon Ministry of Public Health — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-what-we-still-need-from-dorina\">D. What we still need from Dorina</h2>\n<ul><li>Confirmed current role, employer, and dates for profile prompts (D5) — the prompts refuse to invent these.</li><li>Preferred AI assistant/tools, so wording quirks can be tuned after the first month of use.</li><li>Preferred Arabic register (formal MSA vs. Lebanese professional) for bilingual outputs.</li><li>Brand palette confirmation (the teal/sand proposal in F1 is a starting point, not a decision).</li><li>Consent process confirmation for any testimonial or participant quote before C1/B7 outputs use real quotes.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<p><em>This library is educational and strategic material. It does not constitute medical, psychological, legal, or HR advice. All generated content must respect the boundaries defined in <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>.</em></p>","toc":[{"id":"engineered-ai-prompts-for-every-asset-in-the-dorina-salman-a","text":"Engineered AI Prompts for Every Asset in the Dorina Salman Authority System","lvl":2},{"id":"how-to-use-this-library","text":"How to use this library","lvl":2},{"id":"01-master-context-block","text":"0.1 Master Context Block","lvl":2},{"id":"02-voice-style-block","text":"0.2 Voice & Style Block","lvl":2},{"id":"03-master-safety-block-for-workshop-and-sensitive-topic-asse","text":"0.3 Master Safety Block (for workshop and sensitive-topic assets)","lvl":2},{"id":"04-evidence-base-reference-list-for-any-prompt-that-asks-for","text":"0.4 Evidence base (reference list for any prompt that asks for sources)","lvl":2},{"id":"a1-strategy-document-generator-updater","text":"A1 — Strategy Document Generator / Updater","lvl":2},{"id":"a2-positioning-statement-bio-refresher","text":"A2 — Positioning Statement & Bio Refresher","lvl":2},{"id":"a3-quarterly-strategy-review","text":"A3 — Quarterly Strategy Review","lvl":2},{"id":"a4-service-offer-one-pager","text":"A4 — Service / Offer One-Pager","lvl":2},{"id":"b1-workshop-concept-designer","text":"B1 — Workshop Concept Designer","lvl":2},{"id":"b2-facilitator-guide-run-of-show-generator","text":"B2 — Facilitator Guide / Run-of-Show Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"b3-participant-handout-generator","text":"B3 — Participant Handout Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"b4-exercise-designer","text":"B4 — Exercise Designer","lvl":2},{"id":"b5-opening-closing-scripts-standalone","text":"B5 — Opening & Closing Scripts (standalone)","lvl":2},{"id":"b6-pre-workshop-email-sequence","text":"B6 — Pre-Workshop Email Sequence","lvl":2},{"id":"b7-post-workshop-follow-up-email-feedback-survey","text":"B7 — Post-Workshop Follow-Up (email + feedback survey)","lvl":2},{"id":"b8-corporate-workshop-proposal-generator","text":"B8 — Corporate Workshop Proposal Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"c1-landing-page-copy-generator","text":"C1 — Landing Page Copy Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"c2-lead-magnet-generator","text":"C2 — Lead Magnet Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"c3-dm-outreach-script-generator","text":"C3 — DM & Outreach Script Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"c4-partnership-pitch-generator","text":"C4 — Partnership Pitch Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"d1-monthly-linkedin-calendar-generator","text":"D1 — Monthly LinkedIn Calendar Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"d2-individual-linkedin-post-generator","text":"D2 — Individual LinkedIn Post Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"d3-post-rewrite-tightening","text":"D3 — Post Rewrite & Tightening","lvl":2},{"id":"d4-comment-engagement-reply-assistant","text":"D4 — Comment & Engagement Reply Assistant","lvl":2},{"id":"d5-linkedin-profile-section-refresher","text":"D5 — LinkedIn Profile Section Refresher","lvl":2},{"id":"d6-linkedin-article-newsletter-long-form","text":"D6 — LinkedIn Article / Newsletter Long-Form","lvl":2},{"id":"e1-instagram-carousel-generator","text":"E1 — Instagram Carousel Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"e2-instagram-reel-script-generator","text":"E2 — Instagram Reel Script Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"e3-instagram-stories-sequence-generator","text":"E3 — Instagram Stories Sequence Generator","lvl":2},{"id":"e4-caption-hashtag-refresher","text":"E4 — Caption & Hashtag Refresher","lvl":2},{"id":"e5-linkedin-instagram-repurposing-engine","text":"E5 — LinkedIn → Instagram Repurposing Engine","lvl":2},{"id":"f1-graphic-design-brief-generator-single-asset","text":"F1 — Graphic Design Brief Generator (single asset)","lvl":2},{"id":"f2-carousel-visual-specification","text":"F2 — Carousel Visual Specification","lvl":2},{"id":"f3-brand-template-system-brief","text":"F3 — Brand Template System Brief","lvl":2},{"id":"g1-approval-qa-reviewer-run-on-every-asset-before-dorina-app","text":"G1 — Approval QA Reviewer (run on EVERY asset before Dorina approves)","lvl":2},{"id":"g2-compliance-red-team","text":"G2 — Compliance Red-Team","lvl":2},{"id":"g3-monthly-analytics-review","text":"G3 — Monthly Analytics Review","lvl":2},{"id":"g4-repurposing-engine-content-multiplication","text":"G4 — Repurposing Engine (content multiplication)","lvl":2},{"id":"g5-monthly-planning-session-the-operating-rhythm","text":"G5 — Monthly Planning Session (the operating rhythm)","lvl":2},{"id":"g6-awareness-day-campaign-planner","text":"G6 — Awareness-Day Campaign Planner","lvl":2},{"id":"a-prompt-chaining-map-which-prompt-feeds-which","text":"A. Prompt-chaining map (which prompt feeds which)","lvl":2},{"id":"b-non-negotiables-summary","text":"B. Non-negotiables (summary)","lvl":2},{"id":"c-evidence-base","text":"C. Evidence base","lvl":2},{"id":"d-what-we-still-need-from-dorina","text":"D. What we still need from Dorina","lvl":2}],"words":7546,"mins":33},{"slug":"instagram-extension-engine","file":"INSTAGRAM_EXTENSION_ENGINE.md","title":"Instagram Extension Engine","blurb":"The month-7 expansion: account architecture, formats, repurposing pipeline, risks.","track":"Execution","n":14,"html":"<h1 id=\"instagram-extension-engine-dorina-salman\">Instagram Extension Engine — Dorina Salman</h1>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> The phase-2 channel. Instagram launches <strong>only after LinkedIn is stable</strong> (target: Month 7, ~February–March 2027, per the roadmap) and runs primarily as a <strong>repurposing engine</strong> — LinkedIn authority content, reshaped for a younger, more visual, more Arabic-first audience. It must never double Dorina's workload: the rule is <em>create once on LinkedIn, adapt twice for Instagram</em>.</p>\n<p>Everything here inherits the same boundaries: educational content only — never therapy, diagnosis, or crisis support; referral signposting always present; <strong>no post publishes without Dorina's approval of copy and visual.</strong></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-instagram-second-not-first-not-never\">1. Why Instagram second (not first, not never)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>LinkedIn first</strong> because the buyers (HR, managers, SME owners) and the professional-authority context live there.</li><li><strong>Instagram second</strong> because: (1) stigma-reduction and literacy content reaches young Lebanese professionals and the diaspora far better there; (2) workshop moments (photos, reels) are inherently visual; (3) WHO framing — mental health as a basic human right, literacy and awareness as recommended actions — deserves the wider, more personal channel; (4) future products (toolkits, open workshops) will sell to individuals, and individuals live on Instagram.</li><li><strong>Never before it's sustainable:</strong> launch criteria below are hard gates.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Launch gates (all must be true):</strong></p>\n<ol><li>LinkedIn cadence ≥90% consistency for 3 consecutive months.</li><li>At least 4 workshops delivered (so there is real visual material).</li><li>Visual identity finalized (palette, templates, Arabic typography).</li><li>Dorina confirms the extra ~30 min/week of approval time.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-account-architecture\">2. Account architecture</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Handle:</strong> @dorinasalman (or nearest available; consistent with LinkedIn URL).</li><li><strong>Type:</strong> Professional (Creator) account.</li><li><strong>Bio (draft):</strong> &gt; Workplace wellbeing educator 🇱🇧 | مدرِّبة توعية بالصحة النفسية ببيئة العمل &gt; Practical stress &amp; burnout prevention — systems, not slogans &gt; Not therapy — education. In crisis? Contact emergency services / a professional. &gt; 👇 Workshops &amp; resources</li><li><strong>Link:</strong> one link-in-bio page → workshop inquiry, LinkedIn profile, referral/resources page (the resources page lists professional pathways, aligned with Lebanon's National Mental Health Programme materials).</li><li><strong>Highlights:</strong> About • DESK Method • Workshops • Testimonials • Resources (referral signposts) • AR محتوى عربي.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-content-formats-mix\">3. Content formats &amp; mix</h2>\n<p>Cadence: <strong>2 feed posts + 2–3 stories per week</strong> (deliberately modest), 4 reels/month maximum.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Share</th><th>Source</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Carousels</strong></td><td>40%</td><td>Direct adaptation of LinkedIn carousels (DESK how-tos, WHO risk lists, boundary scripts)</td><td>Redesign to IG ratio (4:5), tighter text, Arabic-first versions prioritized</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reels (30–60s)</strong></td><td>30%</td><td>Talking-head micro-lessons; workshop clips (with participant consent); text-on-screen tips</td><td>Hook in first 2 seconds; captions always (silent viewing); bilingual subtitles</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quote/stat cards</strong></td><td>15%</td><td>WHO-attributed stats and Dorina's own aphorisms</td><td>Attribution on the image itself, not just the caption</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stories</strong></td><td>(weekly)</td><td>Behind-the-scenes, polls, question stickers, workshop days</td><td>Polls/questions double as content research for LinkedIn</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personal/advocacy posts</strong></td><td>15%</td><td>Story pillar; stigma-reduction; Arabic-first</td><td>Highest resonance format for the Lebanese/diaspora audience</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Arabic strategy:</strong> Instagram is the Arabic-first channel. Target ≥50% Arabic or bilingual content (vs. LinkedIn's English-lean). Advocacy and stigma content leads in Arabic; systems content can stay bilingual.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-the-repurposing-pipeline-create-once-adapt-twice\">4. The repurposing pipeline (create once, adapt twice)</h2>\n<p>Weekly flow, attached to the existing Thursday–Friday production rhythm:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Select:</strong> from this week's LinkedIn posts (and the archive), pick the 2 best candidates for IG (criteria: visual potential, emotional resonance, evergreen value).</li><li><strong>Adapt copy:</strong> shorten to IG caption norms — hook line, 3–6 short lines, question or soft CTA, hashtags (8–12 mixed AR/EN, e.g., #الصحة_النفسية #WorkplaceWellbeing #Lebanon #BurnoutPrevention).</li><li><strong>Adapt visual:</strong> re-layout to 4:5 or 9:16 using the IG template set (<code>PREMIUM_PROMPT_LIBRARY.md</code> §G4–G5).</li><li><strong>Approval gate:</strong> copy + visual reviewed and approved by Dorina alongside the LinkedIn batch (same Friday session, ~30 extra minutes).</li><li><strong>Schedule:</strong> approved posts only; posting days Tuesday + Saturday (offset from LinkedIn days to spread presence).</li><li><strong>Engage:</strong> 10 min/day, 3 days/week — replies, story interactions. DMs disclosing personal distress get the referral response from <code>RISK_ETHICS_AND_REFERRAL_PROTOCOL.md</code>, verbatim, always.</li></ol>\n<p>Reels production is batched monthly: one recording session (4 reels), scripts pre-approved, edited clips re-approved before posting.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-90-day-launch-plan-months-79-of-the-master-roadmap\">5. 90-day launch plan (Months 7–9 of the master roadmap)</h2>\n<p><strong>Weeks 1–2 — Seeding:</strong> bio, highlights, link page live; 6 foundation posts published over two weeks (who I am, what I do / don't do, DESK overview carousel, best-of-LinkedIn adaptation ×2, one Arabic advocacy post); announce on LinkedIn. <strong>Weeks 3–6 — Rhythm:</strong> standard cadence; first reel (workshop clip or micro-lesson); first story poll series (data harvested for content). <strong>Weeks 7–12 — Compound:</strong> testimonial content; collaboration posts with one partner account (HR community, university club, or aligned organization); Ramadan-aware module if dates align (<strong>confirm annually</strong>); first IG-driven workshop inquiry expected by week 12.</p>\n<p><strong>90-day KPIs:</strong> 500+ followers, 8%+ average engagement on carousels, 2+ workshop inquiries attributable to IG, ≥90% posting consistency, zero boundary incidents.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-channel-specific-risk-notes\">6. Channel-specific risk notes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Younger, more vulnerable audience:</strong> IG comments/DMs are likelier to include personal disclosures. The referral response script is mandatory, unmodified; no advice-giving in DMs, ever. Comment moderation: hide abusive content, never argue publicly.</li><li><strong>Aesthetic drift:</strong> IG rewards \"wellness aesthetics\" — resist lotus-and-beach clichés; keep the calm-authority identity (<code>POSITIONING_AND_BRAND_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> §10) so the brand stays educational, not lifestyle.</li><li><strong>Workshop footage:</strong> only with written participant consent (consent line built into workshop registration — see <code>WORKSHOP_SYSTEM_BLUEPRINT.md</code> §7); default to audience-free angles.</li><li><strong>Every advocacy post carries the short disclaimer</strong> in caption or image footer: educational content — not therapy or crisis support; seek qualified help / emergency services if struggling.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-we-still-need-from-dorina-ig-specific\">7. What we still need from Dorina (IG-specific)</h2>\n<ol><li>Handle preference and confirmation she wants IG at all in year one (the engine is optional; LinkedIn alone can carry year one).</li><li>Comfort on camera for reels — talking-head vs. text-on-screen-only determines the reel format mix.</li><li>Arabic register preference for captions (Lebanese dialect vs. Modern Standard Arabic — recommendation: dialect for stories/reels, MSA-leaning for carousels).</li><li>Consent workflow sign-off for workshop footage.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-sources\">8. Sources</h2>\n<ol><li>WHO — Mental health at work: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work</a></li><li>WHO — Mental health: strengthening our response: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response</a></li><li>Lebanon MoPH — National Mental Health Programme: <a href=\"https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.moph.gov.lb/en/Pages/6/553/the-national-mental-health-program</a></li><li>WHO — World Mental Health Day: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-mental-health-day</a></li></ol>\n<p><em>Educational and strategic material only — not medical, psychological, legal, or HR advice.</em></p>","toc":[{"id":"1-why-instagram-second-not-first-not-never","text":"1. Why Instagram second (not first, not never)","lvl":2},{"id":"2-account-architecture","text":"2. Account architecture","lvl":2},{"id":"3-content-formats-mix","text":"3. Content formats & mix","lvl":2},{"id":"4-the-repurposing-pipeline-create-once-adapt-twice","text":"4. The repurposing pipeline (create once, adapt twice)","lvl":2},{"id":"5-90-day-launch-plan-months-79-of-the-master-roadmap","text":"5. 90-day launch plan (Months 7–9 of the master roadmap)","lvl":2},{"id":"6-channel-specific-risk-notes","text":"6. Channel-specific risk notes","lvl":2},{"id":"7-what-we-still-need-from-dorina-ig-specific","text":"7. What we still need from Dorina (IG-specific)","lvl":2},{"id":"8-sources","text":"8. Sources","lvl":2}],"words":1054,"mins":5},{"slug":"external-strategic-review","file":"EXTERNAL_STRATEGIC_REVIEW.md","title":"External Strategic Review","blurb":"Independent verdict, issues by severity, gating checklist, improvements incorporated.","track":"Governance","n":15,"html":"<h1 id=\"external-strategic-review-dorina-salman-mental-health-manage\">External Strategic Review — Dorina Salman Mental Health Management Authority Package</h1>\n<p><strong>Scope of this review:</strong> I assessed the package through the consolidated review brief, which includes the full deliverable list, section headings, and representative excerpts from all 15 documents. A handful of findings below concern internal consistency and completeness that can only be <em>confirmed</em> against the full-text files; I've flagged those explicitly as \"verify.\" Everything else is judged on the brief as written.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-overall-verdict\">1. Overall verdict</h2>\n<p><strong>Strong package, not yet sendable. Conditional pass — ship after the Critical and High fixes below.</strong></p>\n<p>This is genuinely premium, execution-ready work. The central strategic decision — building authority on an explicit, binding <em>non-clinical</em> boundary and treating that boundary as the product rather than a limitation — is both ethically right and commercially astute, and it is carried consistently across every document. The positioning wedge (administrative-systems craft applied to workplace wellbeing) occupies real white space. The operational depth (run sheets, verbatim scripts, handouts, objection handling, approval gates) is well beyond typical strategy decks.</p>\n<p>It is held back from \"send\" status by a small number of issues that are disproportionately important because of the subject matter: safety-critical facts that must be verified (crisis numbers, Arabic crisis copy), an internal-tooling name that leaks in a sendable artifact, a core biographical claim that may be factually wrong, and a few coherence/integrity gaps that a careful recipient will notice. None are structural; all are fixable in a focused editing pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-issues-by-severity\">2. Issues by severity</h2>\n<h3 id=\"critical-do-not-send-until-resolved\">Critical — do not send until resolved</h3>\n<p><strong>C1. Verify every crisis and emergency number before this touches a real person.</strong> The referral and crisis scripts hard-code Lebanese contact points (a national support line and an emergency number). In a mental-health context, a wrong or out-of-date number in a crisis script is a direct safety harm, not a typo. Confirm the current national emotional-support/suicide-prevention line, its operating hours (if it is not 24/7, the crisis script's \"can we call together right now?\" needs a fallback), and the correct emergency number for a medical emergency versus general emergency. Treat this as a blocking, independently-verified item.</p>\n<p><strong>C2. The review deliverable's filename leaks internal tooling.</strong> The fifteenth document (the \"external review\" file) is currently named after the internal review tool/model, and its headers likely repeat it. This directly violates the package's own scrub rule and would expose internal tooling the moment the folder or portal is shared. Rename it (e.g., <em>External Strategic Review</em>), strip the tool/model name from its headers, and update every reference to it in the README, index page, and ZIP.</p>\n<p><strong>C3. Run a complete forbidden-terms scrub across all files, the HTML portal, and the ZIP.</strong> The leaked filename in C2 is proof the scrub is not finished. Do a full automated sweep for every term on the brief's exclusion list before packaging. This is a two-minute check that prevents an embarrassing and trust-damaging leak.</p>\n<p><strong>C4. Confirm the core employment facts before publishing the profile and story.</strong> The entire brand narrative rests on \"twelve years behind the executive desk\" — it anchors the headline, the About section, a flagship long-form article title, and the proposal's credibility case. The brief itself notes this conflicts with the stated current role (Administrator at a marketing company) and the \"Feb 2014–Present\" law-firm entry. Publishing an inaccurate work history on a public profile undermines the very authority the package is building. Lock down: current employer, whether the law-firm role has ended, and the honest way to phrase tenure. This also raises C4b: <strong>the current-employer conflict</strong> — building a public wellbeing brand and soliciting workshops while employed full-time at a marketing firm may breach an employment contract or create a conflict; the \"what we still need from Dorina\" set should explicitly ask about moonlighting constraints and runway.</p>\n<p><strong>C5. Have all Arabic copy reviewed by a native Lebanese-Arabic professional — especially the crisis and referral scripts.</strong> Colloquial tone, register, and gendered forms in a crisis message are high-stakes; a near-miss reads as inauthentic or, worse, cold at the exact moment it matters most. The package already shows good instincts (gendered verb forms, \"confirm dialect comfort\" notes), but the crisis/referral Arabic must be validated by a person, not assumed correct.</p>\n<h3 id=\"high-fix-before-sending\">High — fix before sending</h3>\n<p><strong>H1. Resolve the Instagram-timing contradiction.</strong> The Instagram engine and the content calendar place the IG launch at <strong>Month 7 (~February–March 2027)</strong> behind hard launch gates, but the README reading-order and the Prompt Library's Instagram section label it <strong>\"Month 7+.\"</strong> Pick one (the gated Month-7 logic is the well-argued version) and correct the others. Contradictions like this puncture the \"premium, coherent\" impression.</p>\n<p><strong>H2. Verify the safety-gate prompts actually exist in the Prompt Library.</strong> The approval workflow and the library's own instructions depend on a specific automated boundary/fact-check prompt and a QA prompt, referenced by number/section. The inventory in the brief only shows Parts A–E (through the Instagram prompts) and no corresponding section. The safety workflow cannot cite a control that isn't present. Confirm those prompts — and the graphics/automation prompts the introduction promises — are actually written and correctly numbered, or fix the references.</p>\n<p><strong>H3. Correct the Ramadan 2027 scheduling.</strong> The flagship \"Ramadan-aware\" module sits in Month 7 (March 2027), but Ramadan 2027 is expected to begin in <strong>early-to-mid February 2027</strong> (subject to local sighting) and to run mostly through February into early March. As placed, the primary module lands largely <em>after</em> Ramadan for that year. Center it on Month 6 (February), keep March as the tail, and retain the excellent \"confirm by local sighting each year\" rule.</p>\n<p><strong>H4. Fix dangling and incorrect internal cross-references.</strong> The marketing document points to a file that isn't in the package (a \"LinkedIn profile optimization\" file that is actually the LinkedIn Authority Engine), hedged with \"if in package.\" For a premium deliverable, run a full internal link audit so every cross-reference resolves.</p>\n<p><strong>H5. Add the pre-launch safety and legal prerequisites the package currently omits.</strong> Three gaps that matter precisely because the work is emotionally charged: (a) <strong>professional/public liability insurance</strong> for running workshops — check availability and cost in Lebanon; (b) <strong>local legal review</strong> of the disclaimers, the trademark claim, and the employment/moonlighting question — the package should not assert legal protection as settled fact (see M4); (c) a <strong>lightweight, non-clinical credential/training track</strong> — e.g., Psychological First Aid or Mental Health First Aid for lay facilitators, plus a facilitation certificate. This would materially harden both the authority claim and the safety posture for someone with no facilitation or mental-health-adjacent training running rooms where disclosures <em>will</em> happen. Frame it strictly as literacy/facilitation training, never as clinical practice.</p>\n<p><strong>H6. State an honest total time budget.</strong> \"~2–3 hours/week\" recurs as if it covers the whole engine, but it really covers content production. Add daily engagement, weekly marketing review, workshop prep and delivery, and the approval gates, and the real figure is meaningfully higher — an important reality for someone employed full-time. A one-line weekly-time table prevents an early credibility gap between plan and lived experience.</p>\n<h3 id=\"medium-should-fix\">Medium — should fix</h3>\n<p><strong>M1. Trademark reality-check on the signature framework.</strong> The framework is used pervasively with a  symbol. The term is descriptive for desk/office wellbeing, may already be in use, and has had no availability search. Before using  in public materials, do a name search in Lebanon and any target markets; otherwise drop the  or footnote it as \"pending.\" An unexamined trademark claim on public documents is a small but real overreach.</p>\n<p><strong>M2. Recalibrate growth KPIs as ambitious/stretch.</strong> Going from ~4 to 1,500+ connections and 2,500+ followers in 12 months for a brand-new niche voice is optimistic; LinkedIn's invitation throttles (roughly ~100/week, often lower) cap the connection pace regardless of effort. Keep the targets as stretch goals, label them as such, and add a floor/expected band so early months don't read as failure.</p>\n<p><strong>M3. Pressure-test the Lebanon monetization assumptions.</strong> Given the economic environment, domestic corporate training budgets are thin and volatile. Ensure the \"conservative\" revenue model does not over-index on paid domestic corporate workshops; the diaspora/GCC remote offering and USD-\"fresh\" pricing are likely the real year-one revenue engine. The Opportunity Map's constraints panel is the right place to make this explicit.</p>\n<p><strong>M4. Soften legal overclaims.</strong> The summary states the boundary \"reduces legal/reputational risk when reviewed with local counsel.\" Downgrade to risk-*reduction* language and defer to local counsel. A package this careful about not overclaiming clinical outcomes should apply the same discipline to legal claims about itself.</p>\n<p><strong>M5. Add a safeguarding log to the metrics.</strong> KPIs are growth- and revenue-weighted. For this subject matter, also track a small safeguarding set — referrals made, protocol adherence, any crisis-adjacent events — so the ethical commitments are measured, not just asserted.</p>\n<p><strong>M6. De-emphasize \"manage your mental health\" in public hero copy.</strong> The package does careful work to redefine \"mental health management\" as non-clinical self-management, but the phrase still reads as quasi-clinical to a lay audience. Lead public-facing copy with \"workplace wellbeing\" and \"mental health literacy\"; reserve \"mental health management\" for the internal, carefully-scoped framing where it's already well-defined.</p>\n<p><strong>M7. Reconcile the README with the full deliverable set.</strong> The reading-order table lists 13 documents; the package contains 15. Decide intentionally whether the external-review file and the HTML portal appear in the reading order, and make the counts consistent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"low-polish\">Low — polish</h3>\n<p><strong>L1.</strong> Name the other major Lebanese seasonal moments explicitly in the seasonal spine — Christmas and Easter (Lebanon's mixed calendar), Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha, and Independence Day (Nov 22) — rather than folding them into generic \"year-end.\"</p>\n<p><strong>L2.</strong> Avoid stacking the Instagram launch, the highest-Arabic-content push, and the Ramadan module in the same month; that's a heavy load during a lower-energy period. Consider sequencing.</p>\n<p><strong>L3.</strong> Keep \"emotional organization\" tightly tethered to the administrative-systems metaphor so it doesn't drift into reading like emotion-regulation therapy.</p>\n<p><strong>L4.</strong> Fold name-spelling confirmation and the \"implied third language\" (don't fabricate it) into the same fact-confirmation pass as C4.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-required-edits-before-sending-gating-checklist\">3. Required edits before sending (gating checklist)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Verify and, if needed, correct all crisis/emergency numbers and helpline hours; add a fallback in the crisis script.</strong> (C1)</li><li><strong>Rename the external-review file, strip the internal tool/model name from it, and update all links.</strong> (C2)</li><li><strong>Run a full forbidden-terms scrub across every file, the HTML portal, and the ZIP.</strong> (C3)</li><li><strong>Confirm employment facts and current-employer constraints; fix the \"12 years / executive desk\" narrative to match reality before any profile goes live.</strong> (C4)</li><li><strong>Native Lebanese-Arabic review of all Arabic copy, crisis/referral scripts first.</strong> (C5)</li><li><strong>Reconcile the Instagram launch timing to one month across all documents.</strong> (H1)</li><li><strong>Confirm the referenced safety-check and QA prompts (and the promised graphics/automation prompts) actually exist and are correctly numbered.</strong> (H2)</li><li><strong>Move the Ramadan module to February 2027 as primary; keep the annual-confirmation rule.</strong> (H3)</li><li><strong>Audit and fix all internal cross-references.</strong> (H4)</li><li><strong>Add the insurance, local legal review, and non-clinical training prerequisites; add an honest total-time budget.</strong> (H5, H6)</li></ol>\n<p>Items 1–5 are true blockers. Items 6–10 should be done in the same pass so the package goes out coherent and complete.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-strengths-keep-these-intact\">4. Strengths (keep these intact)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>The non-clinical boundary as strategy, not disclaimer.</strong> Making the credential gap the <em>foundation</em> of credibility — and encoding it in a binding protocol that explicitly supersedes every other document — is the package's best idea. It is ethically sound and commercially smart: it lowers the liability friction that would otherwise make companies hesitate to book.</li><li><strong>Disciplined evidence base.</strong> Grounding in WHO and Lebanon's national programme, with strict attribution and a \"don't invent data\" rule, gives the authority claim a real spine and models the behavior the brand needs.</li><li><strong>Safety-forward operations.</strong> The approval gates (human sign-off on <em>both</em> text and graphic, no autopost, a STOP protocol), the tiered red-flag system, and crisis scripts that never promise secrecy and hand over rather than treat are responsible and rehearsable.</li><li><strong>Genuinely differentiated positioning.</strong> \"Administrative systems × wellbeing\" and \"emotional organization\" is a distinct archetype in a field crowded with clinicians and motivational speakers — credible without a clinical license and aligned to how work actually breaks people.</li><li><strong>Execution-ready, not aspirational.</strong> Verbatim run sheets, exercises, handouts, DM and objection scripts, feedback forms, and phased pilots-before-paid mean the recipient can start within weeks, not re-plan for months.</li><li><strong>Local and bilingual credibility.</strong> Arabic delivery, Ramadan sensitivity, and alignment with (rather than competition against) the national ecosystem make this land as Lebanon-native.</li><li><strong>Good consulting hygiene.</strong> The recurring \"what we still need from Dorina\" sections surface open questions without blocking the plan — exactly right for a package that depends on facts only she can confirm.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> the thinking is sound and the craft is high. Clear the five Critical items and the five High-priority edits, and this is a confidently sendable, premium package.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"improvements-incorporated-after-review\">Improvements Incorporated After Review</h2>\n<p>The following fixes were applied before packaging:</p>\n<ol><li>The external-review file was renamed to a recipient-safe title and internal model/tool language was removed from the sendable package.</li><li>Instagram timing was reconciled to <strong>Month 7+</strong>, after LinkedIn is stable and approval workflow is working.</li><li>Ramadan-aware content was corrected to a <strong>February–March 2027 window</strong>, with annual confirmation required because Ramadan dates shift by lunar sighting.</li><li>Legal language was softened from “protects legally” to “reduces risk”; Dorina is instructed to seek local legal/employment review before paid workshops.</li><li>The signature framework was changed from a trademark-style label to a proposed brand framework pending name/trademark checks.</li><li>Non-clinical prerequisites were made explicit: Psychological First Aid or comparable mental-health-literacy training, facilitation practice, referral map verification, and insurance/legal checks before paid delivery.</li><li>The time budget was clarified: content alone can be 2–3 hours/week, but the full authority engine requires a larger weekly operating rhythm.</li><li>A safeguarding metrics layer was added: referrals made, boundary events, workshop stop/debrief notes, and protocol adherence are tracked alongside growth KPIs.</li><li>Crisis information was narrowed to source-grounded wording and a verification checkpoint: Embrace Lebanon publicly lists <strong>1564</strong> for the Lifeline; all crisis/emergency numbers and operating hours must be re-verified before printing or delivery.</li><li>Generic references to “nonprofit/humanitarian organization” were changed to “nonprofit/humanitarian organizations” to avoid confusing the package with any unrelated project.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"remaining-human-checks-before-public-use\">Remaining Human Checks Before Public Use</h2>\n<p>These cannot be solved by documentation alone and must be confirmed by Dorina or a qualified local reviewer:</p>\n<ul><li>Dorina’s exact current employment facts, dates, and any employer moonlighting/conflict restrictions.</li><li>Native Lebanese-Arabic review of all public Arabic copy, especially referral and crisis scripts.</li><li>Local legal review of disclaimers, workshop terms, privacy boundaries, and paid-service structure.</li><li>Current official crisis/emergency contacts and hours immediately before printing handouts or running workshops.</li><li>Dorina’s actual completed training/certificates; no credential wording goes public until completed.</li></ul>","toc":[{"id":"1-overall-verdict","text":"1. Overall verdict","lvl":2},{"id":"2-issues-by-severity","text":"2. Issues by severity","lvl":2},{"id":"critical-do-not-send-until-resolved","text":"Critical — do not send until resolved","lvl":3},{"id":"high-fix-before-sending","text":"High — fix before sending","lvl":3},{"id":"medium-should-fix","text":"Medium — should fix","lvl":3},{"id":"low-polish","text":"Low — polish","lvl":3},{"id":"3-required-edits-before-sending-gating-checklist","text":"3. Required edits before sending (gating checklist)","lvl":2},{"id":"4-strengths-keep-these-intact","text":"4. Strengths (keep these intact)","lvl":2},{"id":"improvements-incorporated-after-review","text":"Improvements Incorporated After Review","lvl":2},{"id":"remaining-human-checks-before-public-use","text":"Remaining Human Checks Before Public Use","lvl":2}],"words":2454,"mins":11}];
